(window.__KSHC_DATA=window.__KSHC_DATA||{})["lawBank"]={"questions":[{"id":"civpro-diversity-v2","prompt":"Pilar, a California citizen, sues Dev, a Nevada citizen, in federal court on a state-law fraud claim. She seeks $70,000 in compensatory damages and $10,000 in punitive damages, which the governing state law allows for fraud. Dev moves to dismiss, arguing that a jury will probably award less than $75,000. How should the court rule?","choices":["Deny the motion, because available punitive damages count toward the $80,000 demand.","Grant the motion, because punitive damages are excluded when computing the amount in controversy.","Grant the motion, because Pilar must prove a likely recovery above $75,000 by a preponderance.","Deny the motion, because interest and costs may be added to the claim to reach the $75,000 minimum."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a), the sum the plaintiff claims in good faith controls unless it appears to a legal certainty that she cannot recover more than $75,000 (St. Paul Mercury Indemnity Co. v. Red Cab Co.). Punitive damages count when the governing law permits them, so the $80,000 demand suffices even if the likely verdict is lower. A plaintiff filing originally in federal court need not prove the amount by a preponderance, and § 1332(a) expressly excludes interest and costs.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["diversity","jurisdiction","amount-in-controversy"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1332 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1332"}]},{"id":"civpro-personal-jx","prompt":"Orchid Tool Co., a Florida corporation that does no regular business outside Florida, agrees to build one custom milling machine for Baylight, a California company. Orchid's sales manager negotiates the deal through repeated calls and emails to Baylight in California, and Orchid ships the machine to Baylight's California plant, where it breaks down. Baylight sues Orchid in California for breach of warranty. May the California court exercise personal jurisdiction over Orchid?","choices":["No, because Orchid is not at home in California and does no continuous or systematic business there.","Yes, because Orchid purposefully directed this deal at California, and Baylight's claim arises from it.","No, because one contract with a forum company is too isolated a contact to support specific jurisdiction.","Yes, because it was foreseeable that a machine sold to a California buyer could fail in California."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Specific jurisdiction requires purposeful availment of the forum, a claim that arises out of or relates to those contacts, and consistency with fair play and substantial justice. A contract with a forum resident is not automatically enough (Burger King v. Rudzewicz), but Orchid actively negotiated with a California company and shipped the product into California, and the warranty claim arises from that very transaction (cf. McGee v. International Life). General jurisdiction is unnecessary when specific jurisdiction exists, and mere foreseeability that a product may end up in the forum is not the test (World-Wide Volkswagen v. Woodson).","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["personal-jurisdiction","minimum-contacts","specific-jurisdiction"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. XIV (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fourteenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-content-based","prompt":"A city ordinance bans signs bearing political messages on public sidewalks but allows signs advertising goods or services. A candidate who is fined for placing a campaign sign on a sidewalk challenges the ordinance under the First Amendment. What standard will the court most likely apply?","choices":["Intermediate scrutiny, because rules about where signs may be placed are time, place, and manner rules.","The Central Hudson test, because the ordinance's exemption is limited to commercial advertising.","Strict scrutiny, because the ordinance treats signs differently based on the subject of their message.","Reasonableness review, because the city owns the sidewalks and may decide which uses fit them."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Public sidewalks are traditional public forums, and a law that applies or not depending on the topic or message of a sign is content based on its face, triggering strict scrutiny regardless of the city's benign motive (Reed v. Town of Gilbert). The time, place, and manner test applies only to content-neutral rules, Central Hudson governs restrictions on commercial speech rather than a ban on political speech, and reasonableness review applies only in limited or nonpublic forums.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["speech","public-forum","content-based"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-gender-v2","prompt":"A state nursing school reserves half its seats for women because the legislature believes women are naturally better caregivers. A qualified male applicant who was rejected challenges the policy under the Equal Protection Clause. Which is the best analysis?","choices":["Strict scrutiny applies, and the policy likely fails because it uses an explicit sex classification.","Rational-basis review applies, and the policy likely survives because it benefits women rather than burdening them.","Intermediate scrutiny applies, and the policy likely survives because it compensates women for past discrimination.","Intermediate scrutiny applies, and the policy likely fails because it rests on a sex stereotype."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Sex classifications receive intermediate scrutiny: the state must show an exceedingly persuasive justification, meaning the classification is substantially related to an important governmental objective (United States v. Virginia). Generalizations about the abilities of men and women cannot supply that justification, and in Mississippi University for Women v. Hogan a women-only nursing school failed because women were not disadvantaged in nursing, so the policy perpetuated rather than remedied a stereotype. Sex classifications are not reviewed under strict scrutiny, and \"benign\" classifications still receive intermediate scrutiny.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["equal-protection","gender","intermediate-scrutiny"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. XIV (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fourteenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"civpro-federal-question","prompt":"To settle an injury claim, a railroad gave Rosa and Ivan lifetime free train passes. Years later the railroad stopped honoring the passes, citing a new federal statute that bans free passes. Rosa and Ivan, who are citizens of the same state as the railroad, sue in federal court for breach of contract, alleging in their complaint that the statute does not apply to them. Does the court have subject-matter jurisdiction?","choices":["No, because the federal statute enters the case only as an anticipated defense.","Yes, because the complaint expressly raises and discusses the meaning of the federal statute.","Yes, because the outcome of the case will turn on how the federal statute is interpreted.","No, because federal-question cases also require more than $75,000 in controversy."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the well-pleaded complaint rule, 28 U.S.C. § 1331 jurisdiction exists only when the plaintiff's own claim, properly pleaded, arises under federal law. Rosa and Ivan's claim is for breach of contract; the statute matters only because the railroad will raise it as a defense, and anticipating that defense in the complaint does not create arising-under jurisdiction (Louisville & Nashville R.R. v. Mottley). Federal-question jurisdiction has no amount-in-controversy requirement, and diversity is absent.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["subject-matter","federal-question","well-pleaded-complaint"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1331 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1331"}]},{"id":"civpro-supplemental","prompt":"Nadia sues her former employer in federal court, alleging that she was fired because of her race in violation of Title VII. In the same complaint, she adds a state-law claim that the employer failed to pay her final week's wages when it fired her. Nadia and the employer are citizens of the same state. May the federal court hear the wage claim?","choices":["No, because a state-law claim between nondiverse parties needs an independent basis of jurisdiction.","Yes, because it arises from the same firing and so is part of the same case or controversy.","Yes, because Rule 18 allows a plaintiff to join any claim she has against an opposing party.","No, because § 1367(b) bars supplemental claims asserted by plaintiffs against defendants."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under 28 U.S.C. § 1367(a), a federal court with original jurisdiction over one claim may hear related claims that form part of the same Article III case or controversy, meaning they share a common nucleus of operative fact (United Mine Workers v. Gibbs). The wage claim arises from the same termination as the Title VII claim. Rule 18 permits joinder of claims but never supplies subject-matter jurisdiction, and § 1367(b) limits only claims in cases founded solely on diversity.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["supplemental-jurisdiction","cnoff","1367"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1367 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1367"}]},{"id":"civpro-venue-transfer-v2","prompt":"A diversity action is properly filed in a federal court in State A. On the defendant's motion, the court transfers it to a federal court in State B under 28 U.S.C. § 1404(a) for the convenience of parties and witnesses. There is no forum-selection clause. Which choice-of-law rules should the transferee court apply?","choices":["State B's rules, because a federal court in diversity applies the law of the state where it sits.","State B's rules, because the defendant rather than the plaintiff asked for the transfer.","State A's rules, because a § 1404(a) convenience transfer does not change the governing law.","Federal choice-of-law rules, because a transferred case needs a single uniform rule of decision."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"After a § 1404(a) convenience transfer from a proper venue, the transferee court applies the choice-of-law rules the transferor court would have applied, so a transfer changes courtrooms but not law (Van Dusen v. Barrack). The rule applies even when the plaintiff seeks the transfer (Ferens v. John Deere), so who moved does not matter. Klaxon's forum-state rule is modified by Van Dusen, and different rules apply to a § 1406(a) transfer from an improper venue or a transfer enforcing a forum-selection clause (Atlantic Marine).","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["venue","transfer","choice-of-law","1404"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1404 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1404"}]},{"id":"conlaw-state-action-v2","prompt":"A private corporation owns and operates every street, park, utility, and municipal service in a company town. It bars a resident from distributing religious leaflets on the town's main sidewalk. May the resident bring a First Amendment claim against the corporation?","choices":["No, because the First Amendment restricts only governments, not private owners of property.","Yes, because a private owner who opens its sidewalks to the public must permit expressive activity there.","No, because a private corporation becomes a state actor only if it receives substantial public funding.","Yes, because running an entire town is a function traditionally and exclusively governmental."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Constitutional rights ordinarily constrain only government, but a private party that performs a function traditionally and exclusively reserved to the state is treated as a state actor. Running all the functions of a municipality fits that narrow public-function exception (Marsh v. Alabama). Merely opening private property to the public does not, as the shopping-center cases show (Hudgens v. NLRB), and public funding alone does not create state action (Rendell-Baker v. Kohn).","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["state-action","public-function"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-dormant-commerce","prompt":"To conserve space in its privately owned landfills, State X prohibits disposing of any waste generated outside the state in those landfills, while continuing to allow in-state waste. Congress has not legislated on the subject. An out-of-state waste hauler challenges the law. Is the law likely constitutional?","choices":["No, because it discriminates against out-of-state waste and nondiscriminatory alternatives exist.","Yes, because conserving landfill space is a legitimate purpose and the burden is not clearly excessive.","Yes, because waste has no economic value and so is not an article of interstate commerce.","No, because any state law that burdens interstate commerce is invalid unless Congress approves it."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A law that discriminates against interstate commerce on its face is virtually per se invalid unless the state shows a legitimate local purpose that cannot be adequately served by reasonable nondiscriminatory alternatives (Maine v. Taylor). State X could conserve capacity by limiting all waste, so excluding only out-of-state waste fails (City of Philadelphia v. New Jersey, which also held that waste is an article of commerce). Pike balancing applies only to nondiscriminatory laws, and the market-participant exception does not apply because the landfills are privately owned.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["commerce-clause","dormant-commerce","discrimination"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (Commerce Clause) (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articlei"}]},{"id":"civpro-removal-defense","prompt":"Leah sues Corvan Inc., a citizen of her own state, in state court for breach of a commercial lease. Corvan's answer asserts that a federal statute preempts the state-law claim, and Corvan promptly removes, invoking federal-question jurisdiction. The federal statute does not completely preempt the field or supply a federal cause of action. Leah moves to remand. Should the court remand?","choices":["No, because a preemption defense necessarily raises a substantial and disputed question of federal law.","Yes, because a federal defense, including preemption, does not make a case arise under federal law.","No, because Corvan removed promptly and Leah did not object before the notice was filed.","Yes, because removal on federal-question grounds requires the plaintiff's consent."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Removal under 28 U.S.C. § 1441(a) requires that the case could have been filed originally in federal court, and under the well-pleaded complaint rule a federal question must appear in the plaintiff's claim, not in a defense (Caterpillar Inc. v. Williams). Ordinary preemption is a defense and does not support removal; only the narrow complete-preemption doctrine (for example, LMRA § 301 or ERISA § 502(a)) converts a state claim into a federal one. Promptness of removal does not cure a lack of jurisdiction, and a defendant needs no consent from the plaintiff to remove.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["removal","federal-question","well-pleaded-complaint"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1441 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1441"}]},{"id":"civpro-erie","prompt":"A Pennsylvania citizen sues a New York corporation in federal court in Pennsylvania on a state-law negligence claim arising from an accident in Pennsylvania. The suit is filed after Pennsylvania's two-year limitations period expired. No federal statute or Federal Rule of Civil Procedure prescribes a limitations period for the claim. How should the court treat the limitations issue?","choices":["Apply a federal period, because limitations periods regulate only the timing of suits and are procedural.","Apply Pennsylvania's period, but only because Rule 3 directs federal courts to borrow state time limits.","Apply Pennsylvania's period, because limitations rules are substantive for Erie purposes.","Apply no fixed period, because federal courts hearing state claims use the equitable doctrine of laches."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When no federal statute or Federal Rule is on point, a federal court in diversity applies state law if ignoring it would be outcome-determinative in a way that encourages forum shopping or the inequitable administration of the laws (Guaranty Trust Co. v. York; Hanna v. Plumer). Statutes of limitations are the classic example: York held that a federal court could not substitute equitable laches for the state limitations period. Rule 3 says only when an action is commenced and does not displace state limitations or tolling rules (Walker v. Armco Steel).","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["erie","diversity","substantive-procedural","statute-of-limitations"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 3 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_3"}]},{"id":"civpro-claim-preclusion","prompt":"Marisol's car is hit by a truck driven by Theo. She sues Theo in state court for the $6,000 cost of repairing her car and wins a final judgment. A month later she sues Theo again, in a court of the same state, for $90,000 for back injuries she suffered in the same collision. The state follows the modern transactional approach to preclusion. What result?","choices":["The second suit may proceed, because property damage and personal injury are distinct causes of action.","The second suit may proceed, because her injury claim was never actually litigated or decided.","The second suit is barred only as to the negligence issues that the first court actually decided.","The second suit is barred, because she could have sought both kinds of damages in the first action."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Claim preclusion bars a later suit when there was a valid final judgment on the merits, the parties are the same, and the new claim arises from the same transaction or occurrence, whether or not it was actually litigated (Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24). Both the property and injury claims arise from one collision, so splitting them is forbidden. \"Actually litigated\" is an issue-preclusion requirement, and the older primary-rights view that treats property and injury as separate claims is the minority approach.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["claim-preclusion","res-judicata","transactional-test"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Res judicata (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/res_judicata"},{"title":"Lucky Brand Dungarees, Inc. v. Marcel Fashions Group, Inc. (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/18-1086"}]},{"id":"civpro-general-jx","prompt":"Hollis Corp. is incorporated in Delaware and headquartered in Texas. It earns 15% of its revenue in Ohio, where it also owns two warehouses. A Michigan resident sues Hollis in an Ohio state court over an injury that happened in Michigan and has no connection to Ohio. Ohio has no statute treating business registration as consent to jurisdiction. May the Ohio court exercise personal jurisdiction over Hollis?","choices":["No, because Hollis is at home only in Delaware and Texas, and this claim is unrelated to its Ohio activity.","Yes, because Hollis's Ohio business is continuous and systematic enough to support general jurisdiction.","Yes, because Hollis owns warehouses in Ohio, which gives Ohio courts jurisdiction over any claim against it.","No, because general jurisdiction over a corporation exists only in its state of incorporation."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"General (all-purpose) jurisdiction over a corporation exists where it is essentially at home, paradigmatically its state of incorporation and principal place of business (Daimler AG v. Bauman; BNSF Ry. v. Tyrrell). Substantial sales and property in Ohio do not make Hollis at home there, and owning property does not support jurisdiction over unrelated claims (Shaffer v. Heitner). The principal place of business is also a paradigm forum, and the stem rules out consent-by-registration under Mallory v. Norfolk Southern.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["general-jurisdiction","at-home","daimler"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. XIV (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fourteenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"civpro-12b-waiver","prompt":"Served with a federal complaint, Quinn files a pre-answer Rule 12(b)(6) motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim. The court denies it. Quinn then files an answer that asserts, for the first time, that the court lacks personal jurisdiction over him. How should the court treat the personal-jurisdiction defense?","choices":["As preserved, because Quinn raised it in his answer, which was his first responsive pleading.","As waived, because it was available when Quinn made his Rule 12 motion but was left out of it.","As preserved, because personal-jurisdiction objections may be raised at any time before trial.","As waived, because personal jurisdiction must be challenged before any other defense is raised."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Rule 12(g)(2) and 12(h)(1), the defenses of lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process, and insufficient service are waived if omitted from a Rule 12 motion that the party makes, or if no motion is made, from the first responsive pleading. Because Quinn filed a Rule 12 motion without the defense, raising it in the later answer is too late. The defense may be joined with others in one motion in any order; only failure to state a claim (Rule 12(h)(2)) and lack of subject-matter jurisdiction (Rule 12(h)(3)) survive longer.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["rule-12","waiver","personal-jurisdiction"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 12 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_12"}]},{"id":"conlaw-standing","prompt":"An environmental group sues a federal agency, claiming that it unlawfully funds a project abroad that threatens an endangered species. The group relies on its member Rhea, who swears that she observed the species at the site years ago and 'intends to return someday,' though she has no concrete plans. A federal statute authorizes 'any person' to sue. Does the group have standing?","choices":["Yes, because the group's members share a sincere, long-standing interest in protecting the species.","Yes, because Congress authorized any person to sue, which satisfies Article III on its own.","No, because a vague intention to return someday does not show an actual or imminent injury to Rhea.","No, because an organization cannot sue to vindicate injuries suffered by its individual members."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Article III standing requires an injury in fact that is concrete, particularized, and actual or imminent, fairly traceable to the defendant, and likely to be redressed by a favorable ruling. 'Someday' intentions without concrete plans do not establish an imminent injury, and a citizen-suit provision cannot eliminate that constitutional minimum (Lujan v. Defenders of Wildlife). A mere interest in a problem is not an injury (Sierra Club v. Morton), and organizations may sue on behalf of members who themselves have standing (Hunt v. Washington State Apple Advertising Comm'n).","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["standing","injury-in-fact","justiciability"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. III (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articleiii"}]},{"id":"conlaw-takings-v2","prompt":"A new zoning rule reduces the value of a parcel by 75% but leaves the owner with a viable commercial use. The rule causes no physical occupation and was not imposed as a condition on a permit. The owner sues for just compensation. Which is the best takings analysis?","choices":["The rule is a per se taking under Lucas, because it destroys most of the parcel's economic value.","The rule cannot be a taking, because the owner retains a viable commercial use of the parcel.","The court applies Nollan and Dolan, asking whether the rule is roughly proportional to the parcel's impacts.","The court applies Penn Central, weighing economic impact, expectations, and the action's character."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A regulation that deprives an owner of all economically beneficial use is a per se taking (Lucas v. South Carolina Coastal Council), but a severe partial loss is not. Absent a physical occupation, total wipeout, or exaction, the claim is decided under Penn Central Transportation Co. v. New York City, which weighs the economic impact, interference with distinct investment-backed expectations, and the character of the government action. Partial losses can still be compensable under Penn Central, and Nollan/Dolan apply only to permit conditions.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["takings","regulatory","penn-central"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. V (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fifth_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-free-exercise-v2","prompt":"A city requires permits for indoor gatherings and lets officials grant case-by-case exemptions for any 'good cause.' Officials have granted exemptions for several secular events but deny a church's request to hold a worship service. There is no evidence of hostility toward religion. What level of review most likely applies to the denial?","choices":["Strict scrutiny, because the system of discretionary exemptions means the rule is not generally applicable.","Rational-basis review, because the permit rule is facially neutral and covers secular and religious gatherings.","Intermediate scrutiny, because the rule regulates only the time, place, and manner of assemblies.","Rational-basis review, because strict scrutiny requires proof that officials acted from religious hostility."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Employment Division v. Smith, a neutral and generally applicable law that incidentally burdens religion gets only rational-basis review. But a law is not generally applicable when it provides a mechanism for individualized exemptions, or when it treats comparable secular activity more favorably than religious exercise (Fulton v. City of Philadelphia; Tandon v. Newsom), so strict scrutiny applies. Proof of animus is one route to strict scrutiny (Church of Lukumi Babalu Aye), but not a required one.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["free-exercise","smith","general-applicability"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-procedural-dp","prompt":"A state agency plans to cut off Dana's monthly disability benefits after a reviewing physician concludes that her condition has improved. Dana argues that she is entitled to a full evidentiary hearing before the payments stop. In deciding what process is due, how will the court analyze her claim?","choices":["By asking whether terminating benefits on this record is rationally related to a legitimate state interest.","By balancing her private interest, the risk of error and value of more safeguards, and the government's interest.","By requiring a pretermination evidentiary hearing, because government benefits are a protected property interest.","By applying strict scrutiny, because terminating subsistence benefits burdens a fundamental right."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Once a protected property interest is at stake, the process due is set by Mathews v. Eldridge, which balances the private interest, the risk of erroneous deprivation and the probable value of additional safeguards, and the government's interest, including administrative burden. Mathews itself held that disability benefits could be terminated without a prior evidentiary hearing, distinguishing the need-based welfare benefits in Goldberg v. Kelly. Rational-basis and strict scrutiny are substantive tests, not the measure of required procedures.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["procedural-due-process","mathews"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. XIV (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fourteenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"civpro-summary-judgment-v2","prompt":"After discovery closes, a defendant moves for summary judgment on causation, an element the plaintiff must prove at trial. The defendant cites the record and shows that the plaintiff has produced no admissible evidence of causation, but it submits no expert testimony of its own. Has the defendant met its initial burden under Rule 56?","choices":["No, because the movant must submit its own affidavits or expert evidence negating causation.","No, because causation is a question of fact that must ordinarily be reserved for the jury at trial.","Yes, because a movant without the trial burden may point to the absence of evidence on an element.","Yes, because the court may weigh the record and decide that the defendant's account is more credible."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A party that will not bear the burden of proof at trial may discharge its initial Rule 56 burden by pointing out that the record lacks evidence on an essential element of the nonmovant's case; it need not negate that element with its own evidence (Celotex Corp. v. Catrett). The burden then shifts to the plaintiff to cite materials showing a genuine dispute. Causation can be decided on summary judgment when no reasonable jury could find it, but the court may not weigh evidence or assess credibility (Anderson v. Liberty Lobby).","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["summary-judgment","rule-56","celotex"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_56"}]},{"id":"conlaw-preemption","prompt":"A federal statute requires that a specific warning appear on every container of a certain industrial solvent, and it says nothing about state law. State Y, viewing the warning as alarmist, enacts a law forbidding solvent containers sold in the state from displaying that warning. A manufacturer challenges the state law. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["It is valid, because the federal statute contains no express preemption clause.","It is valid, because product safety labeling is a field of traditional state police power.","It is preempted, because any federal regulation of a product occupies the entire field.","It is preempted, because complying with both the federal and state laws is impossible."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under the Supremacy Clause, federal law preempts state law expressly, by occupying a field, or by conflict. Conflict preemption exists when compliance with both is impossible or the state law obstructs federal objectives; here it is impossible to include and omit the same warning (Mutual Pharmaceutical Co. v. Bartlett). The absence of an express clause and the presumption against preempting traditional state powers do not save a direct conflict, while a single federal labeling requirement does not by itself show intent to occupy the whole field.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["preemption","supremacy-clause","conflict-preemption"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. VI (Supremacy Clause) (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articlevi"}]},{"id":"civpro-compulsory-counterclaim","prompt":"A supplier sues a retailer in federal court for $200,000 unpaid under their supply contract. The retailer defends on the merits but does not assert its claim that the goods delivered under that same contract were defective. After judgment for the supplier, the retailer files a new federal action against the supplier for breach of warranty on those goods. What result?","choices":["The new action is barred, because the warranty claim arose from the same transaction.","The new action may proceed, because counterclaims are optional for a defendant who litigates the merits.","The new action may proceed, because the defect issue was never actually litigated in the first suit.","The new action is barred, because Rule 13 makes counterclaims mandatory whether or not they are related."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Rule 13(a), a counterclaim that arises out of the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim is compulsory and must be pleaded, unless it requires adding a party the court cannot reach or was already pending in another action. A compulsory counterclaim that is omitted is barred in later litigation. The issue-preclusion concept of 'actually litigated' does not save it, and Rule 13(b) makes unrelated counterclaims permissive rather than mandatory.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["counterclaim","rule-13","transaction-occurrence"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 13 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_13"}]},{"id":"civpro-issue-preclusion","prompt":"Amos sues Brook Corp. for breach of a supply contract, and Brook defaults. The court enters a valid default judgment for Amos. Later, Brook sues Amos on a different claim that Brook was not required to raise in the first action, and Amos argues that Brook is precluded from denying that the contract was valid, since the first judgment necessarily rested on a valid contract. Under the general rule of the Restatement (Second) of Judgments, is Brook precluded from litigating the contract's validity?","choices":["Yes, because a default judgment is a valid and final judgment on the merits.","No, because issues resolved by default were never actually litigated.","Yes, because the contract's validity was necessarily decided as part of the judgment.","No, because issue preclusion applies only when a later suit involves the same claim."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Issue preclusion requires that the same issue was actually litigated and determined, that its determination was essential to a valid final judgment, and that the party to be bound had a full and fair opportunity to litigate it (Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27). Issues resolved by default, consent, or stipulation are not actually litigated (§ 27 cmt. e), so they carry no issue-preclusive effect. A default judgment does have claim-preclusive effect on the same claim, and issue preclusion is designed to apply across different claims. Note that some states, including California, give a default judgment issue-preclusive effect on matters necessarily decided when the defendant had actual notice.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["issue-preclusion","collateral-estoppel","default-judgment"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Arizona v. California, 530 U.S. 392 (2000) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/530/392/"}]},{"id":"civpro-1367b","prompt":"Pia, an Iowa citizen, sues Delta Power, a Nebraska citizen, in federal court solely on diversity, seeking $500,000 for her husband's death. Delta impleads Owen Crane, an Iowa citizen, under Rule 14. Pia then amends to assert a state-law negligence claim directly against Owen arising from the same accident. May the court exercise supplemental jurisdiction over Pia's claim against Owen?","choices":["Yes, because the claim arises from the same accident and so forms part of the same case or controversy.","Yes, because Owen entered the case through Delta's impleader rather than through Pia's own choice.","No, because § 1367(b) bars a plaintiff's claim against a Rule 14 party that would destroy diversity.","No, because Rule 14 does not permit a plaintiff to assert claims against a third-party defendant."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In a case founded solely on diversity, 28 U.S.C. § 1367(b) withholds supplemental jurisdiction over claims by plaintiffs against persons made parties under Rules 14, 19, 20, or 24 when exercising jurisdiction would be inconsistent with § 1332, codifying Owen Equipment & Erection Co. v. Kroger. Pia and Owen are both Iowa citizens, so her claim fails even though it shares a common nucleus with the main claim. Rule 14(a)(3) does permit such a claim procedurally, and Delta's own claim against Owen is unaffected because § 1367(b) restricts only plaintiffs.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["supplemental-jurisdiction","1367b","diversity","impleader"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1367 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1367"}]},{"id":"civpro-forum-defendant","prompt":"Tomas, a Texas citizen, sues Calla Inc., a corporation incorporated and headquartered in California, in California state court on a state-law claim for $300,000. After being properly served, Calla files a timely notice of removal to federal court in California, relying solely on diversity. Tomas moves to remand within 30 days. How should the court rule?","choices":["Deny remand, because the parties are completely diverse and more than $75,000 is in controversy.","Deny remand, because the forum-defendant rule applies only when the plaintiff is also a forum citizen.","Remand, because a case removed from California must be sent to a federal court outside California.","Remand, because a properly joined and served forum-state defendant cannot remove on diversity alone."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2), a case removable only on diversity may not be removed if any properly joined and served defendant is a citizen of the state where the action was brought. Diversity removal protects out-of-state defendants from local bias, which a California defendant does not need in California. The plaintiff's citizenship is irrelevant to the rule, removal goes to the district embracing the place where the state action is pending (§ 1441(a)), and because most courts treat the rule as procedural, Tomas properly moved to remand within 30 days (§ 1447(c)).","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["removal","forum-defendant","diversity"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1441 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1441"}]},{"id":"civpro-rule-23a","prompt":"A consumer sues a supplement maker in federal court, seeking damages for fraud on behalf of 40,000 buyers in all 50 states. The court finds that the class is numerous, that whether the label was false is a question common to every buyer, and that the named plaintiff is a typical and adequate representative. It also finds that the buyers' claims are governed by materially different state laws and that each buyer's reliance would have to be proved individually, so that individual issues would dominate any trial. The plaintiff argues that, because Rule 23(a) is satisfied, the court must certify the damages class under Rule 23(b)(3). Is the plaintiff correct?","choices":["No, because common questions must also predominate over individual ones, and here they do not.","Yes, because a class that meets all four Rule 23(a) prerequisites is entitled to certification.","Yes, because one common question that drives the litigation is enough to satisfy Rule 23(b)(3).","No, because a class seeking money damages may be certified only under Rule 23(b)(1) or (b)(2)."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Rule 23(a) requires numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy of representation for every class, but the class must also fit one of the Rule 23(b) categories. A Rule 23(b)(3) damages class requires findings that common questions predominate over questions affecting only individual members and that a class action is superior to other methods of adjudication. A single common question satisfies commonality under Rule 23(a)(2) (Wal-Mart Stores, Inc. v. Dukes), but predominance is far more demanding (Amchem Products, Inc. v. Windsor), and the court's findings that state laws vary and reliance is individual defeat it. Rule 23(b)(3) is the usual vehicle for damages classes, with notice and opt-out rights under Rule 23(c)(2)(B).","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["class-action","rule-23","predominance"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_23"}]},{"id":"conlaw-tpm","prompt":"A city requires every performer at its public-park bandshell to use the city's sound equipment and technician, to keep noise from disturbing nearby homes. The rule applies regardless of a performance's message, and performers remain free to perform there and elsewhere. A rock group argues that the city could control noise through less restrictive means, such as decibel limits. Is the rule likely valid?","choices":["No, because a speech restriction in a public forum must use the least restrictive means available.","Yes, because narrow tailoring here does not require the least restrictive means.","Yes, because in a public park the city may adopt any reasonable restriction that is viewpoint neutral.","No, because requiring city equipment leaves performers without ample alternative channels for their music."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A content-neutral time, place, and manner regulation in a public forum is valid if it is narrowly tailored to serve a significant government interest and leaves open ample alternative channels for communication. Narrow tailoring here means the rule does not burden substantially more speech than necessary; it need not be the least restrictive means (Ward v. Rock Against Racism). Least-restrictive-means analysis belongs to strict scrutiny, the 'reasonable and viewpoint neutral' test governs nonpublic forums, and performers here retain ample alternatives.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["speech","tpm","public-forum","narrow-tailoring"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-prior-restraint","prompt":"A newspaper obtains a leaked internal government report about a failed military program and plans to publish it. The government seeks an injunction barring publication, arguing that publication would embarrass officials and might harm national security. It offers no evidence that publication would cause direct, immediate, and irreparable harm. Should the court issue the injunction?","choices":["Yes, because the report was leaked unlawfully, so the newspaper has no right to publish it.","Yes, because protecting national security is a compelling interest that justifies a narrowly drawn order.","No, because the government has not overcome the heavy presumption against prior restraints.","No, because prior restraints on the press are never permitted, whatever the government's showing."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Prior restraints, including court orders forbidding publication, come to court bearing a heavy presumption against their constitutional validity, and the government carries a heavy burden to justify them (New York Times Co. v. United States; Near v. Minnesota). Speculative harm to national security or embarrassment to officials does not suffice, and a publisher who lawfully obtains information is not stripped of protection because someone else leaked it. The bar is not absolute, however: Near recognized narrow exceptions, such as publishing troop movements in wartime.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["prior-restraint","speech","press"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-discriminatory-intent","prompt":"A city requires police applicants to pass a written verbal-skills test. Black applicants fail it at four times the rate of white applicants. City officials knew of the disparity when they decided to keep the test, but there is no evidence that they kept it because of its effect on Black applicants. An unsuccessful applicant sues under the Equal Protection Clause. What standard applies?","choices":["Strict scrutiny, because the test has a substantial disproportionate effect on a racial group.","Intermediate scrutiny, because the test's impact is severe even though no racial motive is shown.","Strict scrutiny, because officials knowingly retained a test with a racially disparate effect.","Rational-basis review, because disparate impact alone does not prove discriminatory purpose."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A facially neutral law triggers heightened scrutiny under the Equal Protection Clause only if the challenger proves discriminatory purpose; disparate impact alone is not enough (Washington v. Davis). Purpose means the decision was made 'because of,' not merely 'in spite of,' its adverse effect, so awareness of the disparity does not suffice (Personnel Administrator v. Feeney). Impact may be evidence of intent (Arlington Heights), but without more the test receives rational-basis review; Title VII disparate-impact claims are a separate statutory matter.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["equal-protection","disparate-impact","intent"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. XIV (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fourteenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-physical-taking","prompt":"A state law requires landlords to let cable companies install a small cable box and wires on the roofs of apartment buildings. The equipment occupies about 1.5 cubic feet, and the law serves the public interest in access to cable television. A landlord sues the state for just compensation. What is the best analysis?","choices":["It is a per se taking, because the law authorizes a permanent physical occupation, however small.","It is analyzed under Penn Central, and the minimal economic impact likely defeats the claim.","It is not a taking, because the landlord retains title and full use of nearly the entire building.","It is a taking only if the law deprives the landlord of all economically beneficial use of the roof."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A government-authorized permanent physical occupation of property is a per se taking requiring just compensation, regardless of its size or the public interest served (Loretto v. Teleprompter Manhattan CATV Corp.). The Penn Central balancing test and the Lucas total-deprivation rule govern use restrictions, not physical appropriations. The small size of the occupation affects only the amount of compensation, and Cedar Point Nursery v. Hassid (2021) extended per se treatment to regulations granting a right of physical access.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["takings","physical-occupation","loretto"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. V (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fifth_amendment"}]},{"id":"civpro-domicile-student","prompt":"Jonah grew up in Texas and moved to New York to attend a three-year law school. He rents an apartment in New York and has a New York library card, but he firmly plans to return to Texas to practice after graduation. During his second year, he sues a New York citizen in federal court based on diversity. What is Jonah's state citizenship?","choices":["New York, because he resides there and rents his home there during school.","Texas, because he has never intended to remain in New York for the indefinite future.","New York, because citizenship is fixed by residence when the suit is filed.","Texas, because a student keeps the domicile of origin until finishing school."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An individual's state citizenship for diversity is his domicile: the place where he is physically present with the intent to remain indefinitely. Jonah lives in New York but intends to return to Texas, so he keeps his Texas domicile. Citizenship is measured when the suit is filed, but it turns on domicile rather than residence, and a domicile of origin can change once a person establishes presence in a new state with intent to stay.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["diversity","domicile","citizenship"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1332 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1332"}]},{"id":"civpro-aggregation-single-plaintiff","prompt":"Mira, a Florida citizen, sues Gage, a Georgia citizen, in federal court. She asserts a $50,000 claim for breach of a loan agreement and an unrelated $30,000 claim for damage Gage did to her boat last summer. Is the amount-in-controversy requirement satisfied?","choices":["No, because claims may be aggregated only when they arise from the same transaction.","No, because each claim in a diversity case must independently exceed $75,000.","Yes, because a single plaintiff may aggregate all claims against a single defendant.","Yes, because the boat claim falls within supplemental jurisdiction over the loan claim."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A single plaintiff may aggregate all of her claims against a single defendant, related or not, to exceed $75,000 under 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a). The total here is $80,000. Aggregation does not depend on a transactional relationship; supplemental jurisdiction would not help because neither claim alone satisfies § 1332 and the claims share no common nucleus of fact.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["diversity","amount-in-controversy","aggregation"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1332 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1332"}]},{"id":"civpro-removal-30-day-window","prompt":"A complaint that plainly states a federal civil-rights claim is served on Ortega on January 1. On February 10, Ortega files a notice of removal based on that federal claim. The plaintiff moves to remand one week later. Should the court remand?","choices":["No, because a defendant has 60 days after service to remove a federal-question case.","No, because the one-year limit on removal after commencement has not yet run.","Yes, because a notice of removal must be filed within 21 days of service, like an answer.","Yes, because the notice was due within 30 days after Ortega was served."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under 28 U.S.C. § 1446(b)(1), a notice of removal must be filed within 30 days after the defendant receives the initial pleading through formal service, when the case is removable on its face. Ortega filed 40 days after service. The one-year cap in § 1446(c)(1) limits only late removals on diversity grounds, and the 21-day period is the answer deadline under Rule 12(a). Because untimeliness is a procedural defect, the plaintiff must seek remand within 30 days after removal, as she did (§ 1447(c)).","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["removal","timing","1446"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1446 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1446"}]},{"id":"civpro-rule11-safe-harbor","prompt":"Defense counsel believes that several factual allegations in the plaintiff's complaint have no evidentiary support. On March 1, counsel serves a Rule 11 sanctions motion on plaintiff's counsel and files it with the court the same day. On March 10, plaintiff's counsel withdraws the challenged allegations. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":["No, because the motion was filed before the 21-day safe-harbor period for withdrawal or correction ran.","Yes, because the allegations violated Rule 11 when signed, and later withdrawal does not cure that.","Yes, because the safe-harbor period applies only to sanctions that the court initiates on its own.","No, because a Rule 11 motion must wait until the court has ruled on the merits of the claim."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Rule 11(c)(2) requires a sanctions motion to be made separately and served on the opponent, but it may not be filed with the court if the challenged paper is withdrawn or appropriately corrected within 21 days after service. Filing the same day defeated the safe harbor, and the allegations were withdrawn within 21 days. The safe harbor applies to party motions, not to court-initiated show-cause orders under Rule 11(c)(3), and a motion need not await a merits ruling.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["rule-11","sanctions","safe-harbor"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_11"}]},{"id":"conlaw-viewpoint-discrimination","prompt":"A city issues permits for rallies in a public park. It grants permits for rallies opposing a proposed downtown development but denies permits for rallies supporting it, explaining that it wants to keep 'pro-developer' messages out of the park. How will a court most likely treat the permit policy?","choices":["As a content-neutral time, place, and manner rule, because it regulates only permits for the park.","As viewpoint discrimination, which is presumptively unconstitutional in any type of forum, public or not.","As a permissible subject-matter limit, because both sides address the same development topic.","As a regulation of commercial speech, because the rallies concern a real-estate development."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Viewpoint discrimination, favoring one side of a debate on a subject the government has otherwise allowed, is an especially egregious form of content discrimination and is forbidden even in limited and nonpublic forums (Rosenberger v. Rector & Visitors of Univ. of Va.). Allowing only the anti-development side is the paradigm. A rule that turns on the speaker's position is not content neutral, a subject-matter limit would exclude the topic entirely, and political advocacy about development is not commercial speech.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["speech","viewpoint-discrimination","public-forum"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-commercial-speech-misleading","prompt":"A state bans a dietary-supplement company from running ads claiming that its pills 'cure diabetes,' a claim the company knows is false. The company argues that the ban is a content-based restriction on speech. How should the court evaluate the ban?","choices":["The ban must satisfy strict scrutiny, because it targets a particular message based on its content.","The ban must satisfy Central Hudson, because it restricts advertising for a lawful product.","The ban is valid without further scrutiny, because false commercial speech is not protected.","The ban is invalid, because the state must try requiring a disclaimer before banning the claim."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Central Hudson Gas & Electric v. Public Service Commission, commercial speech is protected only if it concerns lawful activity and is not misleading; false or inherently misleading commercial speech may be prohibited outright. Central Hudson's intermediate-scrutiny steps apply only to truthful, non-misleading ads, and commercial speech is not subject to strict scrutiny merely because a regulation is content based. The preference for disclaimers applies to potentially misleading speech (In re R.M.J.), not to knowingly false claims.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["speech","commercial-speech","central-hudson"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-anti-commandeering","prompt":"Congress enacts a statute requiring the chief law enforcement officer of every county to conduct background checks on prospective handgun buyers until a national system is running. A county sheriff objects to being drafted into federal service. Is the requirement constitutional?","choices":["Yes, because the Supremacy Clause binds state officers to enforce federal law within their jurisdiction.","Yes, because the duty is temporary and imposes only a minor ministerial burden on local officials.","No, because the Commerce Clause does not give Congress power to regulate handgun sales.","No, because Congress cannot command state executive officers to administer a federal regulatory program."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under the Tenth Amendment anti-commandeering doctrine, Congress may not compel state legislatures to enact laws (New York v. United States) or command state executive officers to administer a federal regulatory program (Printz v. United States). The burden's size or duration does not matter. The Supremacy Clause binds state judges to apply federal law (Testa v. Katt) but does not let Congress conscript state executives, and Congress could regulate handgun sales directly or use conditional spending instead.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["federalism","anti-commandeering","tenth-amendment"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. X (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/tenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"conlaw-eleventh-amendment-damages","prompt":"Hale, a citizen of State Q, sues State Q's Department of Transportation in federal court for money damages under a federal statute enacted under the Commerce Clause. The statute expressly authorizes private damages suits against states. State Q has not consented to suit. How should the court rule on State Q's immunity defense?","choices":["Uphold it, because Congress cannot use its Commerce Clause power to abrogate the states' immunity.","Reject it, because the Eleventh Amendment's text bars only suits by citizens of other states.","Reject it, because Congress unmistakably expressed its intent to abrogate the states' immunity.","Reject it, because suing a state agency rather than the state itself avoids sovereign immunity."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"States are immune from private damages suits in federal court unless they consent or Congress validly abrogates immunity, and the immunity extends to suits by a state's own citizens (Hans v. Louisiana) and to arms of the state such as agencies. Congress may abrogate under § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment (Fitzpatrick v. Bitzer) but not under its Article I Commerce Clause power, however clearly it states its intent (Seminole Tribe v. Florida). A plaintiff seeking only prospective relief may instead sue a state official under Ex parte Young.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["sovereign-immunity","eleventh-amendment","abrogation"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. XI (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/eleventh_amendment"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-nerve-center","prompt":"Lumen Corp. is incorporated in Delaware. Its officers direct and control the company from its headquarters in Chicago, Illinois, but most of its employees, plants, and sales are in Ohio. For purposes of diversity jurisdiction, of which state or states is Lumen a citizen?","choices":["Delaware and Ohio, where most of its business activity occurs.","Delaware and Illinois, where its nerve center is located.","Delaware only, the state that granted its corporate charter.","Delaware, Illinois, and Ohio, where it has major operations."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(1), a corporation is a citizen of every state where it is incorporated and of the state where it has its principal place of business. Hertz Corp. v. Friend defines the principal place of business as the nerve center, the place where high-level officers direct, control, and coordinate the corporation's activities, usually its headquarters. The older 'business activities' test that might point to Ohio is no longer the rule, and a corporation has only one principal place of business.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["diversity","corporate-citizenship","hertz"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1332 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1332"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-llc-citizenship","prompt":"Redwood Partners LLC is organized under Delaware law and has its principal place of business in California. Its two members are an Oregon citizen and a Nevada citizen. Redwood sues Vasquez, a Nevada citizen, in federal court for $400,000 on a state-law contract claim. Does the court have diversity jurisdiction?","choices":["Yes, because an LLC is a citizen of its state of organization and its principal place of business.","Yes, because the LLC itself is the party, so the citizenship of its members is irrelevant.","No, because an LLC takes the citizenship of each of its members, and one member is from Nevada.","No, because unincorporated entities such as LLCs cannot sue in federal court on diversity."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Section 1332(c)(1)'s rule for corporations does not apply to unincorporated associations, which take the citizenship of all their members (Carden v. Arkoma Associates); the federal courts of appeals uniformly apply that rule to LLCs. Because one Redwood member is a Nevada citizen, Redwood is a Nevada citizen and complete diversity with Vasquez is lacking. LLCs can sue in diversity when all members are diverse from the other side, and CAFA has a special rule for unincorporated associations only in class actions (§ 1332(d)(10)).","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["diversity","llc","citizenship"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1332 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1332"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-smj-on-appeal","prompt":"After a three-day jury trial in a case brought solely on diversity, the defendant loses. On appeal, the defendant argues for the first time that it and the plaintiff were citizens of the same state when the suit was filed, and the record confirms this. They are the only parties. What should the court of appeals do?","choices":["Affirm, because the defendant waived the objection by not raising it in the district court.","Affirm, because diversity is measured by the parties' citizenship when judgment is entered.","Vacate the judgment only if the defendant shows that the federal forum caused it prejudice.","Vacate the judgment, because a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction may be raised at any time."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be created by consent, waiver, or estoppel; a court must dismiss whenever it determines that jurisdiction is lacking, even on appeal and even when the party that invoked the federal forum later objects (Rule 12(h)(3); Capron v. Van Noorden). Diversity is measured at the time of filing (Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group), and with only two nondiverse parties there is no dispensable party whose dismissal could cure the defect. No showing of prejudice is required.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["subject-matter","waiver","diversity"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 12 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_12"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-removal-unanimity","prompt":"Pell sues Ash and Birch in state court, asserting only a federal civil-rights claim. Both defendants are properly joined and served. Ash files a timely notice of removal, but Birch, who prefers state court, refuses to consent. Pell moves to remand within 30 days. How should the court rule?","choices":["Remand, because every properly joined and served defendant must consent to removal under § 1441(a).","Deny remand, because the consent of all defendants is required only when removal rests on diversity.","Deny remand, because any single defendant may remove a case that arises under federal law.","Remand, because Birch's refusal to consent deprives the federal court of subject-matter jurisdiction."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"When a civil action is removed solely under 28 U.S.C. § 1441(a), all defendants who have been properly joined and served must join in or consent to the removal (§ 1446(b)(2)(A)). The rule applies to federal-question removals as well as diversity removals; exceptions exist for specific statutes such as federal-officer removal (§ 1442) and CAFA (§ 1453). The unanimity requirement is procedural rather than jurisdictional, so the defect must be raised by a timely remand motion, as Pell did.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["removal","unanimity","1446"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1446 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1446"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-removal-one-year","prompt":"Rao, an Arizona citizen, sues Kent, a New Mexico citizen, and Lowe, an Arizona citizen, in Arizona state court on state-law claims seeking $500,000. Eighteen months later, after discovery shows that the claim against Lowe is weak, Rao voluntarily dismisses Lowe. Kent removes on diversity grounds within 30 days. Nothing suggests that Rao joined or kept Lowe in the case to prevent removal. Is the removal proper?","choices":["Yes, because Kent removed within 30 days after the voluntary dismissal first made the case removable.","No, because diversity removal is barred more than one year after commencement, absent bad faith.","Yes, because a plaintiff's voluntary dismissal of the nondiverse defendant always permits removal.","No, because a case that was not removable when filed cannot become removable through later events."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When a case that was not removable at the outset becomes removable later, the defendant may remove within 30 days after receiving the paper that makes it removable (28 U.S.C. § 1446(b)(3)), but a diversity removal may not occur more than one year after commencement unless the plaintiff acted in bad faith to prevent removal (§ 1446(c)(1)). Kent satisfied the 30-day window but not the one-year cap. Rao's voluntary dismissal of Lowe is what made the case removable at all, but it does not override the one-year limit.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["removal","one-year-bar","diversity"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1446 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1446"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-mallory-consent","prompt":"A Pennsylvania statute requires out-of-state corporations that register to do business there to consent to general personal jurisdiction in Pennsylvania courts. Norrock Rail, a Virginia corporation headquartered in Virginia, registered years ago and operates some track in Pennsylvania. A former employee who worked only in Ohio and Virginia sues Norrock in Pennsylvania state court over an injury unrelated to Pennsylvania. Norrock argues that jurisdiction violates due process. How should the court rule on that argument?","choices":["Accept it, because under Daimler a corporation is subject to general jurisdiction only where it is at home.","Accept it, because the claim neither arises out of nor relates to Norrock's Pennsylvania activities.","Reject it, because a corporation that registers under such a statute has consented to jurisdiction there.","Reject it, because operating track in Pennsylvania makes the railroad essentially at home there."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In Mallory v. Norfolk Southern Railway Co. (2023), the Supreme Court held that due process does not bar a state from exercising jurisdiction over a corporation that registered to do business under a statute requiring consent to suit on any claim, following Pennsylvania Fire Insurance Co. v. Gold Issue Mining (1917). The 'at home' test of Daimler and the relatedness requirement of specific jurisdiction limit jurisdiction over nonconsenting defendants, but consent is an independent basis. The Court left open a dormant Commerce Clause challenge, and some track in Pennsylvania would not make the railroad at home there.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["personal-jurisdiction","consent","mallory","general-jurisdiction"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Mallory v. Norfolk Southern Railway Co. (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/21-1168"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-ford-relatedness","prompt":"Axle Motors, a national automaker, advertises, sells, and services its Ranger sedan throughout Montana. A Montana resident is injured in Montana when her Ranger's airbag fails. She bought the car used from a seller in another state, and Axle originally designed, built, and sold that car outside Montana. Axle argues that a Montana court lacks specific jurisdiction because none of its Montana contacts caused her injury. How should the court rule?","choices":["Decline jurisdiction, because specific jurisdiction requires that the defendant's forum contacts caused the injury.","Decline jurisdiction, because the car reached Montana only through a consumer's unilateral resale.","Exercise jurisdiction, because Axle's Montana sales are substantial enough to make it at home there.","Exercise jurisdiction, because the claim relates to Axle's cultivation of a Montana market for that model."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Specific jurisdiction requires that the claim 'arise out of or relate to' the defendant's forum contacts, and the 'relate to' half does not demand strict causation. In Ford Motor Co. v. Montana Eighth Judicial District Court (2021), Ford's systematic advertising, sales, and servicing of the same model in the forum supported jurisdiction over in-state accidents even though the particular cars were sold elsewhere. The unilateral-activity cases involve defendants that did not serve the forum market, and substantial sales do not make a corporation at home for general jurisdiction.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["personal-jurisdiction","specific-jurisdiction","ford","relatedness"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Ford Motor Co. v. Montana Eighth Judicial District Court (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/19-368"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-venue-1391","prompt":"Kim, who lives in Nevada, sues two individuals in federal court on a state-law claim. Defendant Ruiz lives in the Northern District of California, and defendant Shaw lives in the District of Oregon. All the events giving rise to the claim occurred in the District of Nevada. Under 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b), where is venue proper?","choices":["Only in the District of Nevada, where the events occurred.","In N.D. California or D. Oregon, where the two defendants reside.","In any district in Nevada, California, or Oregon, at Kim's choice.","Only in the district where the first-named defendant, Ruiz, resides."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Venue lies in a district where any defendant resides if all defendants reside in the same state (§ 1391(b)(1)), or in a district where a substantial part of the events or omissions occurred (§ 1391(b)(2)); the fallback in § 1391(b)(3) applies only when no district qualifies under the first two. Ruiz and Shaw live in different states, so residence-based venue is unavailable, leaving the District of Nevada, where the events occurred. The plaintiff's residence plays no role in general federal venue.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["venue","1391"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1391 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1391"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-forum-non-conveniens","prompt":"A small plane crashes in Scotland, killing Scottish passengers. Their estates sue the American manufacturer in a U.S. federal court, partly because U.S. law offers strict liability and larger damages than Scottish law. The witnesses and wreckage are in Scotland, and Scottish courts are available to hear the claims. The defendant moves to dismiss for forum non conveniens. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The court must deny dismissal, because the plaintiffs would recover substantially less under Scottish law.","The court may dismiss, because less favorable law abroad ordinarily does not weigh heavily against dismissal.","The court should transfer the case to the Scottish courts under § 1404(a) rather than dismiss it.","The court must deny dismissal, because the plaintiffs' choice of a U.S. forum is entitled to strong deference."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Forum non conveniens permits dismissal when an adequate alternative forum exists and private and public interest factors strongly favor it. The possibility of a less favorable change in law ordinarily is not given substantial weight unless the alternative remedy is so inadequate that it is no remedy at all (Piper Aircraft Co. v. Reyno), and a foreign plaintiff's forum choice deserves less deference than a domestic plaintiff's. Section 1404(a) transfers only to another federal district, so a foreign forum requires dismissal.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["forum-non-conveniens","venue","piper"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1404 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1404"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-erie-hanna","prompt":"In a diversity action, the plaintiff serves the summons and complaint by leaving copies at the defendant's home with an adult who lives there, as Rule 4(e)(2)(B) permits. The forum state's law requires in-hand delivery to the defendant personally for this type of claim. The defendant moves to dismiss for insufficient service. How should the court rule?","choices":["Grant the motion, because the state rule could change the outcome, so Erie requires applying it.","Grant the motion, because methods of service are substantive whenever the claim arises under state law.","Deny the motion, because a valid Federal Rule that directly covers the issue controls in federal court.","Deny the motion, because federal courts may disregard state law on any matter labeled procedural."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When a Federal Rule of Civil Procedure directly covers the point in dispute, the court applies it if it is valid under the Rules Enabling Act and the Constitution, without running the Erie outcome-determination analysis (Hanna v. Plumer; Shady Grove v. Allstate). Rule 4(e)(2)(B) governs service and regulates procedure, so it applies. The modified outcome test applies only when no federal rule or statute is on point, and even then a federal court may not simply disregard state law because it seems procedural.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["erie","hanna","service-of-process","rules-enabling-act"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 4 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_4"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-plausibility","prompt":"Ivy sues two competing manufacturers under federal antitrust law, alleging a price-fixing conspiracy. Her complaint alleges only that both defendants raised prices by similar amounts during the same quarter and, 'upon information and belief, entered into an agreement.' The defendants move to dismiss under Rule 12(b)(6). How should the court rule?","choices":["Deny the motion, because Rule 8 requires only fair notice of the claim and the grounds on which it rests.","Deny the motion, because a complaint survives unless no set of facts could support the plaintiff's claim.","Grant the motion, because antitrust conspiracies must be pleaded with particularity under Rule 9(b).","Grant the motion, because parallel pricing plus a conclusory allegation of agreement is not plausible."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Rule 8(a)(2) requires a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief, which means well-pleaded factual allegations that make the claim plausible, not merely conceivable (Bell Atlantic v. Twombly; Ashcroft v. Iqbal). Parallel pricing is consistent with independent action, and the label 'agreement' is a legal conclusion the court need not accept. Conley v. Gibson's 'no set of facts' standard was retired by Twombly, and Rule 9(b) particularity applies to fraud and mistake, not antitrust conspiracy.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["pleading","rule-8","twombly","iqbal"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 8 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_8"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-relation-back","prompt":"Wanda is injured on a cruise and, shortly before the limitations period expires, sues 'Sunline Cruises,' a sales affiliate named on the front of her ticket. The ticket's fine print identified 'Sunline Marine S.A.' as the carrier. Sunline Marine learned of the suit and Wanda's error within the Rule 4(m) period. After the limitations period runs, Wanda amends to name Sunline Marine, which argues that she knew its identity from the ticket. Does the amendment relate back?","choices":["Yes, because the inquiry focuses on what the new defendant knew or should have known, not on Wanda's knowledge.","No, because Wanda had the carrier's correct name on her ticket, so suing the affiliate was a choice, not a mistake.","No, because an amendment that brings in a new defendant cannot relate back once limitations has run.","Yes, because any amendment relates back if it arises from the same occurrence as the original pleading."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Rule 15(c)(1)(C), an amendment changing the party relates back if it arises from the same conduct, and within the Rule 4(m) period the new party received notice so that it will not be prejudiced and knew or should have known that it would have been sued but for a mistake about the proper party's identity. Krupski v. Costa Crociere S.p.A. (2010) held that the question is what the new defendant knew or should have known, not what the plaintiff knew. The same-occurrence test of Rule 15(c)(1)(B) alone is not enough to change parties.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["amendment","rule-15","relation-back","krupski"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 15 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_15"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-statutory-interpleader","prompt":"An insurer holds a $50,000 life-insurance fund that three rival beneficiaries claim: two are Ohio citizens and one is a Kentucky citizen. The insurer is also an Ohio citizen. It wants to resolve all of the claims in one federal action. Which route gives a federal court subject-matter jurisdiction?","choices":["Rule 22 interpleader, because Rule 22 relaxes the usual diversity requirements for interpleader suits.","Statutory interpleader, which needs only minimal diversity among the claimants and a fund of $500 or more.","Rule 22 interpleader, because the $50,000 fund falls below the minimum for statutory interpleader.","Neither route, because the insurer shares Ohio citizenship with two of the three rival claimants."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Statutory interpleader under 28 U.S.C. § 1335 requires only that two or more adverse claimants be of diverse citizenship and that the fund be $500 or more, deposited with the court (State Farm Fire & Casualty Co. v. Tashire); it also provides nationwide service of process (§ 2361). Rule 22 interpleader is only a joinder device and needs an independent basis such as § 1332, which fails here for lack of complete diversity between the insurer and claimants and because $50,000 is under $75,000. The stakeholder's own citizenship does not matter under § 1335.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["interpleader","1335","rule-22","minimal-diversity"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1335 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1335"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-cafa","prompt":"A Maryland citizen files a class action in Maryland state court against a bank incorporated in Delaware and headquartered in Virginia, on behalf of 5,000 customers nationwide, alleging improper fees under state law. The claims average $1,500 each, totaling $7.5 million. Fewer than one-third of the class members are Maryland citizens. May the bank remove the case?","choices":["No, because no class member's individual claim exceeds the $75,000 amount in controversy.","No, because class actions asserting only state-law claims must remain in state court.","Yes, because CAFA needs only minimal diversity, 100 members, and over $5 million in total.","Yes, but only if every class member is diverse from the bank, since complete diversity is required."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Class Action Fairness Act, 28 U.S.C. § 1332(d), gives federal courts jurisdiction over class actions with at least 100 members, more than $5 million in aggregate controversy, and minimal diversity between any class member and any defendant; § 1453 makes such cases removable. The local-controversy and home-state exceptions do not apply because fewer than one-third of the class are Maryland citizens and the bank is not a Maryland citizen. Individual claims need not exceed $75,000, and complete diversity is not required.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["class-action","cafa","removal"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1332 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1332"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-work-product","prompt":"After a warehouse fire, the defendant's insurer, anticipating litigation, sends an investigator to interview the only eyewitness and prepare a signed statement. The eyewitness has since died. The plaintiff requests the statement in discovery, and the defendant objects on work-product grounds. What is the most likely ruling?","choices":["Production is denied, because materials prepared in anticipation of litigation are absolutely protected.","Production is denied, because the statement was taken by the insurer's investigator acting for the defendant.","Production is required, because work-product protection covers only documents written by attorneys.","Production is required, because the plaintiff has substantial need and cannot obtain an equivalent elsewhere."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Rule 26(b)(3)(A) protects documents prepared in anticipation of litigation by or for a party or its representative, expressly including an insurer or agent, so the statement is work product. Ordinary work product is discoverable on a showing of substantial need and inability to obtain the substantial equivalent without undue hardship, which the witness's death establishes (see Hickman v. Taylor). Attorneys' mental impressions and opinions receive near-absolute protection under Rule 26(b)(3)(B), but a witness statement is mostly factual.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["discovery","work-product","rule-26"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 26 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_26"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-esi-sanctions","prompt":"After litigation became reasonably foreseeable, a defendant's IT staff negligently failed to suspend an automatic-deletion policy, and relevant emails were permanently lost. They cannot be restored or replaced through other discovery, and the loss prejudices the plaintiff. There is no evidence that the defendant intended to deprive the plaintiff of the emails. Under Rule 37(e), what may the court do?","choices":["Order measures no greater than necessary to cure the prejudice, but not an adverse-inference instruction.","Instruct the jury that it must presume the lost emails would have been unfavorable to the defendant.","Enter a default judgment, because the defendant failed to take reasonable steps to preserve the emails.","Impose no remedy at all, because the emails were lost through negligence rather than intentional conduct."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Rule 37(e), as amended in 2015, governs lost electronically stored information that should have been preserved. If reasonable steps were not taken and the information cannot be restored or replaced, the court may, on finding prejudice, order measures no greater than necessary to cure the prejudice (Rule 37(e)(1)). Presuming the information was unfavorable, giving an adverse-inference instruction, or dismissing or entering default requires a finding of intent to deprive (Rule 37(e)(2)). Negligence still permits curative measures.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["discovery","sanctions","rule-37e","esi"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 37 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_37"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-jury-beacon","prompt":"In federal court, Lark sues Monroe, seeking an injunction against Monroe's continued use of Lark's trade secret and $300,000 in damages for past misappropriation. Both claims turn on whether Monroe misappropriated the secret. Lark timely demands a jury. How should the court proceed?","choices":["Decide the injunction claim first without a jury, because the equitable claim predominates.","Have the jury resolve the shared fact issues first, and then have the judge rule on the injunction.","Try the entire case to the judge, because seeking equitable relief waives the right to a jury.","Let the jury decide both claims, including whether to issue the injunction, since one is legal."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Seventh Amendment preserves the jury right for legal claims such as damages. When legal and equitable claims share common factual issues, the legal claims must be tried to the jury first, or together, so that the judge's equitable ruling does not preclude the jury's findings (Beacon Theatres v. Westover; Dairy Queen v. Wood). The judge then decides the injunction consistent with the jury's findings. Seeking equitable relief does not waive the jury right, and the remedy of an injunction remains for the court.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["jury-trial","seventh-amendment","beacon-theatres"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. VII (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/seventh_amendment"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-jmol-50b","prompt":"At no point before the case is submitted to the jury does the defendant move for judgment as a matter of law. The jury returns a verdict for the plaintiff, and judgment is entered. Ten days later, the defendant files a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law under Rule 50(b), arguing that no reasonable jury could have found causation. How should the court rule?","choices":["Decide it on the merits, because it was filed within 28 days after the entry of judgment.","Grant it if the judge, weighing the evidence independently, would have found for the defendant.","Deny it, because the defendant made no Rule 50(a) motion before the case went to the jury.","Deny it, because judgment as a matter of law is unavailable once a jury has returned a verdict."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A Rule 50(b) motion is a renewal of a Rule 50(a) motion made before the case is submitted to the jury; a party that made no pre-verdict motion cannot seek judgment as a matter of law afterward, even if the post-verdict motion is filed within 28 days. When the motion is proper, the court asks whether a reasonable jury had a legally sufficient basis for its verdict, without weighing evidence or credibility. The defendant could still seek a new trial under Rule 59, which has no pre-verdict motion requirement.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["jmol","rule-50","post-trial-motions"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_50"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-parklane","prompt":"A group of shareholders sued Corwin Corp. and, after a full trial, won a finding that Corwin issued a materially false proxy statement. Nell, another shareholder, knew of that suit and could easily have joined it as a plaintiff but chose to wait for the outcome. She now sues Corwin for damages and asks the federal court to bar Corwin from relitigating the falsity issue. Corwin defended the first suit vigorously, and no judgments conflict. What result is most likely?","choices":["Preclusion will be granted, because Corwin had a full and fair opportunity to litigate the issue.","Preclusion will be denied, because Nell was not a party to the first suit, so mutuality is lacking.","Preclusion will be granted, because offensive and defensive nonmutual uses are treated alike.","Preclusion will likely be denied, because Nell could easily have joined the first suit but chose to wait."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Federal courts have abandoned strict mutuality, but offensive nonmutual issue preclusion is discretionary under Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore. A trial court generally should not allow it when the plaintiff could easily have joined the earlier action, because that would reward wait-and-see plaintiffs; other factors include the defendant's incentive to litigate, inconsistent prior judgments, and procedural opportunities unavailable in the first action. Defensive nonmutual use is more readily permitted (Blonder-Tongue), so the two are not treated identically.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["issue-preclusion","nonmutual","offensive","parklane"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322 (1979) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/439/322/"}]},{"id":"v3-civpro-interlocutory-appeal","prompt":"In a federal trade-secret case, the district court (1) denies the defendant's motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction and (2) grants the plaintiff a preliminary injunction barring the defendant from using a customer list. The defendant promptly appeals both orders without seeking certification from the district court. Which order may the court of appeals review now?","choices":["Only the preliminary injunction, which is immediately appealable under § 1292(a)(1).","Only the jurisdictional ruling, which qualifies under the collateral-order doctrine.","Both orders, because each conclusively resolves a disputed issue between the parties.","Neither order, because review must await final judgment absent certification."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the final-judgment rule (28 U.S.C. § 1291), most interlocutory orders must await a final judgment, but § 1292(a)(1) expressly allows immediate appeal from orders granting or denying injunctions. A denial of a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction is not a collateral order because it can be effectively reviewed after final judgment. Certification under § 1292(b) is a separate, discretionary route that the injunction appeal does not need.","subject":"civilProcedure","tags":["appeals","final-judgment-rule","interlocutory","1292"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"28 U.S.C. § 1292 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1292"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-raich-aggregation","prompt":"Federal law bans possessing marijuana. Rosa grows marijuana at home solely for her own medical use, as her state's law permits; none of it is sold or crosses state lines. She argues that Congress lacks power to reach her purely local, noncommercial activity. Is the federal ban constitutional as applied to her?","choices":["No, because her activity is intrastate and noncommercial, so it lies beyond the commerce power.","Yes, because her activity, taken in the aggregate with others', substantially affects an interstate market.","No, because the state law authorizing medical use controls over federal law on matters of health.","Yes, because the spending power lets Congress regulate any activity that affects the general welfare."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Congress may regulate activities that substantially affect interstate commerce, and it may aggregate local activities that are part of an economic class when it rationally concludes that leaving them unregulated would undercut regulation of an interstate market (Gonzales v. Raich, following Wickard v. Filburn). Home-grown marijuana is a fungible commodity with an established interstate market. Under the Supremacy Clause the federal law prevails over the state authorization, and the spending power is not a general regulatory power.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["commerce-clause","aggregation","raich"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articlei"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-morrison-noneconomic","prompt":"Congress creates a federal civil damages remedy for victims of gender-motivated violence. It relies on extensive findings that such violence, in the aggregate, reduces national economic productivity and interstate travel. A defendant sued under the statute argues that Congress lacked power under the Commerce Clause to enact it. Is the remedy a valid exercise of the commerce power?","choices":["Yes, because Congress's detailed findings establish a substantial effect on interstate commerce.","Yes, because Congress may regulate any activity that, in the aggregate, has economic consequences.","No, because noneconomic, intrastate violence cannot be aggregated to show a substantial effect on commerce.","No, because Congress may regulate only the channels and instrumentalities of interstate commerce."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The commerce power reaches the channels and instrumentalities of interstate commerce and activities that substantially affect it, but aggregation applies to economic activity. In United States v. Morrison, the Court held that gender-motivated violence is noneconomic, intrastate conduct and that congressional findings could not substitute for that limit, following United States v. Lopez. The Court also rejected § 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment as a basis because the remedy reached private actors, not state action.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["commerce-clause","morrison","lopez"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articlei"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-spending-coercion","prompt":"Congress expands a long-standing joint federal-state health program for low-income people. Under the new law, any state that refuses to adopt the expansion will lose all of its existing federal funding for the program, which accounts for over 10% of a typical state's total budget. A state challenges the condition. Is the condition valid?","choices":["Yes, because Congress may attach any condition to federal funds as long as it is stated unambiguously.","Yes, because the condition is related to the federal interest in the program, as Dole requires.","No, because Congress may not use spending conditions to influence state health-care policy.","No, because threatening all existing program funds is so coercive that states have no real choice."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Congress may attach conditions to federal funds if they are unambiguous, related to the federal interest in the program, not barred by another constitutional provision, and not so coercive that pressure turns into compulsion (South Dakota v. Dole). In NFIB v. Sebelius, threatening the loss of all existing Medicaid funds, a large share of state budgets, to induce acceptance of a new expansion was a 'gun to the head' and unconstitutionally coercive. Relatedness and clarity alone do not save a coercive condition, though Congress may use funding conditions to influence state policy.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["spending-power","conditional-spending","coercion","nfib"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (Spending Clause) (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articlei"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-murphy-commandeering","prompt":"A federal statute makes it unlawful for any state to 'authorize' sports gambling by law. State Z repeals its own laws prohibiting sports betting at casinos, and the federal government contends that the repeal is an unlawful authorization. State Z challenges the federal statute. Is the prohibition on state authorization constitutional?","choices":["No, because Congress may not dictate what a state legislature may or may not enact.","Yes, because it only forbids state action and never commands the state to act.","Yes, because a valid federal statute preempts conflicting state laws under the Supremacy Clause.","No, because the commerce power does not reach gambling conducted entirely within one state."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In Murphy v. NCAA (2018), the Court held that a federal ban on states authorizing sports gambling unconstitutionally commandeered state legislatures. The anti-commandeering rule bars both commands and prohibitions directed at state lawmaking, so the action/inaction distinction does not matter. Preemption requires a federal law that regulates private actors, which this provision did not do; Congress could regulate sports gambling directly under the Commerce Clause.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["anti-commandeering","tenth-amendment","murphy"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. X (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/tenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-privileges-immunities","prompt":"State A charges residents $25 and nonresidents $2,500 for a commercial shrimp-fishing license. The state cannot show that nonresidents cause any distinct problem or that the fee difference reflects any added cost they impose. A nonresident individual who fishes for a living sues under the Privileges and Immunities Clause of Article IV. What result?","choices":["The fee is valid, because a state may prefer its own citizens in allocating its natural resources.","The fee is invalid, because it burdens nonresidents' pursuit of a livelihood without a substantial reason.","The fee is valid, because fishing licenses, like hunting licenses, are not fundamental rights under the clause.","The fee is invalid, because Article IV forbids states from charging nonresidents more than residents."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Article IV's Privileges and Immunities Clause protects individual nonresidents' fundamental rights, including pursuing a livelihood, against discrimination by a state. Discrimination is permitted only if there is a substantial reason for it and the discrimination bears a substantial relationship to that reason; State A's showing fails (Toomer v. Witsell). Recreational hunting is not fundamental (Baldwin v. Fish & Game Comm'n), but commercial fishing is, and not every residency distinction is invalid.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["privileges-and-immunities","article-iv","nonresidents"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. IV (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articleiv"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-market-participant","prompt":"State B owns and operates a cement plant. During a regional shortage, the state decides to sell the plant's cement only to State B residents. An out-of-state construction company that had regularly bought from the plant challenges the policy under the dormant Commerce Clause. What result?","choices":["The policy is invalid, because it facially discriminates against out-of-state purchasers.","The policy is valid, because cement sold within the state is not part of interstate commerce.","The policy is valid, because the state is acting as a market participant.","The policy is invalid unless no nondiscriminatory alternative could meet the state's needs."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under the market-participant exception, a state that buys or sells goods in the market may favor its own residents without violating the dormant Commerce Clause (Reeves, Inc. v. Stake, involving a state-owned cement plant). The strict test for discriminatory regulation therefore does not apply. The exception is limited: a state may not impose conditions that regulate downstream markets (South-Central Timber v. Wunnicke), and it does not shield discrimination from Article IV's Privileges and Immunities Clause, which in any event does not protect corporations.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["dormant-commerce","market-participant"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. I, § 8 (Commerce Clause) (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articlei"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-seila-removal","prompt":"Congress creates an agency with broad rulemaking and enforcement authority over a major sector of the economy. The agency is led by a single director who serves a five-year term and may be removed by the President only for 'inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office.' A regulated firm challenges the removal restriction. Is it constitutional?","choices":["Yes, because Humphrey's Executor permits for-cause protection for the heads of independent agencies.","Yes, because the for-cause standard still leaves the President ample power to remove the director.","No, because the Appointments Clause requires that every agency head be confirmed by the Senate.","No, because a single director with significant executive power must be removable by the President at will."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Article II vests executive power in the President, which generally includes power to remove principal officers. In Seila Law LLC v. CFPB (2020), the Court held that Congress may not insulate the single director of an agency wielding significant executive power from at-will removal; Collins v. Yellen (2021) applied the same rule to another single-director agency. Humphrey's Executor, which had upheld for-cause protection for members of a multimember commission, never reached a single director and was itself overruled in Trump v. Slaughter (2026), which held that the President may remove FTC commissioners at will. Morrison v. Olson's for-cause allowance involved an inferior officer with limited duties, and the defect here lies in the removal restriction, not in the method of appointment.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["separation-of-powers","removal","seila-law"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Seila Law LLC v. Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/19-7"},{"title":"Trump v. Slaughter (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/25-332"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-buckley-appointments","prompt":"Congress creates a commission to enforce federal election law through civil lawsuits and rulemaking. The statute provides that four of its six voting members will be appointed by the Speaker of the House and the President pro tempore of the Senate. Is this appointment scheme constitutional?","choices":["No, because officers with significant enforcement power must be appointed under Article II's procedures.","Yes, because Congress may appoint the officials who enforce laws governing congressional elections.","Yes, because the Senate's presiding officer participates, which satisfies the advice-and-consent role.","No, because the President alone must appoint every federal official, including inferior officers."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Anyone exercising significant authority under federal law is an officer of the United States who must be appointed under the Appointments Clause: principal officers by the President with Senate consent, and inferior officers by the President, the courts of law, or department heads if Congress so provides (Buckley v. Valeo). Congress may not appoint officers who enforce the law. Congress may vest appointment of inferior officers in the courts or department heads, so presidential appointment of every official is not required.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["separation-of-powers","appointments-clause","buckley"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. II, § 2 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articleii"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-jackson-utility","prompt":"A privately owned electric utility holds a state-granted monopoly and is heavily regulated by a state commission, which approved the utility's general tariff allowing service to be cut off for nonpayment. The utility terminates Ruth's service without notice or a hearing. Ruth sues the utility, claiming a denial of procedural due process. Is the termination state action?","choices":["Yes, because the utility holds a state-granted monopoly and is pervasively regulated by the state.","No, because regulation, monopoly status, and tariff approval do not make the cutoff the state's act.","Yes, because providing electric service is a public function traditionally and exclusively for the state.","Yes, because the commission's approval of the cutoff tariff makes the state responsible for it."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A private entity's conduct is state action only if there is a sufficiently close nexus between the state and the challenged action, the state coerced or significantly encouraged it, or the entity performs a traditionally exclusive public function. In Jackson v. Metropolitan Edison Co., the Court held that a heavily regulated private utility with a monopoly did not engage in state action by terminating service under a tariff the commission had approved without ordering. Utility service is not a traditionally exclusive government function.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["state-action","regulated-entity","jackson"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. XIV (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fourteenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-economic-due-process","prompt":"A state law forbids opticians from fitting or duplicating eyeglass lenses without a prescription from an ophthalmologist or optometrist. An optician challenges the law under the Due Process Clause, showing that the requirement is often unnecessary and raises costs for consumers. What standard of review applies?","choices":["Strict scrutiny, because the law burdens the fundamental right to pursue a chosen occupation.","Intermediate scrutiny, because the law imposes significant costs on both consumers and businesses.","Rational-basis review, under which any conceivable legitimate reason will suffice.","Rational-basis review, but the state must prove with evidence that the law actually improves health."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Economic and social-welfare regulations that do not burden fundamental rights receive rational-basis review: the law is upheld if it is rationally related to any conceivable legitimate purpose, and the challenger bears the burden. In Williamson v. Lee Optical, the Court upheld a similar prescription requirement even though it was often wasteful, because the legislature might have thought it served health. The state need not produce evidence, and pursuing an occupation is not a fundamental right triggering strict scrutiny.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["substantive-due-process","rational-basis","economic-regulation"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. XIV (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/fourteenth_amendment"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-sffa","prompt":"A public university considers each applicant's race as one 'plus' factor among many in a holistic review, citing the educational benefits of diversity. The program has no end date, and the university measures success by comparing its enrollment to broad racial categories. A rejected applicant sues under the Equal Protection Clause. What result is most likely?","choices":["The program is valid, because race is only one plus factor within an individualized review.","The program is valid under intermediate scrutiny, because it aims to help underrepresented groups.","The program is invalid, because the Equal Protection Clause prohibits every government use of race.","The program is invalid, because its open-ended, unmeasurable goals cannot survive strict scrutiny."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Racial classifications, including those intended to benefit minorities, receive strict scrutiny. In Students for Fair Admissions v. President & Fellows of Harvard College (2023), the Court held that race-conscious admissions programs failed because their diversity interests were not sufficiently measurable, they used race as a negative and relied on stereotypes, and they lacked a logical end point, effectively displacing Grutter v. Bollinger's plus-factor approach. The Court did not hold that every use of race is forbidden, noting that applicants may discuss how race affected their own lives.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["equal-protection","race","affirmative-action","sffa"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Students for Fair Admissions, Inc. v. President & Fellows of Harvard College (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/20-1199"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-limited-forum","prompt":"A public school district opens its classrooms after hours to community groups for 'educational, civic, and recreational' activities. It refuses access to a club that wants to teach children moral character from a religious perspective, while allowing a scouting group to teach character from a secular perspective. The club sues. What result?","choices":["The club wins, because shutting out a religious perspective on an allowed topic is viewpoint discrimination.","The district wins, because the club's religious instruction falls outside the forum's reserved purposes.","The district wins, because allowing the club in would amount to an establishment of religion.","The club wins, because opening school property after hours turns it into a traditional public forum."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In a limited public forum, the government may reserve the forum for certain groups or topics, but its restrictions must be reasonable and viewpoint neutral. Excluding a religious perspective on a subject the forum otherwise allows, such as moral character, is viewpoint discrimination (Good News Club v. Milford Central School; Lamb's Chapel). Allowing private religious speech on equal terms does not violate the Establishment Clause, and opening property for limited purposes does not create a traditional public forum.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["speech","limited-public-forum","viewpoint-discrimination","religion"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-kennedy-bremerton","prompt":"A public high school football coach, after games and once players had left the field, knelt at midfield and offered a brief, quiet personal prayer. No students were asked or pressured to join. The district disciplined him, reasoning that allowing the prayer would make it look as if the school endorsed religion. The coach sues. Under current Supreme Court doctrine, what result is most likely?","choices":["The district prevails, because a reasonable observer would view the prayer as the school endorsing religion.","The coach prevails, because his quiet prayer was private religious expression the school could not suppress.","The district prevails, because speech by a public employee during work hours is government speech.","The coach prevails, but only because the district's policy failed the secular-purpose prong of the Lemon test."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (2022), the Court held that a coach's brief, quiet personal prayer was protected by the Free Exercise and Free Speech Clauses: it was not within his official duties, so it was private speech rather than government speech under Garcetti. The Court rejected the Lemon test and its endorsement offshoot, holding that the Establishment Clause is interpreted by reference to historical practices and understandings, and found no coercion of students. Because the district relied on that abandoned framework, its action could not satisfy strict scrutiny.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["establishment-clause","free-exercise","kennedy-v-bremerton","public-employee"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Kennedy v. Bremerton School District (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/21-418"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-fulton","prompt":"A city contracts with private agencies to certify foster families. The contract bars refusing certification based on sexual orientation, but a city official may grant exceptions in the official's 'sole discretion.' A Catholic agency will not certify same-sex couples because of its religious beliefs, and the city refuses to renew its contract without considering an exception. The agency sues under the Free Exercise Clause. Is the city's refusal likely to be upheld?","choices":["Yes, because the nondiscrimination rule is neutral and generally applicable, so only rational-basis review applies.","Yes, because eliminating discrimination is a compelling interest that justifies every application of the policy.","No, because the city has no compelling reason to deny this agency an exception while reserving power to grant others.","No, because the Free Exercise Clause requires exemptions for religious objectors to nondiscrimination laws."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In Fulton v. City of Philadelphia (2021), the Court held that a contract provision allowing exceptions at an official's sole discretion made the nondiscrimination requirement not generally applicable, so strict scrutiny applied. The question is not whether the city has a compelling interest in its policy generally but whether it has one in denying an exception to this particular agency, and the city's willingness to consider exceptions undercut that claim. Fulton did not overrule Employment Division v. Smith, so neutral and generally applicable laws still receive rational-basis review.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["free-exercise","fulton","general-applicability"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Fulton v. City of Philadelphia (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/19-123"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-bruen-test","prompt":"A state requires applicants for a license to carry a handgun in public to show 'proper cause,' meaning a special need for self-defense beyond that of the general community. A law-abiding applicant who was denied a license sues under the Second Amendment. Under New York State Rifle & Pistol Ass'n v. Bruen, how does the court evaluate the requirement?","choices":["It applies intermediate scrutiny, weighing the state's public-safety interest against the burden.","It applies strict scrutiny, because keeping and bearing arms is a fundamental individual right.","It asks whether the law is rationally related to the legitimate goal of reducing gun violence.","It asks whether the law fits the Nation's historical tradition of firearm regulation."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Bruen (2022) held that when the Second Amendment's plain text covers an individual's conduct, the Constitution presumptively protects it, and the government must justify its regulation by showing that it is consistent with the Nation's historical tradition of firearm regulation. The Court rejected the means-end scrutiny that lower courts had used, and it struck down New York's proper-cause requirement. Neither rational-basis review nor strict scrutiny is the test.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["second-amendment","bruen"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"New York State Rifle & Pistol Ass'n v. Bruen (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/20-843"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-rahimi","prompt":"A federal statute prohibits a person subject to a domestic-violence restraining order from possessing a firearm when a court has found, after notice and a hearing, that the person poses a credible threat to the physical safety of an intimate partner. A defendant charged under the statute argues that no Founding-era law was identical to it. Is the statute constitutional on its face?","choices":["Yes, because history supports disarming people whom a court finds to threaten others' physical safety.","No, because Bruen requires the government to identify a historical twin of the modern law.","No, because only a criminal conviction, not a civil order, can justify disarming a citizen.","Yes, because Congress may disarm anyone it reasonably deems not responsible enough to own guns."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In United States v. Rahimi (2024), the Court upheld 18 U.S.C. § 922(g)(8)(C)(i) against a facial challenge. Historical surety and 'going armed' laws established a tradition that individuals found by a court to pose a clear threat of physical violence to others may be temporarily disarmed. Bruen requires a relevantly similar historical analogue, not a 'historical twin,' and the Court rejected the government's broader argument that anyone not 'responsible' may be disarmed.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["second-amendment","rahimi","historical-tradition"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"United States v. Rahimi (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-915"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-smith-baseline","prompt":"A state's criminal drug law bans possession of peyote and contains no exceptions. Two members of a Native American church are fired for ingesting peyote at a religious ceremony and are denied unemployment benefits because they were fired for work-related misconduct. They claim the denial violates the Free Exercise Clause. What standard applies?","choices":["Strict scrutiny, because the law substantially burdens a sincere religious practice.","Rational-basis review, because the law is neutral and generally applicable.","Intermediate scrutiny, because the law regulates religious conduct, not belief.","Strict scrutiny, because Sherbert v. Verner governs unemployment-benefit denials."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Employment Division v. Smith (1990), a neutral law of general applicability that incidentally burdens religious practice does not violate the Free Exercise Clause and need not be justified by a compelling interest. Sherbert v. Verner's compelling-interest test is confined to systems of individualized exemptions and does not reach across-the-board criminal prohibitions. Congress responded with RFRA, but RFRA cannot be applied to the states (City of Boerne v. Flores).","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["free-exercise","smith","neutral-generally-applicable"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. amend. I (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/first_amendment"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-sheetz-exactions","prompt":"A county ordinance requires every applicant for a single-family building permit to pay a fixed 'traffic impact fee' set by a legislative schedule, without any individualized assessment of a project's effect on traffic. A homeowner pays under protest and argues that the fee is an unconstitutional condition. The county responds that Nollan and Dolan apply only to conditions imposed by administrators case by case. Who is right on that point?","choices":["The county, because legislative fees receive deferential review as general land-use regulation.","The county, because Nollan and Dolan cover demands for interests in land, not monetary fees.","The homeowner, because legislative permit conditions are also subject to Nollan and Dolan.","The homeowner, because a monetary permit fee is treated as a per se physical taking."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Nollan and Dolan, a permit condition must have an essential nexus to, and rough proportionality with, the impacts of the proposed development. Koontz v. St. Johns River Water Management District (2013) extended the test to monetary exactions, and Sheetz v. County of El Dorado (2024) held that the Takings Clause draws no distinction between legislative and administrative permit conditions. Sheetz left open whether a condition imposed on a class of properties must be tailored with the same degree of specificity, and a permit fee is not itself a physical taking.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["takings","exactions","nollan-dolan","sheetz"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Sheetz v. County of El Dorado (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/22-1074"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-mootness-voluntary-cessation","prompt":"A city adopts a policy excluding a church from a playground-resurfacing grant program, and the church sues for an injunction. While the suit is pending, the mayor announces that the city will stop enforcing the policy 'for now,' although the city maintains that the policy was lawful and could be reinstated at any time. The city moves to dismiss as moot. How should the court rule?","choices":["Grant the motion, because no live controversy remains once the city stops enforcing the policy.","Grant the motion, unless the church shows the policy is capable of repetition yet evading review.","Deny the motion, because only the plaintiff's voluntary dismissal can render a pending case moot.","Deny the motion, because the city's voluntary, revocable halt does not show the policy will not return."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A defendant's voluntary cessation of challenged conduct does not moot a case unless the defendant shows that it is absolutely clear the conduct cannot reasonably be expected to recur (Friends of the Earth v. Laidlaw; FBI v. Fikre (2024)). A revocable 'for now' change, coupled with a claim that the policy is lawful, falls short. Capable of repetition yet evading review is a separate exception for short-lived controversies, and cases can become moot in many ways besides the plaintiff's dismissal.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["justiciability","mootness","voluntary-cessation"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. III (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articleiii"}]},{"id":"v3-conlaw-ripeness-preenforcement","prompt":"A state law makes it a crime to publish 'false statements' about political candidates. An advocacy group plans to run ads during an upcoming campaign saying that a candidate voted for 'taxpayer-funded abortion.' A complaint was filed against the group under this law in the previous election, and the state has not disavowed enforcement. The group sues before running the ads. Is the challenge justiciable now?","choices":["Yes, because the group faces a credible threat of enforcement for its planned ads.","No, because the group has not yet been prosecuted and so has not suffered any actual injury.","Yes, because the overbreadth doctrine lets anyone challenge a speech law before it is enforced.","No, because the state courts must first decide whether the planned ads are actually false."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A plaintiff may bring a pre-enforcement challenge when it intends to engage in conduct arguably affected with a constitutional interest, the conduct is arguably proscribed by the law, and there is a credible threat of enforcement (Susan B. Anthony List v. Driehaus). A prior complaint against the same group and the state's refusal to disavow enforcement make the threat credible, so the group need not risk prosecution first. Overbreadth relaxes the rule against asserting others' rights but does not dispense with the plaintiff's own injury, and no abstention doctrine requires a state ruling first here.","subject":"constitutionalLaw","tags":["justiciability","ripeness","pre-enforcement"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"U.S. Const. art. III (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/articleiii"}]},{"id":"contracts-preexisting-duty-v2","prompt":"Partway through excavation under a fixed-price contract to build an office, a contractor hits a layer of bedrock that neither party had any reason to anticipate. The owner orally promises to pay an extra $40,000 for removing it, a fair amount given the added cost, and the contractor finishes the job. The owner then refuses to pay the extra amount. Under the common law, is the owner's promise enforceable?","choices":["Yes, because the modification was fair and equitable in light of circumstances the parties did not anticipate.","No, because the contractor was already bound to finish the excavation and so gave no new consideration.","No, because a modification of a contract to construct a building must be in writing to be enforceable.","Yes, because a modification needs no consideration so long as both parties agree to it in good faith."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"At common law the preexisting duty rule ordinarily makes a promise of extra pay for already-owed work unenforceable. Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 89 recognizes an exception when the modification is fair and equitable in view of circumstances not anticipated when the contract was made, which describes the unforeseen bedrock. Good faith alone is the UCC 2-209 standard for sales of goods, not the common-law rule for construction, and a construction modification is not a Statute of Frauds category.","subject":"contracts","tags":["consideration","modification","preexisting-duty"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"contracts-ucc-mod-v2","prompt":"A retailer and a wholesaler orally agree on the sale of 40 lamps for $400. Before delivery, an unforeseen supply shortage drives up the wholesaler's costs, and the parties orally agree in good faith to raise the price to $700. Neither party ever signs any writing. When the wholesaler tenders the lamps, the retailer refuses to pay more than $400. Is the $700 price enforceable under the UCC?","choices":["Yes, because a modification made in good faith binds the parties under the UCC without new consideration.","No, because the wholesaler gave no new consideration for the retailer's promise to pay the higher price.","No, because the contract as modified is for $500 or more, and no writing satisfies the Statute of Frauds.","Yes, because the original agreement was outside the Statute of Frauds, so any modification of it is too."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"UCC 2-209(1) makes a good-faith modification binding without consideration, but 2-209(3) requires the Statute of Frauds to be satisfied if the contract as modified falls within it. At $700 the modified contract is within UCC 2-201, and nothing here (no writing, merchant confirmation, payment, or acceptance of goods) satisfies it, so the original $400 deal stands. The consideration objection states the common-law rule that 2-209 abolished for goods.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","modification","statute-of-frauds","2-209"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-209 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-209"}]},{"id":"property-fee-simple-v2","prompt":"An owner deeds land \"to the Riverside School so long as the land is used for classroom instruction.\" Years later, the school converts the entire property into administrative offices. The owner, who is still living, has taken no action since learning of the change. At common law, who holds title to the land?","choices":["The school, until the owner exercises a right of entry by suing to recover the land.","The school, because the deed's language merely states a purpose and imposes no limitation.","The owner, because the school's estate ended automatically when the classroom use stopped.","The school, subject to the owner's right to enforce the use restriction as a covenant."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Durational words such as \"so long as\" create a fee simple determinable, leaving the grantor a possibility of reverter. When the stated use ends, the estate terminates automatically and title returns to the grantor without any action. A right of entry that must be exercised belongs to a fee simple subject to condition subsequent (\"but if\" or \"upon condition that\" language), and bare purpose language (\"for the purpose of\") would create no defeasible fee at all.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["future-interests","defeasible-fees","fee-simple-determinable"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"property-landlord-duty-v2","prompt":"Before leasing an apartment, a landlord learns that a concealed furnace vent is cracked and can leak carbon monoxide. The crack is not discoverable on a reasonable inspection, and the landlord says nothing. Three months into the lease, the tenant is injured by a leak. The lease says nothing about repairs, and no statute applies. Is the landlord liable to the tenant?","choices":["No, because a landlord owes no duty as to conditions inside the unit once the tenant takes possession.","Yes, because the landlord failed to disclose a known latent danger the tenant could not reasonably discover.","No, because a landlord's duty of care extends only to common areas that remain under its control.","Yes, because a landlord is strictly liable for injuries from any defect existing when the lease began."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The traditional rule that a landlord owes no duty regarding conditions in the leased premises has exceptions, one of which is a latent defect the landlord knows or should know of, that the tenant could not discover on reasonable inspection, and that the landlord fails to disclose. The concealed cracked vent fits that exception. The no-duty and common-area choices state the general rule without the exception, and landlords are not strictly liable for preexisting defects.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["landlord-tenant","tort-liability","latent-defects"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"contracts-sof-ucc","prompt":"A bakery orally agrees to buy a used commercial oven from a restaurant-supply dealer for $4,800. Before delivery or payment, the dealer backs out, and the bakery sues. Neither party signed any writing, and the dealer does not admit the contract. Is the dealer's Statute of Frauds defense likely to succeed?","choices":["No, because the UCC Statute of Frauds applies only to sales of goods for a price of $5,000 or more.","No, because the Statute of Frauds governs sales of goods only when both of the parties are merchants.","Yes, because a contract for the sale of goods for a price of $500 or more generally requires a signed writing.","Yes, because every contract for the sale of goods between merchants must be evidenced by a signed writing."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"UCC 2-201(1) requires a writing signed by the party to be charged for a sale of goods for a price of $500 or more, and no exception (merchant confirmation, specially manufactured goods, admission, or payment/acceptance) applies here. The $5,000 figure comes from the 2003 amendments to Article 2, which were withdrawn and never adopted. Merchant status matters only for the confirmation exception; a low-priced sale between merchants needs no writing.","subject":"contracts","tags":["statute-of-frauds","ucc","2-201"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-201 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-201"}]},{"id":"contracts-parol","prompt":"A buyer and a seller sign a detailed written contract for the sale of a boat for $60,000. The writing contains a merger clause stating that it is the parties' complete and exclusive agreement. In a later dispute, the buyer offers evidence that, a week before signing, the seller orally agreed to accept $52,000. The buyer does not allege fraud, mistake, or ambiguity. Is the evidence admissible to prove the lower price?","choices":["Yes, because the parol evidence rule bars only agreements reached after the writing was signed.","Yes, because extrinsic evidence is always admissible when it concerns a term as essential as price.","No, because a merger clause makes all evidence outside the writing inadmissible for any purpose.","No, because evidence of a prior agreement may not be used to contradict a final, integrated writing."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The parol evidence rule (UCC 2-202 for goods) bars prior or contemporaneous agreements offered to contradict a writing the parties intended as final, and a fully integrated writing also excludes consistent additional terms. The oral $52,000 price contradicts the written $60,000 price, so it is excluded. The rule does not reach later modifications, and even a merger clause does not bar evidence offered to show fraud, mistake, or the meaning of an ambiguous term.","subject":"contracts","tags":["parol-evidence","integration","merger-clause"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Parol evidence rule (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/parol_evidence_rule"}]},{"id":"contracts-mistake","prompt":"A rancher sells a purebred cow to a neighbor for $800, its value as beef, because both of them believe the cow is barren. Before delivery, the rancher discovers the cow is pregnant and worth about $8,000 as a breeder. The contract says nothing about fertility, and neither party was consciously taking a chance on it. Can the rancher avoid the contract?","choices":["No, because the parties' mistake concerned only the cow's market value, a risk every seller bears.","Yes, because both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption that materially affects the exchange.","Yes, but only if the neighbor knew or had reason to know of the rancher's mistake when contracting.","No, because relief for mistake is available only when the other party caused the error by fraud."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A mutual mistake about a basic assumption on which the contract was made, having a material effect on the agreed exchange, makes the contract voidable by the adversely affected party unless that party bore the risk (Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 152, 154; Sherwood v. Walker). Barrenness went to the nature of the cow, not merely its price, and neither party assumed the risk. The knew-or-had-reason-to-know test belongs to unilateral mistake, and fraud is a separate ground for relief.","subject":"contracts","tags":["mistake","mutual-mistake","voidable"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"property-adverse-v2","prompt":"An owner gives a neighbor permission to garden on a strip of the owner's land. The neighbor gardens there openly, exclusively, and continuously for 20 years and, after the first few years, privately comes to believe the strip is hers. She never tells the owner that she claims it. The limitations period is 10 years, and no tax-payment statute applies. Has the neighbor acquired title by adverse possession?","choices":["Yes, because 20 years of open, exclusive, and continuous use is twice the statutory period.","Yes, because her belief that she owned the strip made her possession hostile from that point on.","No, because use that begins with permission is not hostile until clearly repudiated.","No, because she never held color of title, such as a defective deed, to the strip of land."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Adverse possession requires hostile possession that is also actual, open and notorious, exclusive, and continuous for the statutory period. Possession that begins with the owner's permission stays permissive until the possessor clearly repudiates the permission in a way the owner would notice; a private change of belief is not enough. Color of title is not required in most jurisdictions and only affects constructive possession of a larger parcel.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["adverse-possession","hostility","permission"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Adverse possession (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/adverse_possession"}]},{"id":"property-recording","prompt":"O conveys Blackacre to A, who does not record. O then conveys Blackacre to B, who pays value and has no notice of A's deed. A records the next day; B records a week later. The recording act provides: \"No conveyance is valid against a subsequent purchaser for value without notice thereof whose conveyance is first duly recorded.\" Who owns Blackacre?","choices":["B, because B paid value without notice of A's deed.","B, because A's deed was unrecorded when B bought.","A, because between grantees recording is irrelevant.","A, because B did not record before A did."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The statute's \"without notice\" and \"first duly recorded\" language makes it a race-notice act: a subsequent purchaser prevails only if it took for value without notice and also recorded before the prior grantee. B satisfied the first requirement but lost the race to A, so A's earlier deed controls. B's answer would be correct under a pure notice statute, and recording does matter; it is what lets a later purchaser cut off an earlier grantee.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["recording-acts","race-notice","bona-fide-purchaser"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"contracts-anticipatory","prompt":"In March, a supplier contracts to deliver 1,000 custom brackets to a manufacturer on September 1. In May, the supplier writes that it \"will not deliver the brackets under any circumstances.\" The manufacturer sues for breach in June. The supplier moves to dismiss, arguing that no performance was yet due. How should the court rule?","choices":["Deny the motion, because an unequivocal repudiation lets the other party sue for breach right away.","Grant the motion, because an action for breach cannot be brought before the date set for performance.","Grant the motion, because the manufacturer first had to demand adequate assurance of performance.","Deny the motion, but only if the manufacturer tendered its own performance before bringing the suit."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A clear, unequivocal statement that a party will not perform is an anticipatory repudiation, and under UCC 2-610 (and the common law) the aggrieved party may treat it as a total breach and sue immediately. Demanding adequate assurance under 2-609 is the tool for ambiguous signs of trouble, not a prerequisite after an express refusal. The aggrieved party need not tender its own performance first; it is excused by the repudiation.","subject":"contracts","tags":["anticipatory-repudiation","breach","ucc","2-610"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-610 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-610"}]},{"id":"contracts-third-party","prompt":"Dana sells her business to Eli. In the written contract, Eli promises Dana that he will pay Dana's $20,000 debt to Faye. Before Faye learns of the promise or does anything in reliance on it, Dana and Eli agree to cancel Eli's promise. Faye later learns of both events and sues Eli. Is Eli liable to Faye?","choices":["Yes, because an intended beneficiary's rights vest as soon as the contract is made.","No, because Faye is only an incidental beneficiary of the contract between Dana and Eli.","No, because Dana and Eli rescinded the promise before Faye's rights vested.","Yes, because a promise to pay the promisee's creditor cannot be rescinded without the creditor."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Faye is an intended (creditor) beneficiary, but under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 311 the promisor and promisee may modify or rescind the promise until the beneficiary's rights vest, which happens when she manifests assent at a party's request, sues on the promise, or materially changes position in justifiable reliance. None of that occurred before the rescission, so Faye has no claim against Eli, though she can still collect from Dana. The immediate-vesting rule reflects an older approach to donee beneficiaries, not the modern rule.","subject":"contracts","tags":["third-party-beneficiary","vesting","modification"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"contracts-condition","prompt":"A written contract for the sale of a house states that the buyer's obligation is \"subject to the buyer obtaining a mortgage loan at an interest rate of no more than 7% by June 1.\" Despite diligent, good-faith applications, the buyer cannot obtain a loan below 8% by June 1 and refuses to close. The seller sues for breach. Is the buyer liable?","choices":["Yes, because the buyer promised to obtain financing and did not obtain it by June 1.","Yes, because the buyer bore the risk that interest rates would rise before the deadline.","No, because the financing condition failed, so the buyer's duty to buy never became due.","No, because the rise in interest rates made the buyer's performance impracticable."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A condition precedent is an event that must occur before a duty of performance becomes due. Obtaining a loan at 7% or less was a condition of the buyer's duty, not a promise, and the buyer made the good-faith efforts that an implied promise would require; when the condition failed, the duty never arose. Impracticability is the wrong doctrine: the buyer is excused by the contract's own condition, not by an unforeseen event.","subject":"contracts","tags":["conditions","condition-precedent","financing"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"contracts-perfect-tender","prompt":"A restaurant contracts to buy 500 blue linen napkins, to be delivered in a single shipment by May 1. The seller delivers on May 1, but 20 of the napkins are green. The time for performance has expired, and the seller had no reason to believe green napkins would be acceptable. May the restaurant reject the entire shipment?","choices":["Yes, because in a single-delivery contract a buyer may reject if the tender fails in any respect.","No, because the defect is minor and does not substantially impair the value of the whole shipment.","No, because the seller has a right to cure any defective tender within a reasonable time afterward.","Yes, but only if the nonconformity is serious enough to be a material breach of the contract."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the perfect tender rule of UCC 2-601, a buyer in a single-delivery contract may reject the whole tender, accept it, or accept some commercial units if the goods or tender fail in any respect to conform. The substantial-impairment standard applies to installment contracts (2-612) and revocation of acceptance, and material breach is the common-law test. Cure is unavailable here because the time for performance has passed and the seller had no reasonable grounds to think the tender would be accepted (2-508).","subject":"contracts","tags":["perfect-tender","ucc","rejection","2-601"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-601 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-601"}]},{"id":"property-mortgage-priority","prompt":"An owner borrows $100,000 from Bank 1 and gives it a mortgage, which Bank 1 does not record. A month later the owner borrows $50,000 from Bank 2, which has no knowledge of Bank 1's mortgage, and gives Bank 2 a mortgage that it records at once. Bank 1 records the following week. The jurisdiction has a race-notice statute. Which mortgage has priority?","choices":["Bank 2's, because it lent without notice of Bank 1's mortgage and recorded first.","Bank 1's, because its mortgage was created first and both were eventually recorded.","Bank 1's, because the earlier and larger loan is entitled to priority once recorded.","Bank 2's, because a race-notice statute protects whoever records first, regardless of notice."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A mortgagee for value is a purchaser under the recording acts. Under a race-notice statute, a later mortgagee prevails over an earlier unrecorded mortgage only if it took without notice and recorded first; Bank 2 did both, so its mortgage is first in priority. First-in-time controls only when the later lender fails one of those requirements, and loan size is irrelevant. Notice still matters under race-notice; ignoring notice describes a pure race statute.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["mortgage","priority","recording-acts","race-notice"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"property-easement-implication","prompt":"Olivia owns two adjacent lots. A buried sewer line, with a visible cleanout cap in the yard, runs from the house on Lot 1 across Lot 2 to the street. Olivia sells Lot 1 to Pat; the deed says nothing about the line. Rerouting it to avoid Lot 2 is possible but would cost $30,000. Olivia later sells Lot 2 to Quinn, who knew of the line and now demands that Pat remove it. May Pat keep using the line?","choices":["No, because an easement implied from prior use requires strict necessity, which is absent here.","Yes, because an apparent, continuous prior use that is reasonably necessary survived the severance.","No, because a buried pipe cannot be an apparent use, since it is not visible on the surface.","Yes, because Pat's continued use of the line has ripened into an easement by prescription."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An easement implied from prior use arises when land in common ownership is severed and, before severance, a use of one part benefiting the other was apparent and continuous and is reasonably necessary to the dominant parcel. The sewer line meets each element: underground utilities count as apparent when reasonable inspection (here, the cleanout cap) would reveal them, and a $30,000 reroute shows reasonable necessity. Strict necessity is required only for an easement by necessity, and no prescriptive period has run.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["easement","implied-by-prior-use","quasi-easement"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"property-implied-warranty","prompt":"A residential lease states that the tenant \"accepts the apartment as is and waives any claim regarding its condition.\" In January, the building's heating system fails, and the landlord ignores the tenant's repeated requests to repair it for six weeks. In a jurisdiction that recognizes the implied warranty of habitability, which statement best describes the tenant's position?","choices":["The tenant waived the warranty by signing a lease that expressly accepted the premises as is.","The tenant's only remedy is to vacate within a reasonable time and claim constructive eviction.","The warranty is breached only by defects existing at the start of the lease, not later failures.","The landlord breached the warranty, and the as-is clause does not waive the tenant's rights."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The implied warranty of habitability obliges a residential landlord to keep the premises fit for habitation throughout the tenancy, and courts generally hold that tenants cannot waive it in the lease. Six weeks without heat in winter is a substantial breach, giving the tenant remedies such as rent abatement or withholding, repair-and-deduct where authorized, damages, or termination. Unlike constructive eviction, the tenant need not move out to obtain relief.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["landlord-tenant","habitability","waiver"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"contracts-battle-forms","prompt":"A hardware distributor sends a purchase order for 300 drills to a manufacturer. The manufacturer returns a signed acknowledgment that accepts the order but adds, in its standard terms, a clause disclaiming all implied warranties. The acknowledgment is not expressly conditioned on the distributor's assent to the new terms. The distributor never responds, and the drills are shipped and accepted. Is the disclaimer part of the contract?","choices":["Yes, because between merchants an additional term is included unless the offeror objects.","No, because the added term made the acknowledgment a counteroffer instead of an acceptance.","No, because between merchants an additional term that materially alters the deal drops out.","Yes, because the distributor assented to the term by accepting the drills without objection."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under UCC 2-207(1), a definite and seasonable acknowledgment operates as an acceptance even though it adds terms, unless it is expressly conditioned on assent to them. Between merchants, an additional term becomes part of the contract unless the offer limits acceptance to its terms, the term materially alters the contract, or the offeror objects (2-207(2)). A clause negating implied warranties is a standard example of a material alteration, so it drops out. The counteroffer and last-shot answers apply the common-law mirror-image rule that 2-207 displaced.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","battle-of-forms","2-207","additional-terms"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-207 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-207"}]},{"id":"property-covenant","prompt":"Neighboring landowners Ruiz and Shah sign and record a written agreement in which each promises, for themselves and their successors, not to build any structure over 30 feet tall. Neither conveys any interest in land to the other. Shah later sells to Tran, who knew of the agreement, and Tran begins building a 45-foot tower. Ruiz sues Tran for damages only. Under the traditional rules, will Ruiz recover?","choices":["Yes, because the covenant was written, touches and concerns the land, and Tran had notice.","No, because Ruiz and Shah lacked horizontal privity, so the burden does not run at law.","No, because a covenant limiting building height does not touch and concern the land.","Yes, because Tran took from Shah, putting Tran in vertical privity with the covenantor."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For the burden of a real covenant to run so that a successor is liable in damages, the traditional rule requires a writing, intent to bind successors, touch and concern, notice, vertical privity, and horizontal privity between the original parties (a grantor-grantee or landlord-tenant relationship). Two neighbors exchanging promises have no horizontal privity, so the damages claim fails even though the other elements are met. Ruiz should instead seek an injunction, because an equitable servitude does not require privity.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["covenants","running-with-land","horizontal-privity"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"contracts-accord","prompt":"Gus owes Hana $5,000 on a loan that is due and undisputed. They agree that Hana will accept Gus's used motorcycle in full satisfaction of the debt, with delivery set for next Friday. Gus fails to deliver the motorcycle, and Hana sues for the $5,000. Gus argues that the new agreement discharged the original debt. Who is correct?","choices":["Gus, because the new agreement replaced the loan, and Hana may sue only on that agreement.","Hana, because an unperformed accord does not discharge the debt, so she may sue on either.","Gus, because an accord discharges the original obligation as soon as the parties agree to it.","Hana, because a promise to accept property in place of a liquidated debt lacks consideration."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An accord is an agreement to accept a substitute performance; the original duty is suspended, not discharged, until the satisfaction (the substitute performance) occurs. If the debtor breaches the accord, the creditor may enforce either the original obligation or the accord (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 281). A substituted contract or novation would discharge the debt immediately, but that requires intent to accept the new promise itself in satisfaction. A different performance, like a motorcycle, is valid consideration even for a liquidated debt.","subject":"contracts","tags":["accord-and-satisfaction","discharge","executory-accord"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"contracts-sof-surety","prompt":"A developer is building an office tower. When a lumber supplier threatens to stop deliveries because the developer's general contractor is behind on payments, the developer orally tells the supplier, \"Keep shipping to my site; if the contractor doesn't pay you, I will.\" The supplier keeps shipping, the contractor defaults, and the developer refuses to pay, citing the Statute of Frauds. Is the developer's promise enforceable?","choices":["No, because a promise to answer for the debt of another must be in writing to be enforceable.","No, because the promise was collateral, taking effect only if the contractor failed to pay.","Yes, because the supplier gave consideration by continuing to ship lumber to the job site.","Yes, because the developer's main purpose was to serve its own business interest."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A collateral promise to pay another's debt if the debtor defaults is within the suretyship provision of the Statute of Frauds. Under the main purpose (leading object) exception, however, no writing is needed when the promisor's principal purpose is to secure a benefit for itself; keeping lumber flowing to the developer's own project is such a purpose. Consideration makes a promise binding as a contract but does not satisfy the Statute of Frauds.","subject":"contracts","tags":["statute-of-frauds","surety","main-purpose"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Statute of frauds (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/statute_of_frauds"}]},{"id":"contracts-risk-loss-shipment","prompt":"A Denver seller contracts to sell 50 cases of wine to a Chicago buyer \"F.O.B. Denver.\" The seller delivers conforming wine to a reputable carrier in Denver, makes a reasonable contract for its transport, and promptly notifies the buyer. En route, the truck crashes and the wine is destroyed. The buyer has not yet paid. Must the buyer pay the price?","choices":["Yes, because risk of loss passed to the buyer when the seller duly delivered the wine to the carrier.","No, because under the contract the seller kept the risk of loss until the wine reached Chicago.","No, because risk of loss does not pass until the buyer has paid for or accepted the goods.","Yes, because risk of loss passed to the buyer when the parties formed the sales contract."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"\"F.O.B.\" the seller's city makes this a shipment contract. Under UCC 2-509(1)(a), risk of loss passes to the buyer when the seller duly delivers conforming goods to the carrier, having made a reasonable transport contract and notified the buyer (2-504). The buyer must pay and pursue any claim against the carrier. Risk would have stayed with the seller until arrival only under a destination term such as \"F.O.B. Chicago,\" and payment does not control risk.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","risk-of-loss","shipment-contract","2-509"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-509 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-509"}]},{"id":"contracts-adequate-assurance","prompt":"A manufacturer has a contract to deliver parts to a buyer in 12 monthly shipments, each payable 30 days after delivery. After the third shipment, the manufacturer learns from a reliable trade report that the buyer's largest customer has filed for bankruptcy and that the buyer has begun paying several other suppliers late, though it has paid the manufacturer on time. Under the UCC, what may the manufacturer do?","choices":["Demand adequate assurance in writing and, if commercially reasonable, suspend shipments until it arrives.","Treat the report as an anticipatory repudiation and cancel all of the remaining shipments immediately.","Nothing until a payment owed to it is late, because only a default to it can create insecurity.","Raise the price of all remaining shipments to cover the added credit risk it now faces from the buyer."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"UCC 2-609 lets a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity demand adequate assurance of due performance in writing and, if commercially reasonable, suspend its own performance for which it has not received the agreed return until assurance arrives. Grounds may come from a party's dealings with others. If the buyer fails to give adequate assurance within a reasonable time (not over 30 days), the manufacturer may treat that as a repudiation. Cancelling at once or unilaterally raising the price would itself be a breach, and late payments to others fall short of the insolvency that would let a seller insist on cash under 2-702.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","adequate-assurance","insecurity","2-609"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-609 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-609"}]},{"id":"contracts-unconscionability","prompt":"A furniture store sells a consumer with limited English and no bargaining power a sofa on credit using a dense preprinted form. A clause buried in small print lets the store repossess every item the consumer has ever bought from it if any single payment is missed. The store did not explain the clause. When the consumer misses a payment, the store invokes it. May the court refuse to enforce the clause?","choices":["Yes, if it finds the clause procedurally and substantively unconscionable when the contract was made.","No, because a party who signs a contract is bound by its terms whether or not she read them.","Yes, if it finds the clause produces a harsh result in light of circumstances at the time of enforcement.","No, because the unconscionability doctrine under UCC 2-302 protects only merchants dealing with merchants."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under UCC 2-302 and the common law, a court may refuse to enforce a contract or clause that was unconscionable at the time the contract was made, which generally requires both procedural unconscionability (oppression or unfair surprise, as with hidden fine print and unequal bargaining power) and substantive unconscionability (overly harsh, one-sided terms); see Williams v. Walker-Thomas Furniture Co. The duty-to-read rule yields to unconscionability, the test looks to formation rather than later results, and the doctrine is most often applied to protect consumers.","subject":"contracts","tags":["unconscionability","procedural","substantive","2-302"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-302 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-302"}]},{"id":"property-joint-tenancy-sever","prompt":"Ann, Ben, and Cal own a farm as joint tenants with right of survivorship. Ann conveys her interest to Dee. Ben then dies, leaving a will that devises all his property to Eve. Who owns the farm, and how?","choices":["Cal owns two-thirds and Dee owns one-third, as tenants in common.","Cal and Dee each own one-half, as joint tenants with survivorship.","Cal, Dee, and Eve each own one-third, as tenants in common.","Cal and Dee each own one-half, as tenants in common with each other."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ann's conveyance severed the joint tenancy as to her one-third share, so Dee took that share as a tenant in common, while Ben and Cal remained joint tenants with each other as to the other two-thirds. When Ben died, his share passed to Cal by survivorship; a will cannot defeat the right of survivorship because a joint tenant's interest ends at death. Cal therefore holds two-thirds and Dee one-third, as tenants in common.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["joint-tenancy","severance","concurrent-estates"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"property-fixtures","prompt":"A homeowner installs custom bookshelves that are bolted to the walls and cut to fit the alcoves of her study. She later contracts to sell the house; the contract and deed say nothing about the shelves. Before closing, she removes them, claiming they are personal property because she always meant to take them with her. Is the buyer entitled to the shelves?","choices":["No, because the seller's own intention to keep the shelves determines whether they are fixtures.","No, because items that can be removed without serious damage remain the owner's personal property.","Yes, because their annexation and adaptation to the house objectively show they are fixtures.","Yes, because anything an owner attaches to a house automatically becomes part of the real estate."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Whether a chattel has become a fixture turns on the objective intent shown by its annexation to the realty, its adaptation to the particular use of the property, and the circumstances, not the owner's undisclosed subjective intent. Bookshelves bolted in and custom-cut to fit specific alcoves show an objective intent to make them permanent, so they pass with the house when the contract is silent. Ease of removal is only one factor, and attachment alone does not make every item a fixture.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["fixtures","annexation","intent"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"contracts-mailbox-rejection-then-acceptance","prompt":"On June 1, Ivy mails Jon an offer to sell her pickup truck. On June 3, Jon mails Ivy a rejection. An hour later he changes his mind and mails an acceptance. The acceptance reaches Ivy on June 5, and the rejection arrives on June 6. Is there a contract?","choices":["No, because the rejection was effective when mailed, ending the offer.","No, because an acceptance sent after a rejection is only a counteroffer.","Yes, because the acceptance arrived before the rejection did.","Yes, because an acceptance is always effective upon being dispatched."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A rejection is effective only when received, and when an offeree dispatches a rejection and then an acceptance, the mailbox rule does not apply to the acceptance; whichever communication the offeror receives first controls (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 40). The acceptance arrived first, so a contract formed. Had the rejection arrived first, the later acceptance would operate only as a counteroffer.","subject":"contracts","tags":["acceptance","mailbox-rule","formation","rejection"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"contracts-ucc-firm-offer-three-month-cap-v2","prompt":"On March 1, a lumber dealer gives a contractor a signed written offer to sell 10,000 board feet at a stated price, stating that \"this offer will remain open for 120 days.\" The contractor gives nothing for the promise. On June 20, before the contractor has responded, the dealer revokes. Was the revocation effective?","choices":["No, because a merchant's signed firm offer is irrevocable for the entire period the offer states.","Yes, because an offer unsupported by consideration may be revoked at any time before acceptance.","Yes, because a merchant's firm offer is irrevocable without consideration for no more than three months.","Yes, because a stated term longer than three months makes the merchant's firm-offer promise void."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under UCC 2-205, a merchant's signed written offer that gives assurance it will be held open is irrevocable without consideration for the time stated, but never longer than three months. The offer was irrevocable from March 1 to June 1 and revocable after that, so the June 20 revocation worked. The general common-law rule would have let the dealer revoke at any time, which is wrong during the first three months, and a longer stated term is not void; it is simply cut back to three months.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","firm-offer","2-205","revocation"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-205 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-205"}]},{"id":"contracts-liquidated-damages-reasonable-forecast","prompt":"A contract to build a warehouse requires the builder to pay $1,000 for each day of delay past the completion date. When the contract was made, the owner's losses from delay were hard to estimate but were reasonably expected to be about $1,000 a day. The builder finishes 20 days late, and the owner's actual loss turns out to be about $600 a day. Is the clause enforceable?","choices":["No, because the stipulated amount exceeds the owner's actual loss and so operates as a penalty.","No, because liquidated damages are allowed only when actual damages cannot be calculated at all.","Yes, because it reasonably forecast damages that were hard to estimate when the contract was made.","Yes, because courts enforce any damages figure that commercial parties agree to in advance."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A liquidated damages clause is enforceable if the amount is reasonable in light of the anticipated or actual loss and damages were difficult to estimate; an unreasonably large amount is an unenforceable penalty (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 356; UCC 2-718). A clause that was a reasonable forecast at formation is not invalidated because actual loss later proves somewhat lower. Difficulty of estimation, not impossibility, is required, and courts will not enforce a true penalty even between sophisticated parties.","subject":"contracts","tags":["remedies","liquidated-damages","penalty"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Liquidated damages (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/liquidated_damages"}]},{"id":"contracts-delegation-special-skill","prompt":"A celebrated portrait artist contracts to paint a commissioned portrait of a client's late father for $50,000. Overbooked, the artist delegates the job to a capable but little-known painter, who agrees to do it on the same terms. The client refuses to accept the substitute. Must the client accept performance by the substitute painter?","choices":["Yes, because contractual duties are generally delegable when the delegate is competent to perform.","No, because a duty that depends on the artist's own skill and judgment is not delegable without consent.","Yes, because the original artist remains liable if the substitute's portrait turns out unsatisfactory.","No, because a duty may be delegated only if the contract expressly authorizes a delegation of it."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Duties are generally delegable, but not when the obligee has a substantial interest in having the original promisor perform, as with personal services involving special skill, taste, or judgment (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318). A commission from a celebrated artist is the classic example, so the client may refuse the substitute. The delegating party's continued liability does not make a nondelegable duty delegable, and contracts need not expressly authorize ordinary delegations.","subject":"contracts","tags":["delegation","assignment","personal-services"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"contracts-land-sof-part-performance","prompt":"Kim orally agrees to buy a lot from Lee for $90,000. With Lee's consent, Kim moves onto the lot and spends $40,000 building a garage, but has not yet paid any of the price. Lee then refuses to convey, citing the Statute of Frauds. In a jurisdiction applying the traditional part-performance doctrine, can Kim obtain specific performance?","choices":["No, because part performance requires that the buyer have paid at least part of the purchase price.","No, because an oral land contract is unenforceable, though Kim may recover the value of the improvements.","Yes, because Lee's consent to Kim's taking possession satisfied the Statute's requirement of a writing.","Yes, because possession plus substantial improvements is part performance taking the contract out of the Statute."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Equity will specifically enforce an oral land contract when the buyer's acts unequivocally refer to the contract; under the common formulation, at least two of possession, payment, and substantial improvements suffice (see Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 129). Possession plus a $40,000 garage qualifies even though no money has been paid. Restitution is the remedy only where part performance is lacking or not recognized, and consent to possession is conduct, not a writing.","subject":"contracts","tags":["statute-of-frauds","land","part-performance"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"property-rap-contingent-remainder","prompt":"Olga conveys land \"to Ana for life, then to Ana's first child to reach the age of 30.\" At the time of the conveyance, Ana is alive and has one child, Ben, age 2. The jurisdiction has abolished the destructibility of contingent remainders, and no perpetuities reform statute applies. Under the common-law Rule Against Perpetuities, what is the status of the remainder?","choices":["Valid, because Ben is a life in being who must reach 30 or die within his own life.","Valid, because the remainder must vest, if at all, no later than the moment Ana dies.","Void only if Ana actually has another child, because courts wait to see what happens.","Void, because it might vest in a later-born child more than 21 years after Ana dies."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"At common law an interest is void if there is any possibility it will vest more than 21 years after the death of all relevant lives in being. Ben could die young, Ana could have another child, and Ana could die when that child is an infant, so the first child to reach 30 might do so more than 21 years after Ana's death. Because contingent remainders are not destructible here, the remainder does not fail merely because no child is 30 when Ana dies, so it is not certain to vest or fail at her death. Ben cannot validate the gift because he may not be the taker, and wait-and-see is a reform doctrine, not the common-law rule.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["future-interests","rap","contingent-remainder"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Rule against perpetuities (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/rule_against_perpetuities"}]},{"id":"property-self-help-eviction","prompt":"A residential tenant falls two months behind on rent. The lease says the landlord may \"re-enter and retake possession\" on default. While the tenant is at work, the landlord peacefully changes the locks and puts the tenant's belongings in storage. In most jurisdictions today, was the landlord's conduct lawful?","choices":["No, because a residential landlord generally must go to court to recover possession.","Yes, because the lease expressly authorized re-entry and the landlord used no force at all.","Yes, because a tenant in default on rent forfeits the right to possession immediately.","No, because the landlord first had to give the tenant 30 days' written notice to leave."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The modern majority rule bars self-help eviction of residential tenants; the landlord must use summary eviction (unlawful detainer) or other judicial process, even if the lease purports to authorize re-entry. The older common-law view permitted peaceable self-help, which is why the lease clause is tempting. Advance notice alone would not cure the problem, because lockouts are prohibited regardless of notice.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["landlord-tenant","eviction","self-help"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"property-equitable-conversion-risk-loss","prompt":"Seller and Buyer sign an enforceable contract for the sale of a house, with closing set for 60 days later. The contract does not allocate the risk of loss, no statute addresses it, and neither party has insurance. A month before closing, lightning starts a fire that destroys the house. Under the traditional majority rule, must Buyer close at the full contract price?","choices":["No, because the seller still held legal title and so bore the risk of loss until the closing.","Yes, because under equitable conversion the buyer bore the risk once the contract was signed.","No, because the fire made performance impossible and so discharged both parties' duties.","Yes, but the buyer may reduce the price by the value of the house destroyed in the fire."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under equitable conversion, once an enforceable land sale contract is signed the buyer is treated as the equitable owner and bears the risk of casualty loss not caused by the seller, absent a contrary contract term or statute. The buyer therefore must pay the full price. The seller-bears-risk answer reflects the Uniform Vendor and Purchaser Risk Act, adopted by statute in a minority of states and generally tied to whether the buyer had title or possession; it does not apply here.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["equitable-conversion","risk-of-loss","land-sale"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"property-easement-necessity-ends","prompt":"Opal sells the back half of her land to Ray. The back half has no access to a public road except across Opal's front half, so after the sale Ray builds a gravel driveway across it. Ten years later, the county opens a new public road along the back of Ray's parcel. Opal then blocks the driveway. May Ray continue to use it?","choices":["Yes, because an easement by necessity, once created, is permanent like an express easement.","No, because an easement by necessity ends when the necessity that created it ends.","Yes, because Ray's ten years of use turned the easement into one acquired by prescription.","Yes, because the driveway was an apparent and continuous use when the land was divided."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An easement by necessity arises when a conveyance leaves a parcel landlocked and lasts only as long as the strict necessity continues. Once the county road gave Ray access, the easement ended. Use under an existing easement is by right, not adverse, so it cannot ripen into a prescriptive easement, and there was no preexisting use to support an easement implied from prior use because Ray built the driveway after the sale.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["easement","necessity","termination"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"property-warranty-deed-seisin","prompt":"Uma conveys land to Vic by a general warranty deed. Vic soon learns that Uma owned only an undivided half interest; Uma's sibling owns the other half. The sibling has not asserted any claim or disturbed Vic's possession. Vic sues Uma at once for breach of the covenant of seisin, and Uma argues the suit is premature. Who is right?","choices":["Uma, because no deed covenant is breached until the grantee is actually or constructively evicted.","Uma, because a title defect is actionable only under the covenant of warranty, not that of seisin.","Vic, because the covenant of seisin is breached when the grantee first discovers the title defect.","Vic, because seisin is a present covenant, breached if at all when the deed is delivered."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The covenants of seisin, right to convey, and against encumbrances are present covenants, breached, if at all, at the moment of conveyance; the statute of limitations runs from delivery. Uma did not own the full estate she purported to convey, so the covenant of seisin was breached at delivery, and Vic need not wait to be disturbed. Actual or constructive eviction is required only for the future covenants of warranty, quiet enjoyment, and further assurances.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["deeds","warranty-deed","covenant-of-seisin"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-contracts-indirect-revocation","prompt":"On Monday, Owen, who is not in the boat business, offers to sell his sailboat to Pia for $12,000 and says she has until Friday to decide. Pia gives nothing for that promise. On Wednesday, a reliable mutual friend tells Pia, truthfully, that Owen sold the boat to someone else that morning. On Thursday, Pia hands Owen a written acceptance. Is there a contract between Owen and Pia?","choices":["No, because Pia's reliable information that Owen had sold the boat revoked the offer.","Yes, because Owen expressly promised to keep the offer open until Friday.","Yes, because a revocation takes effect only when the offeror communicates it directly.","No, because Owen's sale to another buyer ended the offer even before Pia learned of it."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An offer is revoked when the offeree receives reliable information that the offeror has taken definite action inconsistent with the offer, such as selling the item to someone else (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 43). Owen's promise to hold the offer open was unsupported by consideration, and he is not a merchant, so it was not a binding option or UCC firm offer. The sale itself did not end the offer; it was Pia's learning of it that did.","subject":"contracts","tags":["offer","revocation","indirect-revocation"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-contracts-option-acceptance-receipt","prompt":"In exchange for $500, Rosa gives Sam a written option to buy her warehouse for $800,000, exercisable by written notice \"on or before May 31.\" On May 29, Sam mails a properly addressed acceptance, which reaches Rosa on June 2. Rosa refuses to sell. Did Sam validly exercise the option?","choices":["Yes, because an acceptance sent by a reasonable means is effective when dispatched.","Yes, because Sam paid for the option, so Rosa could not end it before he accepted.","No, because an acceptance under an option contract is effective only upon receipt.","No, because Rosa remained free to revoke the option until Sam's acceptance arrived."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The mailbox rule protects an offeree from revocation while an acceptance is in transit, but an option holder needs no such protection, so an acceptance under an option contract is not operative until the offeror receives it (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 63(b)). Sam's notice arrived after May 31, so the option lapsed. The option was irrevocable until it expired; the problem is timing of exercise, not revocation.","subject":"contracts","tags":["option-contract","acceptance","mailbox-rule"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-contracts-ucc-2207-knockout","prompt":"A buyer's purchase order for industrial valves states that payment is due 60 days after delivery. The seller's acknowledgment, which otherwise matches the order and is not expressly conditional, states that payment is due 10 days after delivery. Both parties are merchants, neither objects, and the valves are delivered. Under the majority approach to UCC 2-207, when is payment due?","choices":["Sixty days after delivery, because the offeror's terms control and a different term is simply ignored.","Ten days after delivery, because the seller's acknowledgment was the last form sent before shipment.","When the buyer receives the goods, because both conflicting terms drop out and the UCC default applies.","Sixty days after delivery, unless the court finds that the seller's term does not materially alter it."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When an offer and an acceptance contain conflicting (different) terms, the majority \"knockout\" rule strikes both conflicting terms and fills the gap with UCC default provisions. Under UCC 2-310(a), payment is due at the time and place the buyer receives the goods. A minority of courts let the offeror's term control, and the last-shot rule is a common-law approach that 2-207 rejects; the material-alteration test governs additional terms, not conflicting ones, under the majority view.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","battle-of-forms","2-207","different-terms","knockout-rule"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-207 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-207"}]},{"id":"v3-contracts-promissory-estoppel","prompt":"A grandmother hands her grandson a signed note promising to pay him $20,000 \"so that you will not have to work anymore.\" She asks nothing of him in return. Relying on the note, as she expected he would, the grandson quits his job. A year later she refuses to pay. Is her promise enforceable?","choices":["Yes, because quitting his job was a legal detriment that served as consideration for the promise.","No, because promissory estoppel enforces only charitable subscriptions and marriage settlements.","No, because a promise of a gift is unenforceable without consideration, even if signed in writing.","Yes, to the extent needed to avoid injustice, because she should have expected his reliance."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90, a promise that the promisor should reasonably expect to induce action, and that does induce it, is binding if injustice can be avoided only by enforcement, and the remedy may be limited as justice requires (Ricketts v. Scothorn). Quitting work was not consideration because the grandmother did not bargain for it. Charitable subscriptions and marriage settlements are the categories enforceable without proof of reliance, not the only ones where reliance matters.","subject":"contracts","tags":["consideration","promissory-estoppel","reliance"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Promissory estoppel (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/promissory_estoppel"}]},{"id":"v3-contracts-ucc-merchant-confirmation","prompt":"A grain elevator and a feed mill, both merchants, agree by phone that the elevator will sell the mill 5,000 bushels of corn for $25,000. The next day the mill sends the elevator a signed letter confirming the quantity and price. The elevator receives and reads it but never responds. A month later, the elevator refuses to deliver, and the mill sues. Can the elevator successfully raise the Statute of Frauds?","choices":["Yes, because the elevator never signed any writing, and it is the party to be charged here.","Yes, because a party's silence after receiving a written confirmation is never treated as assent.","No, because it did not object within 10 days to a signed confirmation it had reason to know of.","No, because oral contracts between merchants are exempt from the UCC's Statute of Frauds."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under UCC 2-201(2), between merchants a written confirmation sufficient against the sender, received within a reasonable time, satisfies the Statute of Frauds against the recipient who has reason to know its contents unless the recipient objects in writing within 10 days. The elevator's silence therefore costs it the Statute of Frauds defense, though the mill must still prove that the oral contract was actually made. Merchant contracts are not otherwise exempt from the writing requirement.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","statute-of-frauds","2-201","merchant-confirmation"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-201 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-201"}]},{"id":"v3-contracts-sof-one-year-lifetime","prompt":"A company orally hires Tess as a consultant \"for as long as you live\" at $90,000 a year. Two years later, the company fires her without cause and argues that the oral agreement is unenforceable under the Statute of Frauds. Under the majority rule, is the company correct?","choices":["Yes, because both parties expected the employment to continue for much longer than one year.","No, because the contract could be fully performed within a year if Tess died during that year.","Yes, because a promise of lifetime employment falls within the Statute and must be in writing.","No, because contracts for personal services are categorically excluded from the Statute of Frauds."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The one-year provision covers only contracts that by their terms cannot possibly be performed within one year of their making. A lifetime contract is fully performed if the person dies within the year, so it falls outside the Statute regardless of how long the parties expected it to last. Employment contracts are not exempt, though; an oral contract to employ someone for a fixed two-year term would need a writing.","subject":"contracts","tags":["statute-of-frauds","one-year-provision"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-contracts-unilateral-mistake-bid","prompt":"A general contractor solicits bids from electrical subcontractors. Four bids range from $410,000 to $440,000. A fifth bidder, because of an addition error in its worksheet, bids $210,000. The contractor, recognizing that the bid is far out of line, accepts it immediately without comment. When the subcontractor discovers the error and refuses to perform, can the contractor enforce the bid?","choices":["No, because the contractor had reason to know of the material clerical error when it accepted.","Yes, because a mistake by only one party does not allow that party to avoid the contract.","Yes, because the error came from the subcontractor's own carelessness in adding its figures.","No, because a bidder may withdraw a bid that contains any error up until work has begun."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A unilateral mistake about a basic assumption that materially affects the exchange makes a contract voidable if the other party knew or had reason to know of the mistake, or if enforcement would be unconscionable (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153). A bid half the size of every other bid put the contractor on notice of an error, so it cannot snap up the bid. Ordinary negligence does not bar relief, but a party cannot avoid a contract for a mistake the other side had no reason to suspect.","subject":"contracts","tags":["mistake","unilateral-mistake","bids"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-contracts-economic-duress","prompt":"A supplier is the only source of a specialized component that a manufacturer needs to meet a government contract deadline. Midway through their fixed-price contract, the supplier announces it will halt deliveries unless the manufacturer agrees to a 40% price increase, citing no change in its costs. Unable to find another source in time, the manufacturer agrees under protest, and it sues to recover the increase promptly after the final delivery. Will it likely succeed?","choices":["No, because by agreeing and accepting all deliveries, the manufacturer ratified the new price.","Yes, because the supplier's improper threat left the manufacturer no reasonable alternative.","No, because a threat to breach a contract is ordinary economic pressure, not duress.","Yes, because the supplier gave no new consideration for the manufacturer's promise to pay more."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A contract or modification is voidable for economic duress when one party's assent is induced by an improper threat that leaves no reasonable alternative (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 175; Austin Instrument v. Loral). A bad-faith threat to breach unless the other party pays more is improper, and the manufacturer protested and sued promptly once it could, so it did not ratify. Lack of consideration is the wrong theory for goods because UCC 2-209 dispenses with it, though the bad-faith demand also fails 2-209's good-faith requirement.","subject":"contracts","tags":["duress","economic-duress","modification"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-contracts-minor-necessaries","prompt":"Uri, 17, lives on his own without parental support. He signs a one-year apartment lease at $1,200 a month, which is the fair rental value. He pays nothing, and after four months he moves out and disaffirms the lease. The landlord sues. What is the landlord most likely to recover?","choices":["Rent for the full one-year term, because housing is a necessary that a minor cannot disaffirm.","Nothing, because a minor who disaffirms a contract owes nothing for the benefits he received.","Rent for the full term, because Uri ratified the lease by living in the apartment for four months.","The reasonable value of the housing Uri used, but not rent for the rest of the term."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A minor may disaffirm a contract, but a minor who receives necessaries such as housing is liable in quasi-contract for their reasonable value, not for the contract price of the full term. Uri therefore owes the reasonable value of the four months he occupied the apartment. Ratification can occur only after a person reaches majority, so his occupancy while 17 did not ratify the lease.","subject":"contracts","tags":["capacity","minors","necessaries","disaffirmance"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-contracts-parol-oral-condition","prompt":"An inventor and an investor sign a written agreement, with no merger clause, under which the investor will buy a 20% stake in the inventor's company for $500,000. When sued for refusing to pay, the investor offers evidence that at signing the parties orally agreed the writing would not take effect unless the investor's accountant approved the company's books, which the accountant did not do. Is the evidence admissible?","choices":["No, because the evidence contradicts a writing that unconditionally obligates the investor to pay.","Yes, because the rule does not bar proof of an oral condition to the writing's taking effect.","No, because a condition precedent to a written contract is enforceable only if it is also written.","Yes, but only because the writing lacks a merger clause and therefore cannot be fully integrated."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The parol evidence rule applies only to an agreement that has become effective, so evidence that the parties orally agreed the writing would not take effect until a condition occurred is admissible, provided it does not contradict an express term (Pym v. Campbell; Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 217). The writing is silent on the condition rather than contrary to it. A writing can be fully integrated without a merger clause, so the lack of one is not the reason the evidence comes in.","subject":"contracts","tags":["parol-evidence","conditions","condition-to-effectiveness"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Parol evidence rule (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/parol_evidence_rule"}]},{"id":"v3-contracts-substantial-performance","prompt":"A builder contracts to build a house to the owner's specifications, which call for \"Brand R\" plumbing pipe. Through inadvertence, the builder installs Brand C pipe, which is identical in quality and value. The substitution is discovered after the walls are closed, and replacing the pipe would require tearing out much of the house at great expense. The owner refuses to pay the final $40,000 installment. What is the builder most likely entitled to?","choices":["Nothing, because the owner is entitled to receive exactly the pipe the contract specified.","The installment less any difference in value between the house as built and as promised.","The installment less the cost of tearing out the pipe and replacing it with Brand R pipe.","Only restitution for the value of the work, because the builder breached the contract."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under the common law, a builder whose breach is not material and not willful has substantially performed, so the owner's duty to pay is triggered, subject to an offset for damages (Jacob & Youngs v. Kent). When the cost of remedying a defect is grossly disproportionate to the value gained, damages are measured by the diminution in value, which here is nothing. Perfect tender is a UCC rule for goods, not construction contracts, and restitution is a fallback for a party who has not substantially performed.","subject":"contracts","tags":["substantial-performance","constructive-conditions","damages","economic-waste"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-contracts-ucc-seller-cure","prompt":"A seller contracts to deliver 100 office chairs to a buyer by June 30. On June 10, the seller delivers 100 chairs in the wrong color, and the buyer promptly and rightfully rejects them. The seller immediately notifies the buyer that it will deliver chairs in the correct color by June 25. Must the buyer accept that delivery if the chairs conform?","choices":["No, because the buyer's rightful rejection ended the contract along with the seller's right to perform.","No, because a seller may cure only if it reasonably believed the original tender would be accepted.","Yes, but only if the seller also reimburses the buyer's costs of storing the rejected chairs.","Yes, because a seller may cure a rejected tender if the time for performance has not expired."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under UCC 2-508(1), when a buyer rejects a nonconforming tender before the time for performance has expired, the seller may seasonably notify the buyer of its intention to cure and then make a conforming delivery within the contract time. The June 25 delivery is within that time, so the buyer must accept it. The reasonable-grounds requirement in 2-508(2) applies only to cure after the contract time has passed, and the right to cure is not conditioned on reimbursing the buyer's costs.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","cure","2-508","rejection"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-508 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-508"}]},{"id":"v3-contracts-ucc-revocation-of-acceptance","prompt":"A dentist buys a new X-ray machine. After inspecting it and using it for three weeks, she discovers an intermittent electrical fault that could not reasonably have been found earlier and that makes the machine unsafe for patients. She promptly notifies the seller that she is returning the machine and wants her money back. The seller argues that she already accepted it. Who prevails?","choices":["The dentist, because a hidden defect that substantially impairs the machine's value allows revocation.","The seller, because once goods are accepted the buyer's only remedy is damages for breach of warranty.","The dentist, because a buyer may reject goods for any defect within a reasonable time after delivery.","The dentist, but only if the seller knew of the electrical fault at the time it sold the machine."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under UCC 2-608, a buyer may revoke acceptance of goods whose nonconformity substantially impairs their value to her if she accepted without discovering it because of the difficulty of discovery, and she revokes within a reasonable time after she should have discovered it. Revocation lets her recover the price as if she had rejected. The any-defect perfect tender standard applies to rejection before acceptance, not after it. Revocation does not depend on what the seller knew; a buyer who accepted knowing of a defect may revoke only if she reasonably assumed it would be cured and it was not.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","revocation-of-acceptance","2-608","acceptance"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-608 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-608"}]},{"id":"v3-contracts-ucc-risk-loss-merchant-seller","prompt":"An appliance store sells a refrigerator to a customer, who pays in full and agrees to pick it up at the store on Saturday. The store sets it aside with a tag bearing the customer's name and tells her it is ready. On Friday night, a fire that is not the store's fault destroys the refrigerator. The contract says nothing about risk of loss. Who bears the loss?","choices":["The store, because a merchant seller keeps the risk until the buyer receives the goods.","The customer, because the goods were identified to the contract and fully paid for.","The customer, because the store tendered delivery by setting it aside and notifying her.","The store, because title to goods cannot pass to the buyer before physical delivery."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"When the buyer is to pick goods up and no carrier or bailee is involved, UCC 2-509(3) puts the risk on the buyer upon her receipt of the goods if the seller is a merchant; only for a nonmerchant seller does risk pass on tender of delivery. The store is a merchant and the customer never took possession, so the store bears the loss and must refund or supply another refrigerator. Identification, payment, and title do not control risk of loss under Article 2.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","risk-of-loss","2-509","merchant"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-509 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-509"}]},{"id":"v3-contracts-ucc-disclaimer-merchantability","prompt":"A farm-equipment dealer sells a tractor under a written contract. On the back page, in the same small, light-gray type as the surrounding boilerplate, a sentence states: \"Seller disclaims the implied warranty of merchantability.\" The contract contains no \"as is\" language. The tractor proves unfit for ordinary farm use. Is the disclaimer effective?","choices":["Yes, because the clause expressly mentions merchantability, which is what the UCC requires.","Yes, because a disclaimer of implied warranties need only appear somewhere in a signed contract.","No, because a merchant seller cannot disclaim the implied warranty of merchantability at all.","No, because a written disclaimer of merchantability must be conspicuous, and this one is not."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"UCC 2-316(2) allows exclusion of the implied warranty of merchantability, but the language must mention merchantability and, if in a writing, must be conspicuous. Language buried in small boilerplate type that looks like everything around it is not conspicuous, so the disclaimer fails even though it names the warranty. Merchants can disclaim the warranty when they follow these requirements or use \"as is\" type language under 2-316(3).","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","warranties","disclaimer","2-316","merchantability"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-316 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-316"}]},{"id":"v3-contracts-frustration-of-purpose","prompt":"A company rents a hotel ballroom for one evening for $25,000 to host a viewing party for a championship game, a purpose both parties know is the sole reason for the rental. Two days before, the game is unexpectedly canceled and not rescheduled. The ballroom remains available, and the company could still use it for some other event. Must the company pay?","choices":["Yes, because the hotel can still provide the ballroom, so performance has not become impossible.","Yes, because each party assumes the risk that a contract will turn out less valuable than hoped.","No, because the cancellation of the game made the company's own performance impracticable.","No, because the cancellation frustrated the rental's principal purpose, which both parties understood."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Frustration of purpose discharges a party when, without its fault, an event whose nonoccurrence was a basic assumption substantially frustrates its principal purpose, a purpose the other party understood (Krell v. Henry; Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 265). The ballroom can still be supplied and money can still be paid, so neither impossibility nor impracticability applies. The game's cancellation is not a mere change in value or market risk; it destroyed the shared purpose of the deal.","subject":"contracts","tags":["frustration-of-purpose","excuse","impracticability"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-contracts-anti-assignment-clause","prompt":"A software firm agrees to build an app for a retailer for $200,000. The contract states: \"This contract may not be assigned.\" After completing the app, the firm assigns its right to the $200,000 payment to a bank, which promptly notifies the retailer. The retailer nonetheless pays the firm, which spends the money. Is the retailer still liable to the bank?","choices":["No, because the contract expressly prohibited assignment, so the firm's assignment to the bank was void.","No, because a retailer discharges its debt by paying the original contracting party in good faith.","Yes, because the clause did not bar assigning the right to payment, and the retailer paid after notice.","Yes, but only if the retailer later agreed to the assignment after it received the bank's notice."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Unless circumstances show otherwise, a clause barring assignment of \"the contract\" is read to bar only delegation of performance, and it does not prevent assignment of a right to payment earned by full performance (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 322; see also UCC 9-406(d)). Once an obligor receives notice of an assignment, it can discharge the debt only by paying the assignee, so its payment to the firm did not discharge it. Payment to the assignor is a defense only if made before notice.","subject":"contracts","tags":["assignment","anti-assignment-clause","notice"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-contracts-hadley-consequential","prompt":"A mill's crankshaft breaks, and the mill hires a carrier to take it to a manufacturer to serve as a model for a new one. The mill does not tell the carrier that it has no spare shaft and will be idle until the new one arrives, and nothing else would have alerted the carrier. The carrier unreasonably delays delivery by five days. Can the mill recover the profits it lost during the delay?","choices":["Yes, because lost profits are recoverable whenever they are a direct result of the breach.","No, because the lost profits were not reasonably foreseeable to the carrier at contracting.","Yes, because the carrier's unreasonable delay was a breach of its duty of timely delivery.","No, because lost profits are never recoverable in an action for breach of contract."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Hadley v. Baxendale, damages are recoverable only if they arise naturally from the breach or were within the reasonable contemplation of both parties at contracting because of special circumstances communicated to the breaching party. The carrier was never told the mill would shut down without the shaft, so the lost profits were not foreseeable to it. Lost profits are recoverable when foreseeable and proved with reasonable certainty, and the mere fact of breach does not make every consequence compensable.","subject":"contracts","tags":["remedies","consequential-damages","foreseeability","hadley"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-contracts-mitigation-employment","prompt":"A school district wrongfully fires a chemistry teacher in the middle of her one-year contract. The only job then available to her is a lower-paying position managing a retail store in the same town. She declines it and stays unemployed for the rest of the year. In her suit for the salary due under the contract, will her recovery be reduced by what the store would have paid her?","choices":["Yes, because a plaintiff must accept any reasonably available job to mitigate damages.","Yes, because she can recover only losses she could not have avoided by any effort at all.","No, because she need not accept work that is different in kind or inferior to her job.","No, because a wrongfully discharged employee has no duty at all to mitigate damages."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A plaintiff cannot recover losses she could have avoided by reasonable effort, but a wrongfully discharged employee need only accept comparable employment; she need not take a job that is different in kind or inferior (Parker v. Twentieth Century-Fox). Managing a retail store is a different kind of work, so her recovery is not reduced. Had she rejected a comparable teaching position, the salary she could have earned would be deducted.","subject":"contracts","tags":["remedies","mitigation","employment"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-contracts-ucc-lost-volume-seller","prompt":"A boat dealer with an ample supply of a popular model contracts to sell one to a buyer for $50,000; the dealer's cost for the boat is $38,000. The buyer repudiates. The dealer promptly resells that same boat to a new customer for $50,000, a sale it would have made anyway from its inventory. Ignoring incidental costs, what are the dealer's damages?","choices":["$12,000, its lost profit, because it is a lost-volume seller.","Nothing, because it resold the boat for the full contract price.","$50,000, the full contract price, because the buyer repudiated.","Contract price minus market price at tender, which is zero here."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A seller with enough supply to make both sales has lost a sale, not just a buyer, so contract-price-minus-resale or contract-minus-market damages would leave it worse off. UCC 2-708(2) instead awards the profit (including reasonable overhead) it would have made from full performance, here $50,000 minus $38,000. An action for the full price under 2-709 is available only when the buyer has accepted the goods, the goods were lost after risk passed, or they cannot reasonably be resold.","subject":"contracts","tags":["ucc","seller-remedies","lost-volume-seller","2-708"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"UCC § 2-708 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/ucc/2/2-708"}]},{"id":"v3-property-executory-interest-rap","prompt":"Oscar conveys land \"to Alma and her heirs, but if the land is ever used to sell alcohol, then to Bruno and his heirs.\" Bruno is unrelated to Alma, and no perpetuities reform statute applies. Under the common-law Rule Against Perpetuities, what estate does Alma hold?","choices":["A fee simple subject to an executory limitation, with the future interest in Bruno.","A fee simple subject to condition subsequent, with a right of entry kept by Oscar.","A fee simple determinable, with a possibility of reverter retained by Oscar.","A fee simple absolute, because Bruno's executory interest is void and is struck."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Bruno's shifting executory interest could vest centuries from now, so it violates the common-law Rule Against Perpetuities and is struck. Because the limitation is phrased with \"but if\" (a condition subsequent), striking the gift to Bruno leaves Alma's estate without any limitation, a fee simple absolute. Had the grant used durational words such as \"so long as\" and then named Bruno, striking Bruno's interest would leave Alma a fee simple determinable and Oscar a possibility of reverter, which is exempt from the Rule.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["future-interests","rap","executory-interest","defeasible-fees"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Rule against perpetuities (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/rule_against_perpetuities"}]},{"id":"v3-property-vested-remainder-subject-to-open","prompt":"Tara's will devises land \"to my brother Ulf for life, then to the children of my sister Vera.\" At Tara's death, Ulf is alive, and Vera is alive with one child, Will, age 5. What interest does Will hold?","choices":["A contingent remainder, because the class of Vera's children is still open.","An executory interest, because it can take effect only after Ulf's estate.","An indefeasibly vested remainder, because Will is alive and ascertained.","A vested remainder subject to open, because Vera may have more children."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A remainder to a class is vested subject to open when at least one class member is ascertained and there is no condition precedent other than the end of the prior estate, but more members may join. Will is a born, ascertained class member, so his remainder is vested, and his share may shrink if Vera has more children before the class closes. An open class does not make the remainder contingent, and an interest that waits for a life estate to end naturally is a remainder, not an executory interest.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["future-interests","remainders","class-gifts"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-waste-open-mines","prompt":"Liam holds a life estate in a wooded parcel, and Mia holds the remainder. No mining had ever occurred on the land. Liam opens a new gravel pit and sells the gravel. He also cuts some timber to repair the fences on the parcel. Mia sues for waste. Which of Liam's acts is actionable?","choices":["Only cutting the timber for the fence repairs.","Both acts, because each consumed natural resources.","Only opening and operating the new gravel pit.","Neither act, because a life tenant takes all profits."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A life tenant may not commit voluntary (affirmative) waste by depleting the land's natural resources, and under the open mines doctrine may continue only mining that was underway when the life estate began. Opening a new pit is therefore waste. A life tenant may, however, cut timber for repairs and maintenance of the property (estovers), so the fence repairs were permissible.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["life-estate","waste","open-mines"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-joint-tenancy-mortgage-lien-theory","prompt":"Nell and Oren own a house as joint tenants with right of survivorship in a lien-theory jurisdiction. Without telling Nell, Oren mortgages his interest to a lender to secure a personal loan. Oren dies before repaying the loan, leaving a will that gives all his property to his son. Who owns the house, and is it subject to the mortgage?","choices":["Nell owns it all, but subject to the lender's lien on Oren's former half.","Nell owns it all, free of the mortgage, which ended with Oren's interest.","Nell and the son are tenants in common, with the son's half encumbered.","Nell and the son are tenants in common, because a will severs at death."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In a lien-theory state a mortgage is merely a lien and does not transfer title, so one joint tenant's mortgage does not sever the joint tenancy. When Oren died, his interest ended and Nell took the whole by survivorship, and the lender's lien, which attached only to Oren's interest, was extinguished with it. In a title-theory state the mortgage would sever, and a will can never sever because the joint tenant's interest ends at the moment of death.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["joint-tenancy","severance","mortgage","lien-theory"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-property-equity-of-redemption-clog","prompt":"To secure a $200,000 loan, an owner gives a lender a mortgage on her house. A clause in the mortgage provides that if she ever misses a payment, she waives any right to redeem and title passes to the lender automatically. She misses a payment. Before any foreclosure, she tenders the full balance due plus interest and costs. Must the lender accept the tender and release the mortgage?","choices":["No, because she expressly waived her right of redemption as a term of obtaining the loan.","No, because the right to redeem arises only after a foreclosure sale has been completed.","Yes, but only if the state's statutes grant a right to redeem after a foreclosure sale.","Yes, because a mortgagor cannot waive the equity of redemption in the mortgage itself."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Before a foreclosure sale, every mortgagor has an equitable right to redeem by paying the amount due (with any accelerated balance, interest, and costs). Courts refuse to enforce clauses in the mortgage that waive or \"clog\" that equity of redemption, although a waiver given later for separate consideration may be valid. Statutory redemption after the sale is a different right that exists only in some states; the equitable right exists everywhere and ends at the sale.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["mortgage","equity-of-redemption","clogging"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-sublease-vs-assignment","prompt":"Ray leases a store from Landlord for five years at $4,000 a month; the lease says nothing about transfers. After two years, Ray transfers to Sue \"the remainder of the term except the final month,\" and Sue pays Ray. Sue does not promise to perform Ray's obligations under the lease. Six months later Sue stops paying, and Landlord sues Sue directly for rent. Is Sue liable to Landlord?","choices":["Yes, because Sue is in possession and so is in privity of estate with the landlord.","Yes, because any transferee of a lease is bound by all of the original tenant's covenants.","No, because only someone who signed the lease can be liable to the landlord for rent.","No, because Ray kept a reversion, so this is a sublease and Sue has no privity with Landlord."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A transfer of the tenant's entire remaining term is an assignment, which creates privity of estate between landlord and assignee; a transfer of less than the full term, with the tenant retaining a reversion, is a sublease. Because Ray kept the last month, Sue is a subtenant with neither privity of estate nor privity of contract with Landlord, and she did not assume the lease. Landlord's recourse is against Ray, though an assignee who never signed the lease would be liable through privity of estate.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["landlord-tenant","sublease","assignment","privity"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-property-constructive-eviction","prompt":"A tenant leases an office under a lease requiring the landlord to keep the roof in repair. Months later, roof leaks caused by the landlord's failure to repair make most of the office unusable. The tenant complains repeatedly, but the landlord does nothing. The tenant stays in the office for 11 more months, paying rent, then moves out, stops paying, and claims constructive eviction. Is the tenant likely to succeed?","choices":["Yes, because the landlord's breach of its repair duty substantially interfered with the tenant's use.","Yes, because a tenant may claim constructive eviction at any time while the conditions continue.","No, because constructive eviction is available only to residential tenants, not commercial ones.","No, because the tenant did not vacate within a reasonable time after the problem arose."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Constructive eviction requires substantial interference with the tenant's use and enjoyment caused by the landlord's act or failure to perform a duty, notice to the landlord and failure to cure, and the tenant's vacating within a reasonable time. Remaining for nearly a year waives the claim, so the tenant remains liable for rent. Constructive eviction applies to commercial tenants as well; it is the implied warranty of habitability that is limited to residential leases.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["landlord-tenant","constructive-eviction"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-holdover-tenant","prompt":"A tenant's one-year residential lease, with rent payable monthly, expires on June 30. The tenant remains in possession on July 1 without the landlord's consent. Under the general common-law approach, what may the landlord do?","choices":["Evict the tenant or elect to bind the tenant to a new periodic tenancy.","Only evict, because a new tenancy needs the parties' express written agreement.","Only bind the tenant to a new term, because holding over renews the lease itself.","Neither yet, because a holdover tenant gets a 30-day grace period to vacate."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A tenant who wrongfully holds over after the lease ends is a tenant at sufferance, and the landlord may elect either to evict the tenant (and recover damages for the holdover period) or to hold the tenant to a new periodic tenancy, usually on the old lease's terms. Many jurisdictions measure the new period by the rent interval (month-to-month here), while the traditional rule made it year-to-year when the original term was a year or more. No grace period is required.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["landlord-tenant","holdover","periodic-tenancy"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-property-prescriptive-easement","prompt":"For 12 years, Nico openly drives across a corner of his neighbor Oda's field to reach his barn, using the same route several times a week. Oda knows of the use but never objects or gives permission, and she also crosses that corner herself. The prescriptive period is 10 years. Oda now fences off the corner. What right, if any, has Nico acquired?","choices":["An easement by prescription to continue using the route.","Title to the corner by adverse possession of that strip.","None, because Oda's own use made his use nonexclusive.","None, because Oda's silence amounted to her permission."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A prescriptive easement arises from use that is open and notorious, adverse (under a claim of right, not by permission), and continuous for the statutory period. Nico's regular use for 12 years qualifies, and silent acquiescence by a knowing owner is not permission under the majority view. Unlike adverse possession, prescription does not require exclusive use, so Oda's shared use does not defeat it, and Nico gains only a right of use, not title.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["easement","prescription","exclusivity"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-irrevocable-license","prompt":"Penny orally permits her neighbor Quin to lay a water line across her land to Quin's cabin. With Penny's knowledge and relying on her permission, Quin spends $25,000 installing the line. A year later, Penny revokes her permission and demands removal. In most jurisdictions, may Penny revoke?","choices":["No, because Quin's substantial reliance makes the license irrevocable by estoppel.","Yes, because a license is a personal privilege that is revocable at the licensor's will.","Yes, because an oral grant of an interest in land is barred by the Statute of Frauds.","No, because Quin's year of use under the license created an easement by prescription."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A license is ordinarily revocable at will, and an oral attempt to create an easement usually yields only a license. But when the licensor knows the licensee is making substantial expenditures in reasonable reliance on the license, most courts hold the licensor estopped to revoke, treating it as an easement by estoppel for as long as needed to protect the reliance. Use under permission is not adverse, and in any event one year is far short of a prescriptive period.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["license","estoppel","easement"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-easement-abandonment","prompt":"Rae holds an express, recorded right-of-way across Sol's land. Rae stops using it for 15 years because she has another convenient route, but she never states any intention to give it up and does nothing on the land. Sol now plans to build across the right-of-way. Has the easement been extinguished?","choices":["Yes, because 15 years of nonuse exceeds the prescriptive period in the state.","Yes, because an easement ends when the holder no longer needs it for access.","No, because nonuse without an act showing intent to abandon does not end it.","No, because an express easement ends only by a written release from the holder."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"An easement is abandoned only when the holder shows intent to relinquish it by some physical act, such as building a wall across the way; mere nonuse, however long, is not enough. Need matters for easements by necessity, not express easements. Express easements can also end without a writing, for example by merger, prescription by the servient owner, or estoppel, so a written release is not the only route.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["easement","termination","abandonment"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-common-scheme","prompt":"A developer subdivides a tract into 50 lots and records a plat. It sells 45 lots by deeds restricting them to single-family homes and advertises the development as \"exclusively residential.\" The deed to Lot 46, sold to Tia, contains no restriction, but Tia knew of the restrictions in her neighbors' deeds when she bought. Tia now plans to open a gas station. In a jurisdiction following the majority approach, can neighboring lot owners enjoin her?","choices":["No, because a restriction absent from every deed in her chain of title cannot bind her.","No, because Tia was not in horizontal privity with the purchasers of the other lots.","Yes, because a common scheme plus notice implies a reciprocal servitude on her lot.","Yes, because a restriction in most deeds in a subdivision binds all lots without notice."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When a developer sells lots under a general plan or common scheme of restrictions, most courts imply a reciprocal negative servitude on the developer's retained lots, binding a later purchaser who takes with actual, record, or inquiry notice. Tia had actual notice, so the restriction is enforceable against her by injunction. Equitable servitudes need no horizontal privity, but notice is essential; a minority, including California, requires the restriction to appear in a recorded instrument for the burdened lot.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["equitable-servitudes","common-scheme","implied-reciprocal-servitude"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-property-adverse-possession-tacking","prompt":"Ada enters Blackacre without permission and possesses it openly, exclusively, and continuously for 6 years. She then sells her interest by deed to Ben, who possesses it in the same way for 5 more years. The statutory period is 10 years. The record owner now sues to eject Ben. Who prevails?","choices":["The owner, because neither Ada nor Ben alone possessed for the full 10 years.","Ben, because he may tack his possession to Ada's since they were in privity.","The owner, because a deed from an adverse possessor conveys no interest at all.","Ben, because 11 years of combined possession suffices even without any privity."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Successive adverse possessors may tack their periods together when they are in privity, meaning the possession passes by deed, will, descent, or other voluntary transfer. Ada transferred her possessory interest to Ben by deed, so their combined 11 years exceeds the statutory period and Ben has title. Without privity, as when a later possessor ousts an earlier one or enters after an abandonment, the periods could not be combined.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["adverse-possession","tacking","privity"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Adverse possession (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/adverse_possession"}]},{"id":"v3-property-marketable-title-closing","prompt":"A contract for the sale of a house sets closing for August 1. On June 1, the buyer's title search reveals a $150,000 mortgage on the property. The price is $400,000, and the seller plans to pay off the mortgage from the sale proceeds at closing. The contract says nothing else about title. The buyer immediately declares the contract rescinded for lack of marketable title. Was the buyer entitled to do so?","choices":["Yes, because an outstanding mortgage is an encumbrance making title unmarketable.","No, because the seller need not provide marketable title until the closing.","Yes, because a buyer may rescind as soon as it discovers any defect in the title.","No, because a mortgage never affects marketability, since it secures only a debt."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Every land sale contract implies a promise that the seller will deliver marketable title, but absent a contrary term the seller need not have it until closing. A mortgage does make title unmarketable, yet a seller may satisfy it with the purchase money at closing, so the buyer's attempt to rescind two months early was premature and could itself be a repudiation. The buyer could refuse to close only if the encumbrance remained at closing.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["land-sale-contracts","marketable-title","mortgage"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-deed-delivery-oral-condition","prompt":"Gina hands her nephew Hal a signed, properly executed deed to her farm, telling him, \"This is yours once you finish college.\" The deed itself contains no condition. Hal records it. A year later Hal drops out of college, and Gina demands the farm back. Under the majority view, who owns the farm?","choices":["Hal, because a deed handed to the grantee subject to an oral condition passes title free of it.","Gina, because the deed does not take effect until the oral condition in it has been satisfied.","Gina, because Hal's recording of the deed before meeting the condition made the deed void.","Hal, because the act of recording a deed conclusively establishes that it was delivered."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"When a grantor hands a deed directly to the grantee subject to an oral condition that does not appear in the deed, most courts treat the delivery as absolute and disregard the condition, so title passed to Hal. A grantor who wants a conditional delivery should use a third-party escrow, where the condition is enforced. Recording raises only a rebuttable presumption of delivery; it is Gina's manual transfer with intent to pass title that makes the delivery effective.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["deeds","delivery","conditional-delivery"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-property-shelter-rule","prompt":"O sells Greenacre to A, who does not record. O then sells Greenacre to B, a purchaser for value without notice of A's deed, and B records. A then records. Later, B sells to C, who knows all about A's earlier deed. The jurisdiction has a notice statute. As between A and C, who owns Greenacre?","choices":["A, because C had actual notice of A's deed when C bought.","A, because A recorded before C bought, giving C notice.","C, because a recorded deed alone protects a purchaser.","C, because the shelter rule gives C the protection B had."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"B was a bona fide purchaser who prevailed over A under the notice statute the moment B bought. Under the shelter rule, a person who takes from a protected BFP succeeds to the BFP's rights even with notice of the prior claim, because otherwise the BFP could not freely sell what it owns. C's notice is therefore irrelevant. Recording alone does not protect a purchaser under a notice statute; what matters is taking for value without notice.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["recording-acts","shelter-rule","bona-fide-purchaser"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-property-notice-statute","prompt":"O conveys land to A, who does not record. O then conveys the same land to B, who pays value and has no notice of A's deed. Before B records, A records. The jurisdiction's statute provides that an unrecorded conveyance is void against any subsequent purchaser for value without notice. Who owns the land?","choices":["B, because B took for value without notice and need not record first.","A, because A recorded before B did and so won the race to the record.","A, because A's conveyance was earlier in time than the one to B.","B, because B paid value, which alone protects a later purchaser."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under a pure notice statute, a subsequent purchaser for value who takes without notice of a prior unrecorded conveyance prevails, whether or not the subsequent purchaser records first. B qualified when it bought, so A's later recording cannot defeat B. Recording first matters only under race and race-notice statutes, and value alone is not enough; the purchaser must also lack notice.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["recording-acts","notice-statute","bona-fide-purchaser"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-property-junior-foreclosure-subject-to","prompt":"An owner's land is subject to a first mortgage held by Bank 1 and a second mortgage held by Bank 2, both properly recorded. The owner defaults only on the second mortgage, and Bank 2 forecloses. Bank 1 is not made a party. Ida buys the land at the foreclosure sale. What is Ida's position with respect to Bank 1's mortgage?","choices":["She takes free of Bank 1's mortgage, because foreclosure ends all liens on the land.","She takes subject to Bank 1's mortgage but is not personally liable on that debt.","She takes subject to Bank 1's mortgage and becomes personally liable on that debt.","She takes free of Bank 1's mortgage, which is paid first from the sale proceeds."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Foreclosure extinguishes the foreclosing mortgage and interests junior to it that are joined, but it does not affect senior interests. Ida therefore takes the land subject to Bank 1's first mortgage, which Bank 1 can foreclose if the debt goes unpaid. Because Ida did not assume the debt, she is not personally liable on it; a buyer who takes \"subject to\" a mortgage risks losing the land but owes no deficiency. Sale proceeds go to the foreclosing mortgagee and then to juniors, not to seniors.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["mortgage","foreclosure","priority","subject-to"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-property-trade-fixtures","prompt":"A tenant leasing a restaurant space bolts commercial ovens and a walk-in freezer to the floor for use in its business. Shortly before the lease ends, the tenant removes them and repairs the bolt holes. The lease is silent on the matter. The landlord claims the equipment became part of the building. Was the tenant entitled to remove it?","choices":["No, because anything bolted to the building becomes a fixture belonging to the landlord.","Yes, because a tenant may remove trade fixtures before the lease ends if it repairs any damage.","No, because a tenant may remove items it attached only if the lease expressly allows removal.","Yes, because a tenant may remove anything it installed at any time, even after the lease ends."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Chattels a tenant annexes for use in its trade or business (trade fixtures) are presumed not intended to become part of the realty, so the tenant may remove them before the lease ends, provided it repairs any damage caused by removal. A lease clause is not needed. The removal must generally occur by the end of the tenancy; items left behind may be treated as abandoned to the landlord.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["fixtures","trade-fixtures","landlord-tenant"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-property-lucas-total-taking","prompt":"Pilar buys two beachfront lots for $1 million, intending to build homes. The state then enacts a coastal-protection statute that bars any permanent structure on her lots, leaving them without any economically beneficial use. Background principles of nuisance and property law would not have barred the homes. Pilar sues for just compensation. Is she likely to succeed?","choices":["No, because a regulation protecting public resources is a valid use of the police power.","Yes, because a regulation denying all economically beneficial use is a per se taking.","No, because there is no taking unless the government physically occupies the property.","Yes, but only if the statute fails the Penn Central balancing test for regulatory takings."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Lucas v. South Carolina Coastal Council, a regulation that deprives land of all economically beneficial use is a categorical taking requiring compensation, unless the restriction merely restates background principles of nuisance or property law. Because those principles would not have prohibited the homes, Pilar is entitled to compensation without any balancing. Penn Central's multifactor test governs partial regulatory deprivations, and physical occupation is a separate per se category, not a prerequisite.","subject":"realProperty","tags":["takings","regulatory-takings","lucas"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"evidence-hearsay-v2","prompt":"Ten minutes before a customer slips in a grocery aisle, another shopper tells the store manager, \"There is oil all over aisle four.\" The shopper cannot be located for trial. In the customer's negligence action, a cashier who overheard the remark is called to repeat it, and the customer offers it only to show that the store had notice of the hazard. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, how should the court rule on the store's hearsay objection?","choices":["Sustain it, because the shopper is unavailable and the remark does not fit any Rule 804 hearsay exception.","Overrule it, because the remark is offered for its effect on the manager rather than to prove oil was present.","Overrule it, because a remark made to a store's manager is an opposing-party statement of the store.","Sustain it, because notice can be proved only through the testimony of the person who gave the warning."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Hearsay is an out-of-court statement offered to prove the truth of the matter asserted (FRE 801(c)). Offered only to show the manager was warned, the remark is relevant whether or not it was true, so it is nonhearsay and unavailability is irrelevant; the store may request a limiting instruction under FRE 105. It is not an opposing-party statement because the shopper was not the store or its agent.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay","definition","effect-on-listener"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 801 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_801"}]},{"id":"evidence-present-sense-v2","prompt":"While watching an intersection from an office window, a caller tells a 911 operator in a calm voice, \"A blue truck is running the red light right now.\" At trial, the authenticated recording is offered to prove how the truck entered the intersection. The caller testifies but no longer remembers the event. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, is the recording admissible over a hearsay objection?","choices":["Yes, as a present sense impression, because the caller described the event while perceiving it.","No, because a present sense impression is admissible only if the declarant is unavailable to testify.","Yes, as an excited utterance, because a call to a 911 operator is presumed to be made under stress.","No, because a witness who cannot remember an event may be aided only by a recorded recollection."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"FRE 803(1) admits a statement describing an event made while or immediately after the declarant perceived it. Rule 803 exceptions apply regardless of the declarant's availability, and a present sense impression requires no startling event or excitement—here the caller was calm, so the excited-utterance theory fails. Nothing makes Rule 803(5) the exclusive route when a testifying witness has forgotten the event.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","present-sense-impression","803(1)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 803 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_803"}]},{"id":"torts-negligence-per-se-v2","prompt":"A statute requires contractors to fence excavations in order to protect children who may be attracted to them. A contractor leaves an excavation unfenced in violation of the statute. An adult pedestrian, taking an unauthorized shortcut across the site at night, falls into the excavation and is injured. In the adult's negligence action, what is the effect of the statutory violation?","choices":["It establishes negligence per se, because the contractor's violation was an actual cause of the pedestrian's fall.","It establishes negligence per se, unless the contractor shows that compliance was impossible under the conditions.","It does not establish negligence per se, because an adult trespasser is outside the class the statute protects.","It does not establish negligence per se, because falling into a hole is not the type of harm the statute addresses."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A statutory violation sets the standard of care only if the plaintiff is within the class the statute was designed to protect and the harm is of the type it was meant to prevent. Falling into the pit is the very harm the statute addresses, but the statute protects children, not adult trespassers, so per se treatment fails. Excuse doctrines matter only once the statute applies; the adult must instead prove breach of whatever lesser duty is owed to a trespasser.","subject":"torts","tags":["negligence","negligence-per-se","statute"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Negligence per se (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/negligence_per_se"}]},{"id":"torts-strict-liability-v2","prompt":"A demolition company uses every reasonable precaution while blasting rock near a residential neighborhood. Vibrations from the blasts, though unavoidable, crack the foundation of a neighboring house; no debris lands on the property. The homeowner sues the company. Is the company liable?","choices":["No, because the company exercised reasonable care and the vibration damage could not have been avoided.","No, because strict liability for blasting covers only debris that physically enters the plaintiff's land.","Yes, because a cracked foundation permits a jury to infer negligence under res ipsa loquitur.","Yes, because blasting is abnormally dangerous and the damage arose from the risk that makes it so."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"One who carries on an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for harm within the characteristic risk of the activity, no matter how carefully it is conducted (Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 519–520). Early cases limited recovery to debris (trespass) and required negligence for concussion damage, but the modern rule imposes strict liability for vibration damage as well. Res ipsa loquitur is beside the point because the facts establish due care and liability does not depend on fault.","subject":"torts","tags":["strict-liability","abnormally-dangerous-activity","blasting"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Strict liability (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/strict_liability"}]},{"id":"evidence-character-crim","prompt":"Dana is on trial in federal court for assault arising from a bar fight. In the defense case-in-chief, Dana's lawyer proposes to call several witnesses. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, which proposed testimony is admissible?","choices":["A neighbor's opinion that Dana is a peaceful person, although this opens the door to rebuttal.","A neighbor's account of specific times Dana walked away from fights, to show Dana is peaceful.","A coworker's opinion that Dana is an honest person, to suggest Dana did not start the fight.","A neighbor's opinion that Dana is peaceful, which the prosecutor may rebut only with convictions."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An accused may offer evidence of a pertinent trait (FRE 404(a)(2)(A)), but only by reputation or opinion on direct (FRE 405(a)); specific instances are allowed only when character is an essential element of a charge or defense (FRE 405(b)). Honesty is not pertinent to an assault charge. Once Dana offers peacefulness, the prosecution may rebut with its own reputation or opinion witnesses and may cross-examine Dana's witness about relevant specific instances, so it is not limited to convictions.","subject":"evidence","tags":["character-evidence","criminal","404(a)","405"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 404 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_404"}]},{"id":"evidence-impeachment","prompt":"In a federal robbery trial, the defense calls Lee, who is not a party, as an alibi witness. On cross-examination, the prosecutor seeks to impeach Lee's character for truthfulness. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, which of the following is most likely admissible for that purpose?","choices":["Lee's misdemeanor battery conviction from three years ago, subject to Rule 403 balancing.","Lee's arrest last year for felony assault, if the prosecutor has a good-faith basis to ask.","Lee's misdemeanor perjury conviction from six years ago, without any balancing of prejudice.","Lee's felony drug conviction, with release from prison 15 years ago, under Rule 403 balancing."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under FRE 609(a)(2), a conviction for a crime whose elements required proving a dishonest act or false statement—such as perjury—must be admitted, felony or misdemeanor, with no balancing, if it is within 10 years. A misdemeanor not involving dishonesty is never admissible under Rule 609, and a bare arrest is not a conviction (and assault is not probative of truthfulness under Rule 608(b)). A conviction more than 10 years past both conviction and release from confinement faces the stricter Rule 609(b) test—probative value must substantially outweigh prejudice, with written notice—not ordinary Rule 403 balancing.","subject":"evidence","tags":["impeachment","prior-conviction","609"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 609 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_609"}]},{"id":"evidence-authentication-v2","prompt":"A witness viewed a photograph posted on the defendant's public social-media account and saved a screenshot. At trial, the screenshot is offered to show what appeared on that account. The witness testifies that the screenshot accurately depicts the post as she saw it on the account. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, is that testimony sufficient to authenticate the screenshot?","choices":["No, because social-media evidence must be authenticated by the platform's records custodian.","Yes, if it would let a reasonable juror find the screenshot is what the proponent says it is.","No, unless the judge first finds by a preponderance of the evidence that the defendant posted it.","Yes, because printouts of posts on social-media accounts are self-authenticating under Rule 902."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FRE 901(a) requires only evidence sufficient to support a finding that the item is what the proponent claims; the judge screens under the conditional-relevance standard of FRE 104(b) and the jury decides genuineness. A witness with knowledge (FRE 901(b)(1)) can authenticate a screenshot as an accurate depiction of what appeared on the account. No custodian is required, and Rule 902 does not make ordinary printouts self-authenticating; proving the defendant personally authored the post may require additional circumstantial evidence.","subject":"evidence","tags":["authentication","foundation","901","electronic-evidence"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 901 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_901"}]},{"id":"torts-res-ipsa","prompt":"A pedestrian walking on a public sidewalk is struck by a barrel of flour that falls from the second-floor loading door of a warehouse owned and operated by a merchant. No one saw how the barrel came to fall, and the pedestrian has no evidence of what the warehouse workers did. In the pedestrian's negligence action, the merchant moves for judgment at the close of the plaintiff's case. How should the court rule?","choices":["Grant the motion, because the pedestrian has offered no direct evidence of any specific negligent act.","Deny the motion, because a building operator is strictly liable for objects that fall from its premises.","Grant the motion, unless the pedestrian first rules out every other possible cause of the barrel's fall.","Deny the motion, because barrels do not ordinarily fall from a warehouse without the operator's negligence."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Res ipsa loquitur permits an inference of negligence when the event ordinarily does not occur without negligence and the negligence is attributable to the defendant, typically because it controlled the instrumentality (Byrne v. Boadle). The doctrine substitutes circumstantial proof for direct evidence and is enough to reach the jury, which may but need not infer negligence. The plaintiff need not eliminate every other cause, only show negligence by the defendant is more likely than not; liability is still fault-based, not strict.","subject":"torts","tags":["res-ipsa","negligence","breach"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Res ipsa loquitur (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/res_ipsa_loquitur"}]},{"id":"torts-respondeat-v2","prompt":"While making deliveries for an employer, a driver goes three blocks off the assigned route to buy coffee and negligently strikes a pedestrian. The stop takes five minutes, and the driver intends to resume deliveries immediately. The employer has a written policy discouraging personal stops. Is the employer vicariously liable to the pedestrian?","choices":["Yes, because a brief detour incidental to the workday stays within the scope of employment.","No, because any personal errand during work hours is a frolic outside the scope of employment.","No, because the employer's policy discouraging personal stops took the errand outside the job.","Yes, but only if the employer was itself negligent in hiring or in supervising the driver."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under respondeat superior an employer is liable for torts its employee commits within the scope of employment. A minor, foreseeable deviation is a detour that remains within scope; only a substantial departure for the employee's own purposes is a frolic. An employer's prohibition does not by itself remove conduct incidental to the job from scope, and vicarious liability does not require any independent negligence such as negligent hiring.","subject":"torts","tags":["respondeat-superior","vicarious-liability","frolic-detour"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Respondeat superior (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/respondeat_superior"}]},{"id":"evidence-excited-utterance","prompt":"Seconds after a forklift tips over in a warehouse, a worker who saw it happen, still shaking and shouting, yells, \"That driver was going way too fast!\" The worker cannot be located for trial. In the injured coworker's civil suit, the statement is offered to prove the forklift's speed. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, is it admissible?","choices":["No, because the statement assigns blame to the driver rather than simply describing the event.","Yes, as a present sense impression, but only because the worker is unavailable to testify.","Yes, as an excited utterance about a startling event, made while under the stress it caused.","No, because a lay declarant's opinion about speed cannot be repeated by another witness."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FRE 803(2) admits a statement relating to a startling event made while the declarant was under the stress of excitement it caused; the shaking, shouting worker spoke seconds after the crash. Rule 803 exceptions apply whether or not the declarant is available, so unavailability is not the reason for admission. A statement can qualify even though it expresses an opinion or fault, and estimates of speed are classic lay observations.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","excited-utterance","803(2)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 803 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_803"}]},{"id":"evidence-business-records","prompt":"In a breach-of-contract suit, a shipper offers its delivery log to prove when goods were sent. Under company policy, each entry is typed by a dock clerk at the moment a truck is loaded. The shipper's warehouse manager, who did not make the entries but knows how the log is kept, testifies to these facts. The buyer offers no evidence that the log is untrustworthy. Is the log admissible over a hearsay objection?","choices":["No, because the dock clerks who typed the entries must testify to establish their accuracy.","Yes, but only if the shipper first shows that the dock clerks are unavailable to testify.","No, because records offered by the business that created them are inherently self-serving.","Yes, because a qualified witness may lay the foundation without personal knowledge of each entry."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FRE 803(6) covers records made at or near the time by, or from information transmitted by, someone with knowledge, kept in the course of a regularly conducted activity as a regular practice. The custodian or another qualified witness familiar with the record-keeping system—or a Rule 902(11) certification—can establish this foundation, and the declarant's availability is immaterial. The opponent bears the burden of showing untrustworthiness, as where a record was prepared in anticipation of litigation (Palmer v. Hoffman).","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","business-records","803(6)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 803 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_803"}]},{"id":"evidence-attorney-client","prompt":"In a wrongful-death action, the defendant driver privately told his lawyer, \"I had three beers before I drove that night.\" At his deposition, the plaintiff's counsel asks him (1) what he told his lawyer about drinking, and (2) how many beers he drank before driving. The defendant asserts the attorney-client privilege as to both questions. Which question must he answer?","choices":["Neither, because facts a client discloses to a lawyer in confidence become privileged themselves.","Only the second, because the privilege shields communications but not the underlying facts.","Both, because the privilege does not apply to facts that show a client's possible wrongdoing.","Neither, because the work-product doctrine shields all facts that were gathered for litigation."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The attorney-client privilege protects confidential communications between client and lawyer made to obtain legal advice, so the defendant need not reveal what he told his lawyer. It does not protect the underlying facts, which the client must disclose when asked directly. The crime-fraud exception reaches communications seeking help with a future or ongoing crime or fraud, not a client's account of past conduct, and work product protects documents and mental impressions, not facts.","subject":"evidence","tags":["privilege","attorney-client","facts-vs-communications"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Attorney-client privilege (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/attorney-client_privilege"}]},{"id":"evidence-prior-inconsistent","prompt":"In a federal assault trial, a bystander testifies that the victim threw the first punch. On cross-examination, the bystander admits that a week after the fight he gave a detective a signed but unsworn statement that the defendant threw the first punch. The prosecutor offers the statement and asks that the jury be allowed to consider it as proof that the defendant struck first. Under Federal Rule of Evidence 801(d)(1)(A) as amended effective December 1, 2026, how may the statement be used?","choices":["Only to impeach, because it was not given under penalty of perjury at a prior proceeding or deposition.","For its truth and to impeach, because the bystander testifies and can be cross-examined about it.","Only to impeach, because a witness's prior inconsistent statement is never evidence of the facts stated.","For its truth, but only if the court first finds that the bystander is unavailable as a witness."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As amended effective December 1, 2026, FRE 801(d)(1)(A) excludes from hearsay any prior statement of a testifying witness that is inconsistent with the witness's testimony, provided the witness is subject to cross-examination about it. The amendment deleted the former requirement that the statement have been given under penalty of perjury at a trial, hearing, other proceeding, or deposition; under that older rule an unsworn statement to police could be used only to impeach. The federal rule now matches the California approach (Cal. Evid. Code § 1235). Rule 801(d)(1) applies precisely because the declarant testifies, so unavailability is not required, and the statement remains subject to Rules 403 and 613(b).","subject":"evidence","tags":["impeachment","prior-inconsistent","801(d)(1)(A)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 801 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_801"},{"title":"Supreme Court order of April 8, 2026, amending Fed. R. Evid. 801 (supremecourt.gov)","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/frev26_da3i.pdf"}]},{"id":"torts-products","prompt":"A consumer buys a new pressure cooker from a retailer in its sealed factory box. Because of a flaw introduced during assembly—unlike other units from the same line—the lid lock fails during normal use and the consumer is burned. The manufacturer's quality-control program was excellent, and the retailer had no way to inspect the sealed unit. In the consumer's strict products liability action against the retailer, who will most likely prevail?","choices":["The retailer, because it had no reasonable opportunity to inspect the sealed product before it sold it.","The retailer, because the manufacturer's excellent quality control shows the cooker was not defective.","The consumer, because a commercial seller of a defective product is liable even if it took all possible care.","The consumer, but only because the consumer was in privity of contract with the retailer that sold it."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Strict products liability makes every commercial seller in the distribution chain liable for injuries caused by a product that was defective when it left the seller's hands, regardless of fault (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 402A). A unit that departs from its intended design has a manufacturing defect even if quality control was excellent, and the retailer's lack of an opportunity to inspect defeats only a negligence claim. Privity is not required for strict liability, and the retailer may seek indemnity from the manufacturer (some states protect innocent sellers by statute).","subject":"torts","tags":["products-liability","strict-liability","manufacturing-defect"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Products liability (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/products_liability"}]},{"id":"torts-defamation-public","prompt":"A newspaper reporter who despises a sitting senator publishes a story falsely stating that the senator took a bribe. The reporter hoped the story would end the senator's career, but she sincerely believed it, relying on a longtime aide who had always been reliable. A careful reporter would have checked public records that disproved the claim. The senator sues for defamation. Who will most likely prevail?","choices":["The newspaper, because the reporter neither knew the story was false nor seriously doubted its truth.","The senator, because the reporter's hatred and desire to harm him establish constitutional actual malice.","The senator, because a reasonable reporter would have checked the public records before publishing.","The newspaper, because statements about a public official's conduct in office are absolutely privileged."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under New York Times Co. v. Sullivan, a public official must prove by clear and convincing evidence that the defendant published with actual malice—knowledge of falsity or reckless disregard for the truth. Reckless disregard means the defendant actually entertained serious doubts about truth (St. Amant v. Thompson); ill will is not actual malice, and failure to investigate that a reasonable reporter would undertake is only negligence. The privilege is qualified, not absolute—knowing lies about officials are actionable.","subject":"torts","tags":["defamation","actual-malice","first-amendment","nyt-v-sullivan"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"New York Times Co. v. Sullivan, 376 U.S. 254 (1964) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/376/254/"}]},{"id":"evidence-rule-403","prompt":"A defendant is charged in federal court with possessing a firearm after a felony conviction. He offers to stipulate that he has a prior felony conviction. The prosecutor refuses the stipulation and seeks to prove the name and nature of the prior offense—aggravated assault with a firearm. How should the court rule on the defendant's Rule 403 objection?","choices":["Admit them, because the prosecution is entitled to prove each element with evidence of its own choosing.","Admit them, because evidence proving an element of the charged offense is not subject to Rule 403 balancing.","Exclude them, because a prior conviction is propensity evidence that Rule 404(b) bars for every purpose.","Exclude the name and nature, because the offered stipulation leaves them little probative value and real prejudice."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FRE 403 permits exclusion when probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice, and the availability of alternative proof bears on that balance. In Old Chief v. United States, the Court held it an abuse of discretion to reject a stipulation to felon status and admit the name and nature of a prior conviction that invited a propensity inference. The general rule that the prosecution may choose its own evidence yields here because felon status is purely a matter of legal status; Rule 403 applies to all relevant evidence, and Rule 404(b) does not bar other-act evidence offered for a non-propensity purpose.","subject":"evidence","tags":["rule-403","balancing","old-chief","stipulation"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Old Chief v. United States, 519 U.S. 172 (1997) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/519/172/"}]},{"id":"torts-battery","prompt":"As a prank, a student pulls a chair out from under a classmate who is in the act of sitting down. The student wants only to embarrass him and does not want him hurt. The classmate falls to the floor, suffering no physical injury but considerable humiliation. Is the student liable for battery?","choices":["No, because the student intended only embarrassment and did not intend any physical harm.","Yes, because the student knew with substantial certainty the classmate would hit the floor.","No, because the student never touched the classmate's body or anything attached to it.","Yes, but only nominal damages, because the classmate suffered no physical injury at all."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Battery requires an act intended to cause a harmful or offensive contact (or knowledge that such contact is substantially certain to result) and a resulting harmful or offensive contact. Pulling the chair made contact with the floor substantially certain (Garratt v. Dailey), and an indirect contact set in motion by the defendant suffices. No physical injury is required, and the plaintiff may recover for the dignitary harm—here humiliation—not just nominal damages.","subject":"torts","tags":["battery","intentional-torts","intent"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Battery (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/battery"}]},{"id":"evidence-privilege-waiver","prompt":"A client receives a confidential letter from her lawyer evaluating her potential liability for a car accident. Doubting the advice, she posts a photo of the full letter in a public online forum and asks strangers whether the lawyer is right. In the later lawsuit, the injured plaintiff seeks production of the letter. Is the letter still protected by the attorney-client privilege?","choices":["Yes, because only the lawyer who wrote the letter may waive the privilege attached to its contents.","Yes, because a disclosure waives the privilege only when it is made directly to the opposing party.","No, because the client's voluntary disclosure to outsiders waived the privilege as to the letter.","No, because a lawyer's advice about a past accident is never within the attorney-client privilege."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The privilege belongs to the client, who waives it by voluntarily disclosing the communication to third parties outside the privilege; publishing the letter to strangers destroys the confidentiality the privilege protects. Waiver does not depend on disclosure to the adversary. The privilege does cover a lawyer's confidential advice about a client's past conduct—that is its core—so the letter was privileged until the client disclosed it.","subject":"evidence","tags":["privilege","waiver","attorney-client"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 502 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_502"}]},{"id":"evidence-dying-declaration","prompt":"Shot during a robbery, a store clerk tells a paramedic, \"I'm not going to make it—my nephew Ray shot me.\" The clerk survives but remains in a coma and cannot testify. Ray is prosecuted in federal court for attempted murder, and the government offers the clerk's statement as a dying declaration. How should the court rule?","choices":["Admit it, because the clerk believed death was imminent and is now unavailable to testify.","Exclude it, because the exception applies only if the declarant actually died from the wound.","Admit it, because statements identifying an assailant to medical personnel are within the exception.","Exclude it, because in criminal cases the exception is limited to homicide prosecutions."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FRE 804(b)(2) applies only in a prosecution for homicide or in a civil case, to a statement the unavailable declarant made while believing death was imminent about its cause or circumstances. This is an attempted-murder prosecution, so the exception is unavailable even though the clerk believed death was imminent. The declarant need not actually die—only be unavailable—and identifying a shooter is not covered by the medical-diagnosis exception either.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","dying-declaration","804(b)(2)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 804 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_804"}]},{"id":"evidence-state-of-mind","prompt":"Two days before she disappeared, a woman told a friend, \"I'm driving to Reno on Friday,\" and also said, \"Last week my ex-husband threatened to kill me.\" At the ex-husband's federal murder trial, the prosecution offers both statements through the friend. Under Rule 803(3), which statement is admissible for the purpose stated?","choices":["The statement about the Reno trip, to show that she probably went to Reno on Friday.","The statement about the threat, to show that her ex-husband actually threatened her.","Both statements, to show that she went to Reno and that her ex-husband threatened her.","Neither, because a declared plan cannot be used to show that the plan was carried out."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"FRE 803(3) admits a statement of the declarant's then-existing state of mind, including intent or plan, and a statement of intent may be used to prove the declarant later acted on it (Mutual Life Ins. Co. v. Hillmon). The rule expressly excludes a statement of memory or belief offered to prove the fact remembered, so the threat statement cannot prove the threat occurred (Shepard v. United States). Statements to a friend are nontestimonial, so the Confrontation Clause is not implicated.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","state-of-mind","803(3)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 803 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_803"}]},{"id":"evidence-best-evidence","prompt":"In a federal fraud trial, a witness testifies, \"On a phone call, the defendant told me the investors' money was gone.\" The call was recorded, and the recording still exists, but the prosecution does not offer it. The defense objects under the best evidence rule. How should the court rule?","choices":["Sustain it, because a recording of the call exists and is therefore the best evidence of what was said.","Sustain it, unless the prosecution shows that the original recording has been lost or destroyed.","Overrule it, because the best evidence rule applies to writings but not to audio recordings.","Overrule it, because the witness is recounting what he heard, not proving the recording's contents."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FRE 1002 requires an original (or admissible duplicate) only when a party seeks to prove the content of a writing, recording, or photograph. A participant with personal knowledge of a conversation may testify to it even though a recording also exists, because the witness is proving the event, not the recording. Rule 1001 expressly covers recordings, so the rule would apply if the witness's knowledge came only from listening to the tape.","subject":"evidence","tags":["best-evidence","original-document","1002"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 1002 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_1002"}]},{"id":"torts-superseding-cause","prompt":"Tenants repeatedly warn a landlord that the lock on the apartment building's front door is broken and that strangers have been wandering the halls. The landlord does nothing for a month. An intruder enters through the unlocked door and assaults a tenant. In the tenant's negligence action against the landlord, is the intruder's crime a superseding cause?","choices":["Yes, because a third person's intentional crime always breaks the chain of proximate cause.","Yes, because the intruder, not the landlord, was the direct and immediate cause of the harm.","No, because criminal entry was the very risk that made the landlord's inaction negligent.","No, because a negligent defendant is liable for any harm that would not have occurred but for it."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"An intervening act supersedes only when it is unforeseeable and outside the scope of the risk the defendant's negligence created. Where the defendant's conduct is negligent precisely because it exposes others to third-party crime, the crime is foreseeable and does not cut off liability, even though an unforeseeable, independent crime often would. But-for causation alone is not enough; the harm must also fall within the scope of the risk.","subject":"torts","tags":["causation","superseding","intervening","proximate-cause"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Proximate cause (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/proximate_cause"}]},{"id":"torts-iied","prompt":"A debt collector, knowing that a widow is in fragile health and grieving her husband's recent death, calls her nightly for a month, falsely telling her she will be arrested and her children taken away unless she pays her late husband's debt. She suffers severe anxiety and insomnia requiring psychiatric treatment but no physical injury. In her suit for intentional infliction of emotional distress, who will most likely prevail?","choices":["The collector, because emotional-distress claims require proof of a physical injury resulting from the distress.","The widow, because the collector's outrageous conduct intentionally or recklessly caused her severe distress.","The collector, because he was pursuing a legitimate debt and has a privilege to insist on his legal rights.","The widow, but only if the collector's threats were also communicated to third persons who believed them."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"IIED requires extreme and outrageous conduct, intent or recklessness, causation, and severe emotional distress; physical injury is not required. Repeated false threats of arrest and loss of children, aimed at a person known to be especially vulnerable, exceed all bounds of decency. The privilege to pursue legal rights does not cover false threats and harassment, and publication to third parties is an element of defamation, not IIED.","subject":"torts","tags":["iied","intentional-torts","outrageous-conduct"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Intentional infliction of emotional distress (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/intentional_infliction_of_emotional_distress"}]},{"id":"torts-rescue-doctrine","prompt":"A driver negligently crashes, trapping his passenger in a burning car. A passerby pulls the passenger free but is burned. In doing so, the passerby acted somewhat carelessly—though not recklessly—by reaching through the flames rather than opening the rear door. The passerby sues the driver in a pure comparative fault jurisdiction. What is the most likely result?","choices":["The passerby recovers, reduced by her share of fault, because rescue is a foreseeable response to peril.","The passerby recovers nothing, because she voluntarily encountered a known danger and assumed the risk.","The passerby recovers nothing, because the driver's duty of care ran only to persons inside the car.","The passerby recovers in full, because a rescuer's own carelessness is not considered against her."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the rescue doctrine, danger invites rescue, so a negligent defendant owes a duty to a rescuer (Wagner v. International Railway Co.), and a rescue undertaken voluntarily is not treated as assumption of risk. Traditionally only rash or reckless rescue barred recovery; in comparative fault jurisdictions a rescuer's ordinary negligence reduces damages in proportion to her fault rather than being ignored.","subject":"torts","tags":["rescue-doctrine","duty","foreseeability","comparative-fault"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Comparative negligence (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/comparative_negligence"}]},{"id":"evidence-party-opponent-nonhearsay","prompt":"In a federal civil suit arising from a car crash, the plaintiff calls a tow-truck driver who testifies that at the scene the defendant said, \"I'm sorry—I looked down at my phone.\" The defendant is present at trial and objects on hearsay grounds. How should the court rule?","choices":["Sustain it, because the defendant is available and must be called to testify to the remark.","Overrule it, because the remark is a statement against interest by the defendant himself.","Overrule it, because the defendant's own statement offered against him is not hearsay.","Sustain it, because an apology made after an accident is inadmissible to prove fault."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under FRE 801(d)(2)(A), a party's own statement offered against that party is excluded from the definition of hearsay; it need not be against interest when made, and availability is irrelevant. The statement-against-interest exception in Rule 804(b)(3) requires an unavailable declarant, so it is the wrong route here. The FRE contain no general apology exclusion; Rule 409 covers only offers to pay medical expenses, and some state statutes protect expressions of sympathy but not admissions of fault.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay","party-opponent","nonhearsay","801(d)(2)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 801 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_801"}]},{"id":"evidence-404b-nonpropensity","prompt":"A defendant is charged in federal court with burglarizing a house; the burglar drilled out the deadbolt and left a joker playing card on the kitchen table. The government gives proper notice and offers evidence that the defendant committed two earlier burglaries using the same method and leaving a joker each time. He was never charged with those burglaries. The defense disputes identity. Is the evidence admissible?","choices":["No, because evidence of uncharged crimes may never be offered against a criminal defendant.","Yes, to prove identity, because the distinctive method links the defendant to the charged burglary.","Yes, because the earlier crimes show the defendant's propensity to commit similar burglaries.","No, unless the government first proves the earlier burglaries beyond a reasonable doubt."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FRE 404(b) bars other-act evidence to prove character and conforming conduct but permits it for other purposes such as identity, motive, intent, or absence of mistake. A signature modus operandi is a classic identity use, subject to Rule 403 and a limiting instruction on request. The other acts need not have been charged; the jury need only be able to find by a preponderance that the defendant committed them (Huddleston v. United States), and in criminal cases the prosecution must give the notice required by Rule 404(b)(3).","subject":"evidence","tags":["character-evidence","404b","nonpropensity","identity"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 404 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_404"}]},{"id":"evidence-subsequent-remedial-measures","prompt":"A worker's hand is crushed in a punch press. After the accident, the manufacturer adds a guard to the press design. In the worker's federal products liability action, the manufacturer's engineer testifies that a guard was not technically feasible on that press. The worker then offers evidence of the post-accident guard. Is the evidence admissible?","choices":["No, because Rule 407 bars evidence of subsequent remedial measures for every purpose.","Yes, to prove that the press was defectively designed when it was sold to the employer.","No, because the feasibility exception applies to negligence claims but not to product claims.","Yes, to rebut the claim that a guard was infeasible, with a limiting instruction if requested."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FRE 407 excludes subsequent remedial measures offered to prove negligence, culpable conduct, a product or design defect, or the need for a warning. The rule permits such evidence for other purposes, including feasibility of precautionary measures if disputed, ownership, control, or impeachment. Because the manufacturer contested feasibility, the guard is admissible for that purpose but not as proof of defect; the exceptions apply in product cases just as in negligence cases.","subject":"evidence","tags":["rule-407","subsequent-remedial-measures","feasibility"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 407 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_407"}]},{"id":"evidence-recorded-recollection","prompt":"A safety inspector testifying for the plaintiff cannot recall the readings she took at a plant, even after reviewing the notes she wrote during the inspection. She testifies that she wrote the notes while taking the readings and that they were accurate when made. The plaintiff then offers the notes. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, how should the court proceed?","choices":["Allow the notes to be read to the jury, but not received as an exhibit unless the defendant offers them.","Admit the notes as an exhibit, because the inspector laid a proper foundation as the notes' author.","Exclude the notes, because a writing that fails to refresh a witness's memory cannot become evidence.","Allow the notes to be read to the jury, but only if the inspector is found to be unavailable to testify."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under FRE 803(5), a record of a matter the witness once knew but cannot now recall well enough to testify fully and accurately is admissible if it was made or adopted when the matter was fresh and accurately reflects her knowledge. The record may be read into evidence but may be received as an exhibit only if offered by an adverse party. Failure to refresh recollection under Rule 612 is exactly what triggers Rule 803(5), and Rule 803 exceptions do not require unavailability.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","recorded-recollection","803(5)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 803 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_803"}]},{"id":"evidence-prior-consistent-rehabilitate","prompt":"A government witness testifies that she saw the defendant sell drugs. On cross-examination, defense counsel suggests that she invented the story after the government offered her a leniency deal in March. The prosecution then offers her January statement to a friend describing the sale exactly as she testified. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, is the January statement admissible?","choices":["Yes, but only to rehabilitate her, because a prior consistent statement is not evidence of truth.","Yes, for its truth, because it predates the alleged motive and rebuts the charge of fabrication.","No, because a witness's own out-of-court statement may not be used to bolster her testimony.","No, unless the January statement was made under penalty of perjury at a formal proceeding."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FRE 801(d)(1)(B)(i) makes a testifying witness's consistent statement nonhearsay when offered to rebut an express or implied charge of recent fabrication or improper motive, and Tome v. United States requires that the statement predate the alleged motive. Since 2014, Rule 801(d)(1)(B)(ii) also admits consistent statements to rehabilitate a witness attacked on other grounds, such as inconsistency or faulty memory. Rule 801(d)(1)(B) has never required that a consistent statement be made under oath; the oath-and-proceeding requirement applied only to prior inconsistent statements under former Rule 801(d)(1)(A), and it was eliminated effective December 1, 2026.","subject":"evidence","tags":["rehabilitation","prior-consistent","801(d)(1)(B)"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Tome v. United States, 513 U.S. 150 (1995) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/513/150/"}]},{"id":"evidence-judicial-notice-civil-criminal","prompt":"In a federal prosecution for selling drugs within 1,000 feet of a school, the judge takes judicial notice, based on official city maps, that the street corner where the sale occurred is within 1,000 feet of an elementary school. Under Rule 201, how should the jury be instructed about the noticed fact?","choices":["That it must accept the noticed fact as conclusively established for the case.","That it must accept the noticed fact unless the defendant offers contrary evidence.","That it may not consider the fact, since notice is barred on elements in criminal cases.","That it may, but is not required to, accept the noticed fact as conclusive."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FRE 201(b) allows notice of an adjudicative fact not subject to reasonable dispute because it is generally known or accurately and readily determinable from reliable sources such as official maps. Under Rule 201(f), in a civil case the jury must accept the noticed fact as conclusive, but in a criminal case it must be told it may or may not do so, preserving the defendant's right to a jury determination of every element. Nothing in the rule bars notice of facts in criminal cases.","subject":"evidence","tags":["judicial-notice","rule-201","adjudicative-facts"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 201 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_201"}]},{"id":"torts-no-duty-to-rescue","prompt":"A strong swimmer relaxing on a public dock watches a stranger struggle and drown twenty feet away. A life ring hangs within arm's reach, and throwing it would have posed no risk to the swimmer. The swimmer had no relationship with the stranger and did nothing to create the danger. Under the common-law majority rule, is the swimmer liable for the death?","choices":["No, because there is generally no duty to aid a stranger whose peril one did not create.","Yes, because the swimmer could have helped at no risk, which imposes a duty to act reasonably.","No, unless the swimmer was a trained lifeguard, because training creates a duty to rescue anyone.","Yes, because the swimmer's failure to act was a but-for cause of the stranger's drowning."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The common law generally imposes no affirmative duty to rescue a stranger, even when rescue would be easy and safe. A duty arises from a special relationship (such as carrier–passenger or innkeeper–guest), the defendant's creation of the peril, or a voluntary undertaking, none of which exists here; professional training alone does not create a duty while off-duty. Causation is irrelevant without a duty, and a few states impose limited statutory duties to assist.","subject":"torts","tags":["duty","rescue","special-relationship","nonfeasance"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"torts-eggshell-plaintiff","prompt":"A driver negligently rear-ends a stopped car at low speed. An ordinary person in the car would have suffered, at most, bruising. The plaintiff has hemophilia, which the driver could not have known, and suffers severe internal bleeding requiring surgery. For what harm is the driver liable?","choices":["Only the bruising that a person of ordinary health would have suffered in the collision.","None of it, because the preexisting condition is a superseding cause of the plaintiff's harm.","The full extent of the injuries, because a tortfeasor takes the victim as it finds her.","The full extent of the injuries, but only if the driver knew of the plaintiff's condition."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under the eggshell-skull rule, once a defendant has negligently caused some physical injury, it is liable for the full extent of that harm even if a preexisting vulnerability made it far more severe than anyone could foresee. Foreseeability limits the type of harm for which a defendant is liable, not its extent, and the defendant's knowledge of the condition is irrelevant. A preexisting condition is not an intervening act and cannot be a superseding cause.","subject":"torts","tags":["damages","eggshell-plaintiff","causation"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Eggshell skull rule (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/eggshell_skull_rule"}]},{"id":"torts-defamation-private-figure-negligence","prompt":"A local newspaper falsely reports that a private schoolteacher was arrested for drunk driving, confusing her with someone of the same name. The reporter never doubted the story but did not check the booking photo, as a reasonable reporter would have. The teacher sues for defamation. The story concerns a matter of public concern. Under the majority rule, can she recover damages for her proven actual injuries?","choices":["No, because she cannot show the newspaper knew the report was false or was reckless.","Yes, without any proof of fault, because a false accusation of a crime is libel per se.","No, because a good-faith mistake of identity is a complete defense to defamation.","Yes, if she proves that the newspaper was negligent as to the statement's falsity."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Gertz v. Robert Welch, Inc., states may not impose liability without fault when a private figure sues over a matter of public concern, but they may allow recovery of actual damages on proof of negligence as to falsity. Actual malice is required only for public figures or for presumed and punitive damages in such cases. Libel per se historically meant strict liability, but that rule is displaced here by the constitutional fault requirement, and an honest mistake is no defense if it was negligent.","subject":"torts","tags":["defamation","private-figure","fault","gertz"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Gertz v. Robert Welch, Inc., 418 U.S. 323 (1974) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/418/323/"}]},{"id":"torts-shopkeeper-privilege","prompt":"Based on a security video, a store guard reasonably believes a customer slipped a watch into her purse. He politely asks her to accompany him to an office and holds her there for 15 minutes until police arrive. A search reveals she has no watch; the video was misleading. She sues the store for false imprisonment. Who will most likely prevail?","choices":["The customer, because the merchant's privilege applies only when the customer actually took the goods.","The customer, because only a police officer may lawfully detain a person suspected of committing theft.","The store, because it had reasonable grounds and held her in a reasonable manner and for a reasonable time.","The store, because a merchant may hold any suspected shoplifter until police arrive, however long it takes."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The shopkeeper's privilege allows a merchant with reasonable grounds to suspect shoplifting to detain the suspect in a reasonable manner, for a reasonable time, on or near the premises, to investigate. Unlike the older common-law recapture rule, the privilege protects a reasonable mistake, so it applies even though she took nothing. Fifteen minutes of polite detention awaiting police is reasonable, but the privilege does not authorize an unlimited detention.","subject":"torts","tags":["false-imprisonment","shopkeeper-privilege","intentional-torts"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"False imprisonment (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/false_imprisonment"}]},{"id":"torts-express-assumption-risk","prompt":"Before a zip-line tour, a customer signs a clear, conspicuous release of \"all claims arising from the operator's negligence.\" A guide carelessly fails to double-check her harness clip, and she is injured when it opens. No statute addresses such releases. In her negligence action, how will the court most likely treat the release?","choices":["As a bar to the claim, because a clear release of ordinary negligence for recreation is generally valid.","As void, because an exculpatory clause can never release a party from liability for its own negligence.","As void, because a release required as a condition of joining a recreational activity lacks consideration.","As no bar to the claim, because a guide's failure to check equipment is not a risk inherent in the sport."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Express assumption of risk—an exculpatory agreement—generally bars claims for ordinary negligence if it is clear and unambiguous and does not violate public policy. Recreational activities are usually not services of public necessity of the kind that invalidate releases (Tunkl v. Regents of University of California), and access to the activity is consideration. Releases typically cannot excuse intentional, reckless, or grossly negligent conduct; the inherent-risk analysis governs implied primary assumption of risk, not an express release covering negligence.","subject":"torts","tags":["assumption-of-risk","waiver","defenses","exculpatory-clause"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Assumption of risk (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/assumption_of_risk"}]},{"id":"v3-torts-private-necessity","prompt":"During a sudden, violent storm, a boat owner moors his boat at a private dock without permission to avoid sinking. The storm lasts all night; waves slam the boat against the dock, causing $8,000 in damage. The boat owner acted reasonably throughout, and keeping the boat at the dock was the only way to save it. The dock owner sues for the damage. Is the boat owner liable?","choices":["No, because necessity is a complete privilege that bars recovery for harm done in the emergency.","Yes, because private necessity privileges the use of the dock but not the harm the use causes.","No, because the storm, an act of God, rather than the boat owner's conduct caused the damage.","Yes, because necessity is no defense once the dock's owner has refused permission to moor."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Private necessity privileges an entry or use of another's property to avert a serious threat to one's own interests, so the boat owner was not a trespasser and the dock owner could not have cast him off (Ploof v. Putnam). But the privilege is incomplete: the actor must pay for actual damage caused (Vincent v. Lake Erie Transportation Co.). Public necessity, by contrast, is a complete privilege, and the boat owner's deliberate choice to hold the boat against the dock makes the damage attributable to him.","subject":"torts","tags":["necessity","privileges","trespass","intentional-torts"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-torts-deadly-force-property","prompt":"A farmer whose unoccupied barn has been burglarized several times rigs a shotgun to fire at anyone who opens the barn door. He posts no warning. A trespasser who breaks in to steal tools is wounded by the blast. The trespasser sues the farmer for battery. Who will most likely prevail?","choices":["The farmer, because the trespasser was committing a crime and assumed the risk of being injured.","The trespasser, because deadly force may not be used to protect property alone, even by a device.","The farmer, because an owner may use whatever force is needed to prevent repeated thefts.","The trespasser, but only because the farmer failed to post a sign warning of the shotgun."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An owner may use reasonable, non-deadly force to protect property, but deadly force is privileged only when the actor faces a threat of death or serious bodily harm. A mechanical device is privileged only to the extent the owner would have been privileged to use the same force in person, so a spring gun guarding an unoccupied barn is not (Katko v. Briney). The intruder's crime does not bar recovery, and a warning sign would not make deadly force privileged when the owner could not have used it in person.","subject":"torts","tags":["defense-of-property","privileges","battery","spring-gun"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Battery (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/battery"}]},{"id":"v3-torts-products-foreseeable-misuse","prompt":"A manufacturer sells a lightweight folding chair. It knows from customer reports that people commonly stand on such chairs to reach high shelves, but the chair carries no warning against doing so. A buyer stands on the chair to change a light bulb; the seat collapses and she is injured. In her strict liability failure-to-warn action, the manufacturer argues misuse. How will the court most likely treat that argument?","choices":["It is a complete defense, because a product is defective only if it fails in the use for which it was designed.","It is a complete defense, because a buyer who uses a product in an unintended way assumes the risk of injury.","It is irrelevant for every purpose, because strict products liability disregards the plaintiff's own conduct.","It is not a complete defense, because the misuse was foreseeable and is the use the warning should address."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Misuse defeats a products claim only when it is unforeseeable; a manufacturer must design and warn against reasonably foreseeable misuses, and the manufacturer here knew of this one. Because the claim is that the chair needed a warning against standing on it, foreseeable misuse is the heart of the plaintiff's case rather than a defense. In most comparative fault jurisdictions a plaintiff's unreasonable conduct may still reduce damages, so it is not irrelevant for all purposes.","subject":"torts","tags":["products-liability","failure-to-warn","misuse","defenses"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Products liability (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/products_liability"}]},{"id":"v3-torts-licensee-duty","prompt":"A homeowner invites a friend over for dinner. The homeowner knows that a back-porch step is rotted and likely to give way, but the rot is hidden under fresh paint and he says nothing. After dinner, the friend steps on it and is injured. In a jurisdiction that retains the traditional entrant categories, is the homeowner liable?","choices":["No, because a social guest is owed only a duty to refrain from willful or wanton misconduct.","Yes, because a social guest is an invitee, owed a duty to inspect for and repair hidden dangers.","Yes, because a host must warn a social guest of known, concealed dangers she is unlikely to discover.","No, because a host has no duty to repair his premises for a guest who enters for her own benefit."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under the traditional categories, a social guest is a licensee, not an invitee. A possessor owes a licensee a duty to warn of (or make safe) known dangerous conditions that the licensee is unlikely to discover, but no duty to inspect for unknown dangers. The homeowner knew of the hidden rot and gave no warning, so he breached the duty even though he had no obligation to repair; the willful-or-wanton standard applies to undiscovered trespassers.","subject":"torts","tags":["duty","landowner","licensee","premises-liability"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-torts-attractive-nuisance","prompt":"A power company keeps a high-voltage transformer box in an unfenced vacant lot. It knows that children often cut through the lot and that some climb on the box. A locked cage would cost little. A nine-year-old trespasser, unaware of the danger, climbs the box, touches an exposed terminal, and is badly burned. In the child's negligence action, is the company liable?","choices":["Likely yes, because it knew children trespassed there, and the hidden risk was cheap to eliminate.","Likely no, because a possessor owes trespassers only a duty to avoid willful or wanton conduct.","Likely no, because the doctrine applies only if the transformer itself lured the child onto the lot.","Likely yes, because a possessor is strictly liable to child trespassers for artificial conditions."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the attractive nuisance doctrine (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 339), a possessor is liable to a trespassing child for an artificial condition if it knows children are likely to trespass, the condition poses an unreasonable risk of serious harm, children because of their youth will not appreciate the risk, and the burden of eliminating the danger is slight compared with the risk. All elements are met here. The modern rule does not require that the condition lured the child onto the land, and liability is negligence-based, not strict.","subject":"torts","tags":["duty","landowner","attractive-nuisance","child-trespasser"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-torts-nied-bystander","prompt":"A mother at home receives a phone call telling her that her son has just been struck by a negligently driven car a block away. She runs to the scene, arriving about five minutes later, and sees her son lying badly injured in the street. She suffers severe emotional distress. In a jurisdiction that follows the bystander rule of Thing v. La Chusa, can she recover from the driver for negligent infliction of emotional distress?","choices":["Yes, because she is closely related to the victim and her severe distress was foreseeable.","No, because she was never within the zone of physical danger created by the driver.","Yes, because seeing her injured son at the scene satisfies the perception requirement.","No, because she did not perceive the accident while it was injuring her son."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Thing v. La Chusa limits bystander NIED recovery to a plaintiff who is closely related to the victim, is present at the scene of the injury-producing event when it occurs and is then aware it is causing injury, and suffers serious emotional distress. Arriving minutes later and seeing the aftermath does not satisfy contemporaneous perception, though some jurisdictions following the more flexible Dillon v. Legg factors might allow it. The zone-of-danger test is a different, more restrictive approach, so it is the wrong reason here.","subject":"torts","tags":["nied","bystander","duty","emotional-distress"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-torts-pure-economic-loss","prompt":"While digging, a contractor negligently severs an underground power cable owned by the electric utility, cutting power to an industrial park for two days. A factory in the park suffers no personal injury or property damage but loses $200,000 in profits from the shutdown. The factory has no contract with the contractor. Can the factory recover its lost profits in negligence?","choices":["Yes, because the loss of business at the park was a foreseeable result of the negligent digging.","No, because purely economic loss unaccompanied by physical harm is generally not recoverable.","No, because the contractor's digging was not an actual cause of the factory's lost profits.","Yes, because the economic loss rule limits recovery only between parties in a contract."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under the general rule, a defendant whose negligence causes no personal injury or property damage to the plaintiff is not liable for the plaintiff's purely economic loss, even if foreseeable (Robins Dry Dock & Repair Co. v. Flint; Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Economic Harm § 7). The digging was plainly an actual cause of the loss; the bar is a policy limit on duty, grounded in the risk of indeterminate liability, and it applies to strangers, not only contracting parties. The factory could recover if it had suffered physical harm to its own property, and some states recognize narrow special-relationship exceptions.","subject":"torts","tags":["negligence","economic-loss-rule","duty"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-torts-hand-formula","prompt":"A railroad could install a warning light at a rural crossing for $20,000. Without the light, there is a 1% chance over the light's useful life of a crash at the crossing causing $500,000 in harm; the light would prevent it. The railroad does not install the light, and a crash occurs. Applying the Learned Hand formula, what does the analysis suggest?","choices":["No breach, because the burden of the precaution exceeds the expected harm it would avoid.","Breach, because the railroad could foresee that a crash at the crossing was possible.","Breach, because the $500,000 potential harm far exceeds the $20,000 cost of the light.","No breach, because a 1% risk is too remote to be foreseeable as a matter of law."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under United States v. Carroll Towing Co., a defendant is negligent if the burden of an untaken precaution (B) is less than the probability of harm (P) multiplied by the gravity of the loss (L). Here PL is 1% × $500,000 = $5,000, which is less than the $20,000 burden, so the formula suggests the railroad was not negligent. Comparing B to L alone ignores probability, and the analysis does not treat low-probability harms as unforeseeable; it weighs them.","subject":"torts","tags":["negligence","breach","hand-formula","cost-benefit"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-torts-alternative-liability","prompt":"Two hunters, acting independently, each negligently fire a shotgun toward a quail at the same moment. A single pellet strikes a third hunter in the eye. The pellet cannot be traced to either gun, and each shooter was equally likely to have fired it. The injured hunter sues both. What is the most likely result?","choices":["Neither is liable, because the plaintiff cannot prove by a preponderance who caused it.","Each is liable for half the damages, because each had a 50% chance of having caused it.","Both are liable unless one of them proves that his shot did not cause the injury.","Both are liable, because they acted in concert and each answers for the other's shots."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under alternative liability (Summers v. Tice; Restatement (Second) of Torts § 433B(3)), when two or more defendants each acted negligently and one of them caused the harm but the plaintiff cannot determine which, the burden shifts to each defendant to prove that he did not cause it. A defendant who cannot carry that burden is liable for the full harm, not a probability-based share. Concert of action requires an agreement or common design to act tortiously, which independent hunters lack.","subject":"torts","tags":["causation","actual-cause","alternative-liability","summers-v-tice"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-torts-loss-of-chance","prompt":"A physician negligently fails to diagnose a patient's cancer. With a timely diagnosis, the patient would have had a 40% chance of survival; because of the delay, the chance fell to 10%. The patient dies. In a jurisdiction that recognizes the loss-of-chance doctrine and values it proportionally, what may the estate recover?","choices":["Nothing, because the patient was more likely than not to die even with a timely diagnosis.","Full wrongful-death damages, because the delay was a substantial factor in causing the death.","Damages for the lost 40% chance, because that was the patient's full chance of survival.","Damages for the lost 30% chance, measured as that share of full wrongful-death damages."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under the traditional all-or-nothing rule, a plaintiff whose original chance of survival was 50% or less could not prove the negligence more likely than not caused death and recovered nothing. Jurisdictions recognizing loss of chance treat the reduced chance itself as the injury and, under the proportional approach, award the percentage lost—here 40% minus 10%, or 30%—multiplied by full wrongful-death damages (e.g., Matsuyama v. Birnbaum). Using 40% ignores the 10% chance that remained.","subject":"torts","tags":["causation","loss-of-chance","medical-malpractice","damages"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-torts-joint-several-contribution","prompt":"Two drivers negligently collide, and the crash injures a pedestrian, who suffers an indivisible $100,000 injury. The jury assigns 70% of the fault to Driver A and 30% to Driver B. The jurisdiction retains joint and several liability and allows contribution in proportion to fault. Driver A has few assets. The pedestrian seeks to collect the entire judgment from Driver B. What are B's obligations and rights?","choices":["B must pay only $30,000, because each driver pays only its own share of fault.","B must pay the full $100,000 but may seek $70,000 from A in contribution.","B must pay the full $100,000 and may recover all of it from A by indemnity.","B must pay the full $100,000 and has no claim against A, a joint tortfeasor."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under joint and several liability, each tortfeasor whose negligence caused an indivisible injury is liable to the plaintiff for the entire amount, so the pedestrian may collect all $100,000 from B. Contribution then lets a defendant who paid more than its share recover the excess from the other tortfeasor in proportion to fault—here $70,000—with the risk of A's insolvency falling on B. Full indemnity is reserved for situations such as vicarious liability, and many states have replaced or limited joint and several liability by statute.","subject":"torts","tags":["joint-and-several","contribution","multiple-defendants"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-torts-modified-comparative-fault","prompt":"A plaintiff suffers $100,000 in damages in a two-car collision. The jury finds the plaintiff 50% at fault and the defendant 50% at fault. How much would the plaintiff recover in a pure comparative fault state, a modified comparative fault state with a 50% bar, and a modified state with a 51% bar?","choices":["$50,000 under pure comparative fault, but nothing under either modified version.","$50,000 under pure comparative fault and the 50% bar, but nothing under the 51% bar.","$50,000 under pure comparative fault and the 51% bar, but nothing under the 50% bar.","$50,000 under all three, because she is barred only if more at fault than the defendant."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Pure comparative fault reduces the award by the plaintiff's percentage, so she recovers $50,000. Under a 50% bar, a plaintiff whose fault is as great as the defendant's (50% or more) recovers nothing; under a 51% bar, she is barred only if her fault is greater than the defendant's (51% or more), so at 50% she recovers $50,000. The labels are easy to reverse, and an equal split is exactly where the two modified systems diverge.","subject":"torts","tags":["comparative-fault","defenses","damages"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Comparative negligence (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/comparative_negligence"}]},{"id":"v3-torts-primary-assumption-sports","prompt":"During a recreational pickup basketball game, a player is accidentally elbowed in the face by an opponent going for a rebound, breaking her nose. The contact was careless but within the ordinary rough play of the game, and no waiver was signed. She sues the opponent for negligence. Under the majority approach, who will most likely prevail?","choices":["The opponent, because co-participants owe no duty to avoid careless contact inherent in the game.","The player, but her recovery is reduced by her own share of fault for choosing to play the game.","The opponent, because by joining the game the player consented to any injury during play.","The player, because a participant whose careless play causes injury breaches a duty of care."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under implied primary assumption of risk, a co-participant in an active sport owes no duty to protect others from risks inherent in the activity and is liable only for intentional injury or reckless conduct totally outside the range of ordinary activity (e.g., Knight v. Jewett). An elbow during a rebound is an inherent risk, so there is no duty to breach. Comparative fault reduction applies to secondary assumption of risk, and participation is not consent to intentional or reckless injury.","subject":"torts","tags":["assumption-of-risk","defenses","sports","duty"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Assumption of risk (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/assumption_of_risk"}]},{"id":"v3-torts-independent-contractor-nondelegable","prompt":"A department store hires a reputable, licensed independent contractor to repair its escalator. The store does not direct how the work is done. The contractor negligently reassembles a step, and the next morning a customer riding the escalator is injured when the step collapses. The store had no reason to suspect the defect. Is the store liable to the customer?","choices":["No, because a hirer is not vicariously liable for an independent contractor's negligence.","Yes, because the store's duty to keep its premises safe for customers is nondelegable.","No, because the store used reasonable care in selecting a competent, licensed contractor.","Yes, because a store is strictly liable for all injuries suffered by customers on its premises."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Although a hirer generally is not liable for an independent contractor's negligence, important exceptions exist for nondelegable duties, including a possessor's duty to keep business premises reasonably safe for invitees. The store is therefore liable for the contractor's negligence even though the store itself was careful in hiring and supervising. Liability rests on the contractor's negligence imputed to the store, not on strict liability for every injury.","subject":"torts","tags":["vicarious-liability","independent-contractor","nondelegable-duty","premises-liability"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-torts-animals-domestic","prompt":"A dog that has never bitten anyone or shown any aggression suddenly bites a visitor without provocation. The owner had no reason to expect it and was not negligent in controlling the dog. No dog-bite statute applies. Under the traditional common-law rule, is the owner strictly liable for the bite?","choices":["No, because strict liability for a domestic animal requires knowledge of its dangerous propensity.","Yes, because owners of domestic animals are strictly liable for any bites that their animals inflict.","No, because strict liability applies to animals only when livestock trespass onto another's land.","Yes, because an unprovoked bite by itself establishes that the dog had a vicious propensity."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"At common law, owners of wild animals are strictly liable for harm from their dangerous characteristics, but owners of domestic animals are strictly liable only if they know or have reason to know of the particular animal's abnormally dangerous propensity. A first, unforeseeable bite therefore requires proof of negligence. Livestock trespass is a separate strict-liability category, and many states—such as California under Civil Code § 3342—impose statutory strict liability for dog bites.","subject":"torts","tags":["strict-liability","animals","domestic-animal"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Strict liability (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/strict_liability"}]},{"id":"v3-torts-design-defect-rad","prompt":"A riding lawn mower's blades keep spinning when the mower is put in reverse. An automatic blade-stop for reverse was feasible and inexpensive when the mower was made, would not impair its usefulness, and was used by competitors. A child is badly injured when the operator backs over him. The manufacturer shows that the mower was built exactly as designed and that the danger of reversing was obvious to adult operators. In a risk-utility jurisdiction, who will most likely prevail in the child's design-defect claim?","choices":["The manufacturer, because the mower was built exactly as designed, so no defect existed.","The manufacturer, because the obviousness of the danger precludes a design-defect claim.","The child, because an injured bystander may recover against a seller without proving a defect.","The child, because a feasible, inexpensive alternative design would have reduced the risk."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under the risk-utility test, a design is defective if the foreseeable risks could have been reduced or avoided by a reasonable alternative design whose omission makes the product not reasonably safe (Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 2(b)). A cheap, feasible blade-stop used by competitors is strong evidence of defect. Conformity to the intended design defeats only a manufacturing-defect claim, an obvious danger is a factor rather than a bar to a design claim, and bystanders must still prove a defect.","subject":"torts","tags":["products-liability","design-defect","risk-utility","reasonable-alternative-design"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Products liability (Cornell LII Wex)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/products_liability"}]},{"id":"v3-torts-privacy-intrusion","prompt":"A photographer hired by a tabloid flies a drone up to a private citizen's bedroom window and secretly takes photos of her inside her home. Before anything is published, the tabloid decides not to use the photos, and they are never shown to anyone. She sues the photographer for invasion of privacy. Can she recover?","choices":["No, because every invasion-of-privacy tort requires publicity given to private matters.","Yes, for false light, because the secret photos portrayed her in a highly offensive way.","Yes, for intrusion upon seclusion, which requires no publication of the photographs.","No, because the privacy torts protect only against commercial use of a person's likeness."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Intrusion upon seclusion imposes liability for an intentional intrusion, physical or otherwise, into another's private affairs or solitude that would be highly offensive to a reasonable person; publication is not an element. Public disclosure of private facts and false light both require publicity, and false light also requires a false or misleading portrayal. Appropriation is only one of the four privacy torts.","subject":"torts","tags":["privacy","intrusion-upon-seclusion","intentional-torts"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-torts-private-nuisance","prompt":"For twenty years, a concrete plant has operated in an area that was mostly vacant land. A developer then builds homes nearby, and a buyer purchases one, fully aware of the plant's dust and noise. The buyer sues the plant for private nuisance, and the plant argues that the buyer came to the nuisance. How will a court most likely treat the plant's argument?","choices":["As a complete defense, because the buyer knowingly assumed the risk of the dust and noise.","As irrelevant, because the order in which the uses began plays no role in nuisance law.","As one factor bearing on whether the interference is unreasonable, not as an absolute bar.","As a complete defense, because a use that predates its neighbors cannot become a nuisance."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A private nuisance is a substantial and unreasonable interference with another's use and enjoyment of land. Under the majority rule, the plaintiff's coming to the nuisance does not bar the claim but is a factor in deciding whether the interference is unreasonable (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 840D). Courts may also account for priority in fashioning the remedy, as in Spur Industries v. Del E. Webb, where the developer who came to the nuisance had to indemnify the enjoined feedlot.","subject":"torts","tags":["nuisance","private-nuisance","coming-to-the-nuisance"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-torts-interference-contract","prompt":"A software engineer has a two-year employment contract with Company A. Company B, knowing of the contract, offers her a much higher salary to quit immediately and join B. She accepts, breaching her contract with A. B used no fraud, threats, or other independently unlawful means. A sues B. Who will most likely prevail?","choices":["B, because offering better terms is privileged competition even when a contract is already in force.","B, because A's exclusive remedy is a breach-of-contract action against the engineer who quit.","A, but only if it proves B used independently wrongful means such as fraud or misrepresentation.","A, because B intentionally induced the breach of a contract it knew existed, without justification."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Intentional interference with contract requires a valid contract, the defendant's knowledge of it, intentional inducement of breach, and damages, absent justification (Restatement (Second) of Torts § 766). The competition privilege of § 768 protects luring away parties to contracts terminable at will or prospective relations, not inducing the breach of a fixed-term contract. Proof of independently wrongful conduct is required in many states for interference with prospective economic advantage, but not for inducing breach of an existing contract.","subject":"torts","tags":["interference-with-contract","economic-torts","business-torts"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-torts-collateral-source","prompt":"A plaintiff negligently injured in a car crash incurs $30,000 in medical bills, all paid by her own health insurer. At trial, the defendant offers evidence of the insurance payments to reduce the damages award. Under the traditional common-law rule, how should the court rule?","choices":["Exclude it, because benefits from a source independent of the tortfeasor do not reduce damages.","Admit it, because a plaintiff may not recover medical expenses she did not personally pay.","Admit it, because damages compensate the plaintiff, and the insurer has already done so.","Exclude it, because evidence of insurance is admissible only to show a party's ability to pay."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the collateral source rule, payments to the plaintiff from a source wholly independent of the tortfeasor, such as her own insurance, neither reduce the damages recoverable nor are admissible to show the plaintiff was compensated. The tortfeasor should not benefit from the plaintiff's foresight in buying insurance, and the insurer's subrogation rights usually prevent double recovery. Many states have modified the rule by statute or limit recovery to amounts actually paid (e.g., Howell v. Hamilton Meats in California); the liability-insurance rule (FRE 411) addresses a different issue.","subject":"torts","tags":["damages","collateral-source-rule"],"focus":["bar"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-evidence-victim-character-door","prompt":"A defendant charged in federal court with assault claims self-defense. In the defense case, a witness testifies that the alleged victim has a reputation for violence. In rebuttal, the prosecution offers a witness who will testify that the defendant has a reputation for violence. The defendant has not offered evidence of his own character. Is the rebuttal testimony admissible?","choices":["No, because the prosecution may rebut only with evidence of the victim's peaceful character.","Yes, because by attacking the victim's violent trait, the defendant opened the door to his own.","No, because the defendant has not offered any evidence of his own character for peacefulness.","Yes, because a claim of self-defense makes the defendant's character an essential element."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under FRE 404(a)(2)(B), if the court admits a defendant's evidence of the victim's pertinent trait, the prosecution may offer evidence to rebut it and may also offer evidence of the defendant's same trait. The prosecution's options are not limited to the victim's peacefulness, and the defendant need not have offered his own character first. Character is not an essential element of self-defense, so Rule 405(b) does not apply; the separate homicide first-aggressor provision, Rule 404(a)(2)(C), is not needed here.","subject":"evidence","tags":["character-evidence","404(a)(2)","self-defense","door-opening"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 404 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_404"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-habit","prompt":"In a wrongful-death action arising from a collision at a railroad crossing, there are no eyewitnesses. The plaintiff calls a coworker who rode with the decedent across that crossing hundreds of times; she will testify that he invariably came to a full stop and looked both ways there. Is the testimony admissible to show the decedent stopped on the day of the accident?","choices":["No, because it is character evidence offered to show that he acted in conformity with a trait.","Yes, as evidence of a habit, offered to show he acted in accordance with it on that occasion.","Yes, but only if an eyewitness first corroborates that the decedent stopped on that day.","No, because a habit may be proved only by reputation or opinion, not by observed instances."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FRE 406 admits evidence of a person's habit to prove that on a particular occasion he acted in accordance with it, regardless of corroboration or eyewitnesses. A habit is a regular, specific, semi-automatic response to a repeated situation—such as always stopping at a particular crossing—as distinguished from a general character trait like carefulness. Habit is typically proved by specific instances numerous enough to support the inference.","subject":"evidence","tags":["habit","406","character-evidence"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 406 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_406"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-rule-413","prompt":"In a federal sexual-assault prosecution, the government, after giving proper notice, offers testimony from a woman that the defendant sexually assaulted her five years earlier. He was never charged with that assault. The defendant objects that the evidence is offered only to show his propensity to commit sexual assault. How should the court rule?","choices":["Exclude it, because Rule 404(b) forbids using other acts to prove propensity in criminal cases.","Exclude it, because the earlier assault never resulted in a criminal charge or conviction.","Admit it, because Rule 413 allows prior sexual assaults for any relevant purpose, subject to Rule 403.","Admit it, but only to show a common scheme, with an instruction forbidding propensity use."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FRE 413 provides that in a criminal case where the defendant is accused of sexual assault, evidence that he committed any other sexual assault may be considered on any matter to which it is relevant, including propensity—an exception to Rule 404(b)'s bar. The prior act need not have been charged or resulted in a conviction, and the government must disclose the evidence at least 15 days before trial. Courts still screen such evidence under Rule 403; Rules 414 and 415 apply parallel principles to child molestation and civil cases.","subject":"evidence","tags":["character-evidence","413","propensity","sexual-assault"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 413 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_413"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-presumption-301","prompt":"In a federal-question civil case, the plaintiff proves she mailed a properly addressed, stamped letter to the defendant, which raises a presumption that the letter was received. The defendant then offers credible testimony from its mail clerk that the letter never arrived. Under Rule 301, what is the effect of the presumption?","choices":["It shifted only the burden of production; the jury decides receipt on all the evidence.","It shifted the burden of persuasion, so the defendant must prove nonreceipt by a preponderance.","It is conclusive once its basic facts are proved, so the denial of receipt must be disregarded.","It has vanished, and the proof of mailing may no longer be considered on the issue of receipt."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under FRE 301, unless a statute or rule provides otherwise, a presumption in a civil case imposes on the opponent only the burden of producing rebutting evidence; it does not shift the burden of persuasion, which stays with the party who had it originally. Once rebutting evidence is offered, the presumption itself drops out, but the basic facts—proper mailing—remain evidence from which the jury may infer receipt. Rule 302 applies state presumption law to state-law claims, and some states (e.g., California for presumptions affecting the burden of proof) shift the burden of persuasion.","subject":"evidence","tags":["presumptions","301","burden-of-proof"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 301 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_301"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-608b-extrinsic","prompt":"In a federal fraud trial, a defense witness testifies about the timeline of events. On cross-examination, the prosecutor asks in good faith whether she falsely claimed a college degree on a job application last year. She denies it. The prosecutor then offers the application and a registrar's testimony that she never graduated, solely to attack her truthfulness. How should the court rule?","choices":["Admit it, because the lie concerns honesty, and a dishonest act may be proved by any relevant evidence.","Admit it, because once the witness denied the lie, the prosecutor may impeach her by contradiction.","Exclude it, because prior dishonest acts are inadmissible for impeachment unless they led to a conviction.","Exclude it, because specific acts of untruthfulness may be asked about but not proved extrinsically."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FRE 608(b) allows the court to permit inquiry on cross-examination into specific instances probative of a witness's character for truthfulness, but extrinsic evidence may not be used to prove those instances; the examiner must take the witness's answer. Contradiction on a collateral matter is likewise not permitted by extrinsic evidence. The bar does not apply to extrinsic evidence offered for other purposes, such as bias or criminal convictions under Rule 609.","subject":"evidence","tags":["impeachment","608(b)","extrinsic-evidence","collateral"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 608 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_608"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-609-defendant-balancing","prompt":"A defendant on trial in federal court for armed robbery testifies in his own defense. The prosecution seeks to impeach him with his four-year-old felony conviction for armed robbery. Under the Federal Rules of Evidence, what standard governs admission of the prior conviction?","choices":["It is admissible only if its probative value outweighs its prejudicial effect to the defendant.","It is admissible unless its probative value is substantially outweighed by unfair prejudice.","It is admissible without balancing, because robbery is a crime of dishonesty or false statement.","It is inadmissible, because a conviction for the same crime as the one charged is always excluded."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"For a felony not involving a dishonest act or false statement, FRE 609(a)(1)(B) admits a conviction against a testifying criminal defendant only if its probative value outweighs its prejudicial effect to that defendant—a test more protective than Rule 403, which governs other witnesses under Rule 609(a)(1)(A). Robbery is not a crimen falsi offense under Rule 609(a)(2). Similarity to the charged crime weighs heavily against admission but is not an automatic bar.","subject":"evidence","tags":["impeachment","609(a)(1)","prior-conviction","criminal-defendant"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 609 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_609"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-bias-extrinsic","prompt":"In a federal assault trial, the prosecution's key eyewitness identifies the defendant. On cross-examination, defense counsel asks whether the witness is currently suing the defendant for $1 million over an unrelated business dispute; the witness denies it. Defense counsel then offers a certified copy of the complaint in that lawsuit. Is the complaint admissible?","choices":["No, because a witness's denial on cross-examination about a collateral matter must be accepted.","No, because Rule 608(b) bars extrinsic evidence of specific instances offered to impeach a witness.","Yes, but only if the lawsuit also shows that the witness has a character for untruthfulness.","Yes, because bias is not collateral and may be proved extrinsically after the witness denies it."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Bias, interest, or motive is always relevant to credibility, is never considered collateral, and may be proved by extrinsic evidence once a proper foundation has been laid (United States v. Abel). Rule 608(b)'s extrinsic-evidence bar applies only to specific instances offered to show character for truthfulness. A pending lawsuit against the defendant shows a financial motive to testify against him, and a certified public record is self-authenticating under Rule 902(4).","subject":"evidence","tags":["impeachment","bias","extrinsic-evidence"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"United States v. Abel, 469 U.S. 45 (1984) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/469/45/"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-identification","prompt":"Shortly after a robbery, the victim picked the defendant out of a lineup. At trial, the victim testifies and is cross-examined but says she can no longer remember the robber's face. The prosecution then calls the officer who ran the lineup to testify that the victim selected the defendant. Is the officer's testimony admissible?","choices":["No, because the victim's memory loss makes her unavailable, so only Rule 804 can apply.","No, because the officer lacks personal knowledge of who actually committed the robbery.","Yes, because a testifying witness's prior identification of a person is not hearsay.","Yes, but only if the victim's lineup identification was made under penalty of perjury."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FRE 801(d)(1)(C) excludes from hearsay a declarant-witness's prior statement identifying a person as someone she perceived earlier, if she testifies and is subject to cross-examination about it; anyone who witnessed the identification may relate it. Under United States v. Owens, a witness who cannot remember the underlying events is still subject to cross-examination for this rule and the Confrontation Clause. Rule 801(d)(1)(C) has never required that the identification be made under oath; the only oath requirement in Rule 801(d)(1) applied to prior inconsistent statements under former subdivision (A), and it was eliminated effective December 1, 2026.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay","801(d)(1)(C)","identification","confrontation"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"United States v. Owens, 484 U.S. 554 (1988) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/484/554/"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-coconspirator","prompt":"A defendant is charged with conspiring to distribute cocaine. The government offers two statements by an alleged co-conspirator: (1) during negotiations with an undercover agent, \"My partner will bring the product Friday,\" referring to the defendant; and (2) after his arrest, a confession to detectives naming the defendant as his supplier. Which statement is admissible against the defendant as a co-conspirator statement?","choices":["Both, because each was made by a member of the conspiracy and concerns the conspiracy's business.","Only the statement to the agent, because it was made during and in furtherance of the conspiracy.","Neither, because the court may not consider the statements themselves in finding a conspiracy existed.","Both, but only if the government first proves the conspiracy's existence beyond a reasonable doubt."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FRE 801(d)(2)(E) covers statements by a co-conspirator during and in furtherance of the conspiracy; a post-arrest confession to police is neither, and it would also be testimonial under Crawford. The judge decides the preliminary facts by a preponderance of the evidence under Rule 104(a) and may consider the statement itself, though the statement alone cannot establish the conspiracy (Bourjaily v. United States; Rule 801(d)(2)). The statement to an undercover agent, made to advance the deal, qualifies.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay","801(d)(2)(E)","co-conspirator","bourjaily"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Bourjaily v. United States, 483 U.S. 171 (1987) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/483/171/"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-502b-inadvertent","prompt":"In federal civil litigation, the defendant's legal team uses a reasonable, attorney-supervised privilege review, but one privileged memo is inadvertently included in a 50,000-page production. Two days after opposing counsel quotes the memo in a letter, defense counsel promptly demands its return. The plaintiff argues the privilege has been waived for the memo and for all communications on the same subject. Which is correct?","choices":["The memo's privilege is waived, because any disclosure to an adversary destroys confidentiality.","The privilege is waived for the memo and for all undisclosed communications on the same subject.","No waiver occurred, because only a disclosure made personally by the client can waive privilege.","No waiver occurred, because the disclosure was inadvertent and the holder took reasonable steps."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under FRE 502(b), an inadvertent disclosure in a federal proceeding does not waive the privilege if the holder took reasonable steps to prevent disclosure and promptly took reasonable steps to rectify the error, including following Civil Rule 26(b)(5)(B). Subject-matter waiver under Rule 502(a) applies only when a waiver is intentional and fairness requires considering the related communications together. A lawyer acting for the client can waive the privilege by voluntary disclosure, so the client's personal involvement is not required.","subject":"evidence","tags":["privilege","502","waiver","inadvertent-disclosure"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 502 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_502"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-against-interest-criminal","prompt":"At his federal robbery trial, the defendant calls a jail inmate to testify that the defendant's close friend told him, \"I'm the one who robbed the bank, not him.\" The friend invokes the Fifth Amendment and will not testify. No other evidence connects the friend to the robbery, and the friend had told several people he would do anything to keep the defendant out of prison. Is the inmate's testimony admissible?","choices":["Yes, because the friend's invocation of the privilege makes him unavailable, and the statement was against interest.","No, because a statement against penal interest may be offered only by the prosecution in a criminal case.","Probably not, because no corroborating circumstances clearly indicate the statement's trustworthiness.","Yes, because the corroboration requirement applies only to statements offered by the prosecution."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A declarant who invokes a privilege is unavailable, and a confession is against penal interest under FRE 804(b)(3)(A). But in a criminal case, a statement tending to expose the declarant to criminal liability must also be supported by corroborating circumstances that clearly indicate its trustworthiness, considering the totality of circumstances and any corroborating evidence (Rule 804(b)(3)(B), as amended in 2010 and 2024). That requirement applies whether the prosecution or the defense offers the statement, and here the lack of corroboration and the friend's motive to fabricate weigh against admission.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","804(b)(3)","statement-against-interest","corroboration"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 804 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_804"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-former-testimony","prompt":"A defendant's first federal arson trial ends in a hung jury. At that trial, a fire investigator testified for the prosecution and was cross-examined at length by defense counsel. Before the retrial, the investigator dies. At the retrial, the prosecution offers a transcript of the investigator's earlier testimony. The defense objects on hearsay and Confrontation Clause grounds. How should the court rule?","choices":["Exclude it, because testimonial statements are inadmissible unless the witness testifies at trial.","Admit it, because the defendant had an opportunity and similar motive to cross-examine the witness.","Admit it, but only if the court finds the defense's earlier cross-examination was effective.","Exclude it, because former testimony may be offered only against the party who called the witness."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FRE 804(b)(1) admits testimony given at a prior trial, hearing, or deposition when the declarant is unavailable and the party against whom it is offered had an opportunity and similar motive to develop it by cross-examination. Crawford v. Washington likewise permits testimonial hearsay from an unavailable witness whom the defendant had a prior opportunity to cross-examine. What matters is the opportunity, not how effectively it was used.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","804(b)(1)","former-testimony","confrontation"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 804 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_804"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-forfeiture","prompt":"A defendant charged with drug trafficking learns that a former associate has agreed to testify against him. To keep the associate from testifying, the defendant arranges to have him killed. At trial, the prosecution offers the associate's earlier statements to FBI agents describing the trafficking. The defense objects on hearsay and Confrontation Clause grounds. Are the statements admissible?","choices":["Yes, because he wrongfully caused the witness's unavailability, intending to prevent his testimony.","No, because the statements to agents were testimonial and never subjected to cross-examination.","Yes, but only if the defendant has first been convicted of the killing in a separate proceeding.","No, because forfeiture by wrongdoing excuses hearsay objections but not Confrontation rights."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"FRE 804(b)(6) admits a statement offered against a party who wrongfully caused, or acquiesced in wrongfully causing, the declarant's unavailability and did so intending that result. The same conduct forfeits the Confrontation Clause objection, but only when the defendant intended to prevent testimony (Giles v. California)—an intent clearly present here. The judge decides the preliminary facts by a preponderance; no separate conviction is required.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","804(b)(6)","forfeiture","confrontation"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Giles v. California, 554 U.S. 353 (2008) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/554/353/"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-crawford-davis","prompt":"Officers respond to a domestic-disturbance call and find the scene calm. While one officer keeps the husband in the kitchen, another questions the wife in the living room about what happened earlier and has her sign an affidavit describing the assault. At the husband's trial, the wife does not appear, and he never had a chance to cross-examine her. Even assuming a hearsay exception applies, is the affidavit admissible?","choices":["Yes, because statements made to police at the scene of a crime are categorically nontestimonial.","Yes, because the Confrontation Clause is satisfied when a firmly rooted hearsay exception applies.","No, because any statement made to a police officer is testimonial, regardless of its primary purpose.","No, because it is testimonial, given to prove past events rather than to meet an ongoing emergency."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Crawford v. Washington, testimonial hearsay from a witness who does not testify is admissible only if she is unavailable and the defendant had a prior opportunity to cross-examine. Statements are testimonial when the primary purpose of the interrogation is to establish past events for prosecution, and nontestimonial when it is to meet an ongoing emergency (Davis v. Washington; Hammon v. Indiana, decided together). The Ohio v. Roberts reliability test was overruled, and a 911 call describing an emergency in progress can be nontestimonial.","subject":"evidence","tags":["confrontation-clause","crawford","davis","testimonial-hearsay"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Davis v. Washington, 547 U.S. 813 (2006) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/547/813/"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-smith-arizona","prompt":"The lab analyst who tested drugs seized from a defendant has left the lab and does not testify. Instead, the prosecution calls a substitute analyst who never tested the substance. Relying on the absent analyst's notes and report, which he describes to the jury, the substitute testifies to his \"independent opinion\" that the substance was methamphetamine. The prosecution argues the notes were admitted only as the basis for his opinion. Under Smith v. Arizona (2024), how should the court analyze the defendant's Confrontation Clause objection?","choices":["The notes were offered for their truth, so if they are testimonial, admitting them violates the Clause.","There is no violation, because the absent analyst's statements were disclosed only as the basis for the opinion.","There is no violation, because the defendant may fully cross-examine the substitute analyst about his opinion.","There is a violation, because a substitute witness may never testify about testing performed by another."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In Smith v. Arizona, the Supreme Court held that when an expert conveys an absent analyst's statements in support of his opinion, and the statements support that opinion only if true, they are offered for their truth; labeling them basis evidence under Rule 703 or a state analog does not avoid the Confrontation Clause. Whether the statements are testimonial turns on their primary purpose, which the Court left for remand. Earlier cases held forensic certificates testimonial (Melendez-Diaz) and barred surrogate testimony relaying another analyst's certified findings (Bullcoming), but an expert may still offer a genuinely independent opinion without relaying testimonial statements.","subject":"evidence","tags":["confrontation-clause","forensic-evidence","smith-v-arizona","703"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Sixth Amendment (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/constitution/sixth_amendment"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-medical-diagnosis","prompt":"After a car crash, the plaintiff tells an emergency-room doctor, \"My neck has hurt ever since the other car slammed into me from behind while I was stopped at the light. The other driver was texting.\" In the plaintiff's federal civil suit, which parts of the statement are admissible under the exception for statements made for medical diagnosis or treatment?","choices":["The entire statement, because everything a patient tells a treating doctor is within the exception.","Only the description of neck pain, because the exception excludes how an injury was caused.","The neck pain and being hit from behind, but not the claim that the other driver was texting.","None of it, because the exception covers only statements made to a doctor retained to testify."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FRE 803(4) admits statements made for, and reasonably pertinent to, medical diagnosis or treatment that describe medical history, symptoms, or their inception or general cause. Being rear-ended explains the mechanism of injury and is pertinent; an assertion that the other driver was texting assigns fault and is not. The federal rule also covers statements to non-treating doctors consulted for diagnosis, unlike the older common-law approach.","subject":"evidence","tags":["hearsay-exception","803(4)","medical-diagnosis"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 803 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_803"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-spousal-privilege","prompt":"A husband is prosecuted in federal court for tax fraud. His wife is willing to testify that (1) she saw him shredding financial records in the garage when he did not know she was home, and (2) he privately told her during the marriage, \"I hid the money offshore.\" She did not participate in any crime. The husband objects to all of her testimony. How should the court rule?","choices":["He can bar all of her testimony, because a defendant may keep an adverse spouse off the stand.","She may testify about the shredding, but he can bar her from revealing his private statement.","She may testify to both, because the witness-spouse alone holds every marital privilege.","She may testify to neither, because she learned of both matters only because of the marriage."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Trammel v. United States, the spousal testimonial privilege belongs to the witness-spouse, who may choose to testify against the defendant. The separate marital communications privilege protects confidential communications between spouses during marriage and may be asserted by the communicating spouse, so the husband can bar disclosure of his private statement. Observations of acts not intended as communications—such as shredding he did not know she saw—are not privileged communications.","subject":"evidence","tags":["privilege","spousal-privilege","marital-communications","trammel"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Trammel v. United States, 445 U.S. 40 (1980) (Justia)","url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/445/40/"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-702-2023","prompt":"In a federal products case, the plaintiff's toxicologist uses a generally reliable method but draws a conclusion that the defendant argues goes well beyond what her data support. The trial judge declines to rule on that challenge, stating, \"Whether her conclusions follow from the data goes to weight, not admissibility; the jury can sort it out on cross.\" Under Rule 702 as amended in 2023, is the judge's approach correct?","choices":["No, because the proponent must show it is more likely than not that the opinion reliably applies the method.","Yes, because questions about the sufficiency of an expert's basis and application generally go to weight.","Yes, because once the method is shown reliable, Rule 702 leaves its application in the case to the jury.","No, because Rule 702 requires the expert's conclusion itself to be generally accepted in the relevant field."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"FRE 702, as amended effective December 1, 2023, requires the proponent to demonstrate to the court that it is more likely than not that the expert's testimony is based on sufficient facts or data, is the product of reliable methods, and reflects a reliable application of those methods to the case. The amendment rejected decisions treating sufficiency of basis and application as questions of weight for the jury. General acceptance is the Frye test; under Daubert it is only one factor.","subject":"evidence","tags":["expert-testimony","702","daubert","2023-amendment"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 702 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_702"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-704b","prompt":"A defendant prosecuted in federal court raises an insanity defense. His psychiatrist testifies that he has schizophrenia and describes its typical symptoms. Defense counsel then asks, \"In your opinion, was the defendant able to appreciate the wrongfulness of his acts at the time of the offense?\" The prosecution objects. How should the court rule?","choices":["Overrule it, because Rule 704 permits an expert opinion that embraces an ultimate issue in the case.","Sustain it, because a psychiatrist may not testify about a defendant's mental disease in a criminal case.","Sustain it, because an expert may not opine on whether the defendant had a mental state that is a defense.","Overrule it, because the limit on mental-state opinions applies only to experts called by the prosecution."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Although FRE 704(a) generally permits opinions embracing an ultimate issue, Rule 704(b) provides that in a criminal case an expert must not state an opinion about whether the defendant did or did not have a mental state or condition constituting an element of the crime or a defense; those matters are for the jury alone. The expert may describe the diagnosis and its characteristics. The rule applies to experts for either side.","subject":"evidence","tags":["expert-testimony","704(b)","insanity","mental-state"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 704 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_704"}]},{"id":"v3-evidence-410-plea","prompt":"A defendant charged with fraud meets with the federal prosecutor to negotiate a plea and says, \"I took the money, but I want a deal.\" He signs no waiver, and negotiations fail. At trial, he testifies that he never took the money. The prosecution seeks to introduce his statement. Is it admissible?","choices":["Yes, for impeachment only, because he testified at trial inconsistently with the earlier statement.","Yes, because Rule 410 protects only the plea offer itself, not admissions of fact during negotiations.","No, because Rule 410 excludes all statements made during plea talks with any law enforcement officer.","No, because statements in plea talks with a prosecutor are inadmissible against him, absent a waiver."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FRE 410(a)(4) bars, against the defendant who made it, a statement made during plea discussions with an attorney for the prosecuting authority that did not result in a guilty plea. The rule has no impeachment exception—unlike the Harris v. New York rule for Miranda violations—though a defendant may knowingly waive its protection (United States v. Mezzanatto). The rule covers discussions with the prosecutor, not statements to police officers acting on their own.","subject":"evidence","tags":["410","plea-discussions","policy-exclusions","impeachment"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Federal Rule of Evidence 410 (Cornell LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_410"}]},{"id":"crimlaw-larceny-v2","prompt":"Without asking, Dana takes a coworker's laptop from the coworker's desk, intending to use it overnight and return it the next morning. That night Dana decides to keep the laptop, and the next day she sells it. At common law, what is Dana's most serious crime as to the laptop?","choices":["Embezzlement, because Dana was already in possession of the laptop when her intent to steal formed.","No theft crime, because Dana lacked intent to permanently deprive at the moment of the taking.","Larceny, because her wrongful taking was a continuing trespass when the intent to steal arose.","Unauthorized use only, because a taking meant as a temporary borrowing cannot later become larceny."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Larceny requires a trespassory taking and carrying away of another's property with intent to permanently deprive, and the intent ordinarily must coincide with the taking. Under the continuing-trespass doctrine, when the original taking is itself wrongful (taking without permission, even just to borrow), the trespass continues, so a later-formed intent to steal supplies the needed concurrence. Embezzlement fails because Dana was never entrusted with the laptop; she got it by trespass. The doctrine would not help the prosecution if the original taking had been innocent, such as one made under an honest belief that borrowing was permitted.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["larceny","continuing-trespass","mens-rea"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"crimlaw-attempt-v2","prompt":"Intending to burn down a rival's warehouse, Omar pours gasoline along its wooden wall and strikes a match. A gust of wind blows out the match before anything ignites, and a security guard detains Omar. The jurisdiction defines arson as the malicious burning of any building. Is Omar guilty of attempted arson?","choices":["Yes, because Omar intended the burning and his conduct went well beyond mere preparation.","No, because the wind made completion factually impossible at the moment Omar acted.","Yes, but only if the gasoline charred or discolored the wall before the match went out.","No, because no part of the building burned, so the harm the statute targets never occurred."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Attempt requires the specific intent to commit the target crime plus conduct beyond mere preparation—a substantial step under the MPC or dangerous proximity at common law. Dousing the wall and striking a match satisfies either test. Failure caused by an outside force such as the wind is exactly what makes this an attempt rather than arson; factual impossibility is not a defense, and no charring or burning is required for attempt liability.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["attempt","inchoate","impossibility"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimpro-miranda-v2","prompt":"An officer stops Reyes for speeding on a highway. While checking Reyes's license at the driver's window, the officer asks, \"Is there anything illegal in the car?\" Reyes answers, \"Just some cocaine in the console.\" No Miranda warnings were given, and nothing about the stop went beyond a routine traffic detention. Should the court suppress the statement?","choices":["Yes, because the officer's question was reasonably likely to elicit an incriminating answer.","No, because an ordinary traffic stop is not by itself custody for Miranda purposes.","Yes, because Reyes was seized and was not free to leave when the officer asked the question.","No, because Miranda warnings are never required for suspects accused only of misdemeanors."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Miranda applies only to custodial interrogation. Under Berkemer v. McCarty, a routine traffic stop is brief and public, so a motorist is seized for Fourth Amendment purposes but is not in Miranda custody unless the stop escalates to restraints associated with formal arrest. The question was interrogation, but interrogation without custody needs no warnings. Berkemer also rejected any minor-offense exception: once a suspect is in custody, warnings are required whatever the offense.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["miranda","custody","traffic-stop"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Berkemer v. McCarty (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/468/420"}]},{"id":"crimpro-search-incident","prompt":"Police arrest Lena for driving on a suspended license, handcuff her, and lock her in the back of a patrol car. An officer then searches the passenger compartment of her lawfully parked car, which is not being impounded, and finds cocaine in a jacket on the back seat. No other exception applies. Was the search valid as a search incident to arrest?","choices":["Yes, because a custodial arrest of a recent occupant permits a search of the passenger compartment.","Yes, because the jacket was within Lena's reach at the moment the officers first arrested her.","No, because a search incident to a driver's arrest may never extend beyond the arrestee's person.","No, because Lena was secured and the car was unlikely to hold evidence of her license offense."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Arizona v. Gant, police may search a vehicle's passenger compartment incident to a recent occupant's arrest only if (1) the arrestee is unsecured and within reaching distance of the compartment at the time of the search, or (2) it is reasonable to believe the vehicle contains evidence of the offense of arrest. Lena was handcuffed in a patrol car, and a suspended-license offense produces no evidence likely to be in the car. Gant rejected the broad reading of New York v. Belton that any occupant's arrest authorizes a compartment search, and reachability is measured when the search occurs, not when the arrest is made.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["search-incident-to-arrest","gant","vehicle"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Arizona v. Gant (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/07-542"}]},{"id":"crimlaw-felony-murder","prompt":"During an armed robbery of a convenience store, Theo points a loaded pistol at the clerk. When the startled clerk bumps Theo's arm, the gun discharges and kills the clerk. Theo never meant to fire. In a jurisdiction with a traditional felony-murder rule, Theo's most serious homicide liability is:","choices":["Involuntary manslaughter, because the shooting was accidental and Theo lacked malice.","Murder, because the death occurred in the course of an inherently dangerous felony.","Murder, but only if Theo also consciously disregarded an extreme risk to human life.","No homicide, because the clerk's sudden movement was a superseding cause of death."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under the felony-murder rule, a killing committed during the commission or attempted commission of an inherently dangerous felony such as robbery is murder; the intent to commit the felony supplies the malice, so neither intent to kill nor separate depraved-heart recklessness must be proved. The clerk's flinch was a foreseeable reaction to having a gun pointed at him, so it is not a superseding cause. Involuntary manslaughter would fit only if no form of malice, including felony-murder malice, applied.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["felony-murder","homicide","causation"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"crimlaw-self-defense","prompt":"During a heated argument, an acquaintance reaches toward his jacket pocket. Jordan, who is anxious by temperament, honestly believes the man is reaching for a gun and fatally shoots him. The man was unarmed and was pulling out the phone he had been waving moments earlier; a reasonable person in Jordan's position would not have perceived a deadly threat. Is Jordan entitled to acquittal on the ground of self-defense?","choices":["No, because Jordan's belief that deadly force was needed was honest but not reasonable.","Yes, because self-defense turns on what Jordan actually believed at the moment he fired.","Yes, because Jordan had no duty to retreat before responding to a perceived deadly threat.","No, because deadly force is justified only if the attacker turns out to be actually armed."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Deadly force is justified only when the defendant honestly and reasonably believes it is necessary to prevent imminent death or serious bodily harm. A reasonable mistake—for example, about whether the other person is armed—still supports full self-defense, so actual armament is not required. Jordan's belief was honest but objectively unreasonable, so complete self-defense fails; many jurisdictions treat such 'imperfect' self-defense as reducing murder to voluntary manslaughter. The no-retreat rule does not matter when the force itself was not justified.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["self-defense","deadly-force","reasonableness"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimpro-counsel-v2","prompt":"After Malik is indicted for robbery and appointed counsel, police place him in a live lineup without notifying his lawyer, and Malik does not waive counsel. The victim picks him out. The lineup was fairly composed and not suggestive. At trial, may the prosecution introduce testimony that the victim identified Malik at the lineup?","choices":["Yes, because a lineup compels only physical, nontestimonial evidence from the accused person.","Yes, because the lineup was not unnecessarily suggestive and the identification was reliable.","No, because Malik was in custody and received no Miranda warnings before the lineup began.","No, because the post-indictment lineup was a critical stage at which Malik had a right to counsel."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under United States v. Wade and Gilbert v. California, the Sixth Amendment right to counsel attaches when adversary judicial proceedings begin, and a post-indictment live lineup is a critical stage. Testimony about an identification made at a lineup held without counsel or a valid waiver is excluded per se; an in-court identification may still be allowed if the prosecution shows by clear and convincing evidence that it has an independent source. The nontestimonial point defeats only a Fifth Amendment claim, suggestiveness is a separate due process test, and Miranda governs interrogation, not lineups.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["sixth-amendment","right-to-counsel","lineup","identification"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"United States v. Wade (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/388/218"}]},{"id":"crimpro-exclusionary","prompt":"Without a warrant or any applicable exception, officers enter Priya's house and find a rental receipt for a storage unit. Relying solely on the receipt, they obtain a warrant for the unit and seize stolen firearms there. Nothing suggests police would otherwise have learned of the unit. Priya moves to suppress the firearms. How should the court rule?","choices":["Deny the motion, because a judge issued the warrant under which they were seized.","Deny the motion, because only evidence found during the illegal entry is excluded.","Grant the motion, because the firearms are derivative of the unlawful entry.","Grant the motion, because tainted evidence may never be used for any purpose."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The fruit-of-the-poisonous-tree doctrine (Wong Sun v. United States) excludes evidence derived from an unconstitutional search unless the prosecution shows an independent source, inevitable discovery, or attenuation. The warrant rested entirely on the illegally obtained receipt, so it cannot cleanse the taint, and the facts rule out an independent source or inevitable discovery. The exclusionary rule is not absolute, however—illegally obtained evidence can, for example, be used to impeach a testifying defendant—so the 'never for any purpose' choice overstates the rule.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["exclusionary-rule","fruit-of-the-poisonous-tree"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimlaw-accomplice","prompt":"Ari tells his roommate Ben that he plans to burglarize a neighbor's house that night. Ben says nothing, does nothing to help or encourage Ari, and stays home watching TV. After the burglary, Ari gives Ben $200 from the proceeds, and Ben accepts it knowing its source. Is Ben guilty of burglary as an accomplice?","choices":["No, because Ben neither aided nor encouraged Ari before or during the burglary.","Yes, because Ben knew of the plan in advance and did nothing to warn the neighbor.","Yes, because by knowingly accepting the proceeds Ben ratified the burglary.","No, because an accomplice must be present at the scene when the crime occurs."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An accomplice must, with intent that the crime be committed, aid, abet, counsel, or encourage the principal. Mere knowledge, presence, or failure to prevent a crime is not enough absent a legal duty to act, and accepting a benefit afterward is not aid to the crime itself. Presence is not required—someone who supplies tools or a plan in advance can be an accomplice. Ben may be guilty of receiving stolen property for accepting the cash, but not of burglary.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["accomplice","aiding-abetting","mere-presence"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"crimlaw-burglary","prompt":"At 2 p.m., Carla walks through the open door of a neighbor's detached garage, intending to steal a bicycle stored there. She is caught before she touches the bike. In a jurisdiction with a typical modern burglary statute, is Carla guilty of burglary?","choices":["No, because she walked in through an open door, so there was no breaking.","No, because she never took the bicycle or carried it out of the garage.","Yes, because she unlawfully entered a structure with intent to steal inside.","Yes, because any entry onto another's land with a criminal purpose is burglary."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Common-law burglary required a breaking and entering of the dwelling of another at night with intent to commit a felony inside. Typical modern statutes drop the breaking, nighttime, and dwelling requirements and cover unlawful entry into (or remaining in) any building or structure with intent to commit a crime—usually including any theft—inside. The crime is complete on entry with that intent, so taking the bicycle is unnecessary. Entering land without entering a structure is trespass, not burglary.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["burglary","modern-statutes"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"crimlaw-insanity","prompt":"Victor, who has a severe mental illness, kills a neighbor after planning the killing for a week. A psychiatrist testifies that Victor understood he was killing a human being and knew the act was wrong and illegal, but because of his illness he could not resist an overwhelming urge to kill. In a jurisdiction using only the M'Naghten test, is Victor entitled to an insanity acquittal?","choices":["Yes, because his illness left him unable to conform his conduct to the law.","No, because Victor knew the nature of his act and knew that it was wrong.","Yes, because the killing was the product of his mental disease or defect.","No, because a defendant who plans a killing in advance cannot be insane."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"M'Naghten is a purely cognitive test: a defendant is insane only if, because of a mental disease or defect, he did not know the nature and quality of his act or did not know it was wrong. Victor understood both, so he fails the test. Volitional incapacity counts under the irresistible-impulse test and the MPC's 'substantial capacity to conform' prong, and the 'product' test is the Durham rule—neither applies here. Planning is evidence of sanity but is not a legal bar to the defense.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["insanity","mnaghten","defenses"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"crimpro-warrant-requirements","prompt":"A judge issues a warrant to search Hal's home based on a detailed affidavit listing specific stolen jewelry. The warrant form itself, however, describes the items to be seized only as \"evidence of crime,\" and it neither incorporates nor attaches the affidavit. Officers search, limiting themselves to the jewelry described in the affidavit. Did the warrant satisfy the Fourth Amendment?","choices":["Yes, because the affidavit described the jewelry with particularity and showed probable cause.","Yes, because the officers confined their search to the items listed in the affidavit.","No, because a warrant must be supported by sworn testimony from the theft victim.","No, because the warrant itself did not particularly describe the things to be seized."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Warrant Clause requires probable cause supported by oath or affirmation, issuance by a neutral and detached magistrate, and a particular description of the place to be searched and the things to be seized. In Groh v. Ramirez, a warrant that failed to describe the items was facially invalid even though the application did, because the application was neither incorporated by reference nor attached. Officers' restraint during the search does not cure a facially deficient warrant, and the supporting oath may come from any affiant, such as an officer, not necessarily the victim.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["search-warrant","particularity","fourth-amendment"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimpro-good-faith","prompt":"Officers obtain a search warrant from a neutral magistrate based on an affidavit describing a detailed informant's tip and some police corroboration. A reviewing court later holds that the affidavit fell short of probable cause, though the question was close. Nothing suggests the affiant lied or the magistrate abandoned her role. Is the evidence seized under the warrant admissible?","choices":["Yes, because the officers' reliance on the warrant was objectively reasonable.","No, because evidence seized under a warrant lacking probable cause is tainted fruit.","Yes, because exclusion never applies when the error was a judge's rather than police's.","No, unless the magistrate independently verified the informant's reliability first."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under United States v. Leon, evidence obtained in objectively reasonable reliance on a warrant later held invalid is admissible, because excluding it would not deter police misconduct. The exception has limits: it does not apply when the affiant knowingly or recklessly lied, the magistrate wholly abandoned the judicial role, the affidavit was so lacking in indicia of probable cause that reliance was unreasonable, or the warrant was facially deficient. A close probable-cause call falls within the exception, but the exception is not unconditional just because a judge erred.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["good-faith","exclusionary-rule","leon"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"United States v. Leon (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/468/897"}]},{"id":"crimpro-standing","prompt":"Nadia spends two hours in a friend's apartment, with the friend's permission, solely to package cocaine for sale. She has never been there before. Police enter without a warrant or exigency and seize cocaine Nadia brought with her. Can Nadia challenge the entry and search under the Fourth Amendment?","choices":["Yes, because she was legitimately on the premises with the tenant's permission when police entered.","No, because a brief visit for a business purpose gave her no reasonable expectation of privacy there.","Yes, because the cocaine that police seized during the warrantless entry belonged to her.","No, because only a person with a property interest in a home may challenge a search of it."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A defendant may invoke the exclusionary rule only if the search violated her own legitimate expectation of privacy (Rakas v. Illinois). In Minnesota v. Carter, short-term visitors present only for a commercial drug transaction lacked that expectation, while an overnight guest has one (Minnesota v. Olson)—so a property interest is not required. Rakas rejected 'legitimately on the premises' as the test, and owning the seized item does not by itself confer standing in the place searched (Rawlings v. Kentucky).","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["fourth-amendment","standing","expectation-of-privacy"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimlaw-conspiracy","prompt":"Kai and Lu agree to rob a jewelry store. The next day, Kai buys two ski masks for the job. A week later, both decide the plan is too risky and abandon it before going near the store. The jurisdiction follows the majority rule requiring an overt act for conspiracy. Are Kai and Lu guilty of conspiracy to commit robbery?","choices":["No, because neither of them ever came dangerously close to committing the robbery itself.","No, because both of them withdrew from the agreement before any robbery was attempted.","Yes, but only Kai, because he was the conspirator who personally committed the overt act.","Yes, because the agreement plus an overt act completed the crime before they abandoned it."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Conspiracy requires an agreement between two or more persons, intent to agree and to achieve the unlawful objective, and—in most jurisdictions—an overt act in furtherance by any one conspirator. Buying the masks is a sufficient overt act, and it binds every conspirator. Once the crime is complete, withdrawal does not undo it; withdrawal only limits liability for co-conspirators' later crimes (the MPC allows a renunciation defense only if the actor thwarts the crime). 'Dangerous proximity' is an attempt test, not a conspiracy element.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["conspiracy","overt-act","withdrawal"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimlaw-embezzlement","prompt":"Owen leaves his car with Mia, a mechanic, for a brake repair. Mia honestly intends to repair it, but three days later, facing debts, she decides to sell the car and keep the money, and does so. As to Owen's car, Mia's most likely crime is:","choices":["Larceny, because she took Owen's car with intent to permanently deprive him of it.","Larceny by trick, because Owen gave her the car in reliance on her promise to repair it.","Embezzlement, because she fraudulently converted property lawfully entrusted to her.","No crime against Owen, because he handed her the car and her intent formed later."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Embezzlement is the fraudulent conversion of another's property by one already in lawful possession of it. A bailee such as a repair shop has possession, not mere custody, so a later decision to sell is embezzlement rather than larceny, which requires a trespassory taking. Larceny by trick would require that Mia intended to steal when she obtained possession; her intent arose only after she lawfully received the car.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["embezzlement","larceny-by-trick","possession"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"crimlaw-merger","prompt":"Enraged during a bar argument, Sam beats Tom with his fists intending to cause serious injury, and Tom dies. The prosecutor charges felony murder, using felony aggravated battery as the predicate felony. In a jurisdiction that applies the merger doctrine, may Sam be convicted of felony murder?","choices":["Yes, because aggravated battery is an inherently dangerous felony that supports felony murder.","No, because the battery was the very assault that caused death and merges into the homicide.","Yes, because felony murder does not require the prosecution to prove any intent to kill.","No, because felony murder is limited to the traditional enumerated common-law felonies."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under the merger doctrine, an assaultive felony that is an integral part of the homicide itself—such as aggravated battery or assault with a deadly weapon—cannot serve as the felony-murder predicate; otherwise nearly every fatal assault would become murder without proof of malice. Many modern statutes extend felony murder beyond the traditional enumerated felonies to other inherently dangerous felonies, so that is not the reason the theory fails. Sam may still be convicted of murder on the theory that he intended to cause serious bodily harm.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["felony-murder","merger","assaultive-felony"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"crimlaw-duress","prompt":"Gang members seize Ines's young son and credibly threaten to kill him within the hour unless Ines kills a shopkeeper who has refused to pay them. With no realistic chance to contact police, Ines kills the shopkeeper. Charged with murder in a jurisdiction following the common-law majority rule, may Ines rely on duress as a complete defense?","choices":["Yes, because a threat to kill a close family member is a sufficient threat for duress.","Yes, because the threat was imminent and Ines had no reasonable way to escape it.","No, because duress is not a defense to the intentional killing of an innocent person.","No, because duress applies only to threats aimed at the defendant personally."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Duress excuses a crime committed because of a threat of imminent death or serious bodily harm to the defendant or another person, including a family member, that a person of reasonable firmness could not resist, when there is no reasonable escape. At common law and in most jurisdictions, however, duress is not a defense to intentional murder, on the principle that one may not take an innocent life to save one's own or another's. The MPC would let the jury consider duress even for murder, but the stem specifies the majority rule.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["duress","defenses","homicide"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimpro-plain-view","prompt":"Officers lawfully enter Ray's apartment without a warrant to look for a shooter after a bullet is fired through the floor. They notice expensive stereo equipment that seems out of place. Suspecting, but without probable cause, that it is stolen, an officer moves a turntable to read its serial number, and a radio check confirms it was stolen. Is the turntable admissible?","choices":["Yes, because the officers were lawfully present and the turntable was in plain view.","Yes, because moving an object a few inches is too slight an intrusion to be a search.","No, because plain view applies only to items that officers discover inadvertently.","No, because moving it to read the number was a new search unsupported by probable cause."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Plain view permits a warrantless seizure when officers are lawfully present, have lawful access to the object, and its incriminating character is immediately apparent, which means probable cause. In Arizona v. Hicks, moving stereo equipment to expose serial numbers was a separate search unrelated to the exigency, and it required probable cause, which mere suspicion does not supply. Horton v. California eliminated any inadvertence requirement, so that choice gives the wrong reason.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["plain-view","search","probable-cause"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Arizona v. Hicks (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/480/321"}]},{"id":"crimpro-third-party-consent","prompt":"Gail tells police that Dev assaulted her at \"our apartment,\" says she keeps clothes there, and lets officers in with her key while Dev is asleep inside. In fact, Gail had moved out a month earlier, but nothing she said or did suggested that to the officers. They see drugs in plain view. Was the entry valid?","choices":["Yes, because the officers reasonably believed Gail had common authority over the apartment.","No, because Gail had moved out and lacked actual authority to consent to the entry.","No, because Dev was present in the apartment and did not personally consent to the entry.","Yes, because anyone holding a key to a residence may validly consent to a police entry."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Consent may be given by anyone with common authority over the premises, and under Illinois v. Rodriguez an entry is also valid if officers reasonably, though mistakenly, believe the consenting person has that authority. Georgia v. Randolph invalidates a co-tenant's consent only over the express objection of a physically present occupant; a sleeping occupant has not objected. Holding a key alone does not establish authority—the question is whether the officers' belief was reasonable on all the facts.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["consent","apparent-authority","third-party"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimlaw-voluntary-manslaughter-provocation","prompt":"Hearing screams, Rosa rushes outside to find her teenage son bleeding on the ground and Victor, who has just beaten him, standing over him laughing. Victor is no longer attacking. In a sudden rage, Rosa grabs a bat and strikes Victor in the head, intending to kill him, and he dies. Rosa's most serious homicide liability is most likely:","choices":["Murder, because Rosa intended to kill Victor at the very moment she struck him with the bat.","Involuntary manslaughter, because she acted on a sudden rage without any premeditation.","No crime, because Rosa used the force to defend a member of her family from Victor.","Voluntary manslaughter, because she killed in the heat of passion after adequate provocation."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An intentional killing is reduced from murder to voluntary manslaughter when the defendant acted in the heat of passion upon adequate provocation that would cause a reasonable person to lose self-control, without a reasonable opportunity to cool off. Witnessing a serious assault on a close family member is a classic adequate provocation, and Rosa struck at once. Intent to kill does not prevent mitigation, and involuntary manslaughter is for unintentional killings. Defense of others fails because the attack had ended, so there was no imminent threat to her son.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["homicide","voluntary-manslaughter","provocation"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimlaw-false-pretenses-title","prompt":"Pat buys a used car from a dealer and pays with a personal check, knowing the account has no funds and never intending to pay. Relying on the check, the dealer signs over title and lets Pat drive away. Pat's most accurate theft crime is:","choices":["False pretenses, because Pat obtained title to the car by a knowing misrepresentation.","Larceny by trick, because Pat obtained the car by a deliberate misrepresentation.","Embezzlement, because Pat was in lawful possession of the car when converting it.","No crime, because a check is a promise to pay in the future, not a statement of fact."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"False pretenses is obtaining title to another's property by a knowing false representation of a material past or present fact, made with intent to defraud. Larceny by trick involves similar deceit but transfers only possession; here the dealer intended to pass title. Writing a check impliedly represents that funds are available now—a present fact—so the 'mere promise' argument fails.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["theft","false-pretenses","title"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"crimlaw-transferred-intent","prompt":"Intending to kill Abe, Cy fires a pistol at him across a parking lot. The bullet misses Abe and kills Bea, whom Cy had not noticed. As to Bea's death, Cy is most likely guilty of:","choices":["Involuntary manslaughter, because the shot negligently endangered Bea.","Murder, because Cy's intent to kill Abe transfers to Bea.","No homicide, because Cy did not intend any harm at all to Bea.","Murder, but only if Cy could foresee that Bea was in the line of fire."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under the transferred-intent doctrine, when a defendant intends to harm one person but the act harms another, the intent transfers to the actual victim, so Cy is guilty of murdering Bea. Foreseeability of the actual victim's presence is not required. Cy may also be guilty of the attempted murder of Abe, because transferred intent does not erase the crime against the intended victim.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["homicide","transferred-intent"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"crimlaw-attempt-factual-impossibility","prompt":"On a crowded subway, Flynn slips his hand into a passenger's coat pocket intending to steal a wallet. The pocket is empty. Flynn is arrested and charged with attempted larceny. What is the best outcome?","choices":["Not guilty, because no wallet existed, so the intended theft was impossible.","Not guilty, because his mistake about the pocket made it a legal impossibility.","Guilty, because the act would be larceny if the facts were as Flynn believed.","Guilty of larceny, because reaching into the pocket was a trespassory taking."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Attempt requires specific intent plus a substantial step or dangerous proximity. Factual impossibility—where the goal is criminal but an unknown fact, such as an empty pocket, makes completion impossible—is not a defense, and the MPC judges the conduct by the circumstances as the defendant believed them to be. True legal impossibility exists only when the intended act is not a crime at all. Completed larceny fails because nothing was taken.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["attempt","impossibility","inchoate"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"crimlaw-depraved-heart-malice","prompt":"Angry at a former friend, Drew fires several shots into the friend's house at night to \"scare him,\" knowing from the lights and voices that people are inside. One bullet kills a guest. Drew did not intend to hit anyone. Drew's most serious homicide liability is:","choices":["Involuntary manslaughter, because Drew acted recklessly but did not intend to hit anyone.","Voluntary manslaughter, because Drew acted in anger and without any premeditation.","No homicide, because an intent merely to frighten the friend cannot transfer to a guest.","Murder, because firing into an occupied home shows extreme indifference to human life."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Malice includes depraved-heart (implied) malice: conduct showing conscious disregard of an unjustifiably high risk to human life and extreme indifference to its value. Firing into a house known to be occupied is the textbook example, so the killing is murder even without intent to kill or injure. Involuntary manslaughter covers lesser recklessness or criminal negligence, and voluntary manslaughter requires adequate provocation, not mere anger.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["homicide","malice","depraved-heart"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"crimpro-terry-stop-frisk","prompt":"A store guard reports that a man in a red hoodie just left with an unpaid sweater. An officer stops Lee, who matches the description, a block away. Nothing suggests Lee is armed; he is calm and cooperative. As a matter of routine, the officer pats Lee down and finds a handgun. Should the court suppress the gun?","choices":["Yes, because the officer lacked reasonable suspicion that Lee was armed and dangerous.","No, because a lawful investigatory stop automatically authorizes a protective pat-down.","No, because the discovery of a gun confirms that the protective frisk was justified.","Yes, because an officer may frisk a suspect only after placing the suspect under arrest."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Terry v. Ohio, a stop requires reasonable suspicion of criminal activity, while a frisk requires separate reasonable suspicion that the person is armed and dangerous. Suspected shoplifting of a sweater, with nothing else suggesting a weapon, supports the stop but not the frisk. A frisk is judged by what the officer knew beforehand, not by what it uncovers, and it is a pre-arrest protective measure, so an arrest is not a prerequisite.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["terry","stop-and-frisk","reasonable-suspicion"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Terry v. Ohio (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/392/1"}]},{"id":"crimpro-automobile-exception-containers","prompt":"Officers have probable cause to believe heroin is somewhere in Quinn's car, but no warrant. They stop the car on a highway, search the glove box, and then open a locked briefcase in the trunk, where they find heroin. Was the search of the briefcase valid?","choices":["No, because a locked container found in a car requires a separate warrant before opening.","No, because the automobile exception covers the passenger compartment but not the trunk.","Yes, because probable cause to search the car covers containers that could hold heroin.","Yes, but only because the officers could have impounded and inventoried the car anyway."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under the automobile exception, probable cause to believe a vehicle contains contraband permits a warrantless search of every part of the vehicle, including the trunk and closed or locked containers, where the object of the search might be found (United States v. Ross; California v. Acevedo). The passenger-compartment limit belongs to search incident to arrest, not to the automobile exception. The search rests directly on probable cause, so no inventory or inevitable-discovery theory is needed.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["automobile-exception","containers","probable-cause"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"crimpro-miranda-invocation-counsel","prompt":"In custody and after Miranda warnings, Uma says, \"I want a lawyer,\" and questioning stops. Uma stays in custody. Two hours later, a different detective who does not know of the request gives fresh warnings; Uma waives her rights and confesses to an unrelated burglary. Uma never asked to speak with police. Is the confession admissible in the prosecution's case-in-chief?","choices":["Yes, because the confession followed fresh Miranda warnings and a knowing, voluntary waiver.","No, because police may not reinitiate questioning of a suspect in custody who invoked counsel.","Yes, because the second interrogation concerned a different offense from the first one.","No, because any statement taken after an invocation is inadmissible for every purpose at trial."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Edwards v. Arizona, once a suspect in custody unambiguously requests counsel, police may not initiate further interrogation until counsel is present unless the suspect herself reinitiates communication; a waiver after police-initiated questioning is presumed invalid. Arizona v. Roberson extends the bar to questioning about unrelated crimes, and the second detective's ignorance of the request does not matter. Voluntary statements obtained in violation of Edwards may still be used to impeach a testifying defendant, so 'every purpose' overstates the rule.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["miranda","edwards","right-to-counsel","interrogation"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Edwards v. Arizona (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/451/477"}]},{"id":"crimpro-sixth-amendment-offense-specific","prompt":"Wes is indicted for burglary and appointed counsel. While Wes is out on bail, detectives approach him about an unrelated robbery, give Miranda warnings, and obtain a valid waiver. They do not contact his lawyer. Wes confesses to the robbery. Is the confession admissible at the robbery trial?","choices":["No, because once counsel is appointed on any charge, police may not initiate questioning.","Yes, because the Sixth Amendment right to counsel is offense-specific.","No, because police who know a suspect has counsel must contact the lawyer first.","Yes, because the Sixth Amendment right to counsel does not attach until trial."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Sixth Amendment right to counsel attaches at the start of formal adversary proceedings and is offense-specific (McNeil v. Wisconsin; Texas v. Cobb): it covers the charged offense and any offense that is the same under the Blockburger test. The robbery is uncharged and unrelated, so only the Fifth Amendment Miranda rules apply, and Wes validly waived them. Since Montejo v. Louisiana (2009), police may even initiate questioning about a charged offense if the defendant validly waives counsel. A lawyer's ethical no-contact rule does not create a constitutional exclusionary remedy, and attachment occurs at indictment or arraignment, not trial.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["sixth-amendment","offense-specific","interrogation"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"crimpro-double-jeopardy-attachment-jury","prompt":"Ten jurors have been selected for Zoe's assault trial, and none has been sworn, when the prosecutor obtains a dismissal because a key witness is unavailable. Two months later, the state refiles the same charge. Zoe moves to dismiss on double jeopardy grounds. How should the court rule?","choices":["Grant the motion, because jeopardy attached once jury selection began in the case.","Grant the motion, because jeopardy attached when Zoe was arraigned and pleaded.","Deny the motion, because jeopardy attaches only when the first witness is sworn.","Deny the motion, because jeopardy had not attached when the case was dismissed."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In a jury trial, jeopardy attaches when the jury is empaneled and sworn (Crist v. Bretz); in a bench trial, it attaches when the first witness is sworn. Because no juror had been sworn, jeopardy never attached and retrial is not barred. The 'first witness' rule is the bench-trial rule, so that choice reaches the right result for the wrong reason. Had the jury been sworn, a dismissal or mistrial over Zoe's objection would bar retrial absent manifest necessity.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["double-jeopardy","attachment","trial-rights"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-mpc-knowingly-elements","prompt":"A statute in a Model Penal Code jurisdiction provides: \"A person who knowingly possesses a firearm within 1,000 feet of a school is guilty of a felony.\" Eli knowingly carries a pistol on a street he has never visited. He does not realize a school is 800 feet away, though a reasonable person would have noticed the school's signs. Is Eli guilty?","choices":["No, because \"knowingly\" applies to the school-proximity element as well as possession.","Yes, because a reasonable person in Eli's position would have known a school was nearby.","Yes, because the statute's mental-state term modifies only the possession of the firearm.","Yes, because school proximity is an attendant circumstance for which liability is strict."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under MPC § 2.02(4), when a statute prescribes a culpability level without distinguishing among elements, that level applies to every material element unless a contrary purpose plainly appears. \"Knowingly\" therefore governs both possession and the attendant circumstance of being within 1,000 feet of a school, and Eli was not aware of the latter. That a reasonable person would have known shows only negligence, which does not satisfy knowledge.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["mens-rea","mpc","culpability"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-statutory-rape-age-mistake","prompt":"Nineteen-year-old Ty has consensual intercourse with Ivy, who is 15. Ivy told Ty she was 18, showed him a realistic fake ID, and looked older, so Ty's belief was reasonable. The jurisdiction's statutory rape law sets the age of consent at 16 and is silent about mistake. Under the majority approach, does Ty have a defense?","choices":["Yes, because a reasonable mistake about the victim's age negates the required intent.","Yes, because Ivy actively deceived Ty about her age with false identification.","No, because statutory rape is a strict-liability offense as to the victim's age.","No, because a mistake of fact is never a defense to a sex offense of any kind."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Most jurisdictions treat statutory rape as a strict-liability crime with respect to the victim's age, so even a reasonable, victim-induced mistake about age is no defense. A minority of jurisdictions (and the MPC for some offenses involving older minors) allow a reasonable-mistake defense, but the question asks for the majority rule. The 'never a defense to any sex offense' answer overstates the law: in forcible-rape prosecutions, for example, many jurisdictions recognize that a reasonable belief in consent negates the required mental state.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["strict-liability","statutory-rape","mistake-of-fact"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-mistake-of-law-reliance","prompt":"Before opening a fireworks stand, Gus asks his private attorney whether selling a certain type of firework is legal. The attorney mistakenly says it is. Gus sells them and is charged under a statute that prohibits selling those fireworks and requires only that the seller know what he is selling. No court or agency had ever said the sales were lawful. Is Gus's reliance on his lawyer a defense?","choices":["Yes, because his good-faith reliance on counsel shows he lacked any criminal intent.","Yes, because his mistake about the law was reasonable in light of his lawyer's advice.","No, because reliance on a private attorney's erroneous legal advice is not a defense.","No, because a mistake of law is never a defense to a crime under any circumstances."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Ignorance or mistake of law is generally no defense, even if reasonable. A narrow exception covers reasonable reliance on an official statement of the law—a statute, a judicial decision, or an official interpretation by the agency responsible for the law—later found erroneous; a private lawyer's advice does not qualify. Advice of counsel matters only when the statute itself requires knowledge of illegality (as some 'willful' tax crimes do), which this one does not. The 'never' choice is wrong because of the official-reliance exception.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["mistake-of-law","defenses","official-reliance"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-mistake-of-fact-specific-intent","prompt":"Leaving a crowded restaurant, Hana takes a black umbrella from the rack, honestly believing it is her own. It belongs to another diner, and a careful person would have noticed that Hana's umbrella had a different handle. The owner stops Hana at the door. Is Hana guilty of larceny?","choices":["No, because an honest mistake negates the intent to steal, even if unreasonable.","Yes, because her mistake was unreasonable, so it cannot negate the required intent.","Yes, because she intentionally carried away property that in fact belonged to another.","No, because the owner left the umbrella unattended in a place open to the public."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Larceny is a specific-intent crime requiring intent to permanently deprive another of his property. Any honest mistake of fact that negates that intent—here, a belief that the umbrella was hers—is a defense, whether or not it was reasonable. The reasonableness requirement applies to mistakes offered against general-intent crimes. Leaving property on a coat rack does not make it abandoned.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["mistake-of-fact","larceny","specific-intent"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-causation-medical-negligence","prompt":"Ned stabs Vic in the abdomen during a fight. At the hospital, a surgeon negligently fails to notice a nicked artery, and Vic bleeds to death. With proper care, Vic would have survived. Is Ned the legal cause of Vic's death?","choices":["No, because the surgeon's negligence, not the stab wound, was the immediate cause of death.","No, because a third party's negligent intervening act always breaks the chain of causation.","Yes, but only if the stab wound would have been fatal even with proper medical treatment.","Yes, because ordinary medical negligence is a foreseeable result of inflicting the wound."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A defendant is the legal (proximate) cause of death if his act was an actual cause and the result is not so remote or extraordinary that liability would be unfair. Negligent medical treatment of a wound the defendant inflicted is considered foreseeable and does not supersede, so Ned remains liable; only grossly negligent or reckless treatment is sometimes treated as superseding. The wound need not have been fatal without treatment—but for the stabbing, Vic would never have needed surgery.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["causation","intervening-cause","homicide"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-felony-murder-agency","prompt":"Al and Bo rob a liquor store at gunpoint. The owner pulls a shotgun and, acting lawfully, kills Bo. Al escapes and is charged with felony murder for Bo's death. In a jurisdiction that follows the majority agency approach to felony murder, is Al guilty of murder?","choices":["Yes, because Bo's death was a foreseeable result of the armed robbery Al committed.","No, because the fatal shot was fired by the owner rather than by Al or a co-felon.","Yes, because felony murder covers any death occurring during a dangerous felony.","No, because Al did not personally intend that anyone be killed in the robbery."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under the majority agency theory, felony-murder liability extends only to killings committed by the defendant or a co-felon in furtherance of the felony; a killing by a victim, police officer, or other non-felon is not attributed to the felons. Under the minority proximate-cause theory, felons may be liable for foreseeable deaths resulting from the felony, though Commonwealth v. Redline refused liability where a co-felon was justifiably killed. Lack of intent to kill is never why felony murder fails, because the rule dispenses with that intent.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["felony-murder","agency-theory","redline"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-involuntary-manslaughter-omission","prompt":"Mae's 4-year-old daughter has a high fever and becomes lethargic and unresponsive over three days. Mae, who is busy and assumes the illness will pass, does not seek medical care, although any reasonable parent would recognize a grave risk. The child dies of a treatable infection. Mae never actually realized the child might die. Mae's most likely liability at common law is:","choices":["Involuntary manslaughter, for a criminally negligent failure to perform a parental duty.","No homicide, because a failure to act cannot be the basis of homicide liability.","Depraved-heart murder, because her omission created a very high risk of the child's death.","No homicide, because Mae never consciously disregarded a known risk to her daughter."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An omission supports criminal liability when the defendant has a legal duty to act, and parents have a duty to obtain necessary medical care for their children. A death caused by gross (criminal) negligence—a gross deviation from reasonable care—is involuntary manslaughter at common law. Depraved-heart murder requires actual awareness and conscious disregard of an extreme risk to life, which Mae lacked. Lack of awareness defeats murder (and MPC manslaughter, which requires recklessness) but not a criminal-negligence homicide.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["involuntary-manslaughter","omission","criminal-negligence"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-attempt-mpc-substantial-step","prompt":"Planning to rob a bank, Finn spends a week watching its entrances, buys a ski mask and a pistol, and on the chosen day parks across the street with the mask and gun on the seat, waiting for the armored car to leave. Police, tipped off, arrest him in his car. In a jurisdiction following the Model Penal Code, is Finn guilty of attempted robbery?","choices":["No, because Finn never came dangerously close to actually completing the robbery.","Yes, because his conduct was a substantial step strongly corroborating his purpose.","No, because his acts were mere preparation until he entered the bank to demand money.","Yes, because any act done with intent to commit a crime is enough for attempt liability."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under MPC § 5.01, attempt requires purpose plus a substantial step that strongly corroborates the actor's criminal purpose; the Code's examples include reconnoitering the place contemplated for the crime, lying in wait, and possessing materials to be used in the crime at or near the place of its commission. Finn did all three. The common-law dangerous-proximity test asks how close the actor came to completion and might not be met here, but the MPC focuses on what the actor has already done. Not every act with criminal intent suffices—mere preparation is not an attempt.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["attempt","mpc","substantial-step"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-solicitation","prompt":"Hugo offers his neighbor $5,000 to kill Hugo's business partner. The neighbor immediately refuses and reports the offer to police. Nothing further happens. What is Hugo's most serious crime?","choices":["Attempted murder, because offering payment was a substantial step toward the killing.","Conspiracy to commit murder, because Hugo proposed an agreement to kill his partner.","No crime, because the neighbor refused and no step toward the killing was ever taken.","Solicitation of murder, because he asked another to kill, intending the killing."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Solicitation is complete when a person asks, encourages, or commands another to commit a crime with intent that the crime be committed; the other person's refusal is irrelevant. Conspiracy requires an actual agreement—under the traditional bilateral approach, genuine agreement by at least two parties—which never occurred. Most jurisdictions hold that a bare solicitation, without more, is not an attempt.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["solicitation","inchoate"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-pinkerton","prompt":"Cole and Dee agree to run a drug-selling operation together. Without Dee's knowledge, Cole robs a customer at gunpoint to collect a drug debt owed to the operation. In a jurisdiction that follows the Pinkerton rule, is Dee guilty of the robbery?","choices":["No, because Dee did not aid, encourage, or even know about the robbery that Cole committed.","No, because a conspirator is liable only for the specific crime the parties agreed to commit.","Yes, because it was committed in furtherance of the conspiracy and was reasonably foreseeable.","Yes, because a conspirator is liable for every crime a partner commits while it continues."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Pinkerton v. United States, each conspirator is liable for crimes committed by co-conspirators that are within the scope of the conspiracy, in furtherance of it, and reasonably foreseeable as a natural consequence of the agreement. Using force to collect a drug debt owed to the enterprise meets that test even though Dee neither knew of nor assisted it. Pinkerton liability is broader than accomplice liability but not unlimited—crimes outside the conspiracy's scope or unforeseeable ones are excluded. The MPC and many states reject Pinkerton and require accomplice-level participation.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["conspiracy","pinkerton","vicarious-liability"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Pinkerton v. United States (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/328/640"}]},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-conspiracy-bilateral","prompt":"Ross proposes a burglary to Sid, who is secretly an undercover officer. Sid pretends to agree, and Ross buys a crowbar for the job. Ross is arrested before the burglary. The jurisdiction follows the traditional common-law bilateral approach to conspiracy. Is Ross guilty of conspiracy?","choices":["Yes, because Ross himself agreed to and fully intended the commission of the burglary.","Yes, because Ross committed an overt act by buying a crowbar to use in the burglary.","No, because there was no genuine agreement between two people who intended the crime.","No, because Sid's participation amounts to entrapment of Ross as a matter of law."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The traditional bilateral approach requires an actual agreement between at least two guilty minds; when the only other party is an undercover officer feigning agreement, there is no conspiracy. The MPC's unilateral approach asks only whether the defendant agreed, and would convict Ross. An overt act cannot substitute for the missing agreement. Entrapment is not shown merely because police participated—Ross proposed the crime himself. Ross may still be guilty of solicitation.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["conspiracy","bilateral","unilateral"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-withdrawal-accomplice","prompt":"Gia agrees with Hal and Ivo to rob an armored car and gives them a map of its route. After the jurisdiction's overt-act requirement is met, Gia calls both men, tells them she is out, and asks for the map back. They refuse, Gia does nothing further, and the two carry out the robbery using the map. Gia is charged with conspiracy and with robbery. Under the common-law approach, which is correct?","choices":["Gia is guilty of conspiracy only, because her notice to both partners cut off liability for the robbery.","Gia is guilty of both, because she never neutralized the help the map gave the robbers.","Gia is guilty of neither, because she withdrew by telling both partners before the robbery.","Gia is guilty of robbery only, because withdrawal before the crime is a defense to conspiracy."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Conspiracy is complete once the agreement and an overt act occur, so later withdrawal is no defense to the conspiracy charge itself (the MPC permits renunciation only if the actor thwarts the crime). Timely notice to all co-conspirators does cut off Pinkerton liability for their later crimes. But Gia also aided the robbery by supplying the map, and an accomplice withdraws effectively only by neutralizing her assistance—retrieving the map, warning police, or otherwise making a proper effort to prevent the crime. Because the robbers used her map, Gia is liable for the robbery as an accomplice.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["conspiracy","withdrawal","accomplice"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-robbery-vs-extortion","prompt":"Jin walks into a shop and tells the owner, \"Give me $500 now, or next week I'll break your son's legs.\" Frightened, she hands over the money. Jin displays no weapon and threatens no immediate harm. Jin's crime is best described as:","choices":["Robbery, because Jin took money from the owner's presence by intimidation.","Robbery, because a threat of serious injury to a family member suffices.","False pretenses, because the owner passed title to the money due to Jin's words.","Extortion, because the money was obtained by a threat of future harm."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Robbery is larceny from the person or presence of the victim by force or by intimidation, meaning a threat of immediate harm. When property is obtained by a threat of future harm, the crime is extortion (blackmail) under modern statutes rather than robbery; common-law extortion was limited to corrupt collection of fees by public officials, which is why statutes now fill the gap. A threat against a family member can support robbery only if it threatens immediate harm. False pretenses requires a misrepresentation of fact, not a threat.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["robbery","extortion","theft"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-initial-aggressor-escalation","prompt":"Kurt shoves Leo during an argument in a narrow alley. Leo responds by pulling a knife and lunging at Kurt. Kurt, backed against a wall with no safe way to retreat, draws a pistol and shoots Leo dead. Charged with murder, Kurt claims self-defense. Under the majority rule, may Kurt claim self-defense?","choices":["Yes, because Leo's escalation to deadly force restored Kurt's right to defend himself.","No, because an initial aggressor regains self-defense only by withdrawing and saying so.","No, because Kurt started the fight and so had a duty to retreat before using deadly force.","Yes, because a person attacked with a knife never has a duty to retreat in any jurisdiction."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An initial aggressor generally may not claim self-defense, but regains the right in two situations: by withdrawing in good faith and communicating the withdrawal, or when the victim of non-deadly aggression responds with deadly force, escalating the encounter. Kurt's shove was non-deadly, and Leo's knife attack was a disproportionate escalation, so Kurt could respond with deadly force. Retreat was impossible here anyway, and even jurisdictions that require retreat do so only when it can be done in complete safety. The 'never in any jurisdiction' choice is wrong because a minority of jurisdictions do impose a duty to retreat.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["self-defense","initial-aggressor","duty-to-retreat"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-voluntary-intoxication","prompt":"After drinking heavily of his own accord, Max walks out of a bar wearing another patron's expensive jacket, which in his drunken state he mistakes for his own. On the way out he also punches a bouncer. He is charged with larceny of the jacket and battery of the bouncer. How does Max's voluntary intoxication bear on the charges?","choices":["It is a defense to both, because Max lacked the capacity to form any intent at all.","It can negate the intent required for larceny but is no defense to the battery.","It is no defense to either charge, because Max chose to become intoxicated.","It can reduce each charge to a lesser offense but cannot fully defeat either one."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Voluntary intoxication is a defense only to crimes requiring a specific intent (under the MPC, purpose or knowledge), and only if it actually prevented formation of that intent. Larceny requires intent to permanently deprive another of property, which Max lacked because he believed the jacket was his. Battery is a general-intent crime, and voluntary intoxication is no defense to it. Involuntary intoxication, by contrast, can excuse any crime if it causes an insanity-like incapacity.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["intoxication","specific-intent","defenses"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-entrapment-subjective","prompt":"An undercover agent asks Nora, a pharmacist with no criminal record, to sell him oxycodone without a prescription. She refuses. Over six weeks, the agent calls repeatedly, says he is in severe pain and suicidal, and offers ten times the normal price. Nora finally sells him a small amount. In a jurisdiction applying the subjective (federal) test, is Nora likely entitled to an entrapment defense?","choices":["No, because the government may lawfully create an opportunity by posing as a buyer.","No, because a defendant who ultimately agrees to commit the crime shows predisposition.","Yes, because the government induced the crime and she was not predisposed to commit it.","Yes, because the agent had no reasonable suspicion before targeting Nora for the sting."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under the subjective test used by federal courts and most states (Sherman v. United States; Jacobson v. United States), entrapment requires government inducement and a defendant who was not predisposed to commit the crime before government contact. Merely offering an opportunity is not inducement, but repeated pleas, appeals to sympathy, and an extraordinary price after an initial refusal are. Nora's clean record and refusal show lack of predisposition, and her eventual compliance does not prove it. Police need no reasonable suspicion to begin an undercover investigation.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["entrapment","predisposition","defenses"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimlaw-arson-charring","prompt":"Pia throws a lit rag through an open window of her neighbor's house, intending to burn the house. The rag lands on a rug, which burns, and smoke blackens the walls, but the fire is put out before any part of the wooden structure itself is charred. Under the common-law definition, is Pia guilty of arson?","choices":["Yes, because the fire burned the rug and caused smoke damage inside the home.","Yes, because she acted with the malice required for arson of a dwelling.","No, because arson requires destruction of a significant part of the structure.","No, because no part of the structure itself was charred by the fire."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Common-law arson is the malicious burning of the dwelling of another. Burning requires that some part of the structure itself be charred: destruction of a significant part is unnecessary, but scorching, discoloration, or smoke damage is not enough, and burning contents such as a rug does not count. Pia had the required malice, but without charring she is guilty of attempted arson rather than arson. Many modern statutes broaden arson, for example to cover other buildings or damage by explosion.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["arson","property-crimes"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-carpenter-csli","prompt":"Investigating a string of robberies, police obtain 127 days of Tara's historical cell-site location records from her wireless carrier with a court order issued on a showing of \"reasonable grounds\" that the records are relevant, rather than a warrant. The records place her near each robbery. Did obtaining the records violate the Fourth Amendment?","choices":["Yes, because getting months of cell-site location records is a search requiring a warrant.","No, because Tara voluntarily conveyed her location information to the wireless carrier.","No, because the records belong to the carrier, so Tara has no privacy interest in them.","Yes, because police must obtain a warrant for any business records about a suspect."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In Carpenter v. United States (2018), the Court held that acquiring at least seven days of historical cell-site location information is a Fourth Amendment search because it reveals the whole of a person's physical movements, so police generally need a warrant supported by probable cause. The Court declined to extend the third-party doctrine of Smith v. Maryland and United States v. Miller to such records, but those cases still govern ordinary business records like bank records, so the 'any business records' choice overstates the holding. An order on 'reasonable grounds' under the Stored Communications Act is not a warrant.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["fourth-amendment","carpenter","third-party-doctrine"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-jardines-dog-porch","prompt":"Acting on an unverified tip, officers bring a trained drug-sniffing dog onto the front porch of Uri's house. The dog alerts at the front door, and officers use the alert to obtain a search warrant that turns up a marijuana-growing operation. Was the use of the dog on the porch a Fourth Amendment search?","choices":["No, because a dog sniff reveals only contraband, in which no one has a legitimate privacy interest.","Yes, because officers physically entered the curtilage to gather evidence beyond any license.","No, because the front porch is open to visitors, including officers who come to knock and talk.","Yes, because using a drug-detection dog is a search whenever police lack probable cause."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In Florida v. Jardines, the Court held that bringing a drug dog onto a home's front porch is a search under the property-based approach: the porch is curtilage, and the implied license that lets visitors approach and knock does not extend to bringing a dog to explore for evidence. The 'contraband-only' rationale of United States v. Place and Illinois v. Caballes applies to sniffs in public places or during lawful traffic stops, so dog sniffs are not always searches.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["fourth-amendment","curtilage","dog-sniff"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Florida v. Jardines (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/11-564"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-riley-cell-phone","prompt":"After lawfully arresting Vera for robbery, an officer takes her unlocked smartphone from her pocket and scrolls through her photos and texts at the scene, finding messages planning the robbery. There was no warrant and no exigency. Was the phone search valid as a search incident to arrest?","choices":["Yes, because police may search any container found on an arrestee's person at arrest.","Yes, because it was reasonable to believe the phone held evidence of the robbery.","No, because police may not seize an arrestee's phone without first getting a warrant.","No, because police generally need a warrant to search the data on an arrestee's phone."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In Riley v. California, the Court held that the search-incident-to-arrest exception does not extend to the digital data on a cell phone; officers must generally get a warrant, absent a case-specific exigency. Officers may, however, seize and secure the phone while they seek a warrant, so the answer saying the seizure itself needed a warrant misstates the rule. The 'evidence of the offense of arrest' rationale comes from Arizona v. Gant and applies to vehicle passenger compartments, and United States v. Robinson's container rule does not reach digital data.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["search-incident-to-arrest","riley","digital-privacy"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Riley v. California (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/13-132"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-lange-misdemeanor-pursuit","prompt":"An officer sees Will blasting music and honking repeatedly while driving, a misdemeanor, and turns on his lights. Will sees the lights but keeps driving the short distance home and pulls into his attached garage. The officer follows him into the garage without a warrant and gathers evidence that Will is drunk. Nothing suggested danger, escape, or destruction of evidence. Was the warrantless entry justified by hot pursuit?","choices":["No, because pursuit of a misdemeanant does not categorically justify a warrantless home entry.","Yes, because hot pursuit of any suspect who flees from police is a categorical exigency.","Yes, because an attached garage is not part of the home for Fourth Amendment purposes.","No, because police may never enter a home without a warrant to arrest a misdemeanant."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In Lange v. California (2021), the Court held that pursuit of a fleeing misdemeanor suspect does not always qualify as an exigent circumstance; officers must assess case by case whether there is a genuine emergency, such as imminent harm, escape, or destruction of evidence. No such facts existed here, so a warrant was required. An attached garage is part of the home. The 'never' answer is overbroad: when a real emergency exists, police may enter even while pursuing a misdemeanant.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["exigent-circumstances","hot-pursuit","home-entry"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-dickerson-plain-feel","prompt":"During a lawful Terry frisk of Yuri for weapons, an officer feels a small lump in Yuri's jacket pocket and concludes it is not a weapon. The officer then squeezes and manipulates the lump through the fabric until he recognizes it as crack cocaine, and he removes it. Is the cocaine admissible?","choices":["Yes, because an officer may seize any object felt during a lawful protective frisk for weapons.","Yes, because the frisk itself was supported by reasonable suspicion that Yuri was armed.","No, because its identity was not immediately apparent and the manipulation exceeded the frisk.","No, because a weapons frisk may never lead to the seizure of contraband that is not a weapon."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Minnesota v. Dickerson's plain-feel doctrine, an officer conducting a lawful frisk may seize nonthreatening contraband if its incriminating character is immediately apparent from the initial pat-down. Once the officer concluded the lump was not a weapon, further squeezing and manipulation to identify it was a search beyond Terry's protective scope. A valid frisk does not authorize everything that follows it, but plain feel can justify seizing contraband whose identity is immediately apparent, so a frisk can lead to seizing non-weapons.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["terry","plain-feel","frisk"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Minnesota v. Dickerson (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/508/366"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-fernandez-consent","prompt":"Police respond to screams at an apartment. Zack, a resident, blocks the door and says, \"You're not coming in.\" Seeing fresh injuries on his co-tenant Ana, officers arrest Zack for assault on probable cause and take him to the station. An hour later, officers return and Ana consents to a search, which turns up illegal weapons belonging to Zack. Was Ana's consent valid?","choices":["Yes, because Zack was lawfully removed and was not present to object when Ana consented.","No, because Zack's express refusal remained binding on police until he withdrew it.","No, because police may not remove an objecting resident and then ask the co-tenant.","Yes, because a co-tenant's consent always overrides another occupant's objection."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Georgia v. Randolph holds that a physically present co-occupant's express refusal overrides a co-tenant's consent. In Fernandez v. California, the Court held that once the objector is absent because of a lawful detention or arrest, a remaining co-tenant may validly consent even though the objector refused earlier. Removing someone for the purpose of preventing an objection would be a problem, but an arrest supported by probable cause is objectively reasonable. Consent does not always override—had Zack still been at the door objecting, Ana's consent would not have sufficed.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["consent","randolph","co-occupant"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-strieff-attenuation","prompt":"Without reasonable suspicion, an officer stops Bree as she leaves a house. A records check reveals a valid, preexisting arrest warrant for an unpaid traffic ticket. The officer arrests her on the warrant and, searching her incident to that arrest, finds methamphetamine. The officer's error was negligent, not part of a pattern of misconduct. Is the methamphetamine admissible?","choices":["No, because the drugs were discovered only as a result of the unconstitutional stop.","No, because attenuation requires an independent act of free will by the defendant.","Yes, because the officer acted in good faith when he made the investigatory stop.","Yes, because the valid preexisting warrant attenuated the taint of the illegal stop."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In Utah v. Strieff (2016), the Court applied the attenuation doctrine using the Brown v. Illinois factors: temporal proximity, intervening circumstances, and the purpose and flagrancy of the misconduct. Discovery of a valid, preexisting, untainted arrest warrant was a compelling intervening circumstance, and the officer's negligence was not flagrant, so the evidence was admissible. Attenuation does not require a voluntary act by the defendant, and the good-faith exception does not cover an officer's own unconstitutional stop.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["exclusionary-rule","attenuation","strieff"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-davis-good-faith-precedent","prompt":"In 2008, officers searched Cal's car incident to his arrest after he was handcuffed in a patrol car, as binding circuit precedent then permitted. While Cal's case was on direct appeal, the Supreme Court decided Arizona v. Gant, which makes such searches unconstitutional. Should the evidence be suppressed?","choices":["Yes, because new constitutional rules apply to all cases still pending on direct review.","Yes, because the good-faith exception applies only to officers' reliance on warrants.","No, because the officers reasonably relied on binding appellate precedent at the time.","No, because Gant applies only to searches conducted after the decision was issued."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In Davis v. United States (2011), the Court held that searches conducted in objectively reasonable reliance on binding appellate precedent are not subject to the exclusionary rule, even if the precedent is later overruled. Gant does apply retroactively to cases on direct review (Griffith v. Kentucky), so the search was unconstitutional—but retroactivity of the right is distinct from the remedy, and exclusion would not deter reasonable police conduct. The good-faith exception extends beyond warrants to reliance on statutes (Illinois v. Krull), database records (Arizona v. Evans; Herring v. United States), and binding precedent.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["good-faith","exclusionary-rule","retroactivity"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-payton-harris","prompt":"With probable cause but no warrant, consent, or exigency, officers enter Dina's home and arrest her for murder. At the station, after Miranda warnings and a valid waiver, she confesses. Dina moves to suppress the station-house confession as fruit of the unlawful home entry. How should the court rule?","choices":["Grant, because the confession was the product of an arrest made in violation of Payton.","Deny, because with probable cause to arrest, the station confession is not fruit of the entry.","Deny, because Dina's Miranda waiver cured any taint from the unlawful entry into her home.","Grant, because a warrantless arrest is valid only in a public place, so this arrest was void."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Payton v. New York requires an arrest warrant (or consent or exigency) to enter a suspect's home for a routine felony arrest, so the entry was unlawful, and evidence found inside the home would be suppressed. But in New York v. Harris, the Court held that when police have probable cause, a statement made later outside the home is not the product of the illegal entry, because the continued custody was lawful. Miranda warnings alone do not purge Fourth Amendment taint (Brown v. Illinois), so that choice gives the wrong reason, and the Payton violation does not void the arrest itself.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["arrest","payton","fruit-of-the-poisonous-tree"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Payton v. New York (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/445/573"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-berghuis-silence","prompt":"After Miranda warnings, Eli neither invokes nor expressly waives his rights. He sits mostly silent through nearly three hours of questioning. A detective then asks, \"Do you pray to God to forgive you for shooting that boy?\" and Eli says, \"Yes.\" Is the answer admissible in the prosecution's case-in-chief?","choices":["No, because nearly three hours of silence was an invocation of the right to silence.","Yes, because Eli never unambiguously invoked his right to remain silent.","No, because a valid Miranda waiver must be express rather than implied from conduct.","Yes, because police must stop questioning only after a suspect asks for a lawyer."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In Berghuis v. Thompkins, the Court held that a suspect must invoke the right to remain silent unambiguously, as with the right to counsel (Davis v. United States), and that prolonged silence is not an invocation. A waiver may be implied when a suspect who understood the warnings makes an uncoerced statement. The 'only after a lawyer request' answer is wrong because a clear invocation of the right to silence must also be scrupulously honored (Michigan v. Mosley).","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["miranda","invocation","waiver"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-shatzer-break-in-custody","prompt":"While serving a prison sentence for an unrelated crime, Fay is interrogated about a new allegation and invokes her right to counsel. Questioning stops, and she returns to the general prison population. Three years later, a different officer investigating the same allegation gives her Miranda warnings, obtains a waiver, and she confesses. Is the confession admissible?","choices":["No, because after invoking counsel she could never be reinterrogated without counsel present.","No, because she was continuously incarcerated, so she never experienced a break in custody.","Yes, because Edwards protection lapses once the original investigation is no longer active.","Yes, because her return to the general prison population for years ended Edwards protection."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Maryland v. Shatzer, the Edwards presumption that a later waiver is invalid ends once the suspect has experienced a break in Miranda custody of at least 14 days. Release back into the general prison population counts as such a break, because the inmate returns to his accustomed surroundings, even though he remains incarcerated. Edwards protection does not depend on whether the investigation is active—here it was the same investigation, and the confession is admissible because of the break.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["miranda","edwards","shatzer"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-seibert-two-step","prompt":"Following a deliberate department strategy, an officer questions Gwen in custody without warnings until she confesses. After a 20-minute break, the same officer gives Miranda warnings, obtains a waiver, and has her repeat the confession, referring back to her earlier statements. No curative steps are taken. Is the second, warned confession admissible?","choices":["No, because the deliberate two-step tactic made the midstream warnings ineffective.","Yes, because the second confession followed valid Miranda warnings and a waiver.","Yes, because the first statement was voluntary, so the later warnings cured any taint.","No, because any statement made after an unwarned confession is inadmissible fruit."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In Missouri v. Seibert, the Court held that a deliberate 'question first, warn later' strategy renders midstream warnings ineffective when, as here, the questioning is continuous and no curative measures (such as a substantial break or an explanation that the first statement is inadmissible) are taken. By contrast, under Oregon v. Elstad, when the initial failure to warn is not deliberate and the first statement is voluntary, a later warned statement is generally admissible—so post-warning statements are not automatically inadmissible fruit.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["miranda","seibert","elstad"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-crimpro-quarles-public-safety","prompt":"A woman tells officers she was just raped by an armed man who ran into a grocery store. Officers find Hank inside wearing an empty shoulder holster, handcuff him, and before giving warnings ask, \"Where's the gun?\" Hank nods toward some boxes and says, \"The gun is over there.\" Is his statement admissible in the prosecution's case-in-chief?","choices":["No, because Hank was handcuffed and questioned in custody without any Miranda warnings.","Yes, because a brief question asked in a public place does not count as interrogation.","Yes, because the question was prompted by an objectively reasonable concern for public safety.","No, because the public-safety exception applies only to threats against the officers."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under New York v. Quarles, police may ask questions reasonably prompted by a concern for public safety—such as locating a discarded gun in a public store—before giving Miranda warnings, and the answers are admissible. Hank was in custody and the question was interrogation, so without the exception the statement would be suppressed. The exception covers threats to the public generally, not just to the officers.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["miranda","public-safety-exception"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"New York v. Quarles (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/467/649"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-massiah-informant","prompt":"After Ike is indicted for arson and appointed counsel, police place a paid informant in his jail cell with instructions to get Ike talking about the fire. The informant repeatedly steers conversations to the arson, and Ike eventually describes how he set it. Ike did not know the cellmate was working for police. Is Ike's statement admissible at his arson trial?","choices":["No, because the government deliberately elicited it after his right to counsel attached.","Yes, because Ike did not know he was talking to an agent, so there was no interrogation.","Yes, because Ike chose to speak voluntarily to a fellow inmate rather than to police.","No, because the informant questioned Ike in custody without giving Miranda warnings."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Massiah v. United States and United States v. Henry, once the Sixth Amendment right to counsel has attached, the government may not deliberately elicit incriminating statements about the charged offense outside counsel's presence, including through a secret informant. Miranda does not apply to undercover questioning because there is no police-dominated atmosphere (Illinois v. Perkins), which is why the Miranda-based choices fail. An informant who merely listens—a 'listening post'—does not violate Massiah (Kuhlmann v. Wilson), but this informant actively elicited the statements.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["sixth-amendment","massiah","informant"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Massiah v. United States (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/377/201"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-padilla-ineffective-assistance","prompt":"Juan, a lawful permanent resident, pleads guilty to a drug-distribution offense on his lawyer's advice. The lawyer never mentions immigration consequences, although federal law plainly makes the offense a deportable one. Juan later moves to withdraw the plea, credibly showing he would have gone to trial had he known. Was counsel constitutionally ineffective?","choices":["No, because deportation is a collateral consequence beyond the Sixth Amendment's reach.","No, because counsel gave no false advice and merely stayed silent about immigration.","Yes, because counsel failed to advise him of a clear deportation consequence of the plea.","Yes, because any error by counsel in plea bargaining entitles a defendant to withdraw."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Strickland v. Washington, a defendant must show deficient performance and prejudice. Padilla v. Kentucky held that counsel must advise a noncitizen client when a plea carries a clear risk of deportation; failing to do so, including by silence, is deficient, and deportation is not excluded as merely 'collateral.' For a plea, prejudice means a reasonable probability the defendant would have rejected the plea and gone to trial (Hill v. Lockhart; Lee v. United States), which Juan credibly showed. Not every error suffices—both prongs are required.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["ineffective-assistance","strickland","padilla","guilty-plea"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Strickland v. Washington (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/466/668"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-gideon-actual-imprisonment","prompt":"Kim is charged with a misdemeanor punishable by up to one year in jail. She asks for appointed counsel because she cannot afford a lawyer, but the court refuses. Convicted after a bench trial, she is sentenced to 30 days in jail. Was her Sixth Amendment right to counsel violated?","choices":["No, because the right to appointed counsel applies only in felony prosecutions.","Yes, because she received actual jail time without counsel or a valid waiver.","No, because her 30-day sentence was under six months, so the case was petty.","Yes, because any offense punishable by jail requires counsel, whatever the sentence."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Gideon v. Wainwright requires appointed counsel for indigent felony defendants, and Argersinger v. Hamlin and Scott v. Illinois extend the right to misdemeanor cases that result in actual imprisonment. The test turns on the sentence actually imposed, not the authorized one: an uncounseled conviction resulting only in a fine is valid, and Alabama v. Shelton requires counsel even for a suspended jail sentence. The six-month 'petty offense' line governs the right to a jury trial, not the right to counsel.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["right-to-counsel","gideon","misdemeanor"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Gideon v. Wainwright (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/372/335"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-blockburger-lesser-included","prompt":"Lia is convicted in state court of joyriding (taking a car without the owner's consent) for taking a neighbor's car on May 1. The state then charges her with auto theft (taking a car without consent with intent to permanently deprive) for the same taking. Does double jeopardy bar the second prosecution?","choices":["No, because auto theft requires an intent element that joyriding does not.","No, because the second charge carries a more serious penalty than the first.","Yes, because double jeopardy bars any second trial arising from the same events.","Yes, because the two crimes are the same offense under the Blockburger test."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Blockburger v. United States, two offenses are different only if each requires proof of a fact the other does not. Auto theft requires an extra element (intent to permanently deprive), but joyriding requires nothing that auto theft does not, so joyriding is a lesser included offense and the two are the same offense (Brown v. Ohio). Prosecution for the greater offense after a conviction for the lesser is therefore barred. Double jeopardy does not bar every prosecution arising from the same transaction—only those for the same offense.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["double-jeopardy","blockburger","lesser-included"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Blockburger v. United States (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/284/299"}]},{"id":"v3-crimpro-brady-impeachment","prompt":"Before Mo's robbery trial, the lead detective learns that the state's key eyewitness was promised leniency on her own pending charges in exchange for testifying. The prosecutor never learns of the deal, and it is not disclosed. The witness's testimony is the only evidence placing Mo at the scene. After conviction, the deal comes to light. Does Mo have a valid Brady claim?","choices":["Yes, because the undisclosed deal was material impeachment evidence known to the prosecution team.","No, because the prosecutor personally never knew about the deal and acted in complete good faith.","No, because Brady requires disclosure only of exculpatory evidence, not of impeachment material.","Yes, because any nondisclosure of favorable evidence requires automatic reversal of a conviction."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Brady v. Maryland, the prosecution violates due process by suppressing evidence favorable to the accused that is material, regardless of good or bad faith. Favorable evidence includes impeachment evidence such as leniency deals (Giglio v. United States), and the knowledge of police investigating the case is imputed to the prosecution (Kyles v. Whitley). Evidence is material if there is a reasonable probability of a different result, which is met because the witness alone placed Mo at the scene. Not every nondisclosure requires reversal—only material ones.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["brady","giglio","due-process"],"focus":["bar","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Brady v. Maryland (LII)","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/373/83"}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-sb1437-felony-murder","prompt":"Ella agrees to drive Rico to a liquor store he plans to rob. She is unarmed, does not know Rico has a gun, and waits around the corner out of sight. Inside, Rico unexpectedly shoots and kills the clerk; Ella drives him away. The clerk was not a peace officer. Under current Penal Code §§ 188 and 189, can Ella be convicted of murder?","choices":["Yes, because every participant in a robbery that results in a death is guilty of first-degree felony murder in California.","No, because she was not the killer, did not intend to kill, and was not a major participant with reckless indifference.","Yes, because the clerk's killing was a natural and probable consequence of the robbery that she knowingly aided and abetted.","No, because California abolished the felony-murder rule entirely for all killings committed after January 1, 2019."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Senate Bill 1437 (effective 2019) amended Penal Code § 189(e) so that a participant in a listed felony such as robbery is liable for felony murder only if he or she was the actual killer, aided the actual killer with intent to kill, or was a major participant in the felony who acted with reckless indifference to human life (§ 190.2(d)); § 189(f) adds a peace-officer-victim exception. Amended § 188 bars imputing malice based solely on participation in a crime, eliminating the natural-and-probable-consequences theory for murder. Felony murder survives for actual killers and qualifying participants. Under People v. Banks and People v. Clark, an unarmed getaway driver who was absent from the scene and unaware of the gun is generally neither a major participant nor recklessly indifferent; Ella remains guilty of robbery.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["felony-murder","sb-1437","penal-code-189"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 189","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=189."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-1172-6-burden","prompt":"Sal was convicted of second-degree murder in 2016 on a natural-and-probable-consequences theory. He petitions for resentencing under Penal Code § 1172.6; the court finds a prima facie case, issues an order to show cause, and holds an evidentiary hearing. What must happen at that hearing for the court to deny relief?","choices":["Sal must prove by a preponderance of the evidence that he could not be convicted under current law.","The prosecution must show that substantial evidence would support a murder conviction under current law.","The prosecution must prove beyond a reasonable doubt that Sal is guilty of murder under current law.","The court must find that the original jury's verdict rested on a theory of murder still valid today."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Penal Code § 1172.6 (formerly § 1170.95) lets a person convicted of murder under felony murder, the natural and probable consequences doctrine, or another theory imputing malice based solely on participation in a crime seek vacatur and resentencing. After an order to show cause, the prosecution bears the burden of proving beyond a reasonable doubt that the petitioner is guilty of murder under the law as amended by SB 1437, with the court acting as an independent factfinder. The statute expressly provides that a finding of substantial evidence to support a conviction is insufficient. SB 775 extended relief to qualifying attempted murder and manslaughter convictions.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["resentencing","penal-code-1172-6","sb-1437"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 1172.6","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=1172.6."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-beltran-heat-of-passion","prompt":"Tony is tried for murder in California after killing his former partner during a sudden confrontation, and he requests a heat-of-passion voluntary manslaughter instruction (CALCRIM No. 570). Under People v. Beltran, how should the jury decide whether the provocation was legally sufficient?","choices":["Ask whether it would have provoked an ordinary person of average disposition to kill under the same circumstances.","Ask whether Tony himself, given his own temperament and personal history, was in fact provoked to act from passion.","Ask whether it consisted of conduct rather than words, because insulting words alone are never enough in California.","Ask whether it would make an ordinary person of average disposition act rashly, from passion rather than judgment."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Heat-of-passion voluntary manslaughter (Penal Code § 192(a)) has a subjective component—the defendant actually killed while under the heat of passion—and an objective one. In People v. Beltran (2013), the California Supreme Court held that the objective question is whether the provocation would cause an ordinary person of average disposition to act rashly and without due deliberation, from passion rather than judgment—not whether such a person would be moved to kill. The defendant may not set up his own standard of conduct, and in California provocation may come from words as well as conduct.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["voluntary-manslaughter","heat-of-passion","calcrim-570"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 192","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=192."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-imperfect-self-defense","prompt":"In California, Ruth fatally stabs a neighbor who walked toward her shouting during a dispute. The jury finds Ruth actually believed she was in imminent danger of great bodily injury and needed deadly force, but that her belief was unreasonable. Ruth did not start or provoke the confrontation. What is the proper verdict?","choices":["Not guilty, because California self-defense requires only an actual, good-faith belief in imminent danger.","Voluntary manslaughter, because an actual but unreasonable belief in the need to defend negates malice.","Second-degree murder, because an unreasonable belief gives no defense or mitigation in California.","Involuntary manslaughter, because she lacked malice and acted with criminal negligence in using deadly force."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Complete self-defense to a homicide charge (CALCRIM No. 505) requires that the defendant actually and reasonably believed she faced imminent danger of death or great bodily injury and that the force used was necessary. Under In re Christian S. (1994) and CALCRIM No. 571, an actual but unreasonable belief in the need to defend—imperfect self-defense—negates malice and reduces murder to voluntary manslaughter. It is voluntary rather than involuntary manslaughter because the killing was intentional or done with conscious disregard for life. The doctrine is unavailable when the defendant's own wrongful conduct created the circumstances that justified the adversary's attack.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["imperfect-self-defense","calcrim-3470","voluntary-manslaughter"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-1170b-middle-term","prompt":"A California jury convicts Dev of a felony punishable by two, three, or four years in prison. At sentencing, the judge finds by a preponderance of the evidence that the victim was particularly vulnerable—a fact Dev never admitted or stipulated to—and imposes the four-year upper term. Dev has no prior convictions. Was the upper term proper under Penal Code § 1170(b)?","choices":["Yes, because the judge identified an aggravating circumstance and stated reasons on the record.","Yes, because aggravating facts relied on at sentencing need be found only by a preponderance.","No, because the aggravating fact was neither stipulated nor found true beyond a reasonable doubt.","No, because the upper term may be imposed only on a defendant with prior felony convictions."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Since Senate Bill 567 (effective 2022), Penal Code § 1170(b) makes the middle term the presumptive maximum: the court may impose the upper term only when aggravating circumstances justify it and the underlying facts were stipulated to by the defendant or found true beyond a reasonable doubt by the jury (or the judge in a court trial). A narrow exception lets the court rely on prior convictions shown by certified records. The former rule gave judges broad discretion among the three terms with a statement of reasons. The statute also presumes the lower term when factors such as the defendant's youth or past trauma contributed to the offense.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["sentencing","penal-code-1170","sb-567"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 1170","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=1170."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-prop47-shoplifting","prompt":"Lana, who has no prior convictions, walks into an open drugstore during business hours intending to steal cosmetics and leaves with $600 worth of merchandise. The prosecutor files a felony complaint charging second-degree commercial burglary (Pen. Code § 459) based on her entry with intent to steal. What is the correct charging analysis?","choices":["Lana must be charged with misdemeanor shoplifting, not burglary or theft of the same property.","Burglary is proper, because Lana's intent to steal at the moment she entered completed that crime.","Lana may be charged with both burglary and petty theft, with punishment limited by § 654.","Lana must be charged with grand theft, a wobbler, because the merchandise exceeded $400."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proposition 47 (2014) created the crime of shoplifting, Penal Code § 459.5: entering a commercial establishment while it is open during regular business hours with intent to commit larceny of property worth $950 or less. Any such act must be charged as shoplifting, and a person charged with shoplifting may not also be charged with burglary or theft of the same property. Prop 47 also made theft of property worth $950 or less petty theft under § 490.2. These are misdemeanors for defendants without disqualifying priors; Proposition 36 (2024) later added felony-eligible charges for certain repeat theft offenders.","subject":"criminalLaw","tags":["prop-47","shoplifting","penal-code-459-5"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 459.5","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=459.5."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-1001-36-diversion","prompt":"Ari is charged in California with felony assault with a deadly weapon. A psychiatrist diagnosed him with bipolar disorder two years ago and opines that his symptoms would respond to treatment. Ari consents to diversion and waives his speedy-trial right. Opposing mental health diversion under Penal Code § 1001.36, the prosecutor argues Ari has not proven the disorder caused the offense. How should the court treat that issue?","choices":["Require Ari to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that his disorder caused the assault.","Deny diversion, because assault with a deadly weapon is a violent felony excluded from the statute.","Defer to the prosecutor, because diversion under § 1001.36 requires the prosecution's consent.","Presume the disorder was a significant factor absent clear and convincing evidence otherwise."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Since Senate Bill 1223 (effective 2023), if a qualified expert has diagnosed the defendant with a qualifying mental disorder within the last five years, the court must find that the disorder was a significant factor in the offense unless there is clear and convincing evidence that it was not a motivating, causal, or contributing factor. The court then considers suitability: whether the symptoms would respond to treatment, consent and a speedy-trial waiver, agreement to comply with treatment, and whether the defendant would pose an unreasonable risk of danger to public safety (as defined in § 1170.18) if treated in the community. Only listed offenses—such as murder, voluntary manslaughter, rape, and most registrable sex offenses—are excluded, and the court, not the prosecutor, decides.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["diversion","mental-health","penal-code-1001-36"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 1001.36","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=1001.36."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-racial-justice-act","prompt":"In closing argument at a California robbery trial, the prosecutor repeatedly calls the Black defendant an \"animal\" and \"a predator roaming our streets,\" language not drawn from any witness's words or any physical description. Defense counsel moves for relief under the Racial Justice Act, Penal Code § 745. Which statement correctly describes the defendant's burden?","choices":["He must prove the prosecutor acted with purposeful discrimination, as in a Batson or equal protection claim.","He must prove a violation by a preponderance of the evidence, without proving purposeful discrimination.","He must prove by clear and convincing evidence that the language was intended to appeal to racial bias.","No burden applies, because the Act reaches only charging and sentencing disparities, not courtroom language."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Penal Code § 745(a)(2) is violated when a judge, attorney, law enforcement officer, expert, or juror uses racially discriminatory language about the defendant's race, ethnicity, or national origin during trial, whether or not purposeful. The statute defines such language to include racially charged or coded language and language comparing the defendant to an animal, with exceptions for relevantly relating another person's words or giving a neutral physical description. The defendant must prove a violation by a preponderance of the evidence and need not prove intentional discrimination—a deliberate departure from the purposeful-discrimination standard of McCleskey v. Kemp and Batson.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["racial-justice-act","penal-code-745","prosecutorial-misconduct"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 745","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=745."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-truth-in-evidence","prompt":"California officers, without a warrant, search opaque trash bags Paolo left at the curb for collection and find drug paraphernalia. Paolo files a Penal Code § 1538.5 motion arguing only that the search violated the California Constitution's search-and-seizure clause (art. I, § 13), which he contends protects curbside trash. How should the court rule?","choices":["Grant, because the California Constitution independently protects privacy in curbside trash.","Grant, because art. I, § 13 carries its own exclusionary rule apart from the federal one.","Deny, because Proposition 8 abolished the exclusionary rule in California criminal cases.","Deny, because California courts exclude evidence only when federal law requires exclusion."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Truth-in-Evidence provision adopted by Proposition 8 in 1982 (Cal. Const., art. I, § 28(f)(2)) provides that relevant evidence shall not be excluded in criminal proceedings, subject to listed exceptions. In In re Lance W. (1985), the California Supreme Court held that this eliminated any independent state-law exclusionary remedy, so evidence is suppressed under § 1538.5 only when the Fourth Amendment requires it. Under California v. Greenwood, a warrantless search of curbside trash does not violate the Fourth Amendment. The federal exclusionary rule still applies in California, so Prop 8 did not abolish exclusion altogether.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["exclusionary-rule","truth-in-evidence","penal-code-1538-5"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 1538.5","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=1538.5."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-prelim-hearsay-995","prompt":"At Ned's felony preliminary hearing, the only prosecution witness is a detective with seven years of experience who personally interviewed the victim; he relates the victim's account of the assault, and the victim does not testify. Over a hearsay objection, the magistrate holds Ned to answer. Ned then moves under Penal Code § 995 to set aside the information. How should the court rule?","choices":["Deny, because § 872(b) lets probable cause rest on a qualified officer's hearsay testimony.","Grant, because Ned was denied his right to confront the victim at the preliminary hearing.","Grant, because a holding order may not rest entirely on hearsay, even an officer's hearsay.","Deny, because a § 995 motion may challenge only indictments, not informations after a prelim."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Proposition 115 (1990) added Penal Code § 872(b), which allows a probable-cause finding at a preliminary hearing to rest in whole or in part on a law enforcement officer's testimony relating out-of-court statements, if the officer has at least five years of experience or specified POST training. Whitman v. Superior Court (1991) upheld the provision, while requiring an officer with meaningful knowledge of the circumstances rather than a mere reader of reports. A § 995 motion is the proper vehicle to set aside an information when the defendant was committed without reasonable or probable cause, but the commitment here was lawful.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["preliminary-hearing","prop-115","penal-code-995"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 872","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=872."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-pitchess-motion","prompt":"Ivan is charged in California with resisting arrest and battery on a peace officer. He claims the arresting officer attacked him without provocation and then falsified the report. Ivan files a Pitchess motion seeking the officer's records of prior complaints of excessive force and dishonesty. What must Ivan show to obtain in camera review of the records?","choices":["Proof by a preponderance that the officer actually used excessive force and then lied in the report.","Credible, independently corroborated evidence of misconduct that the court finds persuasive.","Good cause by affidavit, with a plausible scenario of officer misconduct material to his defense.","Only a request naming the officer, after which the defense may inspect the full personnel file."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Evidence Code §§ 1043 and 1045, a defendant seeking peace-officer personnel records must file a motion with affidavits showing good cause—materiality to the pending case and a reasonable belief that the agency has the records. Warrick v. Superior Court (2005) holds that good cause requires only a specific, plausible factual scenario of officer misconduct; it need not be credible or persuasive. If the showing is made, the court reviews the records in camera and discloses only relevant information, typically the names and contact information of complainants and witnesses, excluding facts too remote to be of practical benefit; the former bar on complaints more than five years older than the incident was repealed by SB 16 (effective 2022). SB 1421 separately made some records public, but the Pitchess procedure remains.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["pitchess","discovery","evidence-code-1043"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Evid. Code § 1043","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=EVID&sectionNum=1043."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-kelly-new-scientific","prompt":"In a California prosecution, the People offer results from a newly developed forensic technique that identifies people from their gait in surveillance video. No California appellate court has addressed the technique, and the defense objects. Under California law, what is the threshold test for admitting the technique?","choices":["Whether it satisfies the Daubert reliability factors, which California adopted in Sargon.","Whether it is generally accepted as reliable in the relevant scientific community.","Whether the expert presenting it is qualified, leaving any reliability doubts to the jury.","Whether a California appellate court has previously approved the technique as reliable."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"California applies the Kelly rule (People v. Kelly (1976), reaffirmed in People v. Leahy (1994)) to new scientific techniques: the proponent must show the technique is generally accepted as reliable in the relevant scientific community, the witness is properly qualified, and correct scientific procedures were used in the case. Sargon Enterprises v. University of Southern California (2012) confirmed trial courts' gatekeeping role over expert opinion under Evidence Code §§ 801 and 802, but California has not adopted Daubert. Prior published appellate approval can establish general acceptance in later cases, but it is not required for a first-time showing.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["expert-testimony","kelly-frye","scientific-evidence"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-evid-1108-propensity","prompt":"Omar is tried in California for sexual battery of a coworker. The prosecution offers testimony from another woman that Omar sexually assaulted her two years earlier, an incident that never led to charges, to show his propensity to commit sexual offenses. How should the court rule?","choices":["Exclude it under § 1101(a), because character evidence cannot be used to show conduct.","Admit it only to show intent or common plan under § 1101(b), never to show propensity.","Admit it under Evidence Code § 1108 unless § 352 weighing calls for exclusion.","Exclude it, because § 1108 applies only to prior sexual offenses that led to a conviction."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Evidence Code § 1101(a) generally bars character evidence to prove conduct, but § 1108 creates an exception in sex-offense prosecutions: evidence of the defendant's other sexual offenses is admissible to show propensity, subject to exclusion under § 352 if its probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger of undue prejudice, confusion, or undue consumption of time. People v. Falsetta (1999) upheld § 1108 against a due process challenge. Uncharged conduct qualifies; no conviction is required. Section 1109 creates a parallel rule for domestic violence cases, with a presumptive 10-year limit.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["character-evidence","evidence-code-1108","propensity"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Evid. Code § 1108","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=EVID&sectionNum=1108."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-romero-strikes","prompt":"Gabe is convicted in California of a felony, and the court finds two prior strike convictions for robberies he committed 20 years ago; his record since then is minor. Defense counsel wants the court to dismiss one strike for sentencing purposes. Which statement is correct under People v. Superior Court (Romero)?","choices":["The court may strike a prior in furtherance of justice if Gabe falls outside the Three Strikes law's spirit.","The court may dismiss a strike only if the prosecutor moves to dismiss it in furtherance of justice.","The court may not dismiss a strike, because the Three Strikes law removed judicial sentencing discretion.","The court must disregard both strikes automatically, because the prior convictions are over ten years old."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In People v. Superior Court (Romero) (1996), the California Supreme Court held that trial courts retain authority under Penal Code § 1385 to dismiss prior strike allegations on their own motion in furtherance of justice. Under People v. Williams (1998), the court asks whether, in light of the present felony, the prior strikes, and the defendant's background, character, and prospects, the defendant may be deemed outside the scheme's spirit in whole or in part. The Three Strikes law has no washout period, so the age of the priors is a factor, not an automatic bar. Denial of a Romero motion is reviewed for abuse of discretion.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["three-strikes","romero","penal-code-1385"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 1385","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=1385."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-crim-654-multiple-punishment","prompt":"A California jury convicts Nia of two offenses based on a single act, and Penal Code § 654 applies. At sentencing, the judge believes the offense carrying the shorter prison term better reflects Nia's culpability. Under current § 654, what may the judge do?","choices":["Impose sentence under the provision with the longest potential term and stay the other.","Impose concurrent sentences, since concurrent terms do not increase Nia's actual punishment.","Dismiss the conviction carrying the shorter term, since only one conviction may stand.","Impose sentence under either provision, including the shorter one, and stay the other."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Penal Code § 654(a) bars multiple punishment for a single act or omission that is punishable under different provisions. Before Assembly Bill 518 (effective 2022), the court had to punish under the provision providing the longest potential term; now the act may be punished under either provision, but not under more than one. The proper procedure is to impose sentence on the other count and stay its execution—concurrent sentences still count as multiple punishment. Section 654 limits punishment, not convictions, so both convictions stand.","subject":"criminalProcedure","tags":["sentencing","penal-code-654","multiple-punishment"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Penal Code § 654","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PEN&sectionNum=654."}]},{"id":"ethics-communicate-offer","prompt":"Lawyer Ana represents Cole, the plaintiff in a civil contract suit. Cole has never told Ana what amount he would accept or authorized her to decide. Defense counsel sends Ana a written offer to settle for $40,000. Ana thinks the offer is far too low and plans to ignore it as a negotiating tactic, expecting a better offer next month. Under both the ABA Model Rules and the California Rules of Professional Conduct, what must Ana do?","choices":["Promptly tell Cole about the offer, even though she thinks it is too low.","Reject the offer herself, because negotiating tactics are the lawyer's call to make.","Tell Cole about the offer only if she decides to recommend that he accept it.","Wait to tell Cole until the defense makes its final offer shortly before trial."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Whether to settle is the client's decision (Model Rule 1.2(a)), so a lawyer must promptly convey a settlement offer unless the client has already said such an offer is acceptable or unacceptable or has authorized the lawyer to decide (Model Rule 1.4(a) & cmt. [2]). California expressly requires prompt communication of the terms of any written settlement offer in a civil matter (Cal. RPC 1.4.1; Bus. & Prof. Code § 6103.5). Ignoring the offer as a 'tactic' usurps the client's authority over the objective, and screening or delaying offers based on the lawyer's own view is equally improper.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["communication","settlement","client-authority","mpre","california"],"focus":["mpre","california"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"mpre-confidentiality","prompt":"While preparing a client's defense, Lawyer Ben interviews the client's former neighbor and learns that the client was once fired from a job for theft. The firing is also mentioned in an old court record that anyone could look up. At a dinner party, Ben tells friends about the firing. The client never consented. Under the ABA Model Rules, did Ben act properly?","choices":["Yes, because the information came from a third party rather than the client, so it is not privileged.","No, because confidentiality protects all information relating to the representation.","Yes, because information found in a public court record is outside the duty of confidentiality.","No, because the attorney-client privilege protects everything Ben learned while working on the case."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.6(a) protects all 'information relating to the representation of a client,' whatever its source, so it is far broader than the attorney-client privilege, which covers only confidential lawyer-client communications. Information does not lose protection merely because it appears in a public record (ABA Formal Op. 480). The choice that relies on the attorney-client privilege reaches the right result for the wrong reason: the neighbor's statement is not privileged at all; it is protected by the ethical duty of confidentiality.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["confidentiality","privilege","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"mpre-conflict-current-v2","prompt":"Lawyer Chen's firm currently represents Delta Corp. in a commercial lease dispute. A new client asks Chen to sue Delta for injuries she suffered when a Delta delivery truck hit her, a matter unrelated to the lease. Chen reasonably believes she can represent both clients competently and diligently. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Chen take the new case?","choices":["Yes, without anyone's consent, because the two matters are factually unrelated.","Yes, if the new client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing; Delta need not consent.","Yes, if Delta and the new client each give informed consent, confirmed in writing.","No, because suing a current client is a conflict that no client consent can cure."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Suing a current client is direct adversity and a concurrent conflict under Model Rule 1.7(a)(1) even when the matters are unrelated (cmt. [6]). The conflict is consentable under Rule 1.7(b) if the lawyer reasonably believes she can provide competent and diligent representation to each client, the representation is not prohibited by law, and it does not involve one client's claim against another in the same proceeding; each affected client, including Delta, must give informed consent, confirmed in writing. Consent of the new client alone is not enough.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","current-client","direct-adversity","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-fee-splitting","prompt":"Lawyer Dana agrees to pay Ed, a nonlawyer who runs a community 'accident help' center, 20% of the legal fee in every case Ed refers to her. Each referred client is told of the arrangement and consents in writing, the clients' total fees are not increased, and Ed has never tried to influence how Dana handles a case. Under the ABA Model Rules, is the arrangement proper?","choices":["Yes, because each client gave informed written consent to the arrangement.","Yes, because the arrangement does not increase the fee that the clients pay.","No, but only if Ed begins to influence how Dana handles the referred cases.","No, because a lawyer generally may not share legal fees with a nonlawyer."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 5.4(a) prohibits sharing legal fees with a nonlawyer, subject to narrow exceptions (payments to a deceased lawyer's estate, purchase of a practice, employee compensation or retirement plans, and court-awarded fees shared with a sponsoring nonprofit). Paying Ed for referrals also violates Rule 7.2(b)'s ban on giving anything of value for recommending a lawyer. Client consent and the absence of a fee increase do not create an exception, and the violation does not depend on Ed actually interfering with Dana's judgment.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","nonlawyer","professional-independence","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"mpre-truthfulness","prompt":"At a bail hearing, the judge asks defense lawyer Farah whether her client has any prior convictions. Farah knows the client has a prior shoplifting conviction but answers, 'None, Your Honor.' The judge later says the conviction would not have changed the bail amount. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Farah subject to discipline?","choices":["Yes, because she knowingly made a false statement of fact to the tribunal.","No, because the statement turned out not to be material to the bail decision.","No, because the duty of confidentiality barred her from revealing the conviction.","No, because defense counsel may put the prosecution to its proof on every fact."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 3.3(a)(1) forbids a lawyer to knowingly make a false statement of fact or law to a tribunal; the 'material' qualifier applies only to the separate duty to correct an earlier false statement. Confidentiality may justify not volunteering harmful information, but it never permits an affirmative lie (see also Rule 8.4(c)). Defense counsel may require the prosecution to prove every element (Rule 3.1), but that privilege does not extend to misstating facts to the court.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["candor","tribunal","false-statement","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-advertising","prompt":"Lawyer Gil's website states, 'Over 500 jury trials won!' In fact, Gil has tried about 40 cases to verdict. A footnote at the bottom of the page reads, 'Past results do not guarantee future outcomes.' Under the ABA Model Rules, is Gil subject to discipline for the website?","choices":["No, because the disclaimer keeps the statement from creating unjustified expectations.","Yes, because the statement is false, and a disclaimer cannot cure a false statement.","Yes, because lawyer advertising may not refer to the lawyer's past results at all.","No, because the statement is commercial speech protected by the First Amendment."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 7.1 prohibits false or misleading communications about a lawyer's services. An appropriate disclaimer can sometimes keep a truthful claim, such as accurate past results, from creating unjustified expectations (cmt. [3]), but no disclaimer cures a flatly false statement of fact. Truthful past results are not banned outright, and false commercial speech receives no First Amendment protection.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["advertising","misleading","disclaimer","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"mpre-safekeeping","prompt":"Lawyer Hana receives a $12,000 settlement check payable to her client. Because her trust-account bank branch is closed that day, she deposits the check into her firm's operating account, records it accurately, and pays the client in full from that account two days later. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Hana subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because the client was paid in full promptly and suffered no loss at all.","No, because funds held for only a few days need not be placed in a trust account.","Yes, because she commingled the client's funds with her own money.","Yes, because she did not first get the client's consent to use her operating account."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.15(a) requires a lawyer to hold client property separate from the lawyer's own, with client funds kept in a separate trust account. Depositing client funds in an operating account is commingling, even if the records are accurate, the client is paid promptly, and no loss occurs. Short-term or nominal client funds still belong in a trust account (typically an IOLTA account), and client consent does not authorize commingling.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["trust-account","commingling","safekeeping","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"pr-conflict-former","prompt":"Two years ago, Lawyer Ivan drafted a supply contract for Seller Co.; that representation has ended. Buyer Co. now asks Ivan to sue Seller Co. for breach of that same contract. Ivan promises he will not use anything he learned from Seller Co. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Ivan represent Buyer Co.?","choices":["Yes, because his representation of Seller Co. ended before this dispute arose.","Yes, because he will not use or reveal Seller Co.'s confidential information.","No, because a lawyer may not attack his own work product, even with consent.","Yes, but only if Seller Co. gives informed consent, confirmed in writing."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 1.9(a), a lawyer may not represent a new client in the same or a substantially related matter in which the new client's interests are materially adverse to a former client unless the former client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing. Ending the earlier representation does not remove the conflict, and a promise not to misuse confidences (a separate duty under Rule 1.9(c)) does not substitute for consent. Unlike some concurrent conflicts, a former-client conflict is consentable.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","former-client","substantially-related","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-competence","prompt":"A longtime client asks Lawyer Jo, who practices only real estate law, to handle a trademark registration that must be filed within four months. Jo has never done trademark work. Under both the ABA Model Rules and the California Rules of Professional Conduct, may Jo accept the matter?","choices":["Yes, if she can become competent through study in time or associates with a competent lawyer.","No, because a lawyer may accept matters only in fields where she already has experience.","Yes, but only if the client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing, to her inexperience.","Yes, but only if she earns a specialist certification in trademark law before filing."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Competence requires the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness, and preparation reasonably necessary for the representation (Model Rule 1.1). A lawyer new to a field can provide competent representation through necessary study or by associating with a lawyer of established competence (cmt. [2], [4]); California says so expressly (Cal. RPC 1.1(c)). Client consent does not substitute for competence, and certification is not required.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["competence","duty","mpre","california"],"focus":["mpre","california"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"pr-confidentiality-exception","prompt":"During a meeting, Lawyer Kai's client calmly says that tonight he will 'make sure' his business partner never testifies against him, and that he has just bought a gun. Kai reasonably believes the client will try to kill the partner. Under the ABA Model Rules, which statement best describes Kai's options?","choices":["Kai must reveal the information, because the rules require disclosure to prevent a death.","Kai may reveal the information to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent the killing.","Kai may not reveal the information, because no crime has actually been committed yet.","Kai may reveal the information only if the client used Kai's services to plan the crime."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.6(b)(1) permits a lawyer to reveal information to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. The exception is permissive under the Model Rules (some states make it mandatory), and it exists precisely to prevent future harm, so the absence of a completed crime is irrelevant. The 'used the lawyer's services' requirement belongs to the financial crime-fraud exceptions in Rule 1.6(b)(2)–(3).","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["confidentiality","exception","bodily-harm","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-solicitation","prompt":"Lawyer Lena learns from a news report that Max was seriously injured in a car crash. Max has no prior relationship with Lena and has not said he does not want to be contacted. Under the ABA Model Rules, which of the following contacts by Lena, seeking to be hired, is permissible?","choices":["Visiting Max in the hospital to explain his rights and offer a free consultation.","Telephoning Max at home to offer to take the case at a reduced contingent fee.","Mailing Max a truthful letter describing her services and offering to represent him.","Having her investigator stop by Max's house to hand him her card and pitch the firm."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 7.3(b) bars live person-to-person solicitation (in person, live telephone, or real-time video) when a significant motive is pecuniary gain, unless the person is a lawyer, has a family, close personal, or prior professional relationship with the lawyer, or routinely uses such services for business. Offering a free consultation does not change the pecuniary motive, and using an agent to do what the lawyer cannot is itself a violation (Rule 8.4(a)). A truthful targeted letter is permitted, subject to Rule 7.1 and Rule 7.3(c)'s bans on coercion and on contacting someone who has asked not to be solicited.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["solicitation","advertising","targeted-mail","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"pr-contingent-fee","prompt":"Lawyer Nia agrees to represent Omar in a personal injury case for one-third of any recovery. They agree orally, and Nia then emails Omar a letter stating the percentage and explaining that costs will be deducted before the fee is calculated. Omar never signs anything. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Nia subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because her emailed letter confirmed the fee agreement in writing.","No, because the rules require only that the fee basis be communicated, preferably in writing.","No, because a one-third contingent fee is reasonable for a personal injury case.","Yes, because a contingent fee agreement must be in a writing signed by the client."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.5(c) requires a contingent fee agreement to be in a writing signed by the client that states the method of calculating the fee, the percentages, the expenses to be deducted and whether they come out before or after the fee, and any expenses the client must pay regardless of outcome. A lawyer's confirming letter satisfies 'confirmed in writing' standards elsewhere, but not this signed-writing requirement. The 'preferably in writing' standard of Rule 1.5(b) governs non-contingent fees, and a reasonable percentage does not cure the missing signature.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","contingent","writing","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-personal-interest-v2","prompt":"Lawyer Priya is defending a client in a major lawsuit. Midway through the case, Priya begins serious job negotiations with the law firm representing the plaintiff. She believes the negotiations will not affect her work. Under the ABA Model Rules, what is required for Priya to continue the representation?","choices":["A reasonable belief she can still serve the client well, plus informed consent confirmed in writing.","Nothing, because a conflict based solely on the lawyer's own interests need not be disclosed to the client.","Disclosure of the negotiations to the client in writing; the client's consent to the conflict is not required.","Immediate withdrawal, because a conflict arising from job talks with opposing counsel cannot be consented to."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Seeking employment with the firm representing the opposing party creates a personal-interest conflict under Model Rule 1.7(a)(2) (cmt. [10]). It is consentable under Rule 1.7(b) if the lawyer reasonably believes she can still provide competent and diligent representation and the client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing. Disclosure alone is not consent, and the conflict is not automatically non-consentable.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","personal-interest","employment-negotiations","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-supervise-nonlawyer","prompt":"Lawyer Quinn's firm trains its paralegals on the ethics rules and reviews their work. Without Quinn's knowledge, a paralegal whom Quinn directly supervises phones the represented defendant in a firm case to 'clear up' some facts. Quinn learns of the call the next day, instructs the paralegal never to do it again, and promptly informs defense counsel. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Quinn subject to discipline?","choices":["Yes, because a lawyer is responsible for any rule violation committed by her staff.","No, because she had reasonable measures in place and took prompt remedial action.","Yes, because the call would have violated the no-contact rule if Quinn had made it.","No, because a lawyer answers only for misconduct by other lawyers in her firm."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 5.3 requires lawyers to make reasonable efforts to ensure that nonlawyer assistants act compatibly with the lawyer's obligations, and makes a lawyer responsible for an assistant's conduct that would violate the Rules only if she orders or ratifies it, or has supervisory authority and knows of it when its consequences can be avoided or mitigated but fails to take reasonable remedial action (Rule 5.3(c)). Quinn had reasonable measures in place and acted promptly once she learned. Responsibility is not vicarious, but lawyers are answerable for nonlawyer assistants under Rule 5.3.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["supervision","nonlawyer","remedial-action","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-fee-division-v2","prompt":"Lawyer Rosa refers a personal injury client to trial lawyer Sam at another firm. Rosa will do no work on the case but agrees in writing to assume joint financial and ethical responsibility for it. The client agrees, in a writing, that Rosa will receive one-third and Sam two-thirds of the fee, and the total fee is reasonable. Under the ABA Model Rules, is the fee division proper?","choices":["No, because a lawyer may not share in the fee for a case on which she does no work.","No, because paying a lawyer for recommending another lawyer is a prohibited referral fee.","Yes, because Rosa took joint responsibility and the client agreed in writing to the shares.","No, unless each lawyer's share is in proportion to the services each actually performs."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.5(e) permits lawyers in different firms to divide a fee if (1) the division is proportional to services performed or each lawyer assumes joint responsibility for the representation, (2) the client agrees to the arrangement, including each lawyer's share, and the agreement is confirmed in writing, and (3) the total fee is reasonable. Joint responsibility means financial and ethical responsibility as if the lawyers were partners (cmt. [7]), so proportional work is not required. A fee division that satisfies Rule 1.5(e) is not a prohibited payment for a recommendation under Rule 7.2(b).","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","division","referral","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-candor-false-evidence","prompt":"In a civil jury trial, Lawyer Tara's client testifies on direct examination that he never received a warning letter. Tara knows the testimony is false because the client showed her the letter weeks ago. During a recess she urges him to correct it, and he refuses. Under the ABA Model Rules, what must Tara do?","choices":["Withdraw from the case, but say nothing to the court about the false testimony.","Keep the matter confidential, because the client's admission is protected by Rule 1.6.","Disclose the falsity to the court only if the client gives informed consent to it.","Take reasonable remedial measures, including disclosure to the court if necessary."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"When a lawyer's client has offered material evidence the lawyer knows is false, Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) requires reasonable remedial measures, including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal. The lawyer first remonstrates; if that fails and withdrawal will not undo the effect of the false evidence, she must disclose (cmt. [10]). Rule 3.3(c) makes this duty apply even if the information is otherwise protected by Rule 1.6, and it continues to the conclusion of the proceeding, so silent withdrawal or waiting for client consent is not enough.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["candor","false-evidence","remedial-measures","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"pr-prospective-client","prompt":"Pat consulted Lawyer Uma about suing her employer and revealed damaging details about her own workplace misconduct, but she never hired Uma. Uma limited the interview to what was needed to evaluate the case. A month later, the employer asks Uma's partner, Vic, to defend it against Pat's suit. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Vic's firm take the case without Pat's consent?","choices":["Yes, if Uma is timely screened and denied any fee, and Pat gets prompt written notice.","Yes, without any precautions, because Pat never became a client of Uma or of the firm.","No, because Uma's disqualification is imputed to the firm and screening cannot cure it.","No, because the firm may proceed only if Pat gives informed consent, confirmed in writing."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 1.18(c), a lawyer who received information from a prospective client that could be significantly harmful in the same or a substantially related matter is disqualified, and that disqualification is ordinarily imputed to the firm. Rule 1.18(d) lets the firm proceed either with both the client's and the prospective client's informed consent, confirmed in writing, or if the lawyer took reasonable measures to limit the information received and is timely screened, is apportioned no part of the fee, and prompt written notice is given to the prospective client. Prospective clients are owed confidentiality duties (Rule 1.18(b)), so doing nothing is not an option.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["prospective-client","conflicts","screening","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"pr-report-misconduct","prompt":"Over lunch, Lawyer Wen's friend, Lawyer Xavier, admits that he padded his billing records to overcharge a client by $30,000, adding that he has since quietly refunded the money. Xavier is not Wen's client, and Wen did not learn this through any representation. Under the ABA Model Rules, what is Wen's obligation?","choices":["She may report Xavier, but is not required to, because the client was repaid.","She must inform the appropriate disciplinary authority of Xavier's conduct.","She must first urge Xavier to self-report, and may report only if he refuses.","She has no obligation, because Xavier told her about it as a personal friend."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 8.3(a) requires a lawyer who knows that another lawyer has violated the Rules in a way that raises a substantial question about honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness to inform the appropriate professional authority. Intentional overbilling is dishonest conduct (Rule 8.4(c)), and later repayment does not erase the violation. The only exceptions are for information protected by Rule 1.6 or gained in an approved lawyers assistance program; a friend's confidence is neither.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["reporting","misconduct","honesty","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-business-transaction","prompt":"Lawyer Yara is forming an LLC for her client, a baker. The client offers Yara a 10% membership interest in exchange for a $20,000 investment, on terms that are fair to the client. Yara explains the terms in a meeting, the client agrees orally, and Yara then emails the client a summary confirming the deal. Under the ABA Model Rules, has Yara complied with the rules on business transactions with clients?","choices":["Yes, because the terms were fair and the client's informed consent was promptly confirmed in writing.","Yes, because a lawyer may invest in a client's business on terms open to other investors.","No, because she needed to advise in writing about independent counsel and get signed consent.","No, because a lawyer may never acquire an ownership interest in a current client's business."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.8(a) permits a business transaction with a client only if the terms are fair and reasonable and fully disclosed in writing, the client is advised in writing of the desirability of seeking independent counsel and given a reasonable opportunity to do so, and the client gives informed consent in a writing signed by the client to the essential terms and the lawyer's role. A lawyer's email confirming oral consent does not meet the signed-writing requirement. Such investments are not banned outright; they are regulated.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["business-transaction","client","informed-consent","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"mpre-gift-from-client","prompt":"Lawyer Zoe's elderly client, who is not related to her, asks Zoe to revise his will to leave her $50,000 'for years of kindness.' Zoe never suggested a gift. Under the ABA Model Rules, which is the proper course?","choices":["Zoe may draft the bequest herself, because she did not solicit the gift from the client.","Zoe may draft the bequest if the client gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.","Zoe may not accept the bequest at all, even if another lawyer prepares the will.","Zoe may not draft the bequest, but may accept it if another lawyer prepares the will."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.8(c) forbids a lawyer to solicit a substantial gift from a client or to prepare an instrument giving the lawyer a substantial gift, unless the lawyer is related to the client. The rule contains no client-consent exception. The client may still make the gift if an independent lawyer prepares the instrument (cmt. [7]), so the bequest itself is not forbidden.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["gifts","client","wills","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-contact-represented","prompt":"Lawyer Abe represents the plaintiff in a property-line lawsuit. The defendant, who is represented by counsel, phones Abe directly and says, 'My lawyer is too slow. I want to talk settlement with you, and you have my permission.' Under the ABA Model Rules, may Abe discuss settlement with the defendant?","choices":["No, unless the defendant's lawyer consents or the contact is authorized by law or court order.","Yes, because the defendant himself initiated the call and expressly consented to the conversation.","Yes, if Abe first advises the defendant to consult his own lawyer before agreeing.","No, unless Abe confines the discussion to the facts and avoids any settlement terms."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 4.2 bars a lawyer from communicating about the subject of the representation with a person the lawyer knows is represented in the matter, unless that person's lawyer consents or the contact is authorized by law or court order. The rule applies even if the represented person initiates or consents to the contact (cmt. [3]); the consent must come from the person's lawyer. Discussing the facts of the dispute is equally prohibited because it concerns the subject of the representation.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["communication","represented-party","no-contact","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-adverse-authority","prompt":"Lawyer Bea is arguing a motion in a state trial court. She finds two cases directly adverse to her position that opposing counsel has not cited: a decision of her own state's intermediate appellate court, and a decision of another state's supreme court. Under the ABA Model Rules, what must Bea do?","choices":["Disclose both decisions, because each is directly adverse to her client's position.","Disclose the in-state appellate decision; she need not disclose the out-of-state case.","Disclose neither, because finding adverse authority is opposing counsel's job.","Disclose the in-state appellate decision only if the judge asks about contrary authority."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 3.3(a)(2) requires a lawyer to disclose legal authority in the controlling jurisdiction known to be directly adverse to the client's position and not disclosed by opposing counsel. An intermediate appellate decision of the forum state binds its trial courts, so it must be disclosed; a sister state's decision is not controlling and need not be. The duty does not depend on the judge asking.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["candor","tribunal","adverse-authority","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-prosecutor-disclosure","prompt":"Before a robbery trial, Prosecutor Cal learns that the only eyewitness first told police the robber was 'a much taller man' than the defendant. Cal believes the witness's later identification is reliable and that the earlier statement would be inadmissible hearsay. The defense has not asked for it. Under the ABA Model Rules, what is Cal's obligation?","choices":["He need not disclose the statement, because the defense never requested it.","He need not disclose the statement, because he believes it would be inadmissible.","He must timely disclose the statement to the defense, because it tends to negate guilt.","He need not disclose the statement, because he reasonably believes the defendant is guilty."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 3.8(d) requires a prosecutor to make timely disclosure to the defense of all evidence or information known to the prosecutor that tends to negate the guilt of the accused or mitigate the offense. The duty does not depend on a defense request, admissibility, or the prosecutor's view of guilt, and it is broader than the constitutional Brady materiality standard (ABA Formal Op. 09-454).","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["prosecutor","disclosure","exculpatory","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"mpre-sexual-relations","prompt":"While representing Dev in a contentious divorce, Lawyer Ella begins a consensual sexual relationship with him. Dev is a competent adult who signs a written statement consenting to the relationship, and Ella's work on the case remains excellent. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Ella subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because Dev gave informed consent to the relationship in a signed writing.","No, because the relationship has not impaired the quality of her representation.","No, because the rule bars relationships only with clients of diminished capacity.","Yes, because the relationship began after the client-lawyer relationship existed."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.8(j) prohibits sexual relations with a client unless a consensual sexual relationship existed when the client-lawyer relationship began. The prohibition applies regardless of client consent, the client's competence, or the absence of actual harm to the representation.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["sexual-relations","client","conflicts","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"mpre-fee-increase","prompt":"Lawyer Finn's engagement letter sets his rate at $300 per hour and says nothing about future increases. Six months into the litigation, Finn notifies the client that his rate will be $400 per hour starting next month. The client objects. A $400 rate is within the range customary in the community. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Finn begin billing $400 per hour?","choices":["No, unless the client agrees to the reasonable increase after being informed.","Yes, because he gave the client reasonable advance notice of the new hourly rate.","Yes, because the new rate is within the range customary in the community.","No, because a fee agreement may never be modified once the representation begins."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 1.5(a)–(b), any change in the basis or rate of a fee must be communicated to the client, and the fee must be reasonable. When the original agreement did not reserve a right to periodic increases, a mid-representation increase generally requires the client's informed agreement, and the modified fee must be reasonable (ABA Formal Op. 11-458). Notice alone, or a customary rate, does not bind an objecting client, but modifications are permitted with consent.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","modification","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-screening-lateral","prompt":"Lawyer Gia spent two years at Firm A defending Hale Corp. in a fraud suit, working on it extensively. She then joins Firm B, which represents the plaintiff against Hale in that same suit. Hale refuses to consent to Firm B's continued representation. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Firm B continue representing the plaintiff?","choices":["No, because Gia's substantial work on the matter at Firm A makes screening unavailable.","Yes, if Gia is timely screened, gets no part of the fee, and Hale gets written notice.","No, because Gia's conflict is imputed to Firm B unless Hale consents in writing.","Yes, if Gia promises not to discuss the case, even without a formal screen or notice."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Gia herself is personally disqualified under Model Rule 1.9(a), but Rule 1.10(a)(2) prevents imputation to her new firm if she is timely screened, is apportioned no part of the fee, and written notice (describing the screen) is promptly given to the former client. The Model Rule allows screening regardless of how deeply the lateral worked on the matter; the 'substantially participated' limit is the California approach (Cal. RPC 1.10(a)(2)) and that of some other states. An informal promise without a screen and notice does not satisfy the rule.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","screening","lateral","imputation","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"mpre-truthfulness-others","prompt":"In settlement talks over a personal injury claim, plaintiff's lawyer Hugo makes several statements to defense counsel. Under the ABA Model Rules, which statement would subject Hugo to discipline?","choices":["'My client won't take a penny less than $500,000,' when she has authorized $300,000.","'A jury will value this case at over $750,000,' offered as Hugo's view of its worth.","'My client's surgeon has recommended spinal surgery,' when no doctor has recommended it.","'I won't discuss my client's bottom line,' when asked for the lowest amount she would accept."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 4.1(a) prohibits knowingly making a false statement of material fact or law to a third person. Under generally accepted negotiation conventions, estimates of value and statements about a party's intentions regarding an acceptable settlement are ordinarily not treated as statements of material fact (cmt. [2]). Inventing a surgeon's recommendation is a false statement of material fact, while declining to reveal a bottom line is permissible.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["truthfulness","negotiation","third-parties","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-aggregate-settlement","prompt":"Lawyer Ivy represents four homeowners suing the same builder. The builder offers $1 million to settle all four claims. Ivy proposes an allocation based on each home's damage and explains every claim and share to all four clients. Three clients sign written consents. The fourth agrees by phone, and Ivy sends him a letter confirming his oral consent. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Ivy accept the settlement?","choices":["Yes, because the fourth client's oral consent was promptly confirmed in writing by Ivy.","Yes, because a majority of the clients approved and the allocation is fair to all.","No, because an aggregate settlement of separate claims requires court approval.","No, because each client must give informed consent in a writing signed by that client."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.8(g) bars an aggregate settlement for two or more clients unless each client gives informed consent in a writing signed by the client, after disclosure of the existence and nature of all the claims and the participation of each person in the settlement. A lawyer's letter confirming oral consent does not satisfy the signed-writing requirement, and majority approval cannot bind a dissenting or non-signing client. Court approval is required in class actions, not ordinary multi-client settlements.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","aggregate-settlement","multiple-clients","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"mpre-opposing-sides","prompt":"Lawyer Jay represents a driver and her passenger as co-plaintiffs suing another motorist over a collision. After the defendant claims the driver was partly at fault, the passenger wants to add a claim against the driver in the same lawsuit. Both clients want Jay to stay on and are willing to sign written consents. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Jay represent both clients going forward?","choices":["No, because one client's claim against another in the same litigation is not consentable.","Yes, if he reasonably believes he can represent both and each gives informed consent, confirmed in writing.","Yes, if each client gives informed consent in a signed writing after getting independent advice.","No, unless the court approves the joint representation after a hearing on the conflict."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.7(b)(3) makes a conflict non-consentable when the representation involves the assertion of a claim by one client against another client represented by the lawyer in the same litigation. Client consent, however formal, cannot cure it, and the rule does not provide for court approval. Jay may not represent both; whether he may continue for either client depends on Rule 1.9.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","direct-adversity","nonconsentable","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"mpre-material-limitation","prompt":"Lawyer Kim represents Owner in negotiating a lease of office space to Tenant. Kim also owns a 30% stake in a competing building that Tenant is considering. Kim is confident she can bargain hard for Owner anyway. Under the ABA Model Rules, does Kim have a conflict of interest?","choices":["No, because a conflict arises only when a lawyer represents clients with adverse interests.","Yes, because her financial stake creates a significant risk of materially limiting her work.","No, because she reasonably believes her judgment will not actually be affected by the stake.","No, because Tenant is not her client, so the rules do not protect Tenant's interests."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 1.7(a)(2), a concurrent conflict exists when there is a significant risk that the representation will be materially limited by the lawyer's responsibilities to another client, a former client, a third person, or by the lawyer's own interests. Kim's competing ownership stake creates such a risk. Her belief that she can perform well goes to whether the conflict is consentable under Rule 1.7(b)(1), not whether it exists, so she needs Owner's informed consent, confirmed in writing.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","material-limitation","personal-interest","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-confidentiality-crime-fraud","prompt":"Lawyer Leo prepared offering documents for a client's real estate fund. Leo later learns, through confidential client communications, that the client used those documents to defraud investors of $2 million, and that the investors could still recover some of the money if they act quickly. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Leo disclose the fraud?","choices":["Yes, and he must do so, because his services were used to commit the fraud.","No, because the fraud is complete, and disclosure is allowed only to prevent future harm.","Yes, to the extent reasonably necessary to mitigate or rectify the investors' loss.","No, because disclosure is allowed only to prevent death or substantial bodily harm."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.6(b)(3) permits a lawyer to reveal information to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent, mitigate, or rectify substantial financial injury that is reasonably certain to result or has resulted from the client's crime or fraud in furtherance of which the client used the lawyer's services. It covers completed frauds and is permissive, not mandatory. (California has no comparable financial-harm exception.)","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["confidentiality","crime-fraud","financial-harm","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"mpre-confidentiality-ethics-advice","prompt":"Lawyer Mae is unsure whether a client's recent conduct requires her to withdraw. She calls an experienced ethics lawyer at another firm and, to get useful advice, describes the client's situation in detail, including confidential facts. The client did not consent. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Mae subject to discipline?","choices":["Yes, because she revealed confidential information without the client's informed consent.","No, because the ethics lawyer is bound by privilege, so no disclosure legally occurred.","Yes, because she was required to frame the facts as a hypothetical that hides the client.","No, because she may reveal what is reasonably necessary to get advice on her ethics duties."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.6(b)(4) permits a lawyer to reveal information to the extent reasonably necessary to secure legal advice about the lawyer's compliance with the Rules. Using a hypothetical is often wise and is permitted (cmt. [4]), but it is not required when real facts are reasonably necessary. The disclosure is permitted by the rule itself, not because sharing information with another lawyer is somehow not a disclosure.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["confidentiality","ethics-advice","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-subordinate-lawyer","prompt":"Associate Ned asks his supervising partner whether a document requested in discovery must be produced. The question is genuinely debatable, and the partner reasonably concludes the document falls outside the request. Ned follows that instruction. A court later rules the document should have been produced. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Ned subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because he acted on a supervisor's reasonable resolution of an arguable question.","Yes, because a lawyer is bound by the rules even when acting at another's direction.","No, because a subordinate is never responsible for following a partner's instructions.","Yes, because he should have reported the partner's decision to disciplinary authorities."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 5.2(a) states the general rule that a lawyer is bound by the Rules even when acting at another's direction, but Rule 5.2(b) provides that a subordinate does not violate the Rules by acting in accordance with a supervisor's reasonable resolution of an arguable question of professional duty. The safe harbor is not blanket immunity: following an order to commit a clear violation is still sanctionable. A reasonable judgment call on a debatable question is not reportable misconduct.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["supervision","subordinate","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-supervisor-lawyer-v2","prompt":"Partner Olga directly supervises associate Pete. Olga learns that Pete knowingly misstated a key fact in a brief supporting a pending motion; the court has not yet ruled. Olga does nothing, reasoning that the misconduct was Pete's alone. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Olga subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because she did not order or participate in Pete's misstatement to the court.","Yes, because she knew in time to mitigate the harm and failed to take remedial action.","No, because each lawyer is responsible only for his or her own professional conduct.","Yes, because a partner is vicariously responsible for every violation by firm lawyers."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 5.1(c)(2), a lawyer with managerial or direct supervisory authority is responsible for another lawyer's violation if she knows of the conduct when its consequences can be avoided or mitigated but fails to take reasonable remedial action. Because the court had not ruled, Olga could have mitigated the harm by having the brief corrected. Responsibility is not vicarious: firm status alone does not make a partner answerable for every violation.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["supervision","partner","remedial-action","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"mpre-advertising-specialist","prompt":"Lawyer Rae has practiced family law exclusively for 15 years but holds no certification from any certifying organization. She is drafting her website. Under the ABA Model Rules, which statement about what she may say is correct?","choices":["She may call herself a 'certified specialist' in family law, based on her 15 years of experience.","She may claim board certification if her website also says past results do not guarantee outcomes.","She may say she 'specializes in' family law, but may not claim to be a certified specialist.","She may not use the word 'specialist' in any form, because no approved body has certified her."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 7.2(c) (formerly Rule 7.4(d)) forbids a lawyer to state or imply that she is certified as a specialist unless she has been certified by an organization approved by an appropriate authority or accredited by the ABA, and the certifying organization is clearly identified. A lawyer may, however, truthfully state that she 'specializes in' or concentrates in a field based on experience, subject to Rule 7.1's ban on misleading statements (Rule 7.2 cmt.). Experience cannot substitute for certification, and a disclaimer cannot cure a false claim of certification.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["advertising","specialization","certification","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"mpre-advertising-misleading","prompt":"Lawyer Sal, who has won 95% of his trials, puts up a billboard reading, 'Hurt in a crash? We WIN. Guaranteed.' No client or prospective client has complained about it. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Sal subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because the claim is substantially true in light of his 95% trial success rate.","No, because discipline for an advertisement requires that someone relied on it.","No, if he adds a footnote stating that every case is different and results may vary.","Yes, because promising a guaranteed result is misleading whatever his track record."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 7.1 prohibits false or misleading communications about a lawyer's services, including statements likely to create unjustified expectations about results (cmt. [3]). A guarantee of winning is inherently misleading because no lawyer can promise an outcome, however strong his record. A disclaimer that contradicts the headline does not cure it, and discipline does not require proof that anyone relied on the ad.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["advertising","misleading","guarantee","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"mpre-solicitation-live-v2","prompt":"Lawyer Tess, who handles commercial contract disputes, wants new business. Each contact below would be live, by phone or in person, and motivated by pecuniary gain, and none of the people has any prior relationship with Tess. Under the ABA Model Rules, which contact is permissible?","choices":["Calling the CFO of a manufacturer that regularly hires outside counsel for such disputes.","Visiting a homeowner whose house just burned down to offer a free insurance consultation.","Calling a retiree who was just sued by her landlord, offering to defend her at a discount.","Approaching a stranger outside the courthouse who was just served with a lawsuit."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 7.3(b) prohibits live person-to-person solicitation when a significant motive is pecuniary gain, unless the person contacted is a lawyer, has a family, close personal, or prior business or professional relationship with the lawyer, or routinely uses for business purposes the type of legal services offered. A manufacturer's CFO who regularly hires outside counsel for contract disputes falls within the last exception. Free or discounted services do not remove the pecuniary motive.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["solicitation","advertising","live-contact","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-organization-as-client","prompt":"Lawyer Uri, outside counsel to Vista Corp., interviews a mid-level Vista employee about possible bribes paid by the sales department. The employee nervously asks, 'Are you my lawyer too? Will this stay between us?' Under the ABA Model Rules, how should Uri respond?","choices":["Assure her that the conversation is privileged and that he will keep it from Vista's management.","Explain that he represents Vista, not her, and that Vista controls use of her statements.","Tell her that, as company counsel, he represents both Vista and employees acting within their jobs.","Decline to say whom he represents, because clarifying his role might discourage her candor."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 1.13(a), a lawyer retained by an organization represents the entity acting through its constituents, not its individual employees. When the organization's interests may be adverse to the constituent's, Rule 1.13(f) (and Rule 4.3) requires the lawyer to explain the identity of the client. The privilege for the interview belongs to the company, which may waive it, so promising to keep her statements from management would be false.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["organization-client","entity-representation","upjohn","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"pr-frivolous-claims","prompt":"Lawyer Vera's client wants to challenge a 30-year-old state supreme court precedent that bars his claim. Vera believes recent developments in related doctrine give her a good-faith argument that the precedent should be overruled, though she thinks the client will probably lose. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Vera file the claim?","choices":["No, because a claim barred by controlling precedent is frivolous by definition.","No, because a lawyer may assert only claims she believes are likely to succeed.","Yes, because a good-faith argument for reversing existing law is not frivolous.","Yes, because the client, not the lawyer, decides which legal arguments to make."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 3.1 prohibits frivolous claims but expressly allows a claim supported by a good-faith argument for the extension, modification, or reversal of existing law. A claim is not frivolous merely because the lawyer expects it to fail. Legal tactics and arguments are generally for the lawyer, in consultation with the client (Rule 1.2(a)), so the choice that leaves the decision to the client states the wrong reason.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["frivolous-claims","advocacy","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"pr-mandatory-withdrawal","prompt":"Lawyer Wade is drafting documents to transfer assets out of a client's business. Wade learns that the client intends to use the transfers to hide assets from creditors in a scheme Wade now knows is fraudulent. The client insists that Wade finish the documents. Under the ABA Model Rules, what must Wade do?","choices":["Finish the documents, but advise the client in writing that the plan may be unlawful.","Disclose the scheme to the creditors, after which he may withdraw from the matter.","Decide for himself whether to withdraw, because withdrawal here is permitted but optional.","Withdraw, because continuing would require him to assist the client's fraud."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.16(a)(1) requires withdrawal when the representation will result in a violation of the Rules or other law, and Rule 1.2(d) forbids assisting a client in conduct the lawyer knows is fraudulent. Because the client insists on Wade's continued help with the fraud, withdrawal is mandatory, not merely permissive. Disclosure to the creditors may be permitted under Rule 1.6(b)(2), but it is not required.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["withdrawal","mandatory","fraud","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-trial-publicity","prompt":"Shortly before jury selection in a high-profile assault trial, defense lawyer Xena tells reporters that her client 'passed a polygraph test, which proves he is innocent.' The client did pass the test. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Xena subject to discipline?","choices":["Yes, because publicizing test results is likely to materially prejudice a jury trial.","No, because truthful statements about a pending case are protected by the First Amendment.","No, because the limits on trial publicity apply only to prosecutors' statements.","Yes, because a lawyer in a pending case may not make any statement to the media."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 3.6(a) prohibits extrajudicial statements a lawyer knows or reasonably should know will be publicly disseminated and have a substantial likelihood of materially prejudicing an adjudicative proceeding. Statements about the results of any examination or test in a criminal case, especially one tried to a jury, are among those most likely to be prejudicial (cmt. [5]). The rule binds all lawyers in the matter, not just prosecutors, and Rule 3.6(b) permits some statements, such as the general nature of the defense.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["trial-publicity","media","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"pr-advance-litigation-costs","prompt":"Lawyer Yuki represents an injured client on a contingent fee. The client cannot afford the court filing fee, an expert's charges, or this month's rent. Under the ABA Model Rules, which may Yuki pay?","choices":["All three, as long as the client remains personally liable to repay every amount advanced.","The filing fee and expert charges, repayable only if the client recovers, but not the rent.","None of them, because giving financial assistance to a litigation client is prohibited.","All three, because rent may be paid as a modest gift to a client who is indigent."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.8(e)(1) allows a lawyer to advance court costs and litigation expenses, with repayment contingent on the outcome, but otherwise bars financial assistance to a client in connection with pending litigation. The 2020 exception in Rule 1.8(e)(3) for modest gifts toward basic living expenses such as rent applies only to lawyers representing indigent clients pro bono, not to a contingent-fee representation. Living expenses are barred even if the client promises repayment.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","litigation-expenses","financial-assistance","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-mpre-tech-competence","prompt":"Lawyer Ada uses a generative AI tool to draft a brief and files it without reading the cases it cites. Two of the citations turn out to be fictitious. Ada did not know they were fake. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Ada subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because she did not knowingly make a false statement of law to the court.","No, because a lawyer may reasonably rely on widely used legal research software.","Yes, because competence requires understanding a tool's risks and checking its output.","Yes, because the rules bar using generative AI for court filings without client consent."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Competence under Model Rule 1.1 includes keeping abreast of the benefits and risks of relevant technology (cmt. [8]) and the thoroughness and preparation reasonably necessary for the matter. Filing a brief without reviewing the authorities it cites falls short of that standard even if the lawyer lacked the knowledge needed for a Rule 3.3(a)(1) violation. ABA Formal Opinion 512 (2024) does not ban generative AI; it requires lawyers to review and verify its output.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["competence","technology","generative-ai","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-crime-fraud-counseling","prompt":"A restaurant owner asks Lawyer Cy whether paying his workers in cash 'off the books' to avoid payroll taxes would be illegal and what penalties he could face. Cy explains that it would be tax evasion and describes the civil and criminal penalties. When the owner asks how to keep the IRS from noticing, Cy refuses to help. Under the ABA Model Rules, did Cy act properly?","choices":["No, because explaining the penalties helped the owner weigh whether to commit a crime.","No, because once the owner disclosed a criminal plan, Cy was required to withdraw.","Yes, but only if Cy also reports the owner's stated plan to the taxing authorities.","Yes, because a lawyer may discuss the legal consequences of any proposed conduct."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.2(d) forbids a lawyer to counsel or assist a client in conduct the lawyer knows is criminal or fraudulent, but expressly permits the lawyer to discuss the legal consequences of any proposed course of conduct. Cy explained the law and refused to help conceal the scheme, which is exactly the line the rule draws. Withdrawal is mandatory only if the representation will result in a violation (Rule 1.16(a)), and nothing requires Cy to report the client.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["scope","crime-fraud","counseling","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-mpre-prohibited-contingent-fees","prompt":"Lawyer Dee handles both family-law and criminal matters and is considering several fee arrangements. Under the ABA Model Rules, which of the following arrangements is permissible?","choices":["A 25% contingent fee to collect $40,000 of past-due child support under a decree.","A $10,000 fee payable only if her client is acquitted of the felony charge against him.","A fee equal to 10% of whatever property settlement her client receives in the divorce.","A $5,000 bonus, on top of hourly fees, payable only if the court grants the divorce."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.5(d) prohibits contingent fees for representing a defendant in a criminal case and, in domestic relations matters, fees contingent on securing a divorce or on the amount of alimony, support, or a property settlement. The rule does not bar a contingent fee to collect past-due support or alimony owed under an existing order (cmt. [6]). A bonus payable only if the divorce is granted is a prohibited contingency, even when added to hourly fees.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","contingent","domestic-relations","criminal","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-mpre-self-defense-online-review","prompt":"A former client posts an online review calling Lawyer Eli 'incompetent' and blaming him for losing her case. Eli replies publicly in the review thread, quoting the client's confidential admissions to show the case was weak from the start. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Eli subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because a lawyer may reveal information to respond to accusations about his work.","Yes, because the self-defense exception does not cover replies to online criticism.","No, because the client waived confidentiality by publicly discussing the representation.","Yes, because a lawyer may never reveal client information to defend himself, even in a fee suit."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.6(b)(5) permits disclosure to establish a claim or defense in a controversy between lawyer and client, or to respond to allegations in any proceeding concerning the lawyer's representation. ABA Formal Opinion 496 (2021) concludes that a negative online review is not such a controversy or proceeding, so a lawyer may respond only without revealing protected information. A client's public criticism is not a waiver, and the exception does apply in fee suits and malpractice or disciplinary proceedings.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["confidentiality","self-defense","online-review","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-mpre-former-government-lawyer","prompt":"As a lawyer for a state environmental agency, Fay personally and substantially handled an enforcement action against Acme Co. She then joins a private firm, which Acme asks to defend it in that same enforcement action. The agency has not consented. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Fay's firm represent Acme?","choices":["No, because Fay's personal disqualification is imputed to every lawyer in her new firm.","Yes, if Acme, as the client, gives informed consent, confirmed in writing, to the conflict.","Yes, if Fay is screened in time, gets no fee share, and the agency gets written notice.","Yes, without a screen, because the rule restricts only lawyers moving into government jobs."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 1.11(a)(2), Fay may not personally represent Acme in a matter in which she participated personally and substantially as a government lawyer unless the agency gives informed consent, confirmed in writing. Rule 1.11(b) keeps her disqualification from spreading to the firm if she is timely screened, is apportioned no part of the fee, and written notice is promptly given to the agency. Acme's consent is irrelevant because the protected interest is the government's, and Rule 1.11 governs these former-government conflicts rather than the general imputation rule of Rule 1.10.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","former-government","screening","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-mpre-reporting-up","prompt":"In-house lawyer Gwen learns that her company's sales vice president is booking fictitious sales to hit bonus targets, conduct that violates the law and is likely to cause the company substantial injury. The vice president ignores Gwen's warning to stop. Under the ABA Model Rules, what should Gwen do next?","choices":["Report the conduct to government regulators, because it is likely a crime.","Take no further action, because the vice president has authority over sales.","Withdraw from representing the company and explain her reasons to its auditors.","Refer the matter to higher authority, including the board if warranted."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"When a lawyer knows that a constituent is acting in violation of law in a way likely to cause substantial injury to the organization, Model Rule 1.13(b) requires the lawyer to proceed in the organization's best interest, which ordinarily means referring the matter to higher authority, including the highest authority if warranted. Reporting outside the organization is permitted under Rule 1.13(c) only after the highest authority fails to act and only in limited circumstances; it is never the first step. Withdrawal and outside disclosure are premature here.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["organization-client","reporting-up","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-diminished-capacity","prompt":"Lawyer Hal's longtime client, an 88-year-old widow, shows signs of dementia and tells Hal she plans to wire most of her savings to a 'friend' she met online. Hal reasonably believes that, even with support, she has substantial difficulty evaluating information and making decisions, that she is at risk of substantial financial harm unless someone acts, and that she cannot adequately protect her own interests. Under the ABA Model Rules, may Hal contact the client's adult daughter about the situation?","choices":["Yes, revealing only what is reasonably necessary to protect the client's interests.","No, because contacting family would reveal confidential information without consent.","No, because he must first petition a court to appoint a guardian for the client.","Yes, and he may share her entire file so the daughter can take over her affairs."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.14(b), as revised in 2026 (the rule now speaks of a client with 'decision-making limitations' rather than 'diminished capacity'), permits a lawyer to take reasonably necessary protective action when he reasonably believes the client has such limitations, is at risk of substantial physical, financial, or other harm unless action is taken, and cannot adequately act in her own interest. Consulting family members who can protect the client is a recognized protective measure (cmt. [12]). Rule 1.14(c) lets the lawyer reveal information relating to the representation when doing so, but only to the extent he reasonably believes necessary to protect the client's interests, so handing over the entire file goes too far. A guardianship petition is not a prerequisite; the lawyer should favor the least restrictive action (cmt. [15]).","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["diminished-capacity","protective-action","confidentiality","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-disputed-funds","prompt":"Lawyer Ike's signed contingent-fee agreement entitles him to one-third of any recovery. He receives a $90,000 settlement and deposits it in his client trust account. The client now claims the fee should be no more than $20,000. Under the ABA Model Rules, what should Ike do?","choices":["Withdraw his full $30,000 fee, since the signed agreement supports it, and pay the client $60,000.","Pay the client $60,000, take $20,000, and keep the other $10,000 in trust until it is resolved.","Keep the entire $90,000 in trust, paying no one until the fee dispute is fully resolved.","Pay the client $60,000 and move the disputed $10,000 to his operating account pending resolution."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 1.15(e), when the lawyer and client both claim an interest in funds, the disputed portion must be kept separate (in trust) until the dispute is resolved, and undisputed portions must be promptly distributed (cmt. [3]). Here the client concedes a $20,000 fee, so $60,000 belongs to the client and $20,000 to Ike without dispute; only $10,000 is contested. A lawyer may not take disputed funds on the strength of his own reading of the agreement or hold undisputed client money hostage.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["trust-account","disputed-funds","fees","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-mpre-withdrawal-denied","prompt":"Lawyer Jen's litigation client has not paid her bills for four months despite repeated warnings that she would withdraw. Trial is three weeks away. Jen moves to withdraw, as local rules require, but the judge denies the motion. Under the ABA Model Rules, what must Jen do?","choices":["Stop working on the case, because nonpayment after warnings is good cause to withdraw.","Continue only after the client pays the overdue balance, pausing work until then.","Continue representing the client, because the tribunal has ordered her to do so.","Withdraw anyway, provided she gives reasonable notice and returns the client's file."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.16(b)(5) permits withdrawal when a client substantially fails to fulfill an obligation to the lawyer after a warning, but Rule 1.16(c) requires compliance with rules requiring the tribunal's permission. When ordered to continue, a lawyer must continue the representation notwithstanding good cause for terminating it. Slowing or halting work until paid would also violate the duty of diligence (Rule 1.3).","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["withdrawal","tribunal","nonpayment","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-mpre-defendant-testimony","prompt":"Criminal defendant Kurt insists on testifying that he was out of town on the night of the burglary. His lawyer, Lia, reasonably believes the alibi is false, but she does not know that it is false. Under the ABA Model Rules, what should Lia do?","choices":["Refuse to call Kurt, because a lawyer may refuse to offer evidence she reasonably believes is false.","Call Kurt, but tell the judge privately that she doubts the truth of his alibi.","Move to withdraw, because she may not present testimony she reasonably believes is false.","Allow Kurt to testify, because she does not know that his testimony will be false."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 3.3(a)(3) lets a lawyer refuse to offer evidence she reasonably believes is false, but it expressly excepts the testimony of a criminal defendant. Because of the defendant's right to testify, the lawyer must honor the decision unless she actually knows the testimony will be false (cmt. [9]). Telling the judge of her doubts would reveal confidential information and undercut her client, and mere belief of falsity does not require withdrawal.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["candor","criminal-defendant","perjury","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-mpre-witness-payments","prompt":"Lawyer Mo is preparing for a civil trial and plans to call several witnesses. Under the ABA Model Rules, which of the following payments to witnesses is proper?","choices":["Reimbursing an eyewitness for wages she reasonably loses while attending trial.","Paying an expert a fee that increases if the jury awards more than $1 million.","Promising an eyewitness a $1,000 bonus if the jury finds for Mo's client.","Paying an eyewitness's travel costs only if her testimony helps Mo's client win."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 3.4(b) forbids offering an inducement to a witness that is prohibited by law. A lawyer may pay a witness's expenses and may compensate an expert on terms permitted by law, but may not pay an occurrence witness a fee for testifying or pay an expert a contingent fee (cmt. [3]). Reimbursing a fact witness for the reasonable value of time lost in attending or testifying, such as lost wages, is also proper so long as it is not conditioned on the content of the testimony (ABA Formal Op. 96-402). Payments contingent on the content of the testimony or the outcome of the case are improper.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fairness","witnesses","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-mpre-no-contact-organization","prompt":"Lawyer Nora represents a cyclist suing Trucking Co., which is represented by counsel, over a collision with one of its trucks. Without the consent of Trucking Co.'s lawyer, which of the following people may Nora interview about the collision under the ABA Model Rules?","choices":["The truck's driver, who still works for Trucking Co. and whose driving caused the crash.","A former dispatcher who left Trucking Co. last year and who has no lawyer of her own.","Trucking Co.'s safety manager, who regularly consults with the company's lawyers on the case.","Trucking Co.'s vice president of operations, who has authority to settle the claim."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"When an organization is represented, Model Rule 4.2 bars contact without its lawyer's consent with a constituent who supervises, directs, or regularly consults with the organization's lawyer about the matter, who has authority to obligate the organization, or whose act or omission may be imputed to the organization for liability (cmt. [7]). Former employees are not covered, so Nora may interview the dispatcher. She must still avoid seeking privileged information (Rule 4.4(a)) and must comply with Rule 4.3.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["no-contact","organization","represented-party","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-unrepresented-person","prompt":"Lawyer Otto represents a landlord negotiating a move-out agreement with a tenant who has no lawyer. The tenant asks Otto, 'Is this a fair deal for me? Should I sign it?' The tenant's interests conflict with the landlord's. Under the ABA Model Rules, how should Otto respond?","choices":["Tell the tenant the deal is fair, as long as Otto honestly believes that it is.","Refuse to discuss the agreement at all until the tenant retains a lawyer.","Explain that he represents the landlord and suggest the tenant consult a lawyer.","Explain the tenant's legal rights and defenses fully so the tenant is not misled."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Model Rule 4.3, a lawyer dealing with an unrepresented person whose interests conflict with the client's must not state or imply that he is disinterested, must correct any misunderstanding about his role, and may not give legal advice other than the advice to secure counsel. Telling the tenant the deal is fair or explaining the tenant's rights and defenses would be legal advice. Otto may still negotiate the terms and explain his own client's position (cmt. [2]).","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["unrepresented-person","negotiation","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-multijurisdictional-practice","prompt":"Lawyer Pam is licensed only in Colorado and represents a Colorado client in a lawsuit pending in a Colorado state court. She travels to Arizona for a week to depose Arizona witnesses in that case. She does not associate with an Arizona lawyer. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Pam engaged in the unauthorized practice of law in Arizona?","choices":["Yes, because she is practicing in Arizona without associating with an Arizona lawyer.","Yes, unless she first obtains pro hac vice admission from an Arizona court.","No, because a lawyer may practice in any state for up to 30 days in a calendar year.","No, because it is temporary work reasonably related to a case where she may appear."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Rule 5.5(c)(2) permits a lawyer admitted in another U.S. jurisdiction to provide legal services on a temporary basis that are reasonably related to a pending or potential proceeding before a tribunal in which the lawyer is authorized to appear. Association with a local lawyer is a separate, alternative safe harbor (Rule 5.5(c)(1)), and pro hac vice admission is needed only to appear before an Arizona tribunal. The Model Rules have no day-count rule; the limit is that she may not establish a systematic and continuous presence (Rule 5.5(b)).","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["unauthorized-practice","multijurisdictional","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-dishonesty-outside-practice","prompt":"Lawyer Quin's conduct below is unrelated to his law practice. Under the ABA Model Rules, which conduct is most likely to subject him to professional discipline?","choices":["Telling a buyer his used car had never been in an accident, knowing it had been.","Pleading guilty to a single misdemeanor DUI that caused no injury or damage.","Filing for personal bankruptcy after his consulting side business failed.","Losing a breach-of-contract suit brought by the contractor who remodeled his home."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Rule 8.4(c) makes it professional misconduct to engage in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation, whether or not it occurs in the practice of law. Under Rule 8.4(b), criminal conduct is disciplinable when it reflects adversely on honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness; a single minor offense such as a first DUI ordinarily does not, though a pattern may (cmt. [2]). Bankruptcy and losing an ordinary civil suit are not misconduct.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["misconduct","dishonesty","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-cjc-ex-parte","prompt":"Judge Rhee is presiding over a bench trial involving complex medical-device patents. Under the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct, which of the following may the judge do?","choices":["Visit the manufacturing plant on her own to see how the device is made.","Discuss the legal issues in the case with her own law clerk in chambers.","Call a law professor for advice on the patent issue without telling the parties.","Read a letter on the merits sent by one party, then discard it without telling the other."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 2.9(A)(3) permits a judge to consult with court staff, such as law clerks, whose function is to help the judge decide cases. A judge may not independently investigate the facts (Rule 2.9(C)), may obtain a disinterested expert's advice on the law only with advance notice to the parties and a chance to respond (Rule 2.9(A)(2)), and must promptly notify all parties of the substance of an unauthorized ex parte communication received (Rule 2.9(B)).","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["judicial-conduct","ex-parte","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-mpre-cjc-disqualification","prompt":"Judge Soto is assigned a contract case against Orion Inc. She then learns that her husband owns $200,000 of Orion stock. The judge believes she can be impartial and would like to keep the case. Under the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct, what is the proper course?","choices":["Preside, because her husband, rather than the judge herself, owns the shares.","Disclose the interest on the record and keep presiding unless a party files a written objection.","Disclose it and let the parties and lawyers decide, outside her presence, whether to waive.","Recuse herself, because this disqualification cannot be waived even if all parties agree."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Rule 2.11(A)(3) of the Model Code of Judicial Conduct, a judge is disqualified when she knows that she or her spouse has more than a de minimis economic interest in a party. Rule 2.11(C) allows remittal of any disqualification except one based on personal bias: the judge discloses the basis on the record, and the parties and lawyers consider, outside the presence of the judge and court personnel, whether to waive it; if all agree, the judge may proceed and the agreement goes into the record. Silence or a failure to object is not a waiver.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["judicial-conduct","disqualification","remittal","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-mpre-cjc-gifts","prompt":"Under the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct, which of the following benefits falls in the category a judge may accept without any public reporting requirement, regardless of its value?","choices":["Free tickets for the judge and spouse to the county bar association's annual dinner.","An expensive wedding gift from a lawyer who regularly appears before the judge.","A $500 gift card from a litigant shortly after the judge ruled in his favor.","A home mortgage from a bank at the same rate the bank offers to the general public."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 3.13(B)(4) lets a judge accept, without reporting, loans from lending institutions in the regular course of business on the same terms available to persons who are not judges. Invitations to bar-related events and gifts from lawyers or parties likely to appear before the judge fall under Rule 3.13(C), which requires public reporting under Rule 3.15 (and the latter may be barred outright by Rule 3.13(A)). A gift from a litigant just after a favorable ruling would appear to undermine impartiality and is prohibited.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["judicial-conduct","gifts","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-cjc-political-activity","prompt":"Judge Tran was appointed to the bench and is not currently a candidate for any office. Under the ABA Model Code of Judicial Conduct, which of the following political activities is permitted?","choices":["Registering with a political party and voting in its primary election.","Publicly endorsing a longtime friend who is running for the city council.","Buying a $100 ticket to a political party's fundraising dinner.","Contributing $500 to the campaign of a candidate for governor."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Model Code of Judicial Conduct Rule 4.1(A) bars a judge from publicly endorsing or opposing a candidate for any public office, soliciting funds for or contributing to a political organization or candidate, and attending or purchasing tickets for events sponsored by a political organization or candidate. Nothing in the Code prevents a judge from registering with a party and voting. The fact that the endorsed candidate is a friend does not create an exception.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["judicial-conduct","political-activity","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-mpre-malpractice-limitation","prompt":"Lawyer Wanda's engagement letter caps her liability for any malpractice at the amount of fees paid. A new client, who has no other lawyer advising her, reads Wanda's full explanation of the cap and signs the letter, giving informed consent. Under the ABA Model Rules, is Wanda subject to discipline?","choices":["No, because the client gave informed consent to the cap in a signed writing.","Yes, because the client was not independently represented in making the agreement.","No, because a malpractice cap is a civil-liability issue, not a disciplinary one.","Yes, because a lawyer may never agree with a client to limit malpractice liability."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Model Rule 1.8(h)(1) forbids an agreement prospectively limiting the lawyer's malpractice liability unless the client is independently represented in making it; informed consent alone is not enough. Such agreements are not banned outright, and making one improperly is a disciplinary violation regardless of whether it would be enforced in a malpractice suit. Separately, a rule violation does not itself create a civil cause of action (Scope [20]); malpractice requires duty, breach, causation, and damages.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["malpractice","conflicts","liability-limitation","mpre"],"focus":["mpre"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-death-bodily-harm-steps","prompt":"A California client tells Lawyer Ava that tomorrow night he will set fire to his former business partner's occupied home. Ava reasonably believes he will do it and that someone is likely to be killed. There is still time to talk with him. Under Bus. & Prof. Code § 6068(e) and the California Rules of Professional Conduct, which statement is correct?","choices":["Ava must reveal the threat to police, because disclosure is mandatory to prevent a death.","Ava may not reveal the threat, because California's duty of confidentiality has no exceptions.","Ava may reveal it, but if reasonable must first urge him to stop and say she may disclose.","Ava may reveal the threat at once, with no prior steps, because the threatened harm is death."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Bus. & Prof. Code § 6068(e)(2) and Cal. RPC 1.6(b) permit, but do not require, a lawyer to reveal confidential information to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent a criminal act the lawyer reasonably believes is likely to result in death or substantial bodily harm. Before revealing, the lawyer must, if reasonable under the circumstances, make a good-faith effort to persuade the client not to commit the act and inform the client of the lawyer's ability or decision to reveal (Cal. RPC 1.6(c)). A lawyer who chooses not to reveal does not violate the rule.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["confidentiality","bodily-harm","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 6068 (duties of an attorney)","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=6068"}]},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-no-financial-fraud-exception","prompt":"California Lawyer Ben drafted loan documents that his client later used, without Ben's knowledge, to defraud a lender of $3 million. Ben learns of the completed fraud through confidential communications with the client. No one faces any physical danger. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct and Bus. & Prof. Code § 6068(e), may Ben reveal the fraud to the lender to help it recover?","choices":["Yes, because the client used Ben's own legal services to carry out the $3 million fraud on the lender.","Yes, but only to prevent further loss to the lender, not to rectify the loss already caused.","Yes, and in fact he must, because a lawyer may not remain silent about a client's completed fraud.","No, because California's exception covers only crimes likely to cause death or substantial bodily harm."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"California has no counterpart to Model Rule 1.6(b)(2)–(3), which permit disclosure to prevent, mitigate, or rectify financial injury from a client's crime or fraud involving the lawyer's services. The exception created by Bus. & Prof. Code § 6068(e)(2) and Cal. RPC 1.6(b) permits disclosure only to prevent a criminal act likely to result in death or substantial bodily harm. Ben must not assist any further fraud (Cal. RPC 1.2.1) and may need to withdraw (Cal. RPC 1.16), but he may not reveal the client's confidences to the lender without consent.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["confidentiality","crime-fraud","financial-harm","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 6068 (duties of an attorney)","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=6068"}]},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-informed-written-consent","prompt":"California Lawyer Cara represents Client A in a business dispute and is asked to represent Client B against A in an unrelated matter. She reasonably believes she can represent both competently. She explains the conflict and its risks to each client by phone; both agree orally, and she promptly emails each a letter confirming the consent. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, has Cara complied with the conflict rules?","choices":["No, because California requires both written disclosure and written consent.","Yes, because each client's oral informed consent was promptly confirmed in writing.","Yes, because no consent is needed when the two matters are factually unrelated.","No, because suing a current client in any matter is never consentable in California."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Cal. RPC 1.7(a) requires each affected client's 'informed written consent,' which Cal. RPC 1.0.1(e-1) defines to require that both the lawyer's disclosure and the client's consent be in writing. The Model Rules' 'informed consent, confirmed in writing' standard, under which a lawyer's confirming letter suffices, is not enough in California. Direct adversity to a current client is a conflict even in an unrelated matter, but it is consentable.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","informed-written-consent","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-related-opposing-counsel","prompt":"California Lawyer Dan learns that the defendant in his client's breach-of-contract case has hired Dan's sister as its lawyer. Dan reasonably concludes there is no significant risk that the relationship will materially limit his representation. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, what must Dan do to continue?","choices":["Nothing, because without a significant risk of material limitation there is no conflict.","Provide written disclosure of the relationship to his client.","Obtain his client's informed written consent to the conflict.","Withdraw, because siblings may not oppose each other in the same matter."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cal. RPC 1.7(c)(2) requires written disclosure to the client, even when there is no significant risk of a material limitation, if the lawyer knows that another party's lawyer is the lawyer's spouse, parent, child, or sibling, lives with the lawyer, is a client of the lawyer's firm, or has an intimate personal relationship with the lawyer. Informed written consent would be required only if a significant risk existed under Cal. RPC 1.7(b). No rule bars siblings from opposing each other outright.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["conflicts","written-disclosure","relationships","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-security-interest-fees","prompt":"California Lawyer Eve agrees to handle a client's hourly-fee lawsuit only if the client signs a promissory note for her fees secured by a deed of trust on the client's home. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, which statement is correct?","choices":["Only the fee-agreement rules apply, because securing a fee is part of the fee arrangement.","She may never take a security interest in a client's property to secure payment of fees.","She must comply with the business-transaction rule, including informed written consent.","The deal is proper as long as the written fee agreement complies with Bus. & Prof. Code § 6148."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Taking a deed of trust on a client's property to secure fees is acquiring a security interest adverse to the client (Hawk v. State Bar (1988) 45 Cal.3d 589), so Cal. RPC 1.8.1 applies. The terms must be fair and reasonable and fully disclosed in writing, the client must be advised in writing that it may seek the advice of an independent lawyer and be given a reasonable opportunity to do so, and the client must give informed written consent to the terms and the lawyer's role. Such security is not forbidden, and compliance with § 6148's fee-agreement requirements alone is not enough.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["business-transaction","fees","adverse-interest","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-unconscionable-fee","prompt":"After a fee dispute, an arbitrator finds that California Lawyer Finn's $60,000 fee for a fairly simple matter was 'somewhat higher than reasonable' but not shocking, and that the sophisticated client agreed to it with full information and without any overreaching. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, is Finn subject to discipline for the fee?","choices":["Yes, because California, like the Model Rules, prohibits any unreasonable fee.","Yes, because a fee above the local market rate is unconscionable per se in California.","No, because a fee the client agreed to in writing can never support discipline.","No, because California disciplines only fees that are unconscionable or illegal."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Cal. RPC 1.5(a) prohibits making an agreement for, charging, or collecting an unconscionable or illegal fee, whereas Model Rule 1.5(a) uses an 'unreasonable' standard. Unconscionability is judged by factors such as overreaching, the relative sophistication of the parties, the client's informed consent, and gross disproportion between the fee and the value of the services (Cal. RPC 1.5(b)). A fee that is merely somewhat high may be reduced in fee arbitration but is not disciplinable; a client's agreement does not immunize a truly unconscionable fee.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","unconscionable","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-contingency-6147","prompt":"California Lawyer Gia takes a car-accident case on a 40% contingent fee. The client signs the fee agreement, but Gia never signs it, never gives the client a copy, and omits any statement that the fee is not set by law and is negotiable. Gia obtains a $300,000 settlement. Under Bus. & Prof. Code § 6147, what is the effect?","choices":["The agreement is voidable at the client's option, leaving Gia a reasonable fee.","The agreement is enforceable as written, because the client signed it.","The agreement is void, and Gia may recover nothing at all for her work.","The agreement becomes enforceable once Gia signs it and sends the client a copy."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Bus. & Prof. Code § 6147(a) requires a contingency fee contract to be in writing, with a duplicate copy signed by both the attorney and the client provided to the client, and to state the contingency rate, how costs affect the fee and recovery, and (outside medical malpractice cases) that the fee is not set by law and is negotiable. Under § 6147(b), noncompliance makes the agreement voidable at the client's option, and the lawyer is then entitled to a reasonable fee. The Model Rules require only a writing signed by the client.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","contingent","fee-agreement","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 6147 (contingency fee contracts)","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=6147"}]},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-fee-division","prompt":"California Lawyer Hank refers a client to Lawyer Iris at another firm, and the two lawyers agree in writing that Hank will receive 25% of Iris's fee. Hank will do no work and assumes no responsibility for the case. The client consents in writing after full written disclosure that a division will be made, the lawyers' identities, and its terms. The total fee is not increased. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, is the division proper?","choices":["No, because Hank's share is not in proportion to the work he actually performs on the case.","Yes, because the client consented in writing after full written disclosure of the split.","No, because Hank has not assumed joint responsibility for the representation.","Yes, because California does not require client consent to divide fees between firms."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cal. RPC 1.5.1(a) permits lawyers in different firms to divide a fee if the lawyers enter into a written agreement, the client consents in writing (at the time of the agreement or as soon as reasonably practicable) after full written disclosure that a division will be made, of the identities of the lawyers or firms, and of the terms of the division, and the total fee is not increased solely because of the division. Unlike Model Rule 1.5(e), California requires neither proportional work nor joint responsibility, so pure referral fees are allowed when these conditions are met.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["fees","division","referral","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-flat-fee-deposit","prompt":"California Lawyer Jo charges a $2,500 flat fee, paid in advance, to handle a client's misdemeanor case. She tells the client orally that he may insist the fee be held in trust until earned and that he will be refunded any unearned portion; he agrees orally that she may deposit it in her operating account, and she does so. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, did Jo act properly?","choices":["Yes, because a flat fee is earned on receipt and belongs to the lawyer immediately.","Yes, because the client received the required disclosures and agreed to the deposit.","No, because for a flat fee over $1,000, the disclosures and consent must be in a signed writing.","No, because every advance fee, flat or hourly, must stay in a trust account until it is earned."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Cal. RPC 1.15(a) generally requires advance fees to be deposited in a client trust account, but Cal. RPC 1.15(b) allows a flat fee paid in advance to go into the operating account if the lawyer discloses in writing the client's right to require a trust deposit until the fee is earned and the client's right to a refund of any unearned amount. If the flat fee exceeds $1,000, the client's agreement and those disclosures must be in a writing signed by the client. Oral disclosures do not satisfy the rule.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["trust-account","flat-fee","fees","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-client-perjury","prompt":"In a California civil trial, Lawyer Kai's client testifies to a material fact that Kai later learns is false. Kai privately urges the client to correct the testimony, but the client refuses and will not consent to any disclosure. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, which course is proper?","choices":["Disclose the falsity to the court, because the duty of candor overrides confidentiality.","Do nothing more, and rely on the testimony in closing as he would any other evidence.","Disclose the falsity to the court only if the court denies his motion to withdraw.","Take other remedial steps, such as moving to withdraw, but not disclose the falsity."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Cal. RPC 3.3(a)(3) requires reasonable remedial measures when a lawyer learns that material evidence offered by the client is false, 'including, if necessary, disclosure to the tribunal, unless disclosure is prohibited by Business and Professions Code section 6068, subdivision (e) and rule 1.6.' Because no California confidentiality exception applies and the client refuses consent, Kai may not disclose, unlike under Model Rule 3.3(c). He must not use or argue the false testimony and should consider seeking to withdraw under Cal. RPC 1.16.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["candor","false-evidence","confidentiality","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-organization-no-reporting-out","prompt":"California Lawyer Lena is general counsel of a privately held company. She learns that its CEO is falsifying required reports to a state regulator, a violation of law likely to cause the company substantial fines; no one faces physical harm. She reports to the board, which refuses to act. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, what may Lena do?","choices":["Keep acting in the company's best lawful interest, including resigning, but not report outside.","Report the violation to the regulator, to the extent needed to prevent substantial injury to the company.","Report the violation to the regulator, but only after she first resigns from the company.","Take no further action, because she fully satisfied her duty by reporting to the board."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Cal. RPC 1.13(b) requires reporting up the organizational ladder, and if the highest authority fails to act, Cal. RPC 1.13(d) requires the lawyer to continue to proceed as reasonably necessary in the organization's best lawful interest, which may include her right, and where appropriate her duty, to resign or withdraw under Cal. RPC 1.16. Unlike Model Rule 1.13(c), California's rule does not permit reporting out: Cal. RPC 1.13(c) bars revealing information protected by Bus. & Prof. Code § 6068(e), whose only disclosure exception (see Cal. RPC 1.6) is for preventing a criminal act likely to cause death or substantial bodily harm. Resigning first does not create a right to disclose.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["organization-client","reporting-up","confidentiality","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-pr-reporting-duty","prompt":"A former bookkeeper, who is not anyone's client, shows California Lawyer Max bank records that credibly indicate Lawyer Ned took $40,000 from his client trust account for personal use. Max is not certain the misappropriation occurred, and the information is not confidential to any client of Max. Under the California Rules of Professional Conduct, what must Max do?","choices":["Nothing, because California makes reporting another lawyer's misconduct permissive, not mandatory.","Report to the State Bar without undue delay, based on his knowledge of credible evidence.","Report only if he first confirms, with actual knowledge, that the misappropriation occurred.","Confront Ned first, and report only if Ned fails to repay the client within a reasonable time."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Effective August 1, 2023, Cal. RPC 8.3(a) requires a lawyer to inform the State Bar (or a tribunal with jurisdiction), without undue delay, when the lawyer knows of credible evidence that another lawyer has committed a criminal act or engaged in conduct involving dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or reckless or intentional misrepresentation or misappropriation of funds or property that raises a substantial question about that lawyer's honesty, trustworthiness, or fitness. The trigger is knowledge of credible evidence, not certainty that misconduct occurred. Before 2023, California had no such reporting duty; the rule does not require disclosure of information protected by § 6068(e) and Cal. RPC 1.6.","subject":"professionalResponsibility","tags":["reporting","misconduct","misappropriation","california"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"ca-evidence-prop8","prompt":"Without a warrant, officers searched the trash bags Dale left at the curb for collection and found drug packaging. Charged in California state court, Dale moves to suppress, arguing the search violated article I, section 13 of the California Constitution, which California courts once read to protect curbside trash even though the Fourth Amendment does not. How should the court rule?","choices":["Grant it, because the state constitution independently protects curbside trash and California courts enforce that protection by exclusion","Deny it, because Proposition 8 bars excluding relevant evidence for a state-only search violation unless the U.S. Constitution requires it","Grant it, because Proposition 8 preserved every exclusionary rule that California courts had adopted before the measure took effect","Deny it, because Proposition 8 makes all relevant evidence admissible in criminal cases, overriding even Evidence Code § 352 balancing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Proposition 8's Truth-in-Evidence provision (Cal. Const. art. I, § 28(f)(2)) bars excluding relevant evidence in a criminal proceeding unless exclusion is required by the federal Constitution or a later statute passed by a two-thirds vote. In re Lance W. (1985) held it abrogated the exclusionary remedy for searches that violate only state law, so the question becomes the Fourth Amendment, and California v. Greenwood (1988) holds curbside trash unprotected. But Prop 8 is not absolute: it expressly preserves privileges, the hearsay rule, and Evidence Code §§ 352, 782, and 1103, so the 'overrides § 352' choice reaches the right result for the wrong reason.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","evidence","prop-8","search-and-seizure"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ca-community-wages","prompt":"During their marriage, Priya deposited every paycheck from her engineering job into a savings account titled solely in her name. Her husband, Dev, never had access to the account and never deposited anything into it. At their California dissolution, Priya claims the $90,000 balance as her separate property. How is the account most likely characterized?","choices":["Community property, because earnings during marriage are community no matter whose name is on the account","Priya's separate property, because the account is titled in her name alone and Dev never had any access to it","Priya's separate property, because each spouse's earnings belong to the earning spouse until they are commingled","Community property only to the extent Dev proves that he contributed some of his own funds to the account"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Fam. Code § 760, property a married person acquires during marriage while domiciled in California, including earnings, is presumptively community property. Holding the funds in one spouse's name does not change their character; only a valid written transmutation under § 852 could. Dev need not prove a contribution: the burden is on Priya to trace a separate-property source, and her wages are not one.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["california-distinction","community-property","earnings","presumptions"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 760","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=760."}]},{"id":"ca-separate-property","prompt":"During the marriage, Marco inherited an apartment building from his aunt. A property manager, paid from the rents, runs it; Marco deposits the net rents in an account in his name only and never mixes them with marital funds. At divorce, his wife, Lena, argues that at least the rents collected during the marriage are community property. How are the building and its rents characterized under California law?","choices":["The building is Marco's separate property, but the rents collected during the marriage are community property","Both the building and its rents are community property, because the building was acquired during the marriage","Both the building and its rents are Marco's separate property, since rents from separate property stay separate","Both are Marco's separate property, but Lena is entitled to reimbursement for half of the rents collected"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Fam. Code § 770 makes property acquired by gift, bequest, devise, or descent separate property, together with the rents, issues, and profits of that property. California thus rejects the rule of some community property states (such as Texas) that income from separate property is community. Because Marco kept the rents segregated and a manager paid from the rents ran the building, there is no commingling and no community-labor component that would give the community a claim or a reimbursement right.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["california-distinction","separate-property","rents-issues-profits"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 770","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=770."}]},{"id":"ca-no-common-law","prompt":"Avery and Jordan lived together in California for 15 years, shared a surname, and introduced each other as 'my spouse,' but never obtained a marriage license or held a ceremony. Both always knew they were not legally married. When they split up, Avery wants a share of the property Jordan acquired during those years. What is Avery's best legal route to a share?","choices":["A community property claim, because long cohabitation and holding out created a common-law marriage","A quasi-marital property claim as a putative spouse, since they held themselves out as married","A quasi-community property claim, because the property would have been community had they married","A Marvin claim based on an express or implied agreement between the partners"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"California does not recognize common-law marriages contracted in California; it recognizes marriages licensed and solemnized under the Family Code, plus marriages valid where contracted elsewhere (Fam. Code § 308). Putative-spouse status requires a good-faith belief that a valid marriage existed, and both partners knew otherwise. Quasi-community property concerns spouses who acquired property while domiciled outside California. A cohabitant's remedy is a Marvin v. Marvin (1976) claim on an express or implied contract, or an equitable remedy such as a constructive trust.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["california-distinction","marriage","common-law-marriage","marvin"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"ca-anti-slapp","prompt":"At a televised city council hearing on a proposed housing project, resident Nia said the developer had 'bribed the planning staff.' The developer sued Nia for defamation in California superior court, and Nia filed a special motion to strike under CCP § 425.16. How should the court analyze the motion?","choices":["Nia must show the claim arises from protected activity; the developer must then show a probability of prevailing","Nia must show both that the claim arises from protected activity and that her statement was true or privileged","The court strikes the claim only if Nia proves the developer sued primarily to chill her exercise of free speech","The motion fails, because the statute protects only statements by the news media about public figures or officials"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The anti-SLAPP statute uses a two-step test. First, the defendant must show the cause of action arises from protected activity; statements made before a legislative or other official proceeding qualify under § 425.16(e)(1). Second, the plaintiff must demonstrate a probability of prevailing with a legally sufficient claim supported by admissible evidence (often described as 'minimal merit'). The defendant does not have to prove truth at step one, and Equilon Enterprises v. Consumer Cause, Inc. (2002) held that no showing of intent to chill speech is required.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","anti-slapp","civil-procedure"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 425.16","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=425.16."}]},{"id":"ca-demurrer","prompt":"Kai sues a contractor in California superior court, alleging that they entered into a written agreement that the contractor breached. The contractor demurs and attaches a sworn declaration stating that no agreement was ever signed. How should the court treat the declaration in ruling on the demurrer?","choices":["Treat the demurrer as a summary judgment motion and give Kai a reasonable chance to respond with evidence","Consider it, and sustain the demurrer if Kai files no counter-declaration, since the facts are then undisputed","Disregard it, because a demurrer tests only the face of the pleading and judicially noticeable matters","Consider it, because a demurrer may challenge whether the plaintiff can prove the facts essential to the claim"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A California demurrer tests the legal sufficiency of the complaint: the court treats properly pleaded material facts as true and looks only at the face of the pleading and matters subject to judicial notice (CCP § 430.30(a)). Extrinsic evidence contradicting the allegations is not considered. Unlike FRCP 12(d), the CCP has no mechanism for converting a demurrer into a summary judgment motion; the contractor's factual dispute belongs in a motion under CCP § 437c.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","pleading","demurrer"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 430.30","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=430.30."}]},{"id":"ca-comparative-negligence","prompt":"Rina was jaywalking while looking at her phone when Theo, who was speeding, struck her. In her negligence action in California, the jury finds her damages are $200,000 and assigns 60% of the fault to Rina and 40% to Theo. What judgment should Rina receive?","choices":["$0, because her share of the fault is greater than Theo's share","$80,000, because her damages are reduced by her own share of the fault","$0, because her own negligence contributed to causing the injury","$120,000, because her damages are reduced by Theo's share of the fault"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Li v. Yellow Cab Co. (1975) replaced contributory negligence with pure comparative negligence in California: a plaintiff's recovery is reduced by her own percentage of fault, however large. Rina recovers 40% of $200,000. A 50% or 51% bar is the modified comparative negligence rule used in many other states, and contributory negligence is the old common-law complete bar.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","comparative-negligence","torts"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"ca-community-debts","prompt":"During the marriage, Hugo borrowed $40,000 in his own name to buy a boat; his wife, Iris, did not sign and did not know of the loan. Hugo defaulted, and the lender obtained a judgment against Hugo alone. It now seeks to levy on a community bank account that holds Iris's earnings. Under the California Family Code, may it do so?","choices":["No, because Iris neither signed the loan nor was named as a party to the judgment","Only as to half the balance, because Iris's one-half community interest is shielded","Only if the loan was for necessaries of life, such as food, shelter, or medical care","Yes, because the community estate is liable for a debt either spouse incurs during marriage"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fam. Code § 910 makes the community estate liable for a debt incurred by either spouse before or during marriage, regardless of which spouse manages the property and regardless of whether one or both spouses are parties to the debt or the judgment. The lender may reach the entire community account, not just Hugo's half. The necessaries limitation answers a different question, when the non-debtor spouse's separate property is liable (§ 914), and § 911's protection for segregated earnings applies only to the other spouse's premarital debts.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["california-distinction","community-property","debts","creditors"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 910","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=910."}]},{"id":"ca-fact-pleading","prompt":"A complaint filed in California superior court alleges only that the defendant 'is liable to plaintiff for breach of contract and owes plaintiff $50,000.' The defendant demurs. Which standard governs whether the complaint is sufficient?","choices":["Whether it pleads the ultimate facts making up each element of a cause of action, not legal conclusions","Whether it gives the defendant fair notice of the claim, even without alleging facts on each element","Whether its factual content makes liability plausible rather than merely conceivable, as under Twombly","Whether it alleges the evidentiary facts the plaintiff will rely on to prove each element at trial"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"California is a fact-pleading jurisdiction: CCP § 425.10 requires a statement of the facts constituting the cause of action, meaning the ultimate facts supporting each element, not bare legal conclusions and not detailed evidentiary facts. 'Liable for breach of contract' is a conclusion; the complaint should allege the contract, the plaintiff's performance or excuse, the breach, and damages. Fair-notice pleading and the Twombly/Iqbal plausibility test are federal standards under FRCP 8 that California courts do not apply to state-court pleadings.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","pleading","fact-pleading"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 425.10","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=425.10."}]},{"id":"ca-primary-rights","prompt":"Sofia was hurt, and her car was damaged, when a delivery van ran a stop sign. She sued the delivery company in California superior court for the cost of repairing the car and won. A year later, within the limitations period, she sued the company for her personal injuries from the same collision. The company argues claim preclusion. How should the court rule?","choices":["The injury suit is barred, because both claims arise from the same transaction and could have been joined","The injury suit is barred, because a plaintiff must join all related claims in one action or forfeit them","The injury suit may proceed, because harm to her person and harm to her car invade separate primary rights","The injury suit may proceed, because claim preclusion bars only a second suit on the same legal theory"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"California defines a 'cause of action' for claim preclusion by the primary-rights theory: one injury to one primary right is one cause of action, however many legal theories are pleaded. As the California Supreme Court recognized in Holmes v. David H. Bricker, Inc. (1969), tortious conduct that harms both a plaintiff's person and her property invades two distinct primary rights, so a judgment on one does not bar a later suit on the other. (Holmes itself barred the second suit only because it rested on a single breach of warranty, a contract claim, rather than a tort.) The federal and Restatement transactional test would bar the second suit. California's compulsory cross-complaint rule binds defendants, not plaintiffs, and different legal theories for the same primary right would be barred.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","res-judicata","primary-rights"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ca-prop-51","prompt":"In a California negligence action, the jury awards Lucia $100,000 in economic damages and $200,000 in noneconomic damages. It finds Lucia 0% at fault, defendant Ames 60% at fault, and defendant Bell 40% at fault. Bell is insolvent. How much can Lucia collect from Ames?","choices":["$300,000: all of the damages, because the defendants are jointly and severally liable","$180,000: 60% of all of the damages, because each defendant pays only its own share","$260,000: 60% of the economic damages plus all of the noneconomic damages","$220,000: all of the economic damages plus 60% of the noneconomic damages"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Proposition 51 (Civ. Code § 1431.2) keeps joint and several liability for economic damages but makes each defendant's liability for noneconomic damages several only, in proportion to its percentage of fault. Ames therefore owes all $100,000 of economic damages, bearing the risk of Bell's insolvency, plus 60% of the $200,000 in noneconomic damages, for $220,000. The $300,000 answer is pre-Prop 51 joint and several liability, and $260,000 reverses the two categories.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","prop-51","damages","joint-and-several"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1431.2","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1431.2."}]},{"id":"ca-anti-slapp-fees","prompt":"Bao's defamation suit against Carmen arose from Carmen's statements at a school board meeting. The court granted Carmen's anti-SLAPP motion in full but denied her request for attorney's fees, explaining that Bao's suit, while unsuccessful, was not frivolous. Was the fee ruling correct?","choices":["Yes, because fees under the anti-SLAPP statute are awarded in the trial court's discretion","No, because a defendant who prevails on an anti-SLAPP motion is entitled to her fees and costs","Yes, because fees are awarded only if the claim stricken was frivolous or brought for delay","No, but only her costs were mandatory; attorney's fees require a separate contractual basis"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CCP § 425.16(c)(1) provides that a prevailing defendant on a special motion to strike 'shall be entitled to recover' attorney's fees and costs, so an award is mandatory, though the court sets a reasonable amount. The frivolousness standard runs the other way: a plaintiff who defeats the motion recovers fees only if the court finds the motion frivolous or solely intended to cause unnecessary delay (applying CCP § 128.5). Whether Bao's suit was frivolous is irrelevant.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","anti-slapp","attorneys-fees"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 425.16","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=425.16."}]},{"id":"ca-privacy-constitutional","prompt":"A private intercollegiate athletic association requires student athletes to provide monitored urine samples for drug testing. An athlete sues the association under the privacy clause of article I, section 1 of the California Constitution. Which statement best describes how the court will analyze her claim?","choices":["The claim fails at the threshold, because the state privacy clause, like the Fourth Amendment, restricts only government actors and not private associations","The clause reaches the association; she must show a protected interest, a reasonable expectation, and a serious invasion, subject to justification","The clause reaches the private association, and any intrusion it imposes on her privacy is unlawful unless the association proves a compelling interest","The clause reaches the private association, but it protects only informational privacy, so bodily testing claims are left to the Fourth Amendment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Hill v. NCAA (1994) held that California's constitutional privacy right, added by initiative in 1972, applies to private as well as government actors. The plaintiff must show (1) a legally protected privacy interest (informational or autonomy), (2) a reasonable expectation of privacy in the circumstances, and (3) a serious invasion; the defendant may justify the invasion by showing it substantively furthers countervailing interests, and the plaintiff may respond with feasible, less intrusive alternatives. Williams v. Superior Court (2017) confirmed that a compelling interest is required only for an obvious invasion of an interest fundamental to personal autonomy, not for every intrusion. Hill itself upheld the NCAA's testing program.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","privacy","constitutional","hill-v-ncaa"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"ca-punitive-public-entity","prompt":"Angry at a passenger, a city bus driver deliberately slammed on the brakes to throw him to the floor. The passenger timely presented a government claim and then sued both the city and the driver, seeking punitive damages. Assuming he proves malice by clear and convincing evidence, against whom may punitive damages be awarded?","choices":["Against the city as well, because its employee acted with malice in the course of his employment","Against neither, because the Government Claims Act shields public employees from punitive damages","Against the driver only, because public entities are statutorily immune from punitive damages","Against the city only, because the driver's personal liability merges into his public employer's"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Government Code § 818 provides that a public entity is not liable for punitive or exemplary damages. That immunity does not extend to public employees, who can be personally liable for punitive damages for their own malicious conduct (the entity's ability to pay such an award on the employee's behalf is restricted by Gov. Code § 825). Presenting a timely claim preserves the suit but does not expose the city to punitive damages.","subject":"remedies","tags":["california-distinction","punitive-damages","public-entity"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Gov. Code § 818","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=GOV&sectionNum=818."}]},{"id":"ca-answer-30-days","prompt":"On May 1, after reasonable diligence failed to achieve personal delivery, a process server left the summons and complaint with Dana's adult roommate at their apartment and mailed a copy to Dana the same day. Under the California Code of Civil Procedure, when is Dana's response due?","choices":["30 days after service is deemed complete, which is the 10th day after the mailing","30 days after May 1, the day the papers were left with Dana's adult roommate","21 days after May 1, the same period that applies to answers under the federal rules","35 days after the mailing, because service by mail adds 5 days to the 30-day period"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A California defendant has 30 days after service of the summons to file a responsive pleading (CCP § 412.20(a)(3)). Substituted service under CCP § 415.20 is deemed complete on the 10th day after the mailing, and the 30 days run from then. The 5-day mail extension of § 1013 applies to papers served by mail during the litigation, not to service of the summons, and 21 days is the federal Rule 12(a) deadline.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","civil-procedure","responsive-pleading","service"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 415.20","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=415.20."}]},{"id":"ca-compulsory-cross-complaint","prompt":"Vendor sued Buyer in California superior court for the unpaid price of custom software. Buyer answered, denying liability, but filed no cross-complaint. After Vendor won, Buyer filed a new action alleging that Vendor fraudulently induced the same software contract. Vendor moves to dismiss. What result?","choices":["No dismissal, because cross-complaints are permissive in California, like federal crossclaims","No dismissal, because fraud and nonpayment invade different primary rights under California law","No dismissal, because the compulsory rule reaches only related claims against codefendants","Dismissal, because Buyer failed to plead a related claim against Vendor in a cross-complaint"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under CCP § 426.30, a defendant who fails to allege in a cross-complaint any related cause of action it has against the plaintiff when it serves its answer may not later assert that claim. A 'related cause of action' is one arising out of the same transaction, occurrence, or series of transactions (§ 426.10(c)), a test distinct from the primary-rights test used for claim preclusion. Buyer's fraud claim arises from the same contract, so it is barred. Cross-complaints against codefendants or third parties are permissive; the compulsory rule targets claims against the plaintiff.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","cross-complaint","civil-procedure"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 426.30","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=426.30."}]},{"id":"ca-anti-slapp-stay","prompt":"A developer sued an activist for defamation and noticed her deposition. Before the deposition, the activist filed an anti-SLAPP motion. The developer argues it needs the deposition to show that she knew her statements were false. What is the status of discovery?","choices":["Discovery proceeds, because the anti-SLAPP stay takes effect only once the court sets a hearing date","All discovery is stayed, but the court may allow specified discovery on a noticed motion showing good cause","Only discovery from the activist is stayed; the developer may still subpoena third parties without leave","All discovery is stayed, and no discovery of any kind may be ordered until the court rules on the motion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CCP § 425.16(g) stays all discovery proceedings in the action upon the filing of the notice of motion, and the stay lasts until notice of entry of the order ruling on the motion. On noticed motion and for good cause, the court may order that specified discovery go forward, commonly when the plaintiff needs evidence of the defendant's state of mind, such as knowledge of falsity, that only the defendant has. The stay covers all discovery, including third-party subpoenas.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","anti-slapp","civil-procedure","discovery"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 425.16","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=425.16."}]},{"id":"ca-ucl-remedies","prompt":"Glow Co. sued a competitor under California's Unfair Competition Law (Bus. & Prof. Code § 17200), alleging that the competitor's deceptive advertising diverted Glow's customers. Glow seeks (1) an injunction against the ads, (2) its own lost profits, and (3) disgorgement of the competitor's profits from the ads. Which relief is available to Glow under the UCL?","choices":["The injunction and disgorgement of the competitor's ad profits, but not Glow's own lost profits","All three, because the UCL authorizes any relief needed to remedy an unfair business practice","Only the injunction, because lost profits are damages and Glow had no ownership interest in the profits","The injunction and Glow's own lost profits, but not disgorgement of the competitor's ad profits"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A private UCL plaintiff may obtain only injunctive relief and restitution (Bus. & Prof. Code § 17203); damages are not available. Korea Supply Co. v. Lockheed Martin Corp. (2003) held that restitution means restoring money or property in which the plaintiff had an ownership interest, so nonrestitutionary disgorgement of a defendant's profits is unavailable in an individual UCL action. Glow's lost profits are damages. Civil penalties are available only in actions by public prosecutors.","subject":"remedies","tags":["california-distinction","remedies","ucl","restitution"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 17203","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=17203."}]},{"id":"ca-punitive-standard","prompt":"In a California fraud action, the judge instructs the jury that it may award punitive damages if it finds by a preponderance of the evidence that the defendant acted with malice, oppression, or fraud. The defendant appeals the punitive award. Was the instruction proper?","choices":["Yes, because a preponderance of the evidence is the standard for every issue in a civil case","No, because punitive damages require proof beyond a reasonable doubt of an intent to injure","Yes, because the heightened standard applies only to punitive damages claimed against employers","No, because malice, oppression, or fraud must be proven by clear and convincing evidence"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Civil Code § 3294(a) authorizes punitive damages for breach of an obligation not arising from contract only on proof by clear and convincing evidence that the defendant was guilty of oppression, fraud, or malice. 'Malice' includes intent to injure or despicable conduct carried on with willful and conscious disregard of others' rights or safety. Reasonable-doubt proof is not required, and the employer rule in § 3294(b) adds a requirement (advance knowledge, authorization, or ratification by an officer, director, or managing agent) rather than setting the burden of proof.","subject":"remedies","tags":["california-distinction","remedies","punitive-damages","burden-of-proof"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Civ. Code § 3294","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=3294."}]},{"id":"ca-sol-oral-contract","prompt":"In March 2022, Omar orally agreed to remodel Grace's kitchen for $30,000, and Grace paid in full up front. Omar walked off the job in June 2022, a clear breach that Grace discovered immediately. Grace sued for breach of contract in August 2025, and Omar pleads the statute of limitations. Is Grace's claim timely?","choices":["No, because the limitations period for an oral contract is two years from breach","Yes, because California applies a four-year period to all breach of contract claims","Yes, because the period runs only from when Grace learns the full extent of her damages","Yes, because the limitations period for an oral contract is three years from breach"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CCP § 339 sets a two-year limitations period for an action on a contract not founded on a writing, running from breach. Omar breached in June 2022, so the period expired in June 2024. The four-year period of CCP § 337 applies to written contracts (and Com. Code § 2725 sets four years for sales of goods, oral or written), but this was an oral services contract. An ordinary breach claim accrues at breach, not when the full extent of damages becomes known.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","statute-of-limitations","contracts"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 339","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=339."}]},{"id":"ca-sol-written-contract","prompt":"Under a signed written commercial lease, a tenant stopped paying rent in January 2023 and vacated. The landlord sued for the unpaid rent under the lease in October 2026. Under California law, is the claim timely?","choices":["No, because a claim on any contract must be brought within two years","No, because a claim on a written contract must be brought within three years","Yes, because a claim on a written contract may be brought within ten years","Yes, because a claim on a written contract may be brought within four years"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"CCP § 337(a) provides a four-year limitations period for an action on a contract, obligation, or liability founded upon an instrument in writing. The landlord sued within four years of the January 2023 breach. The two-year period of CCP § 339 governs oral contracts, and the longer periods some states use for written contracts do not apply in California.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","statute-of-limitations","contracts"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 337","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=337."}]},{"id":"ca-evidence-code-352","prompt":"Charged with murder, Ray offers evidence that another man, Silas, threatened the victim the day before the killing and was seen running from the victim's building minutes after the shooting. The prosecution objects. Under California law, how should the court evaluate the evidence?","choices":["Admit it only if Ray offers substantial proof of a probability that Silas committed the crime","Exclude it if its probative value is merely outweighed by the risk of confusing or misleading the jury","Admit it if it could raise a reasonable doubt, unless its value is substantially outweighed under § 352","Admit it without § 352 balancing, because the right to present a defense overrides the Code"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"People v. Hall (1986) holds that third-party culpability evidence is admissible if it is capable of raising a reasonable doubt of the defendant's guilt; motive or opportunity alone is not enough, but direct or circumstantial evidence linking the third person to the crime (such as a threat plus flight from the scene) suffices. Such evidence is treated like any other and is subject to Evidence Code § 352, which permits exclusion only when probative value is substantially outweighed by the probability of undue consumption of time or substantial danger of undue prejudice, confusing the issues, or misleading the jury. Hall rejected the older 'substantial proof of a probability' standard, and ordinary application of § 352 generally does not violate the right to present a defense.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","evidence","section-352","third-party-culpability"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Evid. Code § 352","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=EVID&sectionNum=352."}]},{"id":"ca-quasi-community-property","prompt":"While domiciled in New York, Nolan bought stock with his salary, titled solely in his name, and inherited a cabin in New York from his father. He and his wife, Ada, later moved to California, where they are now dissolving their marriage. How will the California court characterize these assets?","choices":["Both are Nolan's separate property, because he acquired both while domiciled in New York","The stock is quasi-community property; the cabin is Nolan's separate property","Both are quasi-community property, because Nolan acquired both of them during the marriage","The stock is Nolan's separate property; the cabin is quasi-community as real property"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fam. Code § 125 defines quasi-community property as property, wherever situated, that either spouse acquired while domiciled elsewhere and that would have been community property had the acquiring spouse been domiciled in California. Salary-funded stock would have been community here, so it is quasi-community property and is divided equally at dissolution like community property (§ 2550). An inheritance would have been separate property in California (§ 770), so the cabin remains Nolan's separate property.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["california-distinction","community-property","quasi-community","dissolution"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 125","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=125."}]},{"id":"ca-anti-slapp-appeal-stay","prompt":"A California trial court denied Dana's anti-SLAPP motion directed at two of the four causes of action in Eli's complaint. The ruling did not rest on any statutory exemption from the anti-SLAPP law. Dana promptly filed a notice of appeal, and Eli asks the trial court to keep the whole case on track for its trial date. What is the effect of Dana's appeal?","choices":["The order is immediately appealable, and the appeal automatically stays trial of the two challenged claims","The order is reviewable only by writ petition, so the trial proceeds unless the appellate court orders a stay","The order is immediately appealable, but whether to stay trial of the challenged claims is discretionary","The order is reviewable only after final judgment, so the case proceeds to trial on all four claims"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CCP § 425.16(i) makes an order granting or denying an anti-SLAPP motion appealable under § 904.1. In Varian Medical Systems, Inc. v. Delfino (2005), the California Supreme Court held that perfecting such an appeal automatically stays, under CCP § 916, all further trial-court proceedings on the merits of the causes of action affected by the motion; the trial court has no discretion to try them while the appeal is pending. One exception: under CCP § 425.17(e), a denial based on the public-interest or commercial-speech exemptions is not appealable.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","anti-slapp","appeal","stay"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 425.16","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=425.16."}]},{"id":"ca-learned-treatise-distinction","prompt":"In a California medical malpractice trial, the plaintiff's expert testifies that a well-known surgical textbook is a reliable authority and that she relied on it. Plaintiff's counsel then asks to read a passage from the textbook to the jury as substantive proof of the standard of care. The defense objects on hearsay grounds. How should the court rule?","choices":["Overrule it, because a reliable treatise may be read into evidence, though not received as an exhibit","Overrule it, because under People v. Sanchez an expert may relate any hearsay she relied upon","Sustain the objection, because California has no general learned-treatise exception for substantive use","Overrule it, because Evidence Code § 1341 admits books of science to prove the facts they state"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Federal Rules let a passage from an established treatise be read as substantive evidence (FRE 803(18)), but the California Evidence Code has no equivalent. Its closest exception, § 1341, covers historical works and books of science or art only when offered to prove facts of general notoriety and interest, not a contested standard of care. An expert may rely on treatises and may be cross-examined with them (§ 721(b)), and People v. Sanchez (2016) lets experts describe generally accepted background knowledge in their field; but Sanchez forbids relating case-specific hearsay as true, and it does not make a treatise independent proof of its contents.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","evidence","learned-treatise","hearsay","experts"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Evid. Code § 1341","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=EVID&sectionNum=1341."}]},{"id":"ca-employment-at-will-presumption","prompt":"Tess worked for a California company for eight years under no written contract and no specified term, earning steady raises and strong reviews. The company had no policy promising termination only for cause. It fired her without giving a reason, and no discriminatory or retaliatory motive is alleged. Does she have a viable claim that the firing breached her employment contract?","choices":["Yes, because after years of satisfactory service, an implied promise to fire only for good cause arises as a matter of law","No; employment without a specified term is presumed at will, and tenure and good reviews alone do not rebut that","Yes, because the implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing bars firing a long-term employee without good cause","No, because California law makes all private employment terminable at will, and the parties cannot agree otherwise"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Labor Code § 2922 provides that employment having no specified term may be terminated at the will of either party on notice to the other. The presumption can be overcome by an express or implied-in-fact agreement limiting termination (Foley v. Interactive Data Corp. (1988)), but Guz v. Bechtel National, Inc. (2000) held that longevity, raises, and favorable reviews do not by themselves create an implied for-cause contract. The implied covenant cannot impose substantive terms beyond the contract, and parties remain free to agree to job security.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","employment","at-will"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Lab. Code § 2922","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=LAB&sectionNum=2922."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-joint-title-2640","prompt":"During the marriage, Rosa used $80,000 she had inherited as the down payment on a house. The deed names Rosa and her husband, Ben, 'as joint tenants,' and community earnings paid the mortgage. The spouses signed no other writing about the house. At their California dissolution, the equity is $500,000. How should the court divide the equity?","choices":["Divide the full $500,000 equally, because Rosa's down payment is presumed a gift to the community","Give Rosa a share of the equity proportional to her down payment, then divide the balance equally","Divide the full $500,000 equally, because each joint tenant owns an undivided half as separate property","Reimburse Rosa $80,000 without interest or appreciation, then divide the remaining $420,000 equally"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Fam. Code § 2581, property acquired during marriage in joint form is presumed community property at dissolution, rebuttable only by a clear statement in the deed or a written agreement that it is separate; neither exists. Section 2640 then entitles a spouse to reimbursement for traceable separate-property contributions to the acquisition of community property (down payments, improvements, principal reduction), without interest or adjustment for appreciation, unless she waived that right in writing. Section 2640 superseded In re Marriage of Lucas (1980), which had presumed such contributions were gifts to the community. A proportional interest is the Moore/Marsden approach for separate-property homes, not for jointly titled community property.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","title-presumption","section-2581","section-2640","reimbursement"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 2640","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=2640."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-2640-transmutation","prompt":"Before marriage, Gil bought a condominium in his own name. During the marriage, his wife, Hana, used $60,000 she had inherited to pay down the principal on Gil's condo loan. Hana signed no writing about the payment, and the condo remains Gil's separate property. At dissolution, what is Hana entitled to?","choices":["Reimbursement of $60,000, without interest, since she signed no transmutation or waiver","Nothing, because her payment on Gil's separate property is presumed to be a gift to him","A separate ownership share in the condo, proportional to her payment, with its appreciation","Nothing, unless she reserved a right to repayment in a writing that satisfies § 852"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Fam. Code § 2640(c) reimburses a spouse for separate-property contributions to the other spouse's separate property during marriage, without interest or adjustment for changes in value, unless there has been a written transmutation under the Family Code's transmutation chapter (§ 850 et seq.) or a written waiver of reimbursement. The writing requirement cuts against Gil, not Hana: to defeat reimbursement he would need a § 852 writing in which Hana expressly declared she was giving him the money. The remedy is reimbursement, not a proportional ownership interest that shares in appreciation.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","section-2640","transmutation","section-852","reimbursement"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 2640","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=2640."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-gift-exception","prompt":"During the marriage, Wes used community funds to buy his wife, June, a $4,000 watch for her birthday and, later, a $250,000 beach cottage titled solely in June's name. The couple is wealthy, and no writing accompanied either gift. At dissolution, which of these assets is June's separate property?","choices":["The cottage, but not the watch","The watch, but not the cottage","Both the watch and the cottage","Neither the watch nor the cottage"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A transmutation of community property into separate property requires a writing containing an express declaration by the adversely affected spouse (Fam. Code § 852(a)). Section 852(c) exempts interspousal gifts of clothing, jewelry, or other tangible articles of a personal nature used principally by the recipient and not substantial in value given the circumstances of the marriage, so the watch is June's. Taking title to the cottage in June's name is not an express declaration by Wes; In re Marriage of Valli (2014) held that form of title cannot override the transmutation statute, so the cottage remains community property.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","transmutation","section-852","gifts","valli"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 852","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=852."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-macdonald","prompt":"Mid-marriage, Frances rolled her community-funded pension into an IRA and named her children from a prior marriage as beneficiaries. Her husband, Al, signed the account's consent form, which said only that he 'consents to the beneficiary designation.' After Frances died, Al claimed a community half of the IRA. What result?","choices":["The children take it all, because Al's signed consent satisfied the writing requirement","The children take it all, because evidence of Al's intent can supplement the signed form","Al keeps his half, because a transmutation of retirement funds must be notarized","Al keeps his half, because the form never expressly stated a change in ownership"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Fam. Code § 852(a), a transmutation is valid only if made in a writing with an express declaration by the spouse whose interest is adversely affected. Estate of MacDonald (1990), on nearly these facts, held that the writing must on its face show an intent to change the character or ownership of the property, and extrinsic evidence cannot supply that intent; a bare consent to a beneficiary designation is not enough. Notarization is not required.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","transmutation","section-852","estate-of-macdonald"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 852","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=852."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-moore-marsden","prompt":"Before marriage, Hal bought a house for $300,000, paying $60,000 down and financing $240,000. When he married, it was worth $400,000. During the marriage, community funds paid $50,000 of loan principal (plus interest and taxes). At separation, the house was worth $600,000. Under the Moore/Marsden formula, what is the community's interest in the house?","choices":["$83,333","$50,000","$100,000","$150,000"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under In re Marriage of Moore (1980) and In re Marriage of Marsden (1982), community payments of principal on a separate-property home give the community a pro tanto interest. The community percentage is community principal paid divided by the purchase price ($50,000 / $300,000 = 1/6). The community's interest is its principal reduction plus that percentage of the appreciation during marriage: $50,000 + 1/6 x ($600,000 - $400,000) = $83,333. Premarital appreciation is Hal's separate property, and interest and tax payments do not count. $50,000 is bare reimbursement; $100,000 applies the community share to all appreciation since purchase; $150,000 treats all marital appreciation as community.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","moore-marsden","apportionment","real-property"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-pereira-van-camp","prompt":"Before marriage, Lin owned a small software consulting firm worth $200,000. During the 10-year marriage, she worked full time running it, drew a modest salary, and through her skill and long hours grew it to $2 million while the industry as a whole was flat. At dissolution, how should the court most likely apportion the growth?","choices":["Treat the reasonable value of Lin's services, less family expenses paid, as community and the rest as separate","Treat all of the growth as Lin's separate property, because the profits of separate property remain separate","Allocate a fair return on Lin's premarital capital to her separate estate and treat the rest as community","Treat the entire firm, including its premarital value, as community because of Lin's full-time labor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When a separate-property business grows during marriage, California courts apportion under Pereira v. Pereira (1909) or Van Camp v. Van Camp (1921). Pereira, favored when growth is mainly due to the spouse's personal efforts, allocates a fair return on the separate capital (often the legal rate of interest) to separate property and the balance to the community. Van Camp, favored when growth comes from the character of the capital or market forces, treats the reasonable value of the spouse's services, less family expenses already paid from the business, as community and the rest as separate. Growth driven by Lin's efforts in a flat industry fits Pereira, and the premarital value stays separate either way.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","pereira","van-camp","business-apportionment"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-education-2641","prompt":"During the marriage, community funds paid $120,000 of Nadia's law school tuition, and she now earns far more as a lawyer. Her husband, Ty, files for dissolution four years after she graduated and argues that her law degree is a community asset. What is the community's remedy?","choices":["An equal division of the present value of the enhanced earning capacity that Nadia's degree produced","Reimbursement of the tuition without interest, just like a reimbursement for separate property contributions","Nothing, because education expenses are treated like the ordinary living expenses of the community","Reimbursement of the education costs with interest, reduced to the extent the community already benefited"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fam. Code § 2641 requires the community to be reimbursed, with interest at the legal rate, for community contributions to education or training that substantially enhances a spouse's earning capacity. The reimbursement may be reduced or modified to the extent the community has substantially benefited from the education; substantial benefit is presumed only when the contributions were made more than 10 years before the proceeding began. Section 2641(d) makes this the exclusive remedy, so California does not divide a degree or enhanced earning capacity as property. Unlike § 2640 reimbursement, § 2641 reimbursement carries interest.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","section-2641","education","reimbursement"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 2641","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=2641."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-personal-injury","prompt":"During the marriage, Elena was injured by a negligent driver and received a $200,000 settlement, which she deposited in an account in her own name and never mixed with other funds. Two years later, she and her husband, Rafe, divorce in California. How should the court treat the settlement funds?","choices":["As community property divided equally, like any other asset acquired during the course of the marriage","As Elena's separate property, because damages for bodily injury always belong to the injured spouse alone","As community property assigned to Elena, unless justice requires otherwise, and even then she gets at least half","As Elena's separate property, subject to reimbursing the community for any medical bills it has paid"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Personal injury damages for a cause of action arising during marriage are community property (Fam. Code § 780), but at dissolution § 2603 assigns uncommingled 'community estate personal injury damages' to the injured spouse unless the interests of justice require another disposition, in which case the injured spouse still receives at least one-half. The damages are separate property only in the situations listed in § 781 (for example, the cause of action arose after separation or the other spouse caused the injury), and the community-reimbursement rule applies to those separate recoveries.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","personal-injury-damages","section-2603","section-781"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 2603","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=2603."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-pension-time-rule","prompt":"Omar earned pension credit with his employer for 20 years in total: 4 years before he married Bea, 12 years during the marriage before they separated, and 4 years after separation. His pension had not vested when they separated. Under the time rule, what share of each pension payment does Bea receive?","choices":["30 percent of each payment, as the pension is paid","None, because the pension had not vested at separation","60 percent of each payment, as the pension is paid","50 percent of each payment, as the pension is paid"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Pension rights earned during marriage are community property even if they are unvested (In re Marriage of Brown (1976)). Under the time rule, the community fraction is the years of service during marriage and before separation divided by total years of service: 12/20 = 60%. Bea owns half of the community fraction, or 30% of each payment. 60% is the entire community share, and 50% ignores the premarital and post-separation service.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","pensions","time-rule","brown"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-date-of-separation","prompt":"In March 2023, Kara told her husband, Leo, that the marriage was over and she wanted a divorce. She moved into the guest room, opened separate accounts, and stopped attending family events with him, but for financial reasons they kept living in the same house until she moved out in June 2024. Kara earned a large bonus for work she performed in late 2023. Under current California law, how is the bonus most likely characterized?","choices":["Community property, because the date of separation cannot precede the date the spouses began living apart","Kara's separate property, because the marriage can have ended in March 2023 despite their shared home","Community property, because earnings remain community until a judgment of dissolution is entered","Community property, because the date of separation is the date a petition for dissolution is filed"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fam. Code § 70 defines the date of separation as the date a complete and final break in the marital relationship occurred, shown by a spouse's expressed intent to end the marriage and conduct consistent with that intent, considering all relevant evidence. The Legislature enacted § 70 to abrogate In re Marriage of Davis (2015), which had required the spouses to be living apart. Earnings after the date of separation are separate property (§ 771), so a bonus for work after March 2023 is Kara's. Neither the filing date nor the judgment date controls.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","date-of-separation","section-70","section-771"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 70","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=70."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-qcp-at-death","prompt":"While domiciled in Illinois, Harold bought stock with his salary, titled solely in his name, and his wife, Wanda, saved part of her salary in an account titled solely in hers. They then moved to California. Harold died domiciled here, with a will leaving all of his property to his son from a prior marriage. What are Wanda's rights in these assets?","choices":["She keeps all of her account; Harold's stock passes entirely under his will to his son","She keeps all of her account and owns half of Harold's stock; his will governs the rest","The two assets are pooled; she takes half of the total and Harold's son takes the other half","She owns half of Harold's stock, but half of her own account passes under Harold's will"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"At death, Prob. Code § 101 gives the surviving spouse one-half of the decedent's quasi-community property, and the decedent may dispose of only the other half. The rule runs one way: the decedent has no testamentary power over the survivor's quasi-community property, so Wanda keeps all of her account. California has no elective share; quasi-community property serves that protective role for property earned in common-law states. Pooling both spouses' quasi-community property is the dissolution approach, not the rule at death.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","quasi-community","probate-code-101","death"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 101","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=101."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-fiduciary-1101","prompt":"During the marriage, Max secretly moved $80,000 of community funds into an account in his brother's name and left it out of his dissolution disclosures, intending to keep it from his wife, Nell. The court finds by clear and convincing evidence that Max acted with fraud. What remedy does the Family Code direct the court to include?","choices":["An award to Nell of 50 percent of the account plus her fees","An equal division of the account, since the funds still exist","Nell's half of the account plus Civil Code punitive damages","An award to Nell of 100 percent of the concealed account"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Spouses owe each other the fiduciary duty of highest good faith and fair dealing (Fam. Code § 721), including full disclosure of community assets (§ 1100(e)). For a breach, § 1101(g) provides an award of 50 percent of any undisclosed or improperly transferred asset plus attorney's fees and costs; when the breach falls within Civil Code § 3294 (oppression, fraud, or malice), § 1101(h) raises the award to 100 percent of the asset. Because Max acted fraudulently, the court may award Nell the entire account; the Family Code's enhanced remedy is the 100 percent asset award rather than a separate punitive damages award.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","fiduciary-duty","section-721","section-1101"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 1101","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=1101."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-premarital-1615","prompt":"Three days before their wedding, Jordan handed Ana the final premarital agreement, which waived her community interest in his future earnings, and urged her to see a lawyer. Ana consulted no one, signed no separate waiver of counsel, and signed the agreement that day. Jordan had fully disclosed his finances, and there was no duress. At their later divorce, is the agreement enforceable?","choices":["Yes, because Jordan fully disclosed his finances and Ana signed it without any duress or fraud","Yes, because a premarital agreement need only be in writing and signed by both of the parties","No, because it is deemed involuntary when she had less than seven days to review the final agreement","No, because a waiver of community property rights is valid only if she had independent counsel"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Fam. Code § 1615(c) deems a premarital agreement involuntary unless the court finds, among other things, that the party against whom enforcement is sought had at least seven calendar days between first receiving the final agreement and signing it, and, if unrepresented, was fully informed in writing of the terms and rights waived and waived counsel in a separate writing. Three days fails the seven-day rule regardless of disclosure and lack of duress. Mandatory independent counsel applies to waivers of spousal support (§ 1612(c)), not property rights; an unrepresented party can waive property rights if § 1615(c)'s safeguards are met.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","premarital-agreement","section-1615","voluntariness"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 1615","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=1615."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-cp-putative-spouse","prompt":"Maria married Tom in a licensed California ceremony. Unknown to Maria, Tom's divorce from his first wife had never become final, so the marriage was void. After 12 years the truth emerged and they separated. Maria sincerely believed the marriage was valid, though she never checked court records that would have revealed the problem. What are her property rights?","choices":["As a putative spouse, she shares the quasi-marital property as if it were community property","None as a spouse, because a void marriage supports only contract claims of the Marvin type","None as a spouse, because her belief in the marriage's validity was not objectively reasonable","As a putative spouse, she takes all quasi-marital property, because Tom acted in bad faith"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Fam. Code § 2251, if either party believed in good faith that a void or voidable marriage was valid, the court declares that party a putative spouse and divides the quasi-marital property (property acquired during the union that would have been community or quasi-community property) as if it were community property. Ceja v. Rudolph & Sletten, Inc. (2013) held the good-faith inquiry is subjective; reasonableness bears only on whether the belief was genuinely held, so Maria's failure to check records is not disqualifying. Tom's bad faith does not give Maria the whole estate; the division is equal.","subject":"communityProperty","tags":["community-property","putative-spouse","quasi-marital-property","section-2251"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Fam. Code § 2251","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FAM&sectionNum=2251."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-holographic","prompt":"On a commercially printed will form, Iris handwrote the names of her beneficiaries and the property each should receive, then signed at the bottom. The form's printed text included 'I give my estate as follows.' The form is undated and unwitnessed, and Iris left no other will. Is it valid in California?","choices":["No, because a holographic will must be dated in the testator's own handwriting to be valid","No, because the printed words must be disregarded, leaving no statement of testamentary intent","No, because a will that is not entirely handwritten must be signed before two witnesses","Yes, as a holographic will, since her signature and the material provisions are in her handwriting"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Prob. Code § 6111(a) validates a will, witnessed or not, if the signature and the material provisions are in the testator's handwriting, and § 6111(c) allows the statement of testamentary intent to appear in the printed portion of a commercial form. A date is not required: an undated holographic will is invalid only to the extent it conflicts with another will whose order of execution is unclear, or when capacity during the possible execution period is in doubt. Iris's handwritten dispositions and signature suffice.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","holographic-will","section-6111","formalities"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 6111","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=6111."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-harmless-error","prompt":"Kenji signed his typed will in front of his neighbor, who signed as a witness. The second intended witness left before signing. Kenji died a month later. His sister offers emails in which Kenji repeatedly called the signed document 'my final will.' Can the will be admitted to probate?","choices":["No, because an attested will must be signed by two witnesses who were present together","Yes, if clear and convincing evidence shows Kenji intended it as his will when he signed it","Yes, if a preponderance of the evidence shows Kenji intended it as his will when he signed","Yes, but only if the missing witness signs the will before it is offered for probate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Prob. Code § 6110 requires two witnesses, present at the same time, who understand the instrument is the testator's will and sign during his lifetime. Under § 6110(c)(2), a will not executed in compliance with that witnessing requirement is treated as if it were if the proponent establishes by clear and convincing evidence that, when the testator signed, he intended it to be his will. The standard is clear and convincing evidence, not a preponderance, and a witness cannot cure the defect by signing after the testator's death.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","attested-will","section-6110","harmless-error"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 6110","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=6110."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-dependent-relative-revocation","prompt":"Pia's typed, properly witnessed will left '$10,000 to my nephew Sam.' Later Pia drew a line through '$10,000' and wrote '$15,000' above it, but she did not sign or date the change, and no one witnessed it. Evidence shows she wanted Sam to get more. Pia has died. What does Sam take?","choices":["$10,000, because the cancellation is disregarded as conditioned on an effective new gift","$15,000, because the handwritten change is a valid holographic codicil to the typed will","Nothing, because the line revoked the original gift and the unsigned new gift is ineffective","$15,000, because the harmless-error rule validates any change the testator clearly intended"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"California allows partial revocation of a will by physical act, such as canceling a provision with intent to revoke it (Prob. Code § 6120). The $15,000 gift fails because it was neither witnessed nor signed, so it is neither an attested nor a holographic codicil, and the harmless-error rule of § 6110(c)(2) cures only defective witnessing, not a missing signature. Under dependent relative revocation, a revocation premised on the mistaken belief that a substitute gift will be effective is disregarded when the testator would prefer the original gift to nothing, so Sam takes $10,000.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","revocation","physical-act","dependent-relative-revocation"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 6120","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=6120."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-divorce-revocation","prompt":"Gabe's will left his entire estate 'to my wife, Dina, but if she does not survive me, to her daughter, Ella.' Ella is Gabe's stepdaughter; he never adopted her. Gabe and Dina later divorced, and Gabe died without changing his will. Dina and Ella both survive him. Who takes Gabe's estate?","choices":["Dina, because Gabe never revoked or amended his will after the divorce","Gabe's heirs, because divorce revokes gifts to Dina and to her relatives","Ella, because the will takes effect as if Dina had not survived Gabe","Gabe's heirs, because Ella's gift required that Dina actually die first"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Prob. Code § 6122, dissolution or annulment of the testator's marriage revokes will provisions in favor of the former spouse (including her nomination as executor or trustee) unless the will expressly provides otherwise, and the property passes as if the former spouse failed to survive the testator. That satisfies the condition on Ella's alternate gift. Unlike UPC § 2-804, California's statute does not revoke gifts to the former spouse's relatives, so Ella takes even though she is Dina's daughter.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","revocation-by-divorce","section-6122","ca-vs-upc"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 6122","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=6122."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-omitted-spouse","prompt":"In 2015, Victor executed a will leaving everything to his brother, Ned. In 2021, Victor married Ruth; he never updated the will, which does not mention a future spouse. Victor died in 2025 with no children and no surviving parents, leaving $300,000 of separate property and a community estate. Ruth signed no waiver and received no nonprobate transfers from Victor. What does Ruth take from Victor's estate?","choices":["Victor's half of the community property only, since his will gives the rest to Ned","Victor's half of the community property and all of his separate property","An elective share of one-third of Victor's estate, community and separate combined","Victor's half of the community property and one-half of his separate property"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A spouse who married the testator after all of his testamentary instruments were executed, and who is not provided for, is an omitted spouse (Prob. Code § 21610) unless a § 21611 exception applies: intentional omission appearing in the instrument, a transfer outside the estate in lieu of a share, or a valid waiver. She receives the decedent's half of the community and quasi-community property plus the share of his separate property she would take in intestacy, capped at one-half. Because Victor left a sibling, that intestate share is one-half (§ 6401(c)). California has no elective share.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","omitted-spouse","section-21610","intestate-share"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 21610","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=21610."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-omitted-child","prompt":"When Wen executed her will, she had one daughter, Ava. The will left her entire estate to her husband, Lou, who is Ava's father, and did not mention children. Two years later, Wen and Lou had a son, Max. Wen died without changing her will, survived by Lou, Ava, and Max. Does Max take an omitted-child share?","choices":["Yes, because he was born after the will was executed, and the will makes no provision for him at all","No, because Wen had a child when she made the will and left substantially all of her estate to Max's other parent","Yes, but only the amount needed to give him a share equal to whatever his sister Ava receives under the will","No, because the omitted-child statute protects only children who were alive when the will was executed"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Prob. Code § 21620 gives a child born or adopted after all of the decedent's testamentary instruments were executed an intestate share, subject to the exceptions in § 21621. One exception applies when the decedent had one or more children when the instrument was executed and devised substantially all of the estate to the other parent of the omitted child, as Wen did with Lou. Ava takes nothing under the will either, and the statute protects after-born children, not those living at execution (except where the decedent mistakenly believed a child was dead or did not know of the child).","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","omitted-child","section-21620","section-21621"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 21621","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=21621."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-anti-lapse","prompt":"Tara's will left $50,000 'to my stepson, Leo,' the son of her late husband. Leo died before Tara, survived by his daughter, Nia. The will contains no survivorship condition and no alternate gift, and the residue goes to a charity. Who takes the $50,000?","choices":["The charity, because anti-lapse protects only the testator's own kin","The charity, because a gift to a predeceased beneficiary always lapses","Nia, because Leo was kin of Tara's deceased spouse","Leo's estate, to pass under his will or to his heirs"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"California's anti-lapse statute, Prob. Code § 21110, substitutes the issue of a predeceased transferee who is kin of the transferor or kin of the transferor's surviving, deceased, or former spouse. Leo is kin of Tara's late husband, so his daughter Nia takes in his place. Some states' statutes cover only the testator's own relatives, and at common law the gift would lapse into the residue. A requirement that Leo survive Tara would have expressed a contrary intention, but the will has none.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","anti-lapse","section-21110"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 21110","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=21110."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-intestacy-cp-sp","prompt":"Mira died intestate, survived by her husband, Cal, and two children: Jo, her child with Cal, and Rex, her son from a prior relationship. Her estate consists of her half of the couple's community property and $300,000 of her separate property. How is her estate distributed?","choices":["Cal takes her half of the community property and one-third of her separate property; the children share the rest","Cal takes her half of the community property and one-half of her separate property; the two children share the rest","Cal takes one-third of her community and separate property alike; the two children share the remainder","Cal takes her half of the community property, and the children take all of her separate property"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under Prob. Code § 6401, the surviving spouse takes the decedent's half of the community and quasi-community property, so Cal ends up owning all of it. His share of separate property depends on who else survives: one-third when the decedent leaves more than one child. Whether the children are also the surviving spouse's children does not matter in California, unlike the UPC, which reduces the spouse's share when the decedent has children who are not the spouse's. Jo and Rex split the other two-thirds.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","intestate-succession","section-6401"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 6401","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=6401."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-pour-over","prompt":"In 2018, Dev signed a revocable trust instrument but put no assets into the trust. A week later, he executed a will leaving his residuary estate 'to the trustee of my 2018 trust.' In 2022, Dev amended the trust to add his niece as a beneficiary. Dev has died. How is the residue distributed?","choices":["To the trustee, under the 2018 terms, because later amendments are not incorporated","To the trustee, to be distributed under the trust as amended in 2022","By intestacy, because the trust held no assets when the will was executed","To the trustee under the 2022 terms only if the amendment had will formalities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Prob. Code § 6300 validates a devise to the trustee of a trust identified in the will whose terms are set out in a written instrument executed before, concurrently with, or shortly after the will, regardless of whether the trust holds any property. Unless the will provides otherwise, the devised property is administered under the trust's terms, including amendments made after the will was executed. Incorporation by reference, by contrast, reaches only a writing in existence when the will was executed, which would freeze the 2018 terms; § 6300 avoids that limit, and trust amendments need not meet will formalities.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","trusts","pour-over","section-6300","incorporation-by-reference"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 6300","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=6300."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-care-custodian","prompt":"Opal, an 88-year-old dependent adult, executed a new will leaving $200,000 to Ray, her paid in-home caregiver, while he was caring for her. Ray is not related to Opal, did not draft the will, and no independent attorney reviewed the gift. Opal's nephew challenges the gift. How does California law treat it?","choices":["It is presumed the product of undue influence unless Ray proves otherwise by clear and convincing evidence","It is valid unless the nephew proves by a preponderance that Ray actively procured the will's execution","It is conclusively presumed the product of undue influence, and Ray may not offer evidence in rebuttal","It is presumed the product of undue influence unless Ray proves otherwise by a preponderance of evidence"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Prob. Code § 21380 presumes that a donative transfer to a care custodian of a dependent adult, in an instrument executed during the period of care (or within 90 days before or after), is the product of fraud or undue influence. The care custodian may rebut the presumption only by clear and convincing evidence and bears all costs, including attorney's fees, if he fails. The presumption is conclusive only for gifts to the person who drafted the instrument and certain persons connected to the drafter. Exceptions include gifts to relatives and gifts covered by an independent attorney's certificate of independent review.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","disqualified-donee","section-21380","undue-influence"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 21380","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=21380."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-no-contest","prompt":"Bea's will leaves her son, Cole, $100,000 and contains a no-contest clause. Cole filed a direct contest alleging undue influence by his sister, supported by witness statements that, when he filed, made it reasonably likely he would win after discovery. After trial, the court rejected the contest. Does Cole forfeit his $100,000?","choices":["Yes, because a no-contest clause is enforced against any beneficiary whose contest ultimately fails","No, because California treats no-contest clauses as void and unenforceable as against public policy","Yes, because the probable-cause exception protects only contests alleging forgery or revocation","No, because the clause is enforceable only against a direct contest brought without probable cause"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Prob. Code § 21311(a)(1), a no-contest clause may be enforced against a direct contest only if the contest was brought without probable cause. Probable cause exists if, when the contest was filed, the facts known to the contestant would cause a reasonable person to believe there was a reasonable likelihood the requested relief would be granted after an opportunity for further investigation or discovery (§ 21311(b)). Losing does not establish a lack of probable cause. California enforces no-contest clauses within these limits, and the exception is not confined to particular grounds.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["wills","no-contest-clause","section-21311","probable-cause"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 21311","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=21311."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-trust-revocability","prompt":"In 2019, Rhea, a California resident, signed a trust instrument transferring an apartment building to her friend Paul as trustee for her grandchildren. The instrument says nothing about whether or how the trust can be revoked. Rhea now signs a letter revoking the trust and delivers it to Paul. Is the trust revoked?","choices":["No, because a trust is irrevocable unless the settlor expressly reserved a power to revoke it","No, because revoking a trust requires the consent of all of the trust's current beneficiaries","Yes, because a California trust is revocable unless its instrument expressly makes it irrevocable","No, because a trust that is silent on revocation can be revoked only by the settlor's will"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Prob. Code § 15400 reverses the traditional common-law presumption: unless a trust is expressly made irrevocable by the trust instrument, the settlor may revoke it. When the instrument provides no exclusive method, § 15401 permits revocation by a writing (other than a will) signed by the settlor and delivered to the trustee during the settlor's lifetime, which is exactly what Rhea did. Beneficiary consent matters for modifying or terminating irrevocable trusts, not for a settlor's revocation of a revocable one.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["trusts","revocation","section-15400","section-15401"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 15400","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=15400."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-trustee-loyalty","prompt":"Without authorization in the trust instrument, court approval, or the beneficiaries' consent, Trustee Quinn bought a trust-owned lot for herself at its full appraised value. Two years later the lot had doubled in value. The beneficiaries sue Quinn. What is the likely outcome?","choices":["No breach, because she paid the full appraised value and the trust itself suffered no loss","No breach, unless the beneficiaries prove she acted in bad faith or concealed the purchase","She breached the duty of loyalty, so the beneficiaries can set aside the sale or recover her profit","She breached the duty to invest prudently, so the beneficiaries recover only interest on the price"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Prob. Code § 16004 forbids a trustee from using or dealing with trust property for her own profit. A trustee's purchase of trust property is a breach of the duty of loyalty even if the price is fair and she acted in good faith (the 'no further inquiry' rule), unless authorized by the trust instrument, the court, or the beneficiaries' informed consent. The beneficiaries may rescind the sale or hold Quinn accountable for her profit, for example through a constructive trust. Fairness review applies to an interested director's transaction under Corp. Code § 310, not to a trustee's self-dealing.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["trusts","trustee-duties","duty-of-loyalty","self-dealing"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 16004","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=16004."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-wt-trust-termination","prompt":"Sal's irrevocable trust pays income to his daughter, Uma, until she turns 35 and then distributes the principal to her; if she dies sooner, the principal goes to her children. The trust has a valid spendthrift clause. Sal has died. Uma, now 28, and her minor and unborn children (through a guardian ad litem) all consent and petition to terminate the trust now. How should the court proceed?","choices":["It must end the trust, because all beneficiaries consent and the settlor is dead","It must deny the petition, because it has no discretion to end a spendthrift trust","It must deny the petition while any material purpose of the trust is unfulfilled","It may end the trust only if it finds good cause, given the spendthrift clause"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under Prob. Code § 15403(a), if all beneficiaries of an irrevocable trust consent, they may petition to modify or terminate it. If continuing the trust is necessary to carry out a material purpose, the court may terminate it only if, in its discretion, the reason for doing so outweighs the interest in accomplishing that purpose, a softer test than the strict common-law Claflin rule. Where the trust is subject to a valid spendthrift restraint, § 15403(b) adds that it may not be terminated unless the court finds good cause. Before a 2017 amendment, the statute denied courts any discretion to terminate a spendthrift trust under this section.","subject":"willsAndTrusts","tags":["trusts","modification-termination","section-15403","claflin","spendthrift"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Prob. Code § 15403","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=PROB&sectionNum=15403."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-apparent-authority","prompt":"For years, Ming, the purchasing manager of Oak Furniture, ordered lumber from Supplier on Oak's account, and Oak always paid. Oak's owner then privately told Ming to stop placing orders over $5,000 without approval, but no one told Supplier. Ming placed an unapproved $12,000 order, which Supplier delivered. Is Oak bound to pay?","choices":["Yes, because Oak's past conduct gave Ming apparent authority in Supplier's eyes","No, because Ming's actual authority was limited before she placed the order","Yes, because an agent who exceeds her authority binds the principal in every case","No, unless Oak later ratifies the order by accepting and using the lumber"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Apparent (ostensible) authority arises when the principal's manifestations to a third party, including a course of dealing in which the principal pays for the agent's orders, lead the third party reasonably to believe the agent is authorized (Cal. Civ. Code §§ 2300, 2317). A secret limit on actual authority does not bind a third party without notice of it. Oak is bound, though it may have a claim against Ming for exceeding her actual authority. Ratification is an alternative route to liability, not a prerequisite here.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["agency","apparent-authority","ostensible-authority"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Civ. Code § 2317","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=2317."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-partnership-liability","prompt":"Ana and Bo opened a food truck together, splitting the work and the profits equally. They never signed a partnership agreement or filed anything with the state, and Bo insists he never intended to be Ana's 'partner.' The business owes Supplier $30,000. Supplier wants to collect from Bo's personal savings. Under California's version of the Uniform Partnership Act, what is Bo's exposure?","choices":["Bo is not liable, because no partnership exists without a written agreement or an intent to form one","Bo is jointly and severally liable, but Supplier generally must first exhaust the partnership's assets","Bo is liable only up to the amount of capital he contributed to the food truck business","Bo is jointly and severally liable, and Supplier may go directly after his personal savings first"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An association of two or more persons to carry on as co-owners a business for profit forms a partnership whether or not they intend to (Corp. Code § 16202), and sharing profits raises a presumption of partnership. Partners are jointly and severally liable for partnership obligations (§ 16306), but a partnership creditor generally may not levy on a partner's personal assets unless a judgment on the same claim against the partnership has gone unsatisfied after exhausting partnership assets, or another statutory exception applies (§ 16307(d)). Liability limited to capital contributed describes limited partners and LLC members, not general partners.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["partnership","formation","partner-liability","rupa","exhaustion"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Corp. Code § 16306","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CORP&sectionNum=16306."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-llc-member-duties","prompt":"Pine LLC is a manager-managed California LLC whose operating agreement says nothing about competition. Rosa, a member who is not a manager and has no management role, opens a business that competes with Pine. The managers cause Pine to sue Rosa for breach of the duty of loyalty. What is the likely result?","choices":["Rosa loses, because every LLC member owes the company the same duty of loyalty a partner owes","Rosa wins, because a member of a manager-managed LLC owes no fiduciary duty solely as a member","Rosa loses, because any member's duty of loyalty bars competing with the LLC during membership","Rosa wins, because LLC members never owe fiduciary duties, even in a member-managed company"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under California's Revised Uniform Limited Liability Company Act, members of a member-managed LLC owe the duties of loyalty and care, but in a manager-managed LLC those duties fall on the managers, and a member has no fiduciary duty to the company or the other members solely by reason of being a member (Corp. Code § 17704.09). Rosa still owes the contractual obligation of good faith and fair dealing, but competing does not breach a loyalty duty she does not have. Members of a member-managed LLC do owe fiduciary duties, so the 'never' choice overstates the rule.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["llc","rullca","fiduciary-duty","manager-managed"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Corp. Code § 17704.09","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CORP&sectionNum=17704.09."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-promoter","prompt":"Before incorporating, promoter Vic signed a five-year lease 'on behalf of Bright Corp., a corporation to be formed.' The landlord never agreed to look only to the corporation. Bright Corp. was formed, moved in, and paid rent for six months, then defaulted. The landlord sues Vic personally. Is Vic liable?","choices":["No, because the corporation ratified the lease, which relates back and replaces Vic","No, because Vic signed only in a representative capacity for the future corporation","Yes, because the corporation's adoption does not release the promoter absent a novation","Yes, but only for rent that came due before Bright Corp. was formed and adopted the lease"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A promoter who signs a pre-incorporation contract is personally liable on it unless the other party agreed to look solely to the corporation. Once formed, the corporation becomes liable if it adopts the contract, expressly or impliedly by accepting its benefits, as Bright did by occupying the premises and paying rent. Adoption does not release the promoter; only a novation, in which the landlord agrees to substitute the corporation and discharge Vic, would do that. 'Ratification' does not fit because there was no existing principal when the lease was signed, so nothing relates back.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["corporations","promoter-liability","pre-incorporation-contract","novation"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-alter-ego","prompt":"Dan is the sole shareholder of Dan's Hauling, Inc., which he formed with $500 of capital. He paid personal bills from the corporate account and kept no separate records. A corporate truck injured Pat, and the corporation cannot pay Pat's $400,000 judgment. Pat seeks to hold Dan personally liable. Under California's alter ego doctrine, what must Pat show?","choices":["A unity of interest and ownership erasing separateness, and that honoring the form would be inequitable","That Dan formed the corporation with a specific intent to defraud future creditors such as Pat","That Dan disregarded corporate formalities, a showing that alone suffices to disregard the entity","That the corporation cannot pay the judgment, since insolvency alone justifies holding Dan personally liable"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"California disregards the corporate entity when (1) there is such a unity of interest and ownership that the separate personalities of the corporation and its owner no longer exist, and (2) treating the acts as those of the corporation alone would sanction fraud or promote injustice (Associated Vendors, Inc. v. Oakland Meat Co. (1962)). Commingling, inadequate capitalization, and disregard of formalities are factors, but no single factor is decisive. The plaintiff need not prove actual fraud or fraudulent intent, only an inequitable result, and a corporation's inability to pay a judgment is not enough by itself.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["corporations","piercing-the-veil","alter-ego"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-duty-of-care","prompt":"The board of Lark Corp., a California corporation, approved a $50 million acquisition at a 90-minute meeting, relying solely on the CEO's oral summary; no director read the agreement or asked for a valuation. No director had a personal interest, and the articles contain no provision limiting director liability. The acquisition failed, and shareholders sue the directors for breach of the duty of care. Does the business judgment rule protect them?","choices":["Yes, because the rule protects every board decision not tainted by a conflict of interest","Yes, because directors may rely on an officer's report without making any further inquiry","No, because directors are liable whenever a business decision causes the corporation a loss","No, because the rule protects only good-faith decisions made after reasonable inquiry"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Corp. Code § 309(a) requires directors to act in good faith, in the corporation's best interests, and with the care, including reasonable inquiry, that an ordinarily prudent person in a like position would use. The business judgment rule shields informed, good-faith decisions from hindsight review, but not a decision made without reasonable inquiry (compare Smith v. Van Gorkom). Reliance on officers' reports is protected only when the director acts in good faith after reasonable inquiry when the need is indicated (§ 309(b)). A bad outcome alone does not establish liability.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["corporations","duty-of-care","business-judgment-rule","section-309"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Corp. Code § 309","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CORP&sectionNum=309."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-interested-director-310","prompt":"Director Kim sold a warehouse she owned to Sol Corp., a California corporation, for $3 million. She fully disclosed her interest and all material facts, and the disinterested directors unanimously approved the purchase without counting her vote. An independent appraisal shows the warehouse was worth $2 million when the deal was approved. A shareholder sues to rescind. What result?","choices":["Rescission is likely, because board approval protects the deal only if it was just and reasonable to Sol","No rescission, because full disclosure and approval by disinterested directors cleanse the deal entirely","No rescission, because Kim did not vote, so her interest could not have affected the board's decision","Rescission is automatic, because any contract between a director and her corporation is void"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Corp. Code § 310(a) protects an interested-director transaction if (1) the shareholders approve it in good faith after full disclosure, without counting the interested director's shares; (2) the disinterested directors approve it after full disclosure and the transaction is just and reasonable to the corporation when authorized; or (3) the person asserting validity proves it was just and reasonable. Unlike Delaware's safe harbor, board approval under § 310(a)(2) still requires fairness, and paying $3 million for a $2 million building is not just and reasonable. Interested-director contracts are not void per se.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["corporations","duty-of-loyalty","interested-director","section-310"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Corp. Code § 310","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CORP&sectionNum=310."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-corporate-opportunity","prompt":"Teo is a director and officer of Coast Realty, Inc., which buys and renovates small apartment buildings and has ample cash on hand. A broker approached Teo, in his role at Coast, about a building that fit Coast's acquisition criteria. Without telling the board, Teo bought the building through his own LLC and made a large profit. Coast sues. What is the likely result?","choices":["No breach, because Teo used his own money and did not use any corporate assets to buy the building","No breach, because a director may pursue an opportunity unless the corporation already invested in it","Teo breached his duty of care, so Coast's recovery is limited to damages for the lost opportunity","Teo breached his duty of loyalty, and Coast may impose a constructive trust on the building or profits"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A director or officer may not usurp a corporate opportunity, one in the corporation's line of business or in which it has an interest or expectancy, especially one that reaches him through his corporate position, without first disclosing it and offering it to the corporation. Coast was actively buying such buildings and had the funds, so taking the deal without disclosure to the board breached the duty of loyalty. The usual remedy is a constructive trust over the property or the profits, and using personal funds is no defense.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["corporations","duty-of-loyalty","corporate-opportunity","constructive-trust"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-derivative-bond","prompt":"A shareholder who owns 1 percent of Ridge Corp., a California corporation, files a derivative suit against its directors. The directors move under Corporations Code § 800 to require the shareholder to furnish security for their expenses. What standard governs the motion?","choices":["The court must require a bond, because the plaintiff holds less than 5 percent of the corporation's outstanding shares","The court may require a bond only if the directors show the plaintiff brought the suit in bad faith or to harass them","The court may order a capped bond if the suit has no reasonable possibility of benefiting the corporation or its shareholders","The court may not require a bond, because a derivative plaintiff sues on the corporation's behalf and owes no security"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under Corp. Code § 800, a defendant may move for an order requiring the derivative plaintiff to furnish a bond on the ground that there is no reasonable possibility the action will benefit the corporation or its shareholders (or, for a defendant other than the corporation, that the defendant did not participate in the challenged transaction). If the court so finds, it sets security for the defendants' reasonable expenses, capped at $50,000. California has no automatic ownership-percentage trigger like New York's, and no showing of bad faith is required. Section 800 also requires the plaintiff to plead demand efforts or the reasons for not making them.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["corporations","derivative-suit","section-800","security-for-expenses"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Corp. Code § 800","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CORP&sectionNum=800."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-ba-section-16b","prompt":"Vera, an officer of a company whose stock is registered under the Securities Exchange Act, bought 1,000 shares at $20 on January 10, sold 1,000 shares at $15 on March 1, and bought 1,000 shares at $10 on April 1. She had no material nonpublic information. A shareholder sues under Section 16(b). How much must Vera pay the company?","choices":["Nothing, because she lost money overall on her trades during the period","$5,000, because a sale may be matched with any purchase within six months","Nothing, because she did not trade on any material nonpublic information","Nothing, because Section 16(b) reaches only a purchase followed by a sale"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Section 16(b) of the Securities Exchange Act requires officers, directors, and more-than-10% beneficial owners to disgorge any profit from a purchase and sale, or sale and purchase, of the company's equity securities within any six-month period. Liability is strict: unlike Rule 10b-5, no use of inside information is required. Courts match the lowest purchase price against the highest sale price within the period, regardless of order or overall gain or loss, so the March 1 sale at $15 matched with the April 1 purchase at $10 yields $5,000.","subject":"businessAssociations","tags":["securities","section-16b","short-swing-profits","rule-10b-5"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-preliminary-injunction","prompt":"A homeowner seeks a preliminary injunction in California superior court to halt construction that would permanently block her easement. The judge finds the interim harm to her would be severe and irreparable while the builder's harm from a delay would be slight, but rates her chance of winning at trial as reasonable rather than strong. The judge denies relief, ruling that she must first show she is more likely than not to prevail. Was that correct?","choices":["Yes, because each factor (likely success, irreparable harm, equities, public interest) must be met","No, because when interim harm is severe the court need not consider the merits of the case at all","Yes, because a preliminary injunction requires clear and convincing proof of success on the merits","No, because the court must weigh likelihood of success and the relative interim harms together"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In California, a trial court ruling on a preliminary injunction weighs two interrelated factors: the likelihood that the plaintiff will prevail on the merits and the relative interim harm to the parties from issuing or denying the injunction (IT Corp. v. County of Imperial (1983)). The greater the showing on one factor, the less is required on the other, though some possibility of success is always required (Butt v. State of California (1992)). The four-part test of Winter v. NRDC, requiring each element, governs federal courts, not California courts.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","preliminary-injunction","it-corp","california-distinction"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-encroachment","prompt":"Relying on a licensed surveyor's mistaken survey, Gwen built a $900,000 home whose foundation extends 18 inches onto her neighbor Hal's unused hillside strip. Removal would cost $400,000, and the strip has minimal value to Hal. Hal sues for a mandatory injunction requiring removal. What is the court most likely to do?","choices":["Order removal, because a continuing trespass on unique land is enjoined regardless of cost","Deny all relief, because the encroachment is minor and Gwen acted entirely in good faith","Deny the injunction, recognize an equitable easement for Gwen, and award Hal damages","Order removal, because balancing the hardships applies only to nuisance, not to trespass"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In deciding whether to grant a permanent injunction, courts consider the adequacy of legal remedies, feasibility, defenses, and the relative hardships. For encroachments, California courts may refuse a removal injunction and instead recognize an equitable easement when the encroacher was innocent (not willful or negligent), the owner will not suffer irreparable injury, and the hardship of removal would be greatly disproportionate to the harm of leaving the structure (e.g., Christensen v. Tucker (1952); Hirshfield v. Schwartz (2001)). The owner is compensated with damages, and a willful encroacher would not receive this balancing.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","permanent-injunction","balancing-hardships","equitable-easement"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-specific-performance-3391","prompt":"Iva, elderly and unaware of recent nearby sales, signed a contract to sell her vacant lot to a developer for $600,000. Comparable lots were then selling for about $1 million. Nothing suggests fraud. Iva refused to close, and the developer sued for specific performance. Under California law, what is the likely result?","choices":["Specific performance is granted, because land is unique and the price is irrelevant","Specific performance is denied, because Iva did not receive adequate consideration","Specific performance is granted, because a signed land contract is presumed fair","Specific performance is denied, because a seller may always elect to pay damages instead"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Civil Code § 3391 bars specific performance against a party who has not received adequate consideration or as to whom the contract is not just and reasonable, among other grounds. The common law generally does not weigh the adequacy of consideration, but California requires it for equitable enforcement. Land is presumed unique, so damages are presumed inadequate (§ 3387), but that presumption does not override § 3391. The developer may still seek damages at law.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","specific-performance","adequacy-of-consideration","civil-code-3391"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Civ. Code § 3391","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=3391."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-constructive-trust","prompt":"Bookkeeper Lars embezzled $100,000 from his employer, Mesa Co., and used it to buy shares of stock now worth $300,000. Lars is solvent. Mesa sues. Which remedy lets Mesa capture the stock's increase in value?","choices":["A constructive trust on the shares, since Mesa can trace its money into them","An equitable lien on the shares, entitling Mesa to their full current value","Conversion damages of $100,000, since restitution may not exceed the loss","None beyond $100,000, since a constructive trust needs a fiduciary relationship"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A constructive trust lets a plaintiff whose property was wrongfully taken trace it into its product and recover the product itself, including its appreciation; Civil Code § 2224 makes one who gains a thing by fraud or another wrongful act an involuntary trustee of it, with no prior fiduciary relationship required. An equitable lien secures only the amount taken and is the better choice when the asset has declined in value. Restitution measured by a conscious wrongdoer's gain may exceed the plaintiff's loss.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","restitution","constructive-trust","tracing","equitable-lien"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Civ. Code § 2224","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=2224."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-reformation","prompt":"Lena and Moss agreed that Lena would sell Moss the 10-acre north parcel of her ranch. The typist who prepared the written contract mistakenly described the 100-acre south parcel, and both signed without noticing. Moss now sues to enforce the contract as written. Which remedy best protects Lena while carrying out the parties' actual bargain?","choices":["Rescission of the contract for mutual mistake, leaving the parties with no sale of either of the parcels","None, because the parol evidence rule bars any proof of terms outside the final signed writing","None, because the statute of frauds bars enforcing any land-sale term missing from the writing","Reformation of the contract to describe the north parcel, proven by evidence of the true agreement"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"When a writing fails to express the parties' actual agreement because of mutual mistake (or one party's mistake known to the other), Civil Code § 3399 permits the court to reform it to reflect what they actually agreed. Parol evidence is admissible to prove the mistake and the true agreement, and the statute of frauds does not bar reforming a writing to match the bargain both parties made. Rescission undoes the deal entirely, which does not carry out the parties' agreement on the north parcel.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","reformation","rescission","mutual-mistake"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Civ. Code § 3399","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=3399."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-reliance-damages","prompt":"A landlord breached a lease for the space where Ari planned to open her first restaurant. Before the breach, Ari spent $80,000 on design work and custom fixtures that are useless elsewhere. Her lost-profit projections are too speculative to prove with reasonable certainty, and the landlord offers no evidence that the restaurant would have lost money. What may Ari recover?","choices":["Her $80,000 in reliance expenditures, as an alternative to the unprovable profits","Nothing, because a new business's damages are always too speculative to award","Her projected profits, because the landlord's breach created the uncertainty","Only nominal damages, because she failed to prove her expected profits with certainty"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Expectation damages such as lost profits must be proven with reasonable certainty. When they cannot be, a plaintiff may recover reliance damages instead: expenditures made in preparation for or performance of the contract, reduced by any loss the breaching party proves the plaintiff would have suffered had the contract been performed. California does not flatly bar new businesses from recovering lost profits (Sargon Enterprises, Inc. v. University of Southern California (2012)), but speculative projections fail, which is when reliance damages fill the gap.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","contract-damages","reliance","expectation","new-business"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-punitive-contract","prompt":"Sterling Supply deliberately stopped delivering parts under a written contract with Rae's Machine Shop, hoping Rae would go out of business so Sterling could buy her shop cheaply. Rae sues only for breach of contract and can prove Sterling's motive by clear and convincing evidence. May she recover punitive damages?","choices":["Yes, because Civil Code § 3294 allows punitive damages on clear and convincing proof of malice","Yes, because a willful breach intended to injure the other party is treated as a tort","No, because punitive damages are unavailable for breach of contract absent an independent tort","No, because punitive damages require proof of physical injury or damage to property"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Civil Code § 3294 authorizes punitive damages only 'for the breach of an obligation not arising from contract.' California courts hold that even a willful, malicious breach of contract does not support punitive damages unless the conduct also constitutes an independent tort, such as fraud or conversion (Applied Equipment Corp. v. Litton Saudi Arabia Ltd. (1994); Cates Construction, Inc. v. Talbot Partners (1999)). Tort recovery for breach of the implied covenant is largely confined to insurance contracts. A pure contract claim cannot carry punitive damages.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","punitive-damages","breach-of-contract","civil-code-3294"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Civ. Code § 3294","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=3294."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-liquidated-damages","prompt":"A commercial lease between two businesses provides that if the tenant terminates early, it will pay $60,000 as liquidated damages. When the lease was signed, $60,000 was a reasonable estimate of the landlord's likely losses. The tenant left early, and the landlord re-let the space within a month, losing only $8,000. Is the clause enforceable?","choices":["No, because the $60,000 greatly exceeds the $8,000 the landlord actually lost","Yes, because the tenant cannot show the clause was unreasonable when the lease was made","No, unless the landlord proves its damages were impracticable or extremely difficult to fix","Yes, because a liquidated damages clause between businesses is enforceable without limit"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Civil Code § 1671(b) treats a liquidated damages provision in a non-consumer contract as valid unless the party seeking to invalidate it proves it was unreasonable under the circumstances existing when the contract was made. Reasonableness is judged prospectively, so the landlord's small actual loss is not decisive. The stricter rule of § 1671(c)–(d), under which the clause is void unless damages would be impracticable or extremely difficult to fix, applies to consumer contracts and residential leases. An unreasonable clause between businesses is still unenforceable.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","liquidated-damages","civil-code-1671"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1671","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1671."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-laches","prompt":"A recorded covenant bars any structure over 20 feet on Zane's lot. Yuki, his neighbor and the covenant's beneficiary, watched for 18 months as Zane built a 30-foot addition at great expense, saying nothing. Soon after it was finished, Yuki sued for an injunction requiring removal and for damages, within the limitations period. Zane raises laches. What is the likely effect?","choices":["Laches may bar the injunction, but it does not bar Yuki's timely claim for damages","Laches bars both the injunction and the damages, since both stem from the same delay","Laches cannot apply to either claim, because Yuki sued within the limitations period","Unclean hands, rather than laches, bars the injunction, because Yuki stayed silent"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Laches is an equitable defense requiring unreasonable delay by the plaintiff and resulting prejudice to the defendant; Yuki's silence while Zane spent heavily on construction supplies both. Because it is equitable, laches can bar equitable relief such as an injunction even when suit is filed within the limitations period, but it generally does not bar a timely legal claim for damages. Unclean hands concerns the plaintiff's own inequitable conduct relating to the subject of the suit, not mere delay.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","equitable-defenses","laches","unclean-hands"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-rem-claim-and-delivery","prompt":"Lender holds a perfected security interest in a contractor's excavator, and the contractor has defaulted. The contractor locked the excavator in a guarded yard and refuses to surrender it. Lender sues in California superior court and wants possession now, before trial. Which prejudgment remedy fits?","choices":["None, because recovering possession of personal property must await a final judgment","A writ of possession, but only if Lender proves the excavator is unique and damages inadequate","A writ of possession, on a showing of the probable validity of its claim and an undertaking","A judgment of ejectment, the action for recovering wrongfully withheld possession of property"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"California's claim and delivery procedure (CCP § 511.010 et seq.), the modern form of replevin, lets a plaintiff obtain a prejudgment writ of possession of specific personal property after a hearing at which it establishes the probable validity of its claim to possession, and generally only after it files an undertaking to protect the defendant. Because it is a legal remedy for recovering a specific chattel, the plaintiff need not show the property is unique. Ejectment is the action to recover possession of real property.","subject":"remedies","tags":["remedies","replevin","claim-and-delivery","ejectment"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"v3-ca-prac-demurrer-leave","prompt":"The court sustained a general demurrer to Vic's original complaint for failure to state facts sufficient to constitute a cause of action, without leave to amend. At the hearing, Vic's counsel described specific additional facts she could allege to supply the missing element. On appeal, what standard governs the denial of leave to amend?","choices":["It is reviewed de novo, and leave must be granted whenever the plaintiff asks for it at the hearing","It is proper unless the plaintiff filed a separate noticed motion for leave to amend before the hearing","It is proper whenever the original complaint, as filed, fails to state any cause of action at all","It is an abuse of discretion if there is a reasonable possibility amendment could cure the defect"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A general demurrer under CCP § 430.10(e) tests whether the complaint states facts sufficient to constitute a cause of action. When a demurrer is sustained without leave to amend, the reviewing court asks whether there is a reasonable possibility the defect can be cured by amendment; if so, denying leave is an abuse of discretion (Blank v. Kirwan (1985)). The plaintiff bears the burden of showing how the complaint could be amended, which Vic's counsel did. Leave is liberally granted, especially for an original complaint, but it is not automatic.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["civil-procedure","demurrer","leave-to-amend","section-430-10"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 430.10","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=430.10."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-prac-summary-adjudication","prompt":"In a California breach of contract action, the defendant moves for summary adjudication of a single issue: that the plaintiff cannot recover the lost-profits component of its contract damages. Granting the motion would leave the contract cause of action, and the plaintiff's other damages, intact. The parties have not stipulated to the motion. How should the court rule?","choices":["Grant it if there is no triable issue, because summary adjudication may resolve any part of any claim","Deny it, because it would not completely dispose of a cause of action or another issue the statute lists","Grant it if there is no triable issue, because any damages issue may be resolved by summary adjudication","Deny it, because California allows summary judgment only on an entire action, never on part of one"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under CCP § 437c(f)(1), summary adjudication may be granted only if it completely disposes of a cause of action, an affirmative defense, a claim for punitive damages under Civil Code § 3294, or an issue of duty. Ruling on one item of contract damages does none of these, so the motion fails (absent the separate joint-stipulation procedure the statute allows). Federal Rule 56 is broader, permitting partial summary judgment on part of a claim or defense. California does permit summary adjudication of whole causes of action and the other listed issues, so it is not all-or-nothing.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["civil-procedure","summary-judgment","summary-adjudication","section-437c"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 437c","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=437c."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-prac-998-offer","prompt":"In a California personal injury case, the defendant served a valid written offer under CCP § 998 to allow judgment for $150,000. The plaintiff let it lapse, went to trial, and obtained a $120,000 judgment. What is the most likely consequence of the § 998 offer?","choices":["Her judgment is reduced to zero, because she rejected an offer larger than her recovery","None, because § 998 penalties apply only if she rejected the offer in bad faith","She loses her postoffer costs and pays the defendant's, possibly including its expert fees","She must pay the defendant's attorney's fees after the offer, in addition to its costs"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Under CCP § 998(c)(1), if a plaintiff fails to accept a defendant's valid § 998 offer and then fails to obtain a more favorable judgment, she cannot recover her own postoffer costs and must pay the defendant's costs from the time of the offer; the court may also, in its discretion, require her to pay a reasonable sum for the defendant's expert witnesses. Section 998 does not itself shift attorney's fees, does not erase the judgment, and does not depend on the rejecting party's motive (the good-faith requirement applies to the offer).","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["civil-procedure","section-998","settlement-offers","costs"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 998","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=998."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-prac-128-7-safe-harbor","prompt":"Believing the plaintiff's fraud claim had no evidentiary support, defense counsel filed and served a motion for sanctions under CCP § 128.7 on the same day, without first giving the plaintiff a chance to withdraw the claim. The claim was in fact baseless. How should the court rule on the motion?","choices":["Grant it, because sanctions are mandatory once a claim is found to lack factual support","Deny it, because the motion must be served 21 days before filing to allow withdrawal","Grant it, because the safe-harbor period applies only to sanctions under federal Rule 11","Deny it, because sanctions may be imposed only on the court's own order to show cause"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CCP § 128.7, modeled on Federal Rule 11, requires that a sanctions motion be served but not filed or presented to the court unless, within 21 days after service, the challenged paper is not withdrawn or corrected. That safe harbor lets the offending party cure, and a motion filed without honoring it must be denied. Sanctions under § 128.7 are discretionary even when a violation is found, and although the court may proceed on its own order to show cause, a party may also move for sanctions if it follows the safe-harbor procedure.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["civil-procedure","sanctions","section-128-7","safe-harbor"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"easy","references":[{"title":"Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 128.7","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&sectionNum=128.7."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-prac-1235-prior-inconsistent","prompt":"Shortly after a shooting, Wren told a detective in an unsworn interview that she saw the defendant fire the gun. At the California trial, Wren testifies that she did not see who fired. The prosecutor offers her statement to the detective, and Wren is given a chance to explain or deny it. How may the jury use the statement?","choices":["Only to impeach her, because the earlier statement was not made under oath","Only to impeach her, because a witness's prior statement is never proof of its truth","As substantive proof, but only if Wren first admits that she made the statement","As substantive proof that the defendant fired, as well as to impeach her"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Evidence Code § 1235 makes a testifying witness's prior inconsistent statement admissible for its truth if offered in compliance with § 770 (the witness has a chance to explain or deny it, or has not been excused). No oath is required. By contrast, FRE 801(d)(1)(A) treats a prior inconsistent statement as substantive evidence only if it was given under penalty of perjury at a trial, hearing, other proceeding, or deposition, so in federal court this unsworn interview could be used only to impeach. California v. Green (1970) upheld California's rule under the Confrontation Clause because the declarant testifies and can be cross-examined. Wren need not admit making the statement; the detective can prove it.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","evidence","hearsay","prior-inconsistent-statement","section-1235"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"medium","references":[{"title":"Cal. Evid. Code § 1235","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=EVID&sectionNum=1235."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-prac-1103-victim-character","prompt":"Charged with assault, Darius claims self-defense, asserting that the alleged victim attacked first. He offers a bartender's testimony that the victim started a violent fight with another patron last year; Darius did not know about that fight. Under the California Evidence Code, is the testimony admissible, and what follows if it is admitted?","choices":["Yes, to show the victim was the aggressor, and the prosecution may then offer evidence of Darius's violent character","No, because character must be proved by reputation or opinion testimony, not by specific instances of conduct","No, because the evidence is relevant only if Darius knew of the earlier fight when he confronted the victim","Yes, to show the victim was the aggressor, but the prosecution may rebut only with the victim's peaceful character"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Evidence Code § 1103(a) lets a criminal defendant offer evidence of the victim's character, by opinion, reputation, or specific instances of conduct, to prove the victim acted in conformity with it, for example as the aggressor. Unlike FRE 405(a), California allows specific instances for this purpose, and the defendant's knowledge matters only when the evidence is offered to show his own reasonable fear. Once the defense offers evidence of the victim's violent character, § 1103(b) lets the prosecution offer evidence of the defendant's character for violence, not merely the victim's peacefulness. All of this remains subject to § 352.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","evidence","character-evidence","section-1103","self-defense"],"focus":["california","defense"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Evid. Code § 1103","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=EVID&sectionNum=1103."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-prac-1240-no-present-sense","prompt":"In a California civil trial over an intersection crash, a pedestrian testifies that a bystander standing next to her said calmly, as the cars approached, 'That blue sedan is running the red light.' Nothing suggests the bystander was excited or startled when she spoke, and she cannot be found. Is the statement admissible to prove the sedan ran the light?","choices":["No, because California has no present-sense-impression exception, and the calm remark was not spontaneous","Yes, as a present sense impression, because the bystander described the event while perceiving it","Yes, as a spontaneous statement, because the bystander was describing the event as it was occurring","Yes, as a contemporaneous statement, because it was made at the same time as the conduct described"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Evidence Code § 1240 admits a statement narrating an event the declarant perceived only if it was made spontaneously while the declarant was under the stress of excitement caused by that perception. California has no counterpart to FRE 803(1)'s present sense impression exception, which would admit this calm, simultaneous description in federal court. Evidence Code § 1241 (contemporaneous statements) is narrower: it covers statements offered to explain, qualify, or make understandable the declarant's own conduct, made while she was engaged in it, not a bystander's description of someone else's driving.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["california-distinction","evidence","hearsay","spontaneous-statement","section-1240"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"hard","references":[{"title":"Cal. Evid. Code § 1240","url":"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=EVID&sectionNum=1240."}]},{"id":"v3-ca-prac-tameny","prompt":"Nell, an at-will employee of a California trucking company, was fired after she refused her supervisor's order to falsify vehicle-inspection records, which state law makes a crime. She sues the company in tort for wrongful termination. Is her claim likely to succeed?","choices":["No, because an at-will employee may be fired for any reason not based on a protected trait","Yes, but she is limited to contract damages, since the claim arises from her employment","No, because she first had to exhaust her administrative remedies with the Labor Commissioner","Yes, because firing her for refusing to break the law violates a fundamental public policy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Tameny v. Atlantic Richfield Co. (1980) recognized a tort claim for wrongful discharge in violation of public policy, available even to at-will employees. The policy must be tethered to a constitutional or statutory provision (or a regulation implementing one), benefit the public, be well established at the time of discharge, and be fundamental and substantial; refusing to commit a crime is the paradigm case. Because the claim sounds in tort, tort damages, including emotional distress and, on proper proof, punitive damages, are available. Exhausting remedies with the Labor Commissioner is not a prerequisite to a Tameny claim.","subject":"californiaPractice","tags":["employment","wrongful-termination","tameny","public-policy"],"focus":["california"],"difficulty":"medium"}]};
